HT_DATA.load("detail", {"Unzc731iCUY":{"wy":"HN treats it as the canonical, near-universally recommended lecture on giving talks and presentations.","q":{"x":"drop what you're doing and watch it now","i":25137612,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":45117990,"author":"loughnane","month":"2025-09","story":"How to Give a Good Talk","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best lecture of its kind, rewatched several times.","quote":"It's the best of its kind that I've seen.","text":"This title reminds me of Patrick Winston's \"How to Speak\" lecture at MIT. It's the best of its kind that I've seen. An hour long and I've watched it several times. Even my highschooler was impressed. [VIDEO] EDIT: no way, it was just posted yesterday. Glad it got the attention."},{"id":40681895,"author":"robomartin","month":"2024-06","story":"Start presentations on the second slide","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Reliable presentation framework used for technical and business talks.","quote":"likely in the top-5 when it come to advice on how to give presentations","text":"In my opinion, Professor Patrick Winston's lecture (MIT) is likely in the top-5 when it come to advice on how to give presentations. I have used his framework multiple times, for both technical and business presentations in groups of a few to hundreds of people and have always had good results. Well worth watching. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38831563,"author":"croo","month":"2024-01","story":"Ask HN: What talks do you consider real gems?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Lecture that follows its own presentation framework.","quote":"is the lecture you need to watch about public speaking and presentation","text":"MIT lecture by Patric Winston: How to speak is the lecture you need to watch about public speaking and presentation. The most awesome thing about the lecture that it is self describing it's tools and framework in an awesome way and built up exactly as it's content describe is should. It's on youtube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38741214,"author":"robviren","month":"2023-12","story":"","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny, practical lecture on slides and communicating well.","quote":"Encourage anyone interested in communicating to watch this.","text":"Encourage anyone interested in communicating to watch this. [VIDEO] He has a pretty funny response to someone asking him to review slides. He immediately says there are too many words and too many slides without even looking. Which I have found to be true 90% of the time. Excellent presentation."},{"id":36703001,"author":"1970-01-01","month":"2023-07","story":"A gambler's guide to giving talks","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Advice for delivering a memorable message in talks.","quote":"The advice is as good as gold.","text":">The most memorable talk I’ve ever seen was a lunchtime keynote by Harry Kroto, a Nobel laureate in chemistry, at an undergraduate research conference in Tallahassee in 2004. I have no idea why I was there, what the conference was about, or what he said— OK, so if your goal is to not have a forgotten message, watch How to Speak by Patrick Winston and then read his book Make It Clear . The advice is as good as gold. [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":32184716,"author":"hazelnut-tree","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: Any good resources you have used becoming a better confidence speaker?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Heuristic rules for improving speaking in critical situations.","quote":"is excellent and very popular","text":"The following presentation called How to speak on YouTube is excellent and very popular. The talk is given by Patrick Winston (1943 – 2019) who was a professor at MIT (Massachusetts Institute of Technology). Description: \"The talk is intended to improve your speaking ability in critical situations by teaching you a few heuristic rules.\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":26025879,"author":"cmehdy","month":"2021-02","story":"A tool to create slides using Markdown easily for you","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Course on speaking, well worth the time.","quote":"An hour of life well spent!","text":"I've got to reference Patrick Wilson's \"How To Speak\" course from MIT OpenCourseWare here[0]. An hour of life well spent! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25537420,"author":"janvdberg","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: Best Talks of 2020?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk on how to give talks, with lessons for any speaker.","quote":"it is really good. Anyone who gives talks can learn a few things from this.","text":"Not sure if this fits the bill, but: How To Speak by Patrick Winston [1] I watched this 'talk' on 'how to give talks' last week. It was uploaded at the end of 2019 and recorded 2018 (Patrick Winston passed away in 2019) and it is really good. Anyone who gives talks can learn a few things from this. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25137612,"author":"omginternets","month":"2020-11","story":"Ask HN: What is the best money you have spent on professional development?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Heuristics for speaking well, dressing your ideas properly.","quote":"drop what you're doing and watch it now","text":"I was lucky enough to get paid for this but I'll say it anyway: learning to speak is the only thing of lasting value I have gotten out of startup accelerators. I would have gladly drop a few grand to acquire that skill. In fact, I'm considering hiring a speaking coach to improve. I've historically been a strong written communicator, but it turns out that speaking is very different from writing. In fact, I had thought of myself as a pretty strong speaker due to my experience giving scholarly presentations ... how wrong I was. Academic talks are a different beast altogether. If you haven't seen Patrick Winston's How to Speak lecture [0], drop what you're doing and watch it now. I'll leave you with a (paraphrased) quote from his lecture: your ideas are like your children and you don't want to send them off into the world dressed in rags. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22212416,"author":"BossingAround","month":"2020-02","story":"How to give a great presentation: advice from a legendary adman (2012)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Patrick Winston's lecture on giving effective talks.","quote":"Very much recommended!","text":"An interesting lecture on the topic is also How To Speak by late MIT professor Patrick Winston [1]. Very much recommended! [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45555951,"author":"ninesnines","month":"2025-10","story":"Paper2video: Automatic video generation from scientific papers","stance":"rec","why":"Teaches making presentations engaging and memorable.","quote":"For anyone thinking of using this: please watch","text":"Ah I guess if you’re very bad at presentations, then this could be beneficial. However, scientific presentations are meant to be communicating science and making things stick to your audience (no matter if it’s scientists or children you’re presenting to). This does not fix that problem at all. For anyone thinking of using this: please watch: [VIDEO] and maybe a talk from Jane Goodall on how to engagingly show your science. I would hate to see a lot of conference presentations be made with this generator. Another thing that improved my personal presentation skills was noting down why I liked a presentation or why I didn’t - what specific things a person did to make it engaging. Just paying attention to that improved my presentation skills enormously"},{"id":31062539,"author":"rg111","month":"2022-04","story":"How to write more clearly, think more clearly, and learn complex material [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Winston's famous talk, related to his book Make It Clear.","quote":"his now famous video- How to Speak","text":"Make it Clear by Patrick Henry Winston [0] is slightly related to this. I learned a lot of things from it. I first got to know about him through his now famous video- How to Speak [1]. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30643588,"author":"thebeardisred","month":"2022-03","story":"Tech talks don’t have to be boring","stance":"rec","why":"Relevant lecture on giving talks.","quote":"I came to mention \"How to Speak","text":"Hear hear! I came to mention \"How to Speak\" ( [VIDEO] ) but it makes even more sense to amplify you thoughts."},{"id":27750889,"author":"Rzor","month":"2021-07","story":"Ask HN: Best online speech / public speaking course?","stance":"rec","why":"Timestamped lecture covering openings, tools, persuading and finishing.","quote":"you probably would like to watch this","text":"I don't know about the course itself, but you probably would like to watch this: [VIDEO] (How To Speak by Patrick Winston) Content: 00:16 - Introduction 03:11 - Rules of Engagement 04:15 - How to Start 05:38 - Four Sample Heuristics 10:17 - The Tools: Time and Place 13:24 - The Tools: Boards, Props, and Slides 36:30 - Informing: Promise, Inspiration, How To Think 41:30 - Persuading: Oral Exams, Job Talks, Getting Famous 53:06 - How to Stop: Final Slide, Final Words 56:35 - Final Words: Joke, Thank You, Examples"},{"id":27408762,"author":"open-source-ux","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: Books for Effective Communication","stance":"rec","why":"Winston's popular talk, with material also in his book.","quote":"I recommend watching the talk above first to help you decide whether the book will appeal to you","text":"Patrick Winston (1943-2019) was a computer scientist at MIT. Every year he gave a popular talk titled How to Speak . Here is a recording of the talk from 2018: How To Speak by Patrick Winston : [VIDEO] A book of his insights was published in 2020: Make It Clear: Speak and Write to Persuade and Inform . I'm half-way through the book and it is good so far. Although the book pitches itself as suitable for anyone who writes or speaks, many of the examples in the book have an emphasis on speaking in an academic or teaching setting. However, there are good insights that anyone can learn from. I recommend watching the talk above first to help you decide whether the book will appeal to you (the material in the lecture is in the book as are additional insights). There are lots of reviews of the book on the Amazon US site too."},{"id":25718651,"author":"dukeofdoom","month":"2021-01","story":"Powerful Life Skills for the New Decade","stance":"rec","why":"Winston's lecture listed among helpful self-improvement resources.","quote":"","text":"This might be helpful. How to Trick Your Brain to Like Doing Hard Things – Atomic Habits by James Clear [LINK] How To Speak by Patrick Winston [VIDEO] Help with Writing: George Orwell -Politics and the English Language [LINK] Philosophy - A Guide to Happiness (7 parts) by Alain de Botton [LINK] Namaste Yoga (DVD set sold on Amazon) Best Yoga series I know. Combines synchronized breathing with movement/meditation. Especially nice to do after a hot bath, when muscles are warmed up. [LINK]"},{"id":25228826,"author":"moocthrow","month":"2020-11","story":"Ask HN: How to Learn the Art of Storytelling?","stance":"rec","why":"Guidance for telling a story to a live audience.","quote":"If your goal is to learn how to tell a story to a live audience see this","text":"If your goal is to learn how to tell a story to a live audience see this: [VIDEO] Otherwise I'd suggest you read up a lot of literature, start with classic folk tales like those collected by the brothers Grimm. The more you read the easier it will become to write a proper story. Lastly as you're reading I'd suggest you start writing material yourself and maybe publishing it online on some web novel sites. At first people will be very critical of what you have written, but with time it should change for the better. Lastly, writing is a habit, just like coding. A habit should be repeated daily to become stronger. Read and write each day, unless you're feeling foggy or in need of rest. After a certain amount of time it should just click, but realize that that amount of time varies from person to person, some may never break that threshold, others are talented and are able to break through just like that. Best of luck!"},{"id":38664579,"author":"pyjarrett","month":"2023-12","story":"Andy Matuschak – Self-Teaching, Spaced Repetition, Why Books Don't Work","stance":"reference","why":"Resource for lecturers among techniques for learning.","quote":"For lecturers, there's \"How to Speak","text":"The real problem is teaching children that \"reading\" is merely decoding words off a page, and \"listening\" is merely hearing the words someone says. The book itself is a way to open the door to learning, not the end point. Reading subject titles first, cross-referencing, and working examples and doing projects help teach and cement ideas. There's techniques like SQ4R [1] for reading, and hugely applicable to general technical and article reading is \"How to read a paper\" [2]. For lecturers, there's \"How to Speak\" [3], and for those attending a talk there's the Cornell Notes system. [4] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [VIDEO] [4]: [LINK]"},{"id":36927846,"author":"erie","month":"2023-07","story":"US Army Field Manual on Leadership (1990) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Winston's opening argument that students need communication skills.","quote":"There ought to be a similar protection for students because students shouldn't go out into life without the ability to communicate","text":"That army metaphor is used by the late PATRICK WINSTON -0:19: The Uniform Code of Military Justice specifies court martial for any officer who sends a soldier into battle without a weapon. There ought to be a similar protection for students because students shouldn't go out into life without the ability to communicate, .... [VIDEO]"},{"id":30647061,"author":"sshine","month":"2022-03","story":"No hello – please don't just say hello in chat","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Don't introduce yourself, act like you were in the room all along.\" This surely is a good advice from someone in the room greeting someone who just entered. It's called a protocol. Assume \"hello\" means something. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["wO2lWmgEK1Y",1],["7vfqkvwW2fs",1],["iuumnjJWFO4",1],["LsGZ_2RuJ2A",1],["H2tuKiiznsY",1],["otAcmD6XEEE",1],["0vJJlVBVTFg",1],["ZhuHCtR3xq8",1],["N1pIYI5JQLE",1],["2sjqTHE0zok",1],["MijmeoH9LT4",1],["-FENubmZGj8",1],["mf5otGNbkuc",1],["RuOvn4UqznU",1],["nv7-YM4wno8",1],["iG9CE55wbtY",1],["zDZFcDGpL4U",1],["QyJZzq0v7Z4",1]]},"IcgmSRJHu_8":{"wy":"HN repeatedly recommends it as a talk that changed how people model data, with ideas that apply well beyond Elm to any typed language.","q":{"x":"the talk is Elm lang focused, but the concepts still apply to other languages.","i":40164027,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40164027,"author":"terminatornet","month":"2024-04","story":"Make invalid states unrepresentable (2023)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Elm-focused talk whose concepts apply to other languages","quote":"the talk is Elm lang focused, but the concepts still apply to other languages.","text":"one of my favorite talks on this topic, \"Making Impossible States Impossible\" by Richard Feldman: [VIDEO] the talk is Elm lang focused, but the concepts still apply to other languages."},{"id":36049123,"author":"terminatornet","month":"2023-05","story":"Why I Use Elm in 2023","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Elm-focused talk with broadly applicable programming advice","quote":"the talks are geared towards elm but I think they offer some great advice applicable to programming in general.","text":"highly recommend the talks \"making impossible states impossible\" and \"make data structures\" by richard feldman. [VIDEO] [LINK] the talks are geared towards elm but I think they offer some great advice applicable to programming in general."},{"id":18218130,"author":"kilian","month":"2018-10","story":"Ask HN: What are some of the best technical talks you've heard?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed how they model data in code","quote":"has really changed the way I approach dealing with data in my code and was very insightful.","text":"Making impossible states impossible\" by Richard Feldman: [VIDEO] has really changed the way I approach dealing with data in my code and was very insightful."},{"id":17933911,"author":"jasim","month":"2018-09","story":"Try OCaml","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Clear, concrete examples of making invalid states unrepresentable","quote":"has given an absolute gem of a talk","text":"The type system is a lot more expressive than the ones found in Java/C#/C++, thanks to Algebraic Data Types. Yaron Minsky (Jane Street, largest OCaml user) coined the term \"make invalid states unrepresentable\" to explain how ADTs help in enlisting the help of the compiler to help us express our intent correctly, and Richard Feldman (NoRedInk, largest Elm user) has given an absolute gem of a talk that dives into that idea with relatable, concrete examples. It is titled \"Making Impossible States Impossible\" and should clarify this concept nicely. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16046190,"author":"joshuahornby","month":"2018-01","story":"Ask HN: What’s your favorite talk from 2017?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed how they think about programming","quote":"Really changed how I think about programming.","text":"\"Making Impossible States Impossible\" by Richard Feldman [VIDEO] Really changed how I think about programming."},{"id":37772754,"author":"terminatornet","month":"2023-10","story":"Strong typing, a hill I'm willing to die on","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how to use types to disallow illegal states","quote":"This talk does a great job at articulating how to use types to disallow invalid/illegal states.","text":"It's a shame so many people have had bad experiences with static typing due to Typescript. In languages like Rust and Elm, static typing truly feels like a super power when I'm coding, particularly when modeling my applications possible states. This talk does a great job at articulating how to use types to disallow invalid/illegal states. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29035356,"author":"amitu","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: Programming talks that made something click for you?","stance":"rec","why":"Listed as a talk that made something click","quote":"","text":"\"Making Impossible States Impossible\" by Richard Feldman (2016): [VIDEO] \"Understanding style\" by Matthew Griffith (2017): [LINK]"},{"id":24687785,"author":"1-more","month":"2020-10","story":"Applying “make invalid states unrepresentable”","stance":"rec","why":"Great talk on making invalid states unrepresentable","quote":"A great talk on this subject is","text":"A great talk on this subject is \"Making Impossible States Impossible\" by Richard Feldman from Elm Conf 2016 [VIDEO]"},{"id":24861327,"author":"jesperlang","month":"2020-10","story":"What can a pipe wrench teach us about software engineering?","stance":"rec","why":"Technique for modeling state that keeps code sane","quote":"It's a great way to keep you sane!","text":"I've seen this way of state modelling pop up more and more recent year in me feeds. Make impossible states impossible. It's a great way to keep you sane! [VIDEO] (Elm) [LINK] (Kotlin)"},{"id":21750572,"author":"jasim","month":"2019-12","story":"Ask HN: How to improve code quality while maintaining decent velocity?","stance":"rec","why":"Advice to think about types first","quote":"Learn to think about types first, and the algorithms as a natural consequence of the structure of your data.","text":"* Learn to think about types first, and the algorithms as a natural consequence of the structure of your data. Richard Feldman: [VIDEO] , * Use a statically typed language that allows fast refactoring. Ideally a typed functional language like Elm, Reason, OCaml, or Haskell. I recently shipped a complex Javascript project (without types) and it is one of the few instances of my decade-old professional career where I had that level of confidence in the robustness of a piece of code I wrote. It remained sturdy in production even in the presence of unforeseen conditions. This was thanks to the habits I picked up from typed FP: parsing and validating all data at the boundaries of the system, better design by modelling data to make inconsistent states impossible, and automatically handling all edge-cases at all times. So even if you don't actively use such a language in your workplace, there really is a payoff to learning it in making you a better programmer overall."},{"id":21477991,"author":"bretthopper","month":"2019-11","story":"Parse, Don’t Validate","stance":"rec","why":"Good talk on the same concept as parse, don't validate","quote":"I just watched a good Elm conference talk about this same concept","text":"I just watched a good Elm conference talk about this same concept called \"Making Impossible States Impossible\" - [VIDEO]"},{"id":18371870,"author":"jasim","month":"2018-11","story":"Book Review: A Philosophy of Software Design","stance":"rec","why":"Deeper dive with many examples on making invalid states impossible","quote":"Richard Feldman dives deeper into the topic with a lot more examples in","text":"That'll be a great addition to the book. Can I also recommend Yaron Minsky's talk on OCaml, specifically the part where he talks about \"making invalid states impossible\": [LINK] I've found this to be the single most valuable programming technique I've learnt. Richard Feldman dives deeper into the topic with a lot more examples in [VIDEO]"},{"id":16008160,"author":"jasim","month":"2017-12","story":"Writing ReasonML Bindings for JavaScript APIs","stance":"rec","why":"Resource on designing types so invalid states cannot occur","quote":"Here are some resources on this wonderful programming technique","text":"There is no escaping the mapping. It doesn't matter if it is Reason or PureScript, Elm, or Fable - you have to parse the incoming data into a static type if you want to enjoy the benefits of types. The good thing is that you can build the rest of your system with a firm guarantee on the shape and structure of the data. No null checks, no missing keys. If you are careful with your parsing you can raise exceptions or return None if the incoming data is not well-formed (missing keys or even extra keys). Reason/BuckleScript when run on a Javascript runtime also has a `Js.t` type which is basically the Javascript object. You do have to define the shape of it similar to how you'd define a record type, but you don't need to convert it into a native record. JS arrays are also natively supported - it maps 1:1 to BuckleScript's array. I like to convert arrays into lists since they are better suited for value-based programming (pattern-matching over [head, ...tail] inside recursive functions). And if you create your types in a way that invalid states are impossible, you've just reduced the cognitive load needed to work on the system for the rest of its life by an order of magnitude. Here are some resources on this wonderful programming technique: - [LINK] - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":15284835,"author":"aczerepinski","month":"2017-09","story":"To type or not to type: quantifying detectable bugs in JavaScript","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on using types to make impossible states unrepresentable","quote":"There's a great talk on this subject by Richard Feldman from Elm Conf.","text":"There's a great talk on this subject by Richard Feldman from Elm Conf. I haven't used typescript so I'm not sure if the techniques translate well. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46965268,"author":"1-more","month":"2026-02","story":"Parse, Don't Validate (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Related talk on making impossible states impossible","quote":"A related talk is Richard Feldman's","text":"A related talk is Richard Feldman's \"Making Impossible States Impossible.\" Richard wrote a number of Elm packages and is the creator of the Roc language. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37529893,"author":"nateabele","month":"2023-09","story":"We use TypeScript not based on preference, but because we want to make money","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for how types eliminate untestable invalid states","quote":"","text":"Sure, but the point is, a halfway decent type system will eliminate whole classes of errors—consequently eliminating whole swaths of things you would ever need to write tests for. What's more, though, is that a good type system will help you 'Make Impossible States Impossible'[0]—even more behavior you don't have to test because bad behavior is simply not representable in your program. Tests are strictly more powerful in the small, because they can be arbitrarily complex, but I would say a (really good) type system is more powerful in the large, because you get way more leverage in terms of overall program correctness. A lot of my personal projects are in Elm (yeah, yeah, bring on the hate)—the models are really well-defined, but the test suites usually only have a handful of tests of behavior I really want to verify. Haven't even played with the property checker yet, but again, that's something that is strictly more powerful than unit tests, but you can only get it* if you have a type system. [0] [VIDEO] * Okay that's not really true: see clojure.spec in Clojure—but there are so few counterexamples that it's true for most practical purposes"},{"id":35800616,"author":"scns","month":"2023-05","story":"What Is Type-Level Programming?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as showing strong type systems already enable this","quote":"","text":"With strong type system a la ML (OCaml, Haskell, F#, Rust, Elm etc) it is allready possible, am i wrong? [VIDEO]"},{"id":16317490,"author":"jasim","month":"2018-02","story":"Reason ML toolchain","stance":"reference","why":"Cited in discussion of typed functional programming","quote":"","text":"ReasonML is IMO quite ready for production work if you use it for building server-side applications with NodeJS or rich front-end applications with React (ReasonReact). The target audience for Reason (at least for the moment) are web application developers who want to level up as programmers and want to build elegant, sturdy production systems. Reason and Elm together are democratizing statically typed functional programming and I believe that the next 10 years are going to see a massive change in the programming language landscape, with the practice of typed FP finally finding adoption in mainstream commercial programming. The allure of Reason is in its packaging of OCaml to the web browser (which is made possible by BuckleScript - the actual OCaml to JS compiler doing the heavy lifting), its syntax that is familiar to the vast majority of programmers today, and the large ecosystem of bindings, libraries, and documentation that the team has put out. OCaml is a very pragmatic language; its main pull is its powerful static type system and immutable, functional programming, but the \"O\" in OCaml stands for \"Objective\" - it is an implementation of CAML with support for object-oriented programming. But you almost never need objects. OCaml's module system and its idioms allows you to write well-encapsulated code without mutation, and when you actually need to write honest to metal imperative code, you can do it with abandon because OCaml is not a \"pure\" functional language like Has"},{"id":15419701,"author":"jon49","month":"2017-10","story":"Diminishing returns of static typing","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for making impossible states impossible via types","quote":"","text":"Languages like F# give a nice sweet spot between static typing and dynamic typing. It has Type Providers that \"generate\" code on the fly as you are typing. You don't need to specify all the types, it will infer many types for you. So, you almost feel like you are writing in a dynamic language but you it tells you if you are writing something incorrectly. I would not consider a language to be modern unless it has Type Providers I consider this to be such an essential feature. I believe Idris and F# are the only languages that have it. People are trying to push TypeScript to add it - who knows if it will happen. Many are saying that if you have a dynamic language you just need to be disciplined and write many tests. With good static typed languages like F# you can't even write tests on certain business logic since the way you write your code you make \"impossible states impossible\", see [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["M4AhABManTw",1],["baxtyeFVn3w",1],["-J8YyfrSwTk",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1]]},"rNu8XDBSn10":{"wy":"HN recommends it as the standard short answer whenever someone confuses Britain, the UK and England.","q":{"x":"The best thing to do is to watch this great, quick and witty primer on the whole thing Britain/UK/England thing","i":9528882,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":19804103,"author":"SapporoChris","month":"2019-05","story":"China’s vast fleet is tipping the balance in the Pacific","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny explainer on UK, Great Britain and England","quote":"I haven't laughed so hard in a long time.","text":"Thank you so much for the introduction to CGP Grey. Watching 'The Difference between the United Kingdom, Great Britain and England Explained' [VIDEO] The line, \"While you can call them all British it's not recommended as the four countries generally do not like each other.\" hooked me, I haven't laughed so hard in a long time. Rest of my day is gone watching these videos, I'm down the rabbit hole."},{"id":15387483,"author":"Mz","month":"2017-10","story":"How supermarkets choose where to open and close","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Witty five-minute explainer on UK, Britain and England","quote":"5 mins of witty, brilliant lowdown on The Difference between the United Kingdom, Great Britain and England Explained.","text":"[VIDEO] 5 mins of witty, brilliant lowdown on The Difference between the United Kingdom, Great Britain and England Explained."},{"id":15202815,"author":"bmelton","month":"2017-09","story":"The Equifax breach may be the worst leak of personal info ever","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Entertaining geography video, Grey's first ever","quote":"I honestly can't remember a CGP Grey video that I wasn't thoroughly entertained by","text":"Others have already chimed in here, but I honestly can't remember a CGP Grey video that I wasn't thoroughly entertained by, even when I already knew all of the facts presented. For amusement's sake, I really enjoy his videos on geography, such as (his first video ever) this one on the UK (which I guess might need updating soon?): [VIDEO] or this one on Scotland: [LINK] or my favorite geographical video, on the Vatican: [LINK] That said, they're all pretty great, so I'd just start with this list of all his videos in ascending order: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9528882,"author":"Twirrim","month":"2015-05","story":"So Long, and Thanks for All the Fish","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Quick witty primer on Britain, UK and England differences","quote":"The best thing to do is to watch this great, quick and witty primer on the whole thing Britain/UK/England thing","text":"Ireland is not part of the UK, but it is part of Britain. Northern Ireland is part of the UK, but that's a completely different country from Ireland. Being UK born or based gives you no privileges over the .ie domain at all. The best thing to do is to watch this great, quick and witty primer on the whole thing Britain/UK/England thing: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38035590,"author":"lathiat","month":"2023-10","story":"Yugoslavia's Digital Twin: when a country's internet domain outlives the nation","stance":"rec","why":"Explains UK, Great Britain and England differences","quote":"A good watch to understand the above","text":"\"The Difference between the UK, Great Britain & England Explained\" by CGP Grey: [VIDEO] A good watch to understand the above"},{"id":21211792,"author":"lathiat","month":"2019-10","story":"The Isle of Man is not in the UK","stance":"rec","why":"Covers differences between UK, Great Britain and England","quote":"CGP Grey has a great video","text":"CGP Grey has a great video: \"The Difference between the United Kingdom, Great Britain and England Explained\" which covers a lot of this. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13289011,"author":"anexprogrammer","month":"2016-12","story":"EveryPolitician: Open dataset on politicians","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the UK's constituent countries","quote":"CGP Grey explains UK","text":"> having the UK, Wales and Scotland all on the list is somewhat confusing Why? UK is comprised of countries with their own legislatures (with devolved and varying powers). England doesn't. Scotland, Wales and Northern Ireland have parliaments, and their own politicians so need an entry. They all elect MPs to the Westminster UK parliament as well. Just as all these areas elect EU MEPs too. CGP Grey explains UK: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12127353,"author":"lathiat","month":"2016-07","story":"Britain explained in 105 seconds","stance":"rec","why":"Clarifies relationship between Britain, UK, Scotland, England","quote":"this CGP Grey video helps a lot","text":"Relatedly unrelated.. if you want to understand the relationship between Great Britain, the UK, Scotland, England, etc.. then this CGP Grey video helps a lot: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["r-TLSBdHe1A",1],["gUnxzVOs3rk",1],["yT9bit2-1pg",1],["oHf1vD5_b5I",1],["QMiubC6LdTA",1],["NSuaUok-wTY",1],["_tpB-B8BXk0",1],["noQcWra6sbU",1],["OpEB6hCpIGM",1],["izxXGuVL21o",1],["uYbdx4I7STg",1],["ra_9zU-mnl8",1],["4YYvBLAF4T8",1],["a9b9IYqsb_U",1],["1qSTcxt2t74",1],["2KZb2_vcNTg",1],["NmGaXEmfTIo",1],["ZUvGfuLlZus",1],["DOWDNBu9DkU",1],["v_UyVmITiYQ",1]]},"Z7Wl2FW2TcA":{"wy":"HN commenters repeatedly recommend it as the best explanation of why the CA system is broken and what alternatives exist.","q":{"x":"best talk I know of this subject","i":4111124,"y":"2012"},"m":[{"id":6724876,"author":"rakoo","month":"2013-11","story":"Moving forward on improving HTTP's security","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Explains the CA problem and how Convergence helps.","quote":"it does a great job at explaining","text":"Perspectives/Convergence really is a great system, but it unfortunately still has several problems: - it completely leaks your browsing history: you basically ask a notary \"what's the certificate you see for kinkyneighbors.com?\". Convergence addresses this, though - it requires network-heavy intermediaries for all the browsing of all the people around the world. - it still doesn't solve authenticity: an attacker could very well be controlling all connections arriving at your house, or leaving the target's server, and fool everyone Convergence/Perspectives should be coupled with certificate pinning, aka storing _really_ trusted authorities (ie verified by hand) on your computer. Guess what ? [Moxie's next project is just that [0] (For anyone curious, I highly recommend Moxie's talk [1] about Convergence, it does a great job at explaining what's the problem, what's Convergence and how it can solve at least part of it) [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":4111124,"author":"4ad","month":"2012-06","story":"Setting out plans to monitor all Internet use in the UK","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Best talk on CA trust and MITM problems.","quote":"best talk I know of this subject","text":"Businesses (and governments) can legitimately buy root keys that allow MITMing any SSL connection or they could just be a CA themselves (any CA can MITM the whole internet). Here's the best talk I know of this subject: BlackHat USA 2011: SSL And The Future Of Authenticity: [VIDEO]"},{"id":3292674,"author":"JonnieCache","month":"2011-11","story":"US judge orders hundreds of sites \"de-indexed\" from Google, Facebook","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk with the right ideas for CA-free encryption.","quote":"Start by watching this Blackhat talk I linked elsewhere in the thread, the guy has the right kind of ideas","text":"Yeah, I edited my list to include a CA-free encrypted replacement for TCP, just before I saw your comment. To all the super-geniuses reading this: burn your socialmobilelocal startup to the ground tomorrow and do this instead. Start by watching this Blackhat talk I linked elsewhere in the thread, the guy has the right kind of ideas: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2965644,"author":"jtdowney","month":"2011-09","story":"Convergence, distributed, secure strategy replacing certificate authorities","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Moxie Marlinspike on web authenticity and Convergence.","quote":"I highly recommend watching his Blackhat 2011 talk","text":"Moxie Marlinspike is the creator of Convergence. I highly recommend watching his Blackhat 2011 talk ( [VIDEO] ) covering authenticity on the web and Convergence."},{"id":11332226,"author":"Strom","month":"2016-03","story":"StartSSL domain validation vulnerability","stance":"rec","why":"Horror examples of CA security failures.","quote":"A great talk by moxie, filled with horror examples of CAs.","text":"You would soon be left without any CAs. [1] People are pretty stupid when it comes to security, and this includes people working for CAs. There have been cases where the CA private key is publicly accessible to the internet without any password. [2] -- [1] Yes, plenty of smart people have been advocating moving away from the current CA system. It's fundamentally broken. [2] A great talk by moxie, filled with horror examples of CAs. The private key example is at 19:20. [VIDEO]"},{"id":10468106,"author":"glass-","month":"2015-10","story":"Sustaining Digital Certificate Security","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces Convergence as a replacement for CAs.","quote":"is a good watch for those unfamiliar with the idea","text":"Convergence was the perfect replacement, but it never gained any traction. Moxie's talk at BlackHat[0] introducing it is a good watch for those unfamiliar with the idea, and if you want to be wistfully frustrated at what could have been. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":5004081,"author":"jcase","month":"2013-01","story":"Enhancing digital certificate security (Fake *.Google.com cert issued)","stance":"rec","why":"Great source on certificate system problems and alternatives.","quote":"The 2011 BlackHat video[3] and blog post[2] by Moxie Marlinspike are great sources of information.","text":"Some developments in this area are: * Convergence.io * DNS-based Authentication of Named Entities (DANE) + DNSSEC * Tack.io For various reasons listed in [1] Convergence is not likely to be implemented (by default) in major browsers. On DANE + DNSSEC, where the cert is authenticated via the information published in your DNS, Moxie Marlinspike has said it better then I can: \"CAs are sketchy, but this is a whole new world of sketchiness. Think, sketchasaurus. Registrars were never built or selected with security in mind, and most of them don’t have a very good track record in this area. Shouldn’t it be laughable that the current first step in deploying DNSSEC is to create an account with GoDaddy?\"[2] The 2011 BlackHat video[3] and blog post[2] by Moxie Marlinspike are great sources of information. IMO, Tack.io is the most viable solution. It's compatible with the current model but removes the thread of one CA being able to compromise all domains. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3254323,"author":"alecco","month":"2011-11","story":"The Sovereign Keys Project","stance":"rec","why":"Great security talk relevant to SSL trust problems.","quote":"A great talk from last DefCon","text":"How does this solution deal with the \"Citibank problem\"? (a website with a huge number of SSL keys from multiple certificate authorities) A great talk from last DefCon [VIDEO]"},{"id":2966064,"author":"jvc26","month":"2011-09","story":"Diginotar and Comodo hacker the same guy and claims to have owned 4 other CAs","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses the Comodo attack and CA problems.","quote":"quite an interesting discussion on the issue.","text":"[VIDEO] The original Comodo attack was discussed by Moxie Marlinspike at Blackhat USA 2011 - quite an interesting discussion on the issue."},{"id":12227574,"author":"anonbanker","month":"2016-08","story":"New attack steals SSNs, e-mail addresses, and more from HTTPS pages","stance":"reference","why":"Talk that remains relevant on SSL weaknesses.","quote":"talk in 2011 keeps staying relevant after all these years.","text":"Moxie's \"SSL is a joke\"[0] talk in 2011 keeps staying relevant after all these years. 0. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12382461,"author":"_jomo","month":"2016-08","story":"The DNSSEC master key securing DNS is about to change","stance":"reference","why":"Introduces Convergence, a CA alternative.","quote":"","text":"I wonder why moxie's Convergence [0] didn't take off. To me it sounds near-perfect. It could handle multiple layers of authenticity, including DNS. The project has been inactive on GitHub for 5 years [1], however the Namecoin project picked it up [2] as \"FreeSpeechMe\" and added support for IPv6, and .bit, .onion, .b32.i2p domains. Now this, too, has been discontinued \"because Mozilla removed the relevant API's as of Firefox 33\". They \"intend to release a replacement soon\". According to Jeremy Rand [3] \"A replacement is in the works (and is mostly working already)\", but the source doesn't seem to be public or I can't find it. Are there any downsides of Convergence? 0: [VIDEO] 1: [LINK] 2: [LINK] 3: [LINK]"},{"id":11962923,"author":"ymse","month":"2016-06","story":"Defending Our Brand","stance":"reference","why":"Anecdote about the Comodo attacker and sslsniff.","quote":"Moxie had an amusing anecdote about this incident","text":"> They issued fraudulent SSL certificates in 2011: [LINK] Moxie had an amusing anecdote about this incident in his Blackhat 2011 talk \"SSL and the future of authenticity\"[0]. Apparently the same IP as was used by the \"sophisticated attacker\" and disclosed by Comodo downloaded sslsniff[1] from moxies server the next day, referred by a video tutorial about intercepting SSL.. 0: [VIDEO] 1: [LINK]"},{"id":7571187,"author":"dobbsbob","month":"2014-04","story":"Were Intelligence Agencies Using Heartbleed in November 2013?","stance":"reference","why":"Shows poor state of CAs with exposed signing certs.","quote":"Moxie Marlinspike's presentation about the shitty state of CAs","text":"I would say they are compromised just by watching Moxie Marlinspike's presentation about the shitty state of CAs and how he was able to find signing certs just laying around in unprotected directories [VIDEO]"},{"id":7615541,"author":"sarahj","month":"2014-04","story":"No, don't enable revocation checking","stance":"reference","why":"Presents Convergence as a decentralized CA replacement.","quote":"Moxie Marlinspike proposed Convergence","text":"Why have a single entity at all? Moxie Marlinspike proposed Convergence ( [VIDEO] ) as a solution - I think that something like that has far more potential wheels to travel than a Namecoin based system. I should be able to choose who I trust, a notary system would allow me to do just that. No central CA systems. The biggest concern I can see is Identity management, but, as mentioned by Moxie, most of these CA don't do anything close to proper Identity management any more - I have a number of certificates bought from quite a few different CA's all made out to my rabbit, at no fixed address. Notaries can, of course, do additional verification - they could even advertise this as a premium. I don't see why this can't be extended to DNS lookup's either. I trust X notaries and pin the results I get, I can choose to trust a majority, or be hyper paranoid and require everyone to agree. No need to run a power hungry blockchain, no single point of technology failure. Technically, all of that is feasible today. And I imagine we will see a number of different technologies combined to form a proper, decentralised, system."},{"id":7127648,"author":"atmosx","month":"2014-01","story":"Did this Tor developer become a victim of NSA's laptop interception program?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as evidence on Comodo's leadership.","quote":"","text":"You are either stupid or subversive. By now we all know Keith Alexander’s view of hackers (socially disabled, semi-autistic kids sitting behind a laptop at 4 a.m. wearing a batman suit and gloves) and his view on technical matters is equal with Comodo’s [2] leader. As for NSA having skilled personnel??? Sure - some of them are skilled - but they don’t take decisions[1]. The stupid guys do (again, K. Alexander is an excellent reference for incompetence add to that that he is a lier[3] and you’ ve got an explosive mix). NOTE: In “War and Peace” - Tolstoy’s masterpiece - there’s a Russian officer (Prince Andrey IIRC) who realizes the insane tragedy of human history: While the Prussian and Russian army coalition (hundreds of thousands of men, with families and lives left behind…) is about to face the fiercest opponent of their era (Napoleon, is a legend among Russian military officials) the two Generals (Prussian and Russian) are fighting and sabotaging each other about who is going to lead the battle… Until it’s too late. They both get easily crashed, without putting up a fight. So you can play naive , stupid , stunned or cunning all you like but human history is FULL of stupid people in position of power. [1] Quora: “Does the NSA have better engineers than Facebook or Google?”: [LINK] [2] “BlackHat USA 2011: SSL And The Future Of Authenticity”: [VIDEO] [3] “K. Alexander lies to congress” - [LINK]"},{"id":6454325,"author":"espeed","month":"2013-09","story":"Switch to HTTPS Now, For Free","stance":"reference","why":"Explains MITM weaknesses of the CA system.","quote":"still vulnerable to MITM attacks due to issues with the CA system","text":"Using encrypted tokens help mitigate stolen session IDs ( [LINK] ). And while HSTS helps protect against sslstrip, you're still vulnerable to MITM attacks due to issues with the CA system. See moxie's 2011 talk \"SSL and the Future of Authenticity\" ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":2949538,"author":"derrida","month":"2011-09","story":"Iran forged the wrong SSL certificate","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses Perspectives and Convergence notaries and privacy at 36:50.","quote":"the implementation provided with Perspectives does not have privacy.","text":"It is indeed based on Perspectives. But the implementation provided with Perspectives does not have privacy. If you contact a notary with Perspectives then that notary has your browsing history. See 36:50 on [VIDEO]"},{"id":2951710,"author":"moxie","month":"2011-09","story":"Iran forged the wrong SSL certificate","stance":"reference","why":"Best reference for how Convergence works and trust agility.","quote":"the best reference is (unfortunately) still the presentation","text":"The problem is that right now, in the common case, the browser/OS vendors can't black-list individual CAs. Their ability to do so with DigiNotar is exceptionally rare, and would not be possible most of the time. Trust agility ensures that clients have the ability to make these trust decisions easily. A client does not necessarily have to be a user , it could still be the browser/OS vendors. For details on how Convergence works, in order to answer your question of how it prevents 1/4th of the SSL internet from going down when Comodo gets hacked, the best reference is (unfortunately) still the presentation: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2956384,"author":"burgerbrain","month":"2011-09","story":"DigiNotar root CA trust removal follow-up","stance":"reference","why":"Supports distrust of certificate authorities like Comodo.","quote":"Comodo had huge issues very recently","text":"Comodo had huge issues very recently, and VeriSign's issues go back about as far as they do (providing intercept services to governments. That wasn't even an 'accident'.) Godaddy? I don't know much about what they do but I wager you wouldn't have to dig deep. Edit: I'm not the only one saying this: [VIDEO] Modding me down will not restore the trust."},{"id":15148263,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2017-09","story":"Gnu Privacy Guard relies on one underfunded person (2015)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"","text":"Wow, I missed that story the first time around. Just catching up on the details. That Comodo CEO Melih Abdulhayoğlu is such a dishonest, incompetent buffoon! I had no idea how terrible a company Comodo is, but now I do thanks to the CEO's own words and actions. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["NHopJHSlVo4",1],["4XpnKHJAok8",1],["IJcuQQ1eWWI",1],["x1bX3F7uTrg",1],["rOwMW6agpTI",1],["RUwS1uAdUcI",1],["njVv7J2azY8",1],["vAHXK2wut_I",1],["DlkMs4ZHHr8",1],["d2lJUOv0hLA",1],["hpgK51w6uhk",1],["u5um8QWWRvo",1],["kLiwvnr4L80",1],["ZWQ0591PAxM",1],["dP1xVpMPn8M",1]]},"_ZG8HBuDjgc":{"wy":"Adams fans repeatedly call it one of his best talks, a funny and moving look at extinction and the environment.","q":{"x":"Watch the whole thing. You will thank me later.","i":22523097,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":43376646,"author":"tombert","month":"2025-03","story":"So Long and Thanks for All the Words: A Toast to Douglas Adams","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Funny educational standup tying anecdotes and animal facts to environment.","quote":"is one of the greatest things I've ever seen.","text":"I don't know what you categorize it as (I usually call it an \"educational standup\"), but his last talk before his death \"Parrots, The Universe, and Everything\" is one of the greatest things I've ever seen. It's very funny, with a lot of interesting anecdotes about his life, with a lot of interesting facts about animals, and he manages to tie the entire thing together with a pretty clever theme about the environment. I genuinely think it's one of the greatest public speeches ever done, at least in the English language. If you haven't seen it, it's an hour and a half long, and it's totally worth it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34345245,"author":"simonebrunozzi","month":"2023-01","story":"When Douglas Adams wrote about Hypercard","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Worth watching even if you saw it years ago.","quote":"If you love Douglas Adams, like I do, watch","text":"If you love Douglas Adams, like I do, watch \"Douglas Adams: Parrots, the Universe and Everything\" [0], if you haven't done so. Heck, even if you did years ago. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23983358,"author":"simonebrunozzi","month":"2020-07","story":"Riding the Rays (1992)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hour-long mind-blowing presentation tied to Last Chance to See.","quote":"you absolutely have to watch this hour-long presentation he gave at UC Santa Barbara in 2001","text":"Douglas Adams is one of my favorite writers, and intellectuals, ever. If you are like me, you absolutely have to watch this hour-long presentation he gave at UC Santa Barbara in 2001 [0], titled \"Parrots, the Universe, and Everything\". Mind blowing. His last book, \"Last chance to see\" [1], which is about endangered species, is heavily related to that talk. I recently learned that Douglas Adams had a chance to play on stage at Pink Floyd's 28 October 1994 concert at Earls Court in London [2], playing guitar on the songs \"Brain Damage\" and \"Eclipse\". He's even responsible for the 1994 album name \"Division Bell\" by Pink Floyd. David Gilmour and Douglas Adams were good friends. Well, what can I say... It sucks that he went away too soon. I'd love to read more of his stuff, and I'd love to know his take on today's world. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":22523097,"author":"simonebrunozzi","month":"2020-03","story":"“The Hitchhiker’s Guide to the Galaxy” turns 42","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Essential talk for every Douglas Adams fan.","quote":"Watch the whole thing. You will thank me later.","text":"Obligatory mention for every fan of Douglas Adams: \"Parrots, the Universe, and everything\". [0] Watch the whole thing. You will thank me later. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21139050,"author":"simonebrunozzi","month":"2019-10","story":"The Hitchhiker’s Guide to the Galaxy: 40 years of parody and predictions","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of Adams's most wonderful talks.","quote":"one of his most wonderful talks","text":"Obligatory mention of one of his most wonderful talks [0]: \"Parrots, the Universe, and everything\" [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43374131,"author":"sidmitra","month":"2025-03","story":"So Long and Thanks for All the Words: A Toast to Douglas Adams","stance":"rec","why":"Recommended Douglas Adams talk.","quote":"I also recommend this video: Parrots, the Universe and Everything","text":"Hitchhikers guide was a personality defining read for me when i was younger. I also recommend this video: Parrots, the Universe and Everything [VIDEO] Just this week i was looking for more humour writing like Douglas Adams, PG Wodehouse. I came across this award which seems interesting. [LINK] I plan to read some of them this year."},{"id":41013675,"author":"Keegs","month":"2024-07","story":"The Later Years of Douglas Adams","stance":"rec","why":"Adams discusses Last Chance to See, his favorite book to write.","quote":"he says it was his favorite book to write.","text":"My favorite book is Douglas Adams’ Last Chance to See . He talks about it here [0] and IIRC he says it was his favorite book to write. Adams took a dark subject - endangered animals and our role in their extinction - and told a story that’s equally hilarious and hopeful. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21138802,"author":"dylan-m","month":"2019-10","story":"The Hitchhiker’s Guide to the Galaxy: 40 years of parody and predictions","stance":"rec","why":"Adams's talk about Last Chance to See.","quote":"He gave a great talk about it, too","text":"In the same way, I'd also recommend Last Chance To See for a slightly different flavour of Douglas Adams. His whimsical style is remarkably well-suited to a non-fiction nature and travel book. I can't say I completely get his science fiction books, but I felt the constraints of a non-fiction book gave me a new appreciation for his sense of humour and his way of balancing that with thoughtful, meaningful exposition. It's one of my favourite books :) He gave a great talk about it, too: [VIDEO]"},{"id":8692472,"author":"leichtgewicht","month":"2014-12","story":"Hawking: AI could end human race","stance":"reference","why":"Douglas Adams humorously discussing extinction.","quote":"Douglas Adams talking, fairly humorously, about extinction","text":"[VIDEO] (Douglas Adams talking, fairly humorously, about extinction) \"ending the human race\" is such an interesting thing. Put aside that bone-headed nazis will tell you that humans have more than one race: We do extinct other species in incredible amounts (ask any greenpeace flyer). If we were extinguished by our own invention seems sort of fair. But by the off-chance that an AI figures out that we are actually worth being, then there might be an realistic chance that it will actually help us survive our own mistake. A bit of a gamble but if curiosity kills the cat then we are lucky to be alive anyways :)"}],"r":[["98LdFA-_zfA",1]]},"DkS1pkKpILY":{"wy":"HN commenters say it convinced them to quit or cut down on drinking, and point to it as a more comprehensive treatment than most articles.","q":{"x":"Andrew Huberman's 2-hour blog post on the negative effects of alcohol[1] convinced me to stop drinking.","i":36578242,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38785018,"author":"robrenaud","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: Favorite Podcast Episodes of 2023?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Episode convinced commenter to cut drinking, improving sleep and weight","quote":"his alcohol episode convinced me to really cut down on drinking","text":"It's Aug 2022 so a bit out of range for the OP, but his alcohol episode convinced me to really cut down on drinking, from about 2 beers (low variance/rarely drunk, just a homebrewer with a craft beer obsession) a day to a day to maximum 2 beers a week. I sleep better and lost 35 pounds in the last year. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37875740,"author":"pipes","month":"2023-10","story":"The drugs did work (2006)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Hit home and led commenter to quit drinking","quote":"Listening to the huberman podcast about alcohol really hit home.","text":"Listening to the huberman podcast about alcohol really hit home. I knew it was bad for me, just not as bad as he explains. It's a horrific drug that most people don't even see as a drug. I am a weekend drinker and I thought that was ok. It's not. I've decided to quit completely. Last drink was 12 days ago. Fuck alcohol. (I also used to take other drugs but that was many years ago). [VIDEO] Oh and I just finished the book: alcohol explained, utterly fantastic."},{"id":36578242,"author":"cl42","month":"2023-07","story":"Against Alcohol","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convinced the commenter to stop drinking","quote":"Andrew Huberman's 2-hour blog post on the negative effects of alcohol[1] convinced me to stop drinking.","text":"Related: Andrew Huberman's 2-hour blog post on the negative effects of alcohol[1] convinced me to stop drinking. Once you stop drinking, it becomes so easy to see just how much alcohol pervades popular culture. People drink constantly on TV. People pressure you to drink in social situations, too. Unfortunately, it often feels like you need a 'good reason' to stop drinking or alternatively admit you were an alcoholic. It's really unfortunate and ... silly? Reddit's \"stopdrinking\" subreddit is also really good[2] if you are struggling with this. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":36696086,"author":"softwaredoug","month":"2023-07","story":"SF's Anchor Brewing Company shutting down after 127 years","stance":"rec","why":"Great Huberman episode on alcohol's health effects","quote":"Huberman has a great episode too","text":"The pandemic has triggered / accelarated a lot of trends One trend I wonder about is cutting back on drinking. From a health perspective, I see more and more health officials loudly saying no healthy amount of alcohol[1]. It's simply poison. Not that I'm aggressively for any kind of prohibition, and don't very rarely drink, but I wonder if we'll see increased awareness and more people just deciding not to drink as much anymore. With this awareness, and the \"modest amount of alcohol are actually healthy\" claims debunked[2], maybe the hipster / wealthy / yuppy crowd will turn more and more away from the many many microbreweries / wineries that have cropped up in the last few decades. 1 - [LINK] Huberman has a great episode too - [VIDEO] 2 - [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":36061547,"author":"givemeethekeys","month":"2023-05","story":"Alcohol affects your body, from first flush of happiness to hangover","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed take on alcohol's effects, deeper than the article","quote":"For a detailed take, listen / watch the Huberman Lab podcast","text":"This article's intended audience seems to be high school children. For a detailed take, listen / watch the Huberman Lab podcast: [VIDEO] (What Alcohol Does to Your Body, Brain & Health | Huberman Lab Podcast #86)"},{"id":34302456,"author":"pstuart","month":"2023-01","story":"How I Felt After Binge-Drinking 'Hangover-Free' Alcohol","stance":"rec","why":"Podcast that helped the commenter give up alcohol","quote":"a recent podcast has helped sway me to effectively give up alcohol","text":"It doesn't specify the actual ingredients. The \"grapes\" bit makes me think perhaps resveratrol? Dihydromyricetin is touted as being protective before and after drinking. It appears to have other benefits as well. I've now become a big fan and follower of Andrew Huberman and a recent podcast has helped sway me to effectively give up alcohol (I was already trending in that direction): [VIDEO] My one remaining active vice is Kratom and I'm pretty comfortable with my relationship with it, considering the value it gives in managing chronic pain. Obviously different strokes for different folks."},{"id":33159098,"author":"guiambros","month":"2022-10","story":"Ask HN: How did you stop drinking?","stance":"rec","why":"Comprehensive explanation of alcohol's effects on body and brain","quote":"I recently saw this one from Dr. Andrew Huberman, which seemed more comprehensive than a lot of what I've read before.","text":"What helped me eliminate almost entirely was understanding the impact it has on the body and the brain. More than \" it's bad for your health \", it helped me understand why it's bad. Even the allegedly harmless \" a glass of red wine a day is good for your health \" (spoiler: no). There are many sources, but I recently saw this one from Dr. Andrew Huberman, which seemed more comprehensive than a lot of what I've read before. [1] What Alcohol Does to Your Body, Brain & Health - [VIDEO]"},{"id":38007955,"author":"quickthrower2","month":"2023-10","story":"41 states sue Meta, claiming Instagram, Facebook are addictive, harm kids","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as reference for effects of regular drinking","quote":"Reference","text":"Chronic (as in regular, not heavy) drinking causes changes in mood, increased stress and body function. A glass of wine a day for example. Reference: [VIDEO] Alcohol is so ingrained in popular culture that criticising it is almost taboo. Culture will play a big part in what people under the legal age to buy consume with family. It can be a good think but I reckon once a month have a glass of wine."}],"r":[["nJPERZDfyWc",1],["pKcmNGvaDUs",1],["wqu_IpkOYBg",1],["rHdS_4GsKmg",1],["wjZofJX0v4M",1],["ZmNOAtZIgIk",1],["7jiPeIFXb6U",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1]]},"sPhpelUfu8Q":{"wy":"HN people point to it for rrb-trees, text editor data structures and bringing Clojure-style persistent structures to C++.","q":{"x":"Immer [0][1] seems like a really serious attempt at bringing high-quality persistent data structures to C++.","i":28405730,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":25273675,"author":"nightowl_games","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: What are the best advanced computer science courses online?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Better undo pattern than Command Pattern from Ableton developer","quote":"Juan Pedro is a next level programmer who I think has pushed the craft forward","text":"Hey. Check out Juan Pedro Bolivar Puente's talks om value oriented design. He worked on Ableton which is a large application. He specifically speaks to Undo. He describes a much better pattern than the Command Pattern. He also released his source for Value Oriented Data structures Juan Pedro is a next level programmer who I think has pushed the craft forward through his work on bringing Value Oriented Design into the C++ world. Talk I [VIDEO] Talk II [LINK] Data Structures: [LINK] Application Structure: [LINK] Text Editor: [LINK] Good luck."},{"id":45439060,"author":"zelphirkalt","month":"2025-10","story":"Type Theory and Functional Programming (1999) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Editor handling huge files with functional data structures","quote":"an impressive talk, in which someone shows their developed editor that can edit huge text files without lag","text":"> [...] which is a must for anything performance oriented. But that's also not really true. Using functional data structures it is possible to write highly performant software. Check out for example \"CppCon 2017: Juan Pedro Bolivar Puente - Postmodern immutable data structures\"[1], an impressive talk, in which someone shows their developed editor that can edit huge text files without lag faster than any mainstream editor today. This is enabled by functional data structures. Usage of functional data structures throughout a project also ensures, that parts can be trivially parallelized, which is not true for traditional algorithms, and therefore can easily scale with number of CPUs available. For more on that subject, you might want to watch a few Joe Armstrong talks. Rust achieves type safety by introducing more language concepts like ownership, borrowing, and lifetimes, to formalize, who has access when and how. It also creates things as immutable by default, unless you make use of `mut`. Unfortunately, Rust's philosophy is shared memory and making shared memory access safe, rather than message passing, which is at odds with FP. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36321573,"author":"modernerd","month":"2023-06","story":"Text Editor Data Structures","stance":"rec","why":"Prototype editor opening a 1GB file instantly using immutable vectors","quote":"See the author instantly opening a ~1GB text file with async loading","text":"You might be interested in ewig and immer by Juan Pedro Bolivar Puente: [LINK] [LINK] See the author instantly opening a ~1GB text file with async loading, paging through, copying/pasting, and undoing/redoing in their prototype “ewig” text editor about 27 minutes into their talk here: [VIDEO] It’s backed by a “vector of vectors” data structure called a relaxed radix balanced tree: [LINK] That original paper has seen lots of attention and attempts at performance improvements, such as: [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":28405730,"author":"bertmuthalaly","month":"2021-09","story":"Joker: A small interpreted dialect of Clojure written in Go","stance":"rec","why":"Introduces Immer persistent data structure library for C++","quote":"Immer [0][1] seems like a really serious attempt at bringing high-quality persistent data structures to C++.","text":"Immer [0][1] seems like a really serious attempt at bringing high-quality persistent data structures to C++. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":23638688,"author":"proverbialbunny","month":"2020-06","story":"Alex: ML-enhanced range index, similar in functionality to a B+ Tree","stance":"rec","why":"Backstory building up to an rrb-tree","quote":"If anyone wants a backstory building up to this data structure checkout this talk","text":"Mmm tasty. If anyone wants a backstory building up to this data structure checkout this talk: [VIDEO] He builds an rrb-tree which uses the same underlying concepts ALEX uses, just without the ML-enhancement."},{"id":20979647,"author":"proverbialbunny","month":"2019-09","story":"You're doing it wrong: B-heap 10x faster than binary heap (2010)","stance":"rec","why":"Fun look at rrb-trees","quote":"Not a benchmark but rrb-trees are pretty neat.","text":"Not a benchmark but rrb-trees are pretty neat. They give an effective O(1) for all operations. Modern languages like Scala's Vector type is an rrb-tree under the hood: [LINK] For more fun: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20604656,"author":"proverbialbunny","month":"2019-08","story":"Build Your Own Text Editor","stance":"rec","why":"Using rrb-trees for large-document text editing with undo","quote":"talks about rrb-trees and how they're a great data structure for writing a text editor that can handle unlimited sized documents without locking up","text":"In case anyone hasn't seen it this CppCon talk [VIDEO] talks about rrb-trees and how they're a great data structure for writing a text editor that can handle unlimited sized documents without locking up, and allows for editing while saving and opening, and an easy way to support an undo command. Most text editors today do not support such functionality. With lockless data structures like an rrb-tree it becomes easy. It really is an ideal way to do write a text editor."},{"id":34216857,"author":"henrydark","month":"2023-01","story":"Functional Programming – How and Why","stance":"reference","why":"Shows immutable structures need not copy all state","quote":"A nice showcase of this idea is in Juan Pedro Bolivar Puente's CppCon '17 talk","text":"Not to disagree, but to be fair modern fp runtimes use data structures that wouldn't have to copy so many bytes as the whole state. A nice showcase of this idea is in Juan Pedro Bolivar Puente's CppCon '17 talk Postmodern Immutable Data Structures: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25719283,"author":"arximboldi","month":"2021-01","story":"Build Your Own Text Editor","stance":"reference","why":"Talks covering the design of a didactic text editor","quote":"It's design is covered in these talks","text":"For another approach: I built a didactic text editor to teach \"value oriented design\" and immutable data-structures in C++: [LINK] It's design is covered in these talks: - Postmodern immutable data structures: [VIDEO] - The most valuable values: [LINK]"},{"id":20877368,"author":"proverbialbunny","month":"2019-09","story":"Show HN: Array with Constant Time Access and Fast Insertion and Deletion","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the rrb-tree data structure","quote":"If interested about the data structure","text":"Interesting. I wonder how similar a Judy array is to an rrb-tree (relaxed radix binary tree), or if they're the same thing. An rrb-tree is an effective O(1) time for every category. Scala's Vector type implements one. If interested about the data structure: [VIDEO] Scala bigO doc: [LINK] Scala's doc on it: [LINK]"},{"id":20795137,"author":"proverbialbunny","month":"2019-08","story":"Why Are So Many Developers Hating on Object-Oriented Programming?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a powerful functional data structure","quote":"this cppcon talk is introduces one, which gives near O(1) time for all uses","text":"One example is streaming. Streams allow for a near infinite array size to be processed, because it works on one element at a time as it comes in. Streams come from the FP world. From that function chaining originates from the FP world and is used in modern languages like Python quite heavily. You mentioned research, so a valid example of this is the difference between Spark and MapReduce. Spark was introduced to optimize processing large sets of data, by streaming it. MapReduce does not. For a more heavy example, a few data structures that are quite powerful come from the FP world. For example, this cppcon talk is introduces one, which gives near O(1) time for all uses (insert, delete, lookup, ...), and can be used in a thread safe way: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18347535,"author":"arximboldi","month":"2018-10","story":"Clojure at Netflix (2013) [slides]","stance":"reference","why":"Bringing Clojure-style persistent data structures to C++","quote":"my goals is to make C++ more like Clojure","text":"Interesting to hear about C++ in this context, since of my goals is to make C++ more like Clojure: [VIDEO] [LINK] There is a lot of I could say about the relationship between C++ and Clojure, but I'll leave it at that for now :-)"},{"id":15653900,"author":"arximboldi","month":"2017-11","story":"Ask HN: How much time do you spend on “marketing” your open source projects?","stance":"reference","why":"Author's own conference talk about his open-source library","quote":"My most popular open-source project [1] I talk about at conferences, mostly C++ ones","text":"My most popular open-source project [1] I talk about at conferences, mostly C++ ones (last example [2]). I was lucky to also land a paper about it at ICFP'17 [3]. On Friday I will talk at the MeetingCpp conference in Berlin about a new library I am building (kind of experimental Redux for C++ with time-travelling debugger!). I work as a freelancer and at the moment I do client work 3 days a week and dedicate the rest of the time to the open-source work. The open-source work includes doing research and implementing these tools, but also preparing the talks, which is a very time consuming task for me. Doing conference talks is very emotionally exhausting, I dread the previous weeks to the talk---but the feeling afterwards is very good and it is a good way to get exposure, I hope. My dream is to some day form a social company with a coop structure that works full-time on open-source, either directly or indirectly helping clients use these tools in their system. I hope I am set up in the right direction. Recently I opened a Twitter account to talk mostly about my open-source stuff [1]. I admittedly hate Twitter as a social network, but I've been convinced that it is a good place for networking in our industry, so I just play the game. I keep telling myself that I should open blog too, but then I realize that one can't simply do everything all the time, so I guess maybe I'll never do it. To your last question: is it unfair to not do marketing to your work? Totally not. Just do wha"}],"r":[["re96UgMk6GQ",1],["UF8uR6Z6KLc",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["AJdEqssNZ-U",1],["72y2EC5fkcE",1],["0ifChJ0nJfM",1]]},"0xS68sl2D70":{"wy":"Recommended as a balanced take on AI and education, with the point that learning takes work that no technology can do for you.","q":{"x":"I think the best take on that I've seen so far was from Derek (Veritasium) in this recent talk","i":44060149,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":48415838,"author":"teabee89","month":"2026-06","story":"Why I'm Skeptical About Efforts to Revolutionize Schooling","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Great, funny talk on why AI won't revolutionize education.","quote":"gave a great (and funny) talk[1] about how AI will not revolutionize education","text":"Derek Muller from the Veritasium channel gave a great (and funny) talk[1] about how AI will not revolutionize education and I'll quote my favorite part: \"The world is full of heavy objects; yet not everyone is ripped\". Meaning that what fails with AI in education is also what fails in education without AI: lack of motivation that a good coach could provide. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":44060149,"author":"panstromek","month":"2025-05","story":"ChatGPT Is a Gimmick","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Balanced take on AI and learning.","quote":"I think the best take on that I've seen so far was from Derek (Veritasium) in this recent talk","text":"If anyone is interested in AI in relation to learning, I think the best take on that I've seen so far was from Derek (Veritasium) in this recent talk: [VIDEO] It's a lot more balanced compared to the doomy attitude in the primary post."},{"id":48323903,"author":"btilly","month":"2026-05","story":"Please Use AI","stance":"rec","why":"Learning requires effort; AI can help minds grow or atrophy.","quote":"The world is full of heavy things, and yet most of us aren't ripped.","text":"I am reminded of Veritasium: What Everyone Gets Wrong About AI and Learning (original video [VIDEO] ) from a year ago. \"The world is full of heavy things, and yet most of us aren't ripped.\" AI is an opportunity. On the one hand, it can be used to let our minds and social lives atrophy. On the other hand, it is an opportunity to help our minds grow. Most people will make the lazy choice. But you can choose to do otherwise. Take, for example, speeches. I do not let AI write my speeches. But my speeches are better for having been critiqued by AI. But the result is still my speech. My thoughts, my ideas, my words, and my meaning. Just improved with rounds of feedback about where it fell flat, where I was likely to lose people, and so on. Feedback that I had to fix. So do not let AI write your speeches. But do use it to push yourself harder."},{"id":47789725,"author":"kevlened","month":"2026-04","story":"Why Sal Khan's AI revolution hasn't happened yet, according to Sal Khan","stance":"rec","why":"Why education tech revolutions fail: learning requires work.","quote":"Learning takes work, and no technology can do the work for you.","text":"Derek Muller of Veritasium has a presentation on why the promises of a revolution in education are never fulfilled. [0] His hottest take is we're already close to the optimal process for learning, so technology isn't going to improve it. Learning takes work, and no technology can do the work for you. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47117409,"author":"srean","month":"2026-02","story":"$30B for laptops yielded a generation less cognitively capable than parents","stance":"rec","why":"Effort is essential to learning.","quote":"The world is full of heavy objects but how many of us are ripped ?","text":"[VIDEO] Veritasium's video: \"Effort is the Algorithm\". The world is full of heavy objects but how many of us are ripped ? -- Derek Muller"},{"id":43746531,"author":"runfaster2000","month":"2025-04","story":"Why is OpenAI buying Windsurf?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues learning comes from repetitions, like the commenter's point.","quote":"This excellent presentation (Veritasium) is making much the same point (learning comes from “reps”).","text":"This excellent presentation (Veritasium) is making much the same point (learning comes from “reps”). [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nmgFG7PUHfo",1],["V2YDApNRK3g",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["AF8d72mA41M",1],["u6XAPnuFjJc",1],["A9UmdY0E8hU",1],["-4EDhdAHrOg",1],["1sd26pWhfmg",1],["GNKFSpJIBO0",1]]},"7jiPeIFXb6U":{"wy":"It is the go-to funny, irreverent critique of notebooks, linked whenever HN debates notebooks versus testable code.","q":{"x":"The parent article links this great talk that goes into more detail than the parent post and is much easier to understand","i":40675952,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":19561819,"author":"kevsim","month":"2019-04","story":"How to separate your data from your code","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Talk critiquing Jupyter notebooks against engineering best practices.","quote":"I highly recommend it.","text":"Jupyter notebooks are really abysmal when it comes to best engineering practices. There's an amazing talk called \"I don't like notebooks\" by Joel Grus at last years JupyterCon [0]. I highly recommend it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18341147,"author":"tcpekin","month":"2018-10","story":"Why Jupyter is data scientists’ computational notebook of choice","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Irreverent critique of notebooks the commenter fully agrees with.","quote":"they're definitely irreverent, but I find myself strongly agreeing with everything he says.","text":"Here they are - they're definitely irreverent, but I find myself strongly agreeing with everything he says. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40675952,"author":"lelandbatey","month":"2024-06","story":"Notebooks Are McDonalds of Code","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed explanation of why notebooks make state hard to track.","quote":"The parent article links this great talk that goes into more detail than the parent post and is much easier to understand","text":"REPL stands for Read–eval–print loop [LINK] A REPL, by its name, is a very narrow version of the broader paradigm of interactive computer programming environments. But Notebooks are not REPLs, unless you use REPL to mean \"interactive programming environment\" and not REPL. Notebooks are much broader than a REPL! In a notebook, you can go back and edit and run individual lines of the notebook without re-running the whole notebook from the start and without re-computing everything that depends on what you just edited. Behavior like this makes it super hard to track the actual state, and super easy to lose track of how things are how they are. That's pretty terrible! The parent article links this great talk that goes into more detail than the parent post and is much easier to understand: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29909812,"author":"carreau","month":"2022-01","story":"IPython 8.0","stance":"rec","why":"Funny talk on notebook flaws, though some points reflect misconfiguration.","quote":"it's a really funny talk, a lot of the points are IMHO invalid as Joel is misinformed on how things can be configured, but still a great watch.","text":"First, yes, this is a common question. IPython does not try to deal with that, it's just the executing engine. Notebooks, do not have to be stored in ipynb form, I would suggest to look at [LINK] , and notebook UI is inherently not design for multi-file and application developpement. So training humans will always be necessary. Technically Jupyter Notebook does not even care that notebooks are files, you could save then using say postgres ( [LINK] ) , and even sync content between notebooks. I'm not too well informed anymore on this particular topic, but there are other folks at [LINK] that might be more aware, you can also ask on discourse.jupyter.org, I'm pretty sure you can find threads on those issues. I think on the Jupyter side we could do a better job curating and exposing many tools to help with that, but there are just so many hours in the day... I also recommend I don't like notebook from Joel Grus, [VIDEO] it's a really funny talk, a lot of the points are IMHO invalid as Joel is misinformed on how things can be configured, but still a great watch."},{"id":27651502,"author":"softwaredoug","month":"2021-06","story":"Ask HN: How do you run long computations without interruption?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for moving code from notebooks into testable libraries.","quote":"I would recommend the talk","text":"Get extensive code out of notebooks into testable libraries. Confirm it works before running a big computation. We try to keep our notebooks slim and factor anything complex into a Python library. We can experiment with this libraries implementation, write unit tests on smaller data sets to confirm it works, before doing a bigger experiment. We can also deploy the library to prod and ensure users get the exact same thing we experimented with. I would recommend the talk “I don’t like notebooks” [VIDEO] (There’s also the alternative nbdev that creates a full software engineering and experimentation setup in 100% notebooks. Not something I particularly like, but it might be another option.)"},{"id":24926400,"author":"tosh","month":"2020-10","story":"I Violated a Code of Conduct","stance":"rec","why":"Critical counterpart to Jeremy Howard's pro-notebook talk; both show strengths and shortcomings.","quote":"I think both talks are very worth watching","text":"If you haven't yet: I think both talks are very worth watching: I don't like notebooks ( [VIDEO] ) by Joel Grus I like notebooks ( [LINK] ) by Jeremy Howard notebooks are a very interesting medium and they have both shortcomings as well as things they are great at and neither were totally obvious to me."},{"id":43391746,"author":"tillahoffmann","month":"2025-03","story":"Show HN: Localscope–Limit scope of Python functions for reproducible execution","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on flaws of Jupyter notebooks.","quote":"Jupyter notebooks are flawed (there's a great talk at","text":"Yes, Jupyter notebooks are flawed (there's a great talk at [VIDEO] ). But they are also very convenient for scrappy work and exploratory data analysis."},{"id":27270463,"author":"gravypod","month":"2021-05","story":"Complexity is a source of income in open source ecosystems (2019)","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on why good software engineering is hard given bad examples.","quote":"I'm wondering what your opinion is of this talk","text":"I'm wondering what your opinion is of this talk: [VIDEO] It has some comments about why good software engineering leads to good software, how good software engineering is hard right now because there's a lot of bad examples which cause people to make more bad code, and some suggestions on how things could be done."},{"id":27277141,"author":"epistasis","month":"2021-05","story":"The Economist's excess deaths model","stance":"reference","why":"Video on deficiencies of notebooks, cited while defending their use for exploration.","quote":"I'm also reminded of this video about the deficiencies of notebooks","text":"I would love to see the tests that people come up for this... Tests can be helpful and informative, or they can be make-work boilerplate that will never reveal any flaws. And in this type of code base, it's hard to imagine the test that's written in code that will be informative. When dealing with data, \"testing\" is most often done visually, with plotting. The process of exploratory data analysis is not anything at all like writing code to an architected specification, and the same practices are not as effective for different types of work. Insisting that there should be automated tests for something like this reminds me of the cultural phenomenon of extreme table-phobia in the 2000s that happened as a reaction to using tables for layout. I had trouble getting people to believe that is was OK to use tables for tabular data, it was extremely frustrating. Or in the last 5-10 years, with design trends preferring spare layouts with lots of empty space, it was super hard to get information dense designs into production, because they were so averse to the idea that people may need information density for some things. I'm also reminded of this video about the deficiencies of notebooks: [VIDEO] Yes, there are huge deficiencies to notebooks. But using them is an entirely different task than the type of code that one would write in an IDE, for code that gets put into a library and will be run by others. I have two work modes: 1) IDE (actually Emacs) where I write tests and the code is "},{"id":26966011,"author":"kenward","month":"2021-04","story":"Nbterm: Jupyter Notebooks in the Terminal","stance":"reference","why":"Joel Grus's notebook workflow using ipython instead of jupyter.","quote":"I believe this is similar to what Joel Grus does [0], except %s/jupyter/ipython.","text":"I've been using the VSCode Insider's release as well and have been loving it and the new native notebook features for all the reasons you've listed already. That's an interesting solution. I believe this is similar to what Joel Grus does [0], except %s/jupyter/ipython. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":24305349,"author":"3JPLW","month":"2020-08","story":"Pluto.jl – a reactive, lightweight, simple notebook","stance":"reference","why":"Talk listing gripes about notebooks, such as hidden state.","quote":"The primary complaint there is that notebooks have a disconnect between the state of the program and the display of the cells.","text":"It completely fixes a huge number of the gripes in the JupyterCon talk I don't like notebooks by Joel Grus. The primary complaint there is that notebooks have a disconnect between the state of the program and the display of the cells. By using a completely reactive mode the state is no longer hidden. It's more akin to a spreadsheet than a notebook. A number of other complaints are completely circumvented by using a file format that's simply a pure Julia file with clever comments. Video: [VIDEO] Slides: [LINK] Previous discussion: [LINK]"},{"id":19301098,"author":"EForEndeavour","month":"2019-03","story":"Xkcd-Style Plots in Matplotlib (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"JupyterCon talk critiquing Jupyter notebooks.","quote":"Video of talk","text":"You're not the only one. Here are slides from a talk that Joel Grus gave at JupyterCon 2018 titled \"I hate Jupyter Notebooks\": [LINK] Video of talk: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41585250,"author":"taeric","month":"2024-09","story":"Bento: Jupyter Notebooks at Meta","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I know I found it far more amusing than I should have when it was released.","text":"I'm assuming you've seen [VIDEO] ? I know I found it far more amusing than I should have when it was released. I will confess that I found Mathematica kind of neat back in the day. I never got as good with it as peers did. I'm curious if that would be different for me today."}],"r":[["kh88fVP2FWQ",1],["rI8tNMsozo0",1],["udlMSe5-zP8",1],["SrKj4hYic5A",1],["cPNkH-7PRTk",1],["lKXe3HUG2l4",1],["9Q6sLbz37gk",1],["DkS1pkKpILY",1]]},"rhgwIhB58PA":{"wy":"The standard link whenever someone claims to be a visual learner or learning styles come up, with sources listed in the description.","q":{"x":"Veritasium covered this in much greater detail but still approachable by the laymen","i":30715436,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29200779,"author":"travisgriggs","month":"2021-11","story":"How imaginary numbers were invented [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Veritasium videos are educational with a subtle spiritual subtext.","quote":"I love Veritasium.","text":"I love Veritasium. Derek has done a number of videos that are awesome like this. The videos are great and educational, but he also manages to subtly weave in an almost spiritual subtext to them as well. For example, in this one, you learn about imaginary numbers in some cool ways, history, how awesome e^ix is, etc, but also this yen-yang balance between embracing not only what is real but taking a chance on what is imaginary. Some of my other favorites: - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK] I think he’s going to lose the $10,000 bet on the car though."},{"id":39636550,"author":"matt_j","month":"2024-03","story":"Eloquent JavaScript 4th edition (2024)","stance":"rec","why":"Good video debunking learning styles.","quote":"Veritasium made a good video about it.","text":"This is debunked to some extent. Veritasium made a good video about it. [VIDEO] Personally I find a multi-pronged approach is necessary to really learn anything. Read it. Read it again. Visual guides are helpful. Work some examples. Make some mistakes, debug them, find the corner cases, write tests. Eventually when I've poked around the material for a while it starts to bed in. Three months later it's forgotten, but when I learn it the next time I move a lot faster!"},{"id":38781647,"author":"irajdeep","month":"2023-12","story":"Things software developers should learn about learning","stance":"rec","why":"Discusses lack of efficacy of visual learning.","quote":"you'll definitely find this video: interesting.","text":"you'll definitely find this video: [VIDEO] interesting. Here they discuss the (lack of)efficacy of \"visual learning\""},{"id":31707314,"author":"ycdavidsmith","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best beginner friendly linear algebra book?","stance":"rec","why":"Disproves the myth that you are a visual learner.","quote":"watch this Veritasium video to disprove the myth that you’re a visual learner","text":"“Introduction to Linear Algebra” by Gilbert Strang is the book. Recommend getting a used older edition as not much has changed. His course at MIT is legendary, completely available online [LINK] And there’s so much good linear algebra stuff on YouTube from 3brown1blue. If you can do one thing now, watch this Veritasium video to disprove the myth that you’re a visual learner: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":31708081,"author":"Diris","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best beginner friendly linear algebra book?","stance":"rec","why":"Very good video with sources listed in description.","quote":"Veritasium has a very good video on the subject.[0]","text":"Veritasium has a very good video on the subject.[0] Sources are in the description but I might as well post them here. Pashler, H., McDaniel, M., Rohrer, D., & Bjork, R. (2008). Learning styles: Concepts and evidence. Psychological science in the public interest, 9(3), 105-119. — [LINK] Willingham, D. T., Hughes, E. M., & Dobolyi, D. G. (2015). The scientific status of learning styles theories. Teaching of Psychology, 42(3), 266-271. — [LINK] Massa, L. J., & Mayer, R. E. (2006). Testing the ATI hypothesis: Should multimedia instruction accommodate verbalizer-visualizer cognitive style?. Learning and Individual Differences, 16(4), 321-335. — [LINK] Riener, C., & Willingham, D. (2010). The myth of learning styles. Change: The magazine of higher learning, 42(5), 32-35.— [LINK] Husmann, P. R., & O'Loughlin, V. D. (2019). Another nail in the coffin for learning styles? Disparities among undergraduate anatomy students’ study strategies, class performance, and reported VARK learning styles. Anatomical sciences education, 12(1), 6-19. — [LINK] Snider, V. E., & Roehl, R. (2007). Teachers’ beliefs about pedagogy and related issues. Psychology in the Schools, 44, 873–886. doi:10.1002/pits.20272 — [LINK] Fleming, N., & Baume, D. (2006). Learning Styles Again: VARKing up the right tree!. Educational developments, 7(4), 4. — [LINK] Rogowsky, B. A., Calhoun, B. M., & Tallal, P. (2015). Matching learning style to instructional method: Effects on comprehension. Journal of educational psycholo"},{"id":30715436,"author":"systemvoltage","month":"2022-03","story":"Amazon's new role-playing game can help you build your AWS skills","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed yet approachable coverage of the learning styles myth.","quote":"Veritasium covered this in much greater detail but still approachable by the laymen","text":"I kind of wish this urban myth dies. There are more common things amongst humans about learning than differences. By a long shot. Veritasium covered this in much greater detail but still approachable by the laymen: [VIDEO] It almost always shutsdown the discussion of improving or criticizing learning methods. Because, there is this deus-ex-machina of learning \"Everyone is different\" and therefore we must not question, we must not contest, we must not improve. Btw, this is also rampant in other dysfunctional fields such as nutrition and product-reviews."},{"id":28919519,"author":"seer","month":"2021-10","story":"Long-term NAEP scores for 13-year-olds drop for first time since testing began","stance":"rec","why":"Recent misconceptions about teaching truths covered interestingly.","quote":"Veritasium did a very interesting video on the subject","text":"While we now know a lot about teaching, a lot of misconceptions have been found recently regarding “truths” about teaching, its a fascinating subject really, and the main problem seems to be lack of rigorous repeatable double blind testing for all of those things. Veritasium did a very interesting video on the subject: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41453800,"author":"mtmickush","month":"2024-09","story":"Kids who use ChatGPT as a study assistant do worse on tests","stance":"reference","why":"Learning styles is a common myth.","quote":"The concept of learning styles for individuals though is a common myth","text":"Glad to hear you were able to find a mechanism that clicked and stuck with it after that! The concept of learning styles for individuals though is a common myth [LINK] [VIDEO] There isn't any evidence that individuals learn best to a single style and generally approaching learning from multiple facets is the best way for everyone to learn!"},{"id":38783389,"author":"Balgair","month":"2023-12","story":"Things software developers should learn about learning","stance":"reference","why":"Derek covers research on rewards for subjects.","quote":"Derek goes into some of the research here","text":"Typically the researchers pay the subjects. It's a very active area of research though, and many reward schemes have been used and studied. Derek goes into some of the research here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37633330,"author":"rramadass","month":"2023-09","story":"The myth of the myth of learning styles","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium on learning styles.","quote":"Veritasium on this topic","text":"Veritasium on this topic : [VIDEO] The most important thing in learning is to actively engage one's Imagination in various ways. Algebraic/Geometric mappings, Multi-level abstractions, Shifting between Abstraction and Concrete Reality, Relationship networks etc. are all needed."},{"id":33814011,"author":"laserlight","month":"2022-12","story":"OpenAI ChatGPT: Optimizing Language Models for Dialogue","stance":"reference","why":"Popular video explaining learning styles is a myth.","quote":"Here's a popular video talking about it [0].","text":"> Find a good textbook or online course that is tailored to your level and learning style. Learning styles is a myth. Here's a popular video talking about it [0]. [0] The Biggest Myth In Education. [VIDEO]"},{"id":31708073,"author":"thaumasiotes","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best beginner friendly linear algebra book?","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as support for learning styles being unsupported.","quote":"","text":"> But aside from the flat statement do you have anything to back it up? Well, here's a comment from elsewhere in the thread: > If you can do one thing now, watch this Veritasium video to disprove the myth that you’re a visual learner: [VIDEO] . ( [LINK] ) I haven't watched the video, but, like your parent comment, I was already aware that \"learning styles\" was a research area supported almost exclusively by fraud. If you want more links, you can find them pretty easily through [LINK] ."},{"id":31708645,"author":"tptacek","month":"2022-06","story":"Ask HN: Best beginner friendly linear algebra book?","stance":"reference","why":"Video suggests multimodal approach helps everyone, not that visuals are useless.","quote":"The point of the video is that it's likely that everyone does better with a multimodal approach.","text":"This video about \"learning styles\" and the idea that there's no such thing as a \"visual learner\" is all over the thread: [VIDEO] It's worth calling out that the video itself doesn't support some of the blunt arguments being made here. The point of the video is that it's likely that everyone does better with a multimodal approach. It thus remains reasonable to seek out books that do a good job with visual representations! No visual components, or, worse, bad diagrams are, according to the video, an impediment to everyone, not just people who have used a disfavored term in their Ask HN question. :) I like 3Blue1Brown as much as everyone else, it's an achievement and kind of a joy to watch, but my experience was that, after many go-rounds over the years, the thing that made any of this actually stick was doing exercises. I tend to bang the less abstract ones out in Sage: [LINK] (you have to do a little bit of extra work to make sure Sage isn't doing too much of the work for you. I'm a fan of Strang's approach. But I'm bad at linear algebra, so, grain of salt."},{"id":31550109,"author":"rg111","month":"2022-05","story":"Ask HN: How to learn math from zero for adults?","stance":"reference","why":"Learning styles thoroughly debunked.","quote":"Veritasium recently made a video, too [0].","text":"I think that the fact that there are special types of learners have been debunked thoroughly. Veritasium recently made a video, too [0]. 3b1b is a fantastic supplementary resource. But you can't start with it, or use it as your primary resource. That would be a mistake. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30324583,"author":"modelviewpotato","month":"2022-02","story":"What does it mean to listen on a port?","stance":"reference","why":"Explains that everyone learns differently might not be true.","quote":"I found this video from Veritasium that explains it","text":"I've seen somewhere that \"everyone learns differently\" might not actually be true. I found this video from Veritasium that explains it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28802389,"author":"fouric","month":"2021-10","story":"Multitasking Doesn't Work","stance":"reference","why":"Example of inaccurate self-assessment of learner type.","quote":"","text":"Two counter-points: First, the point of a study is to be generalizeable - that is, to apply to as many people as possible, usually other than oneself. If a particular technique applies to you, that's great! However, most people aren't you. Starting with a known general principle/technique (most people can't multitask) will, on average, put you in a better position than randomly picking someone's anecdotal evidence. Second, there are many documented cases where self-assessment of one's own traits or capabilities is inaccurate. Performance while cognitively impaired (alcohol & sleep deprivation), assessment of skill as a novice (Dunning-Kruger), and the ability to multitask are all well-known cases of it, with many more lesser known (e.g. whether one is a \"visual\" or \"verbal\" learner[1]) - so, you should be wary about having too much self-confidence in your own capabilities, especially for something as poorly-understood as software engineering. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":28860393,"author":"teleforce","month":"2021-10","story":"Seven Deadly Sins of Introductory Programming Language Design (1996) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited regarding visual learning approach.","quote":"","text":"Very insightful paper, thanks for sharing. Firstly, I'm surprised that having no garbage collection did not make to the list of the seven deadly sins. Secondly, someone here also mentioned about the very useful Turbo Pascal IDE and having an excellent IDE can really help in the visual learning approach that's required by all learners [1]. If any programming languages has the intuitive capability for real-time visual interactions as described by Brett Victor it will be great [2]. Thirdly, the paper failed to mention that beginner friendly programming language has a very high chance to be adopted by the industry and becoming extremely popular. This is self evident by the recent exponential popularity of Python. When the paper was written back in 1996, Python was just another scripting programming language with \"Jack of all trades, master at none\" language. At the time it's playing second fiddle to popular programming languages including Perl, PHP and TCL! The same can be said to D language now but as they've always said, time will certainly tell. Personally I think D language did not tick most of the seven dealy sins boxes and have made the most of the seven significant steps towards more teachable language mentioned in the paper. It has the most Pythonic feel to it compared to other compiled languages. It also the has default garbage collection that will make it an excellent introduction for programming. Of course you can exercise bit manipulation like a madman with D (also ca"},{"id":28485377,"author":"travisgriggs","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: How Do You Learn?","stance":"reference","why":"People struggle to self-identify best learning methods.","quote":"it's really hard for us to self ascribe our best learning methods","text":"I learn any way I can. Having a purpose to learn helps (I need this knowledge, I want this knowledge because I think it's cool, etc). I will try books, videos, whatever works. Like folded steel and alloys, I find that I learn things from lots of different angles, and appreciate the amalgamation. Veritasium did a whole video [ [VIDEO] ] recently pointing out that it's really hard for us to self ascribe our best learning methods. I'll take whatever works and try as many as I can to keep the experience as rich as possible. If there's any unifying theme for me, it's \"I learn by embracing Humility, and avoiding Lazy\"."},{"id":28490568,"author":"a_d","month":"2021-09","story":"Visually stunning math concepts which are easy to explain","stance":"reference","why":"Asks about the video saying visual learners don't exist.","quote":"there is no such thing as a visual learner","text":"What do you think about a recent Veritassium video that discusses the idea that ‘there is no such thing as a visual learner’ ( [VIDEO] ) Just curious."},{"id":28518006,"author":"gobdovan","month":"2021-09","story":"Visually stunning math concepts which are easy to explain","stance":"reference","why":"Video discredits persistent learning styles as differentiator.","quote":"the Veritasium video also presents with the memory experiment","text":"I would say it's worth debating because it is an inexact and unsound principle on which recent education is built on. More than that, in the Romanian Master's cycle, if you want to continue your pedagogical studies such that you become a high school teacher, there is an entire course dedicated to learning styles and there is one more dedicated on the other infamous inexact-semantics concept, the multiple intelligences theory. So I would argue that it has a similarly bad effect on the education domain to any other arbitrary first-principles that lead to sub-optimal research and lack of clarity of what is really going on in some domain, which lead again to sub-optimal methods. Regarding your observation about how some kids simply learn better after seeing some visual representation of the concepts, there could be many reasons for this (they start paying attention when you draw, they fall somewhere on the Bell curve such that they need that extra representation to get it, as the Veritasium video also presents with the memory experiment), but I would doubt that persistent learning styles of your students would be the differentiator, which is what the video discredits ( [VIDEO] )."}],"r":[["zxRgfBXn6Mg",1],["nM9f0W2KD5s",1],["oIG9ztQw2Gc",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["C4VHfmiwuv4",1],["pTn6Ewhb27k",1],["OxGsU8oIWjY",1],["3LopI4YeC4I",1],["HeQX2HjkcNo",1],["mScpHTIi-kM",1],["cZYNADOHhVY",1],["APZCfmgzoS0",1],["QyJZzq0v7Z4",1],["C25qzDhGLx8",1],["CTV-wwszGw8",1]]},"WZskjLq040I":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on ancient games and cuneiform as a charming match with an enthusiastic expert.","q":{"x":"he also did a fantastic video on with Tom Scott","i":45924476,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":45924476,"author":"sothatsit","month":"2025-11","story":"Why I'm Learning Sumerian","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Finkel's fantastic video on the Royal Game of Ur with Tom Scott","quote":"he also did a fantastic video on with Tom Scott","text":"I was lucky enough to meet Irving Finkel a couple of times and he is as eccentric in person as he is in his brilliant lectures, talks, and videos. When I met him all the shelves, and even the floor, in his office was covered with different books, papers, and artifacts. He has amassed quite a collection of interesting things. I met him through my work related to the Royal Game of Ur, which he also did a fantastic video on with Tom Scott: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21910791,"author":"jedimastert","month":"2019-12","story":"How writing began, and other unexpectedly funny stories about cuneiform [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Irving Finkel teaches Tom Scott the Royal Game of Ur","quote":"He's got two incredible videos with Tom Scott","text":"He really is. He's got two incredible videos with Tom Scott where the two of them play what could be considered the worlds oldest game[0] and he introduces Tom and Matt to \"writing\" cuneiform[1]. He's so sharp in both, I love seeing him anywhere else now. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":36279504,"author":"yreg","month":"2023-06","story":"Turn dumb tweets into cuneiform tablets","stance":"rec","why":"Finkel and Tom Scott play the game of Ur","quote":"I enjoyed the interaction.","text":"Irving Finkel made a video with Tom Scott, playing the game of Ur. I enjoyed the interaction. [VIDEO] I'm sure he has many other even better videos, but I will have to yet get to them."},{"id":27883229,"author":"acheron","month":"2021-07","story":"Senet","stance":"rec","why":"Finkel playing the Royal Game of Ur","quote":"There’s a great video on YouTube of Irving Finkel (quite an interesting guy) playing the Royal Game of Ur.","text":"There’s a great video on YouTube of Irving Finkel (quite an interesting guy) playing the Royal Game of Ur. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27883930,"author":"mabub24","month":"2021-07","story":"Senet","stance":"rec","why":"Finkel explains Ur and its playing rules","quote":"This is an interesting video from Dr. Irving Finkel on it and its playing rules.","text":"You might be thinking of Ur, which is roughly 4,500 years old. This is an interesting video from Dr. Irving Finkel on it and its playing rules.[0] [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22300992,"author":"jhallenworld","month":"2020-02","story":"Senet: the original board game of death?","stance":"rec","why":"Playthrough of the Royal Game of Ur","quote":"I like much better the Royal Game of Ur-","text":"I've gotten into ancient games recently, and I've tried Senet with various rule variations- I think it's kind of boring. I like much better the Royal Game of Ur- [VIDEO] I've also been playing with Indian Pachisi, two player is kind of boring, but I still need to try four player / doubles."},{"id":40647683,"author":"Joel_Mckay","month":"2024-06","story":"Anti-Cheat Expert: all your pixels are belong to us","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"\"Okay, so I buy a card I can actually afford on eBay, put it in my machine, buy a game, and get my account permabanned.\" One would have to prove they are a different person/account-location to IT admin support. It is rare, but some companies will simply watch your game-play for a bit to see what you are up to compared to the prior ban details. Note one will likely have to gamble on the compassion of each firms IT admins. \"What refund am I asking for that makes this okay?\" These sellers are almost always knowingly trying to evade the consequences of their behavior, and trying to recoup their money to buy un-flagged hardware. This is pointless, as there are several anti-cheat queries companies can run, and like any antisocial behavior they usually just make the situation worse for themselves by contaminating the market with more flagged hardware. Eventually their sellers reputation score will warn buyers what they are doing. Getting a refund for the \"broken\" equipment is a standard ebay RMA process, and Craigslist/meta-Marketplace returns can be a bit more complex. Keep in mind a ban may eventually lift after 12 months, but IT admins may be less convinced of your intent if a user keeps popping up as a problem. \"This reads as condescending\" It is not my call what companies do with problematic users. And MTG events also bans cheats for marked decks, and pulling unfair tricks that violate the rules. The difference is people will be upset with such antics at the same table. Persona"},{"id":26027292,"author":"Rendello","month":"2021-02","story":"How to write cuneiform","stance":"reference","why":"Finkel teaches Tom Scott the Royal Game of Ur","quote":"He also taught Tom to play The Royal Game of Ur [1]","text":"He also taught Tom to play The Royal Game of Ur [1], a Middle-Eastern precursor to Backgammon that was the Chess of that era in terms of popularity. Irving was the one to decipher the rules from a cuneiform tablet. 1. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23488392,"author":"jhallenworld","month":"2020-06","story":"How dice changed over 2k years to be more fair (2018)","stance":"reference","why":"Example of binary dice used for the Royal Game of Ur","quote":"","text":"I've been trying other non-roman dice: Indian four-sided dice: [LINK] Two-sided dice (cowry shells) for Indian Pachisi- they are terrible! [LINK] More binary dice for the Royal Game of Ur: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["8PmM6SUn7Es",1],["3AtBE9BOvvk",1],["Oo0jm1PPRuo",1],["GsyhGHUEt-k",1],["XGo4PJd1lng",1],["lIFE7h3m40U",1],["Fy0aCDmgnxg",1],["i70wkxmumAw",1],["LnzuMJLZRdU",1],["aVwxzDHniEw",1],["jGWFg7EDnyY",1],["goePYJ74Ydg",1],["OHPkdMGI6D4",1],["zwAD6dRSVyI",1],["Pld8wTa16Jk",1],["8YUWDrLazCg",1],["MFEHFsO-XSI",1],["kCc8FmEb1nY",1],["iHzzSao6ypE",1],["IdpD5QIVOKQ",1]]},"98LdFA-_zfA":{"wy":"HN cites it as the primary source for the claim that Alexander deprecated patterns, and as a prescient talk for programmers.","q":{"x":"Just finished watching a great 1996 lecture by Christopher Alexander","i":18648331,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":18648331,"author":"simonebrunozzi","month":"2018-12","story":"“A pattern language” explained (2016)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"1996 lecture to see the man in action","quote":"Just finished watching a great 1996 lecture by Christopher Alexander","text":"Just finished watching a great 1996 lecture by Christopher Alexander: [VIDEO] I recommend it for those who have read his work, but never seen or heard the man in action."},{"id":49016999,"author":"keithnz","month":"2026-07","story":"All 253 Patterns from Christopher Alexander's a Pattern Language Summarized","stance":"rec","why":"1996 talk moving from patterns to fundamental concepts","quote":"worth checking this out from 1996","text":"worth checking this out from 1996 [VIDEO] kind of ironic he was moving away from patterns to more fundamental concepts just as the software world was just discovering patterns. I think his shift to these more fundamental concepts is a much better way to think about design."},{"id":26382645,"author":"keithnz","month":"2021-03","story":"“Design Patterns” Aren't (2002)","stance":"rec","why":"Alexander moves beyond patterns toward intrinsic design qualities","quote":"this talk that christopher alexander did a number of years ago is quite interesting","text":"this talk that christopher alexander did a number of years ago is quite interesting [VIDEO] one of the interesting things is that he had really moved on from \"patterns\" and was looking for something more intrinisic or \"atomic\" about design. Which actually is a much better guide to designing software."},{"id":20784984,"author":"paloaltokid","month":"2019-08","story":"Japanese architects who treated buildings like living organisms","stance":"rec","why":"Prescient 1996 talk to ACM audience","quote":"For a very prescient talk by Alexander in 1996 to a bunch of ACM folks, see this video","text":"For anyone interested, Christopher Alexander was a huge influence on Ward Cunningham and Kent Beck. You can draw a fairly straight line from Alexander's ideas to new stuff like wiki, Extreme Programming, design patterns, refactoring, etc. For a very prescient talk by Alexander in 1996 to a bunch of ACM folks, see this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12861667,"author":"shkaboinka","month":"2016-11","story":"WebAssembly Stack Machine","stance":"rec","why":"Alexander's philosophy of unfolding wholeness","quote":"if you're brave enough, think about how Christopher Alexander's philosophy","text":"...and if you're brave enough, think about how Christopher Alexander's philosophy of \"unfolding wholeness\" applies so much more to an AST than to the stack-machines of the 1960's: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42298161,"author":"zenoprax","month":"2024-12","story":"The Timeless Way of Software [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Alexander on changing law and norms so living structures emerge","quote":"he talks about how to change law, institutional norms, and so on to make it possible for living structures to emerge.","text":"Christopher discusses this problem in architecture too. [~45 minutes in]( [VIDEO] ) he talks about how to change law, institutional norms, and so on to make it possible for living structures to emerge. I don't know what that would look like for software but in another talk he was bemused by the narrow-mindedness of programmers who only saw the value of A Pattern Language as a mechanical tool rather than as a way to create a more beautiful world."},{"id":41176092,"author":"ebcode","month":"2024-08","story":"Keynote Speech to the 1996 Oopsla Convention","stance":"reference","why":"Video of the 1996 OOPSLA keynote","quote":"A video of this speech, without Jim Coplien's introduction, is on youtube","text":"A video of this speech, without Jim Coplien's introduction, is on youtube: [VIDEO] >> Please forgive me, I'm going to be very direct and blunt for a horrible second. It could be thought that the technical way in which you currently look at programming is almost as if you were willing to be \"guns for hire.\" In other words, you are the technicians. You know how to make the programs work. \"Tell us what to do daddy, and we'll do it.\" That is the worm in the apple. >> What I am proposing here is something a little bit different from that. It is a view of programming as the natural genetic infrastructure of a living world which you/we are capable of creating, managing, making available, and which could then have the result that a living structure in our towns, houses, work places, cities, becomes an attainable thing. That would be remarkable. It would turn the world around, and make living structure the norm once again, throughout society, and make the world worth living in again."},{"id":40352869,"author":"no_identd","month":"2024-05","story":"A Pattern Language","stance":"reference","why":"Alexander renounced patterns in 1996","quote":"Christopher Alexander effectively renounced patterns in 1996","text":"To quote Dorian Taylor[no affiliation, albeit we do follow each other on Twitter & BlueSky] from [LINK] : \"Chances are if you're in software or digital design, you've heard of the book A Pattern Language, well, you may not be aware that Christopher Alexander effectively renounced patterns in 1996 ( [VIDEO] ). He said he had something better… The problem is, that better something happens to be four books and 2500 pages, and weigh 12 pounds. And, it's about buildings, not software. So my service to you is interpreting the text for software, and cutting the reading down by an order of magnitude.\" That \"something better\" being Christopher Alexander's Opus Magnum, \"The Nature of Order\". Dorian's working on this under the name \"The Nature of Software\" here: [LINK] And here: [LINK]"},{"id":40359131,"author":"dorian","month":"2024-05","story":"A Pattern Language","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote where Alexander deprecated patterns","quote":"he explicitly deprecated patterns at his OOPSLA 1996 keynote","text":"If you're going to read the whole Alexander corpus (which I did minus the two hardest-to-find volumes—the Linz Café and the one about carpets), be prepared for it to take on the order of years. While there is for sure a lot of repetition, the insights are frustratingly smeared across the entire thing. Moreover, there is a clear arc to Alexander's career that goes a little like: • Mathematical era (PhD/Notes on the Synthesis of Form, A City is not a Tree) • Pattern era (Timeless Way, APL, and about four case studies) • 15 properties era (Nature of Order) As one might expect, a lot of the earlier work is recapitulated in the later work, but the fact that he explicitly deprecated patterns at his OOPSLA 1996 keynote ( [VIDEO] ) is important. People are aware of APL because of Gang of Four and Richard Gabriel etc but not so much that lecture. As for the fifteen properties in Nature of Order, they mainly concern Euclidean geometry and the ordinary physics one would associate with constructing actual buildings. The evidence that they would need to be adapted to a more generic semiotic-topological domain such as software is the fact that Alexander himself saw fit to draw up (in Book 4) eleven analogous properties pertaining exclusively to colour (a 1:1 correspondence except for four which coalesce two of the geometric properties each). Concepts like \"life\", \"wholeness\", \"center\", \"the fundamental differentiating process\" etc. can be used unchanged."},{"id":40370961,"author":"dorian","month":"2024-05","story":"A Pattern Language","stance":"reference","why":"Alexander calls patterns fragmentary perceptions of deeper structure","quote":"So maybe not an outright renunciation per se, but definitely a deprecation.","text":"He literally says (at 39:48 [VIDEO] ) \"And it's because [the patterns are] only really fragmentary perceptions of this deeper structure that I'm describing [i.e., centers, the 15 properties…], that they are ultimately unsatisfactory; I think they're not capable of delivering the goods.\" So maybe not an outright renunciation per se, but definitely a deprecation."},{"id":34016107,"author":"lolinder","month":"2022-12","story":"Beautiful Software: Christopher Alexander's research initiative on computing","stance":"reference","why":"Alexander hints programmers misunderstood the point of patterns","quote":"I recently rewatched his address to OOPSLA[0]","text":"I recently rewatched his address to OOPSLA[0] and it was funny to see him trying to avoid coming straight out and saying \"you guys completely misunderstood the point of my work!\" He tried to allow for the possibility that he was just misunderstanding, but it was pretty obvious that he knew that the Gang of Four style patterns movement took the surface level organization from his work and completely ignored the moral and ethical point of it all. Glad to see he didn't leave off with that! We could do with more software people studying his actual work, which was building humane, living spaces that make people whole. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":34017551,"author":"AlbertCory","month":"2022-12","story":"Beautiful Software: Christopher Alexander's research initiative on computing","stance":"reference","why":"Alexander is less pompous in person than in writing","quote":"I didn't get that in watching him","text":"Is it possible to sympathize and agree with everything he says, and yet find the writing insufferably pompous? Because that's where I'm at with The Timeless Way of Building. I didn't get that in watching him: [VIDEO] I was picturing someone like Dan Akroyd on SNL as Leonard Pinth Garnell and \"Bad Ballet.\""},{"id":11947986,"author":"shkaboinka","month":"2016-06","story":"Alan Kay has agreed to do an AMA today","stance":"reference","why":"Alexander addressing software community's misapplication of his work at OOPSLA","quote":"video of C.A. addressing the software community's (mis-)application of his work at OOPSLA","text":"I've seen similarities between your work (OOP) and that of Christopher Alexander (Patterns). Do you have anything to say about how your/his works tie together? (Note that Alexander's work is perhaps even more misrepresented in software than OOP has come to be). For example, he talks a lot about how anything that is to evolve naturally, or even properly serve the human component, must be composed of living structure. video of C.A. addressing the software community's (mis-)application of his work at OOPSLA: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["4_qJptwikRc",1],["au0QJYISe4c",1],["VjE0Kdfos4Y",1],["AvEqfg2sIH0",1],["_ZG8HBuDjgc",1],["PSGEjv3Tqo0",1]]},"IgF3OX8nT0w":{"wy":"Recommended in threads on analog computing, photonic chips, AI hardware and tide prediction as an engaging history of Kelvin's machines.","q":{"x":"His video before this one[1] goes into detail on that, and it was incredible.","i":30558936,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":30558936,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2022-03","story":"We're building computers wrong [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Details Kelvin's tide-predicting analog computers; incredible history.","quote":"His video before this one[1] goes into detail on that, and it was incredible.","text":"In the intro to this video he mentions analogue computers predicting tides. His video before this one[1] goes into detail on that, and it was incredible. In the 1800s, Lord Kelvin spent years working on tidal rise and fall patterns, applying Fourier Transforms to break them down into ten individual sine waves, then combining those sine waves back together to predict future tides. And built analog computers to do all the involved integration, multiplying and summing. It's a part of the history of computing I'd never heard of, despite hearing quite a lot about the dawn of electro-mechanical computers in the early 1900s. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":46170266,"author":"o4c","month":"2025-12","story":"Tides are weirder than you think","stance":"rec","why":"Kelvin's tide-predicting machine.","quote":"Veritasium made a great video on Kelvin's Tide-predicting machine in 2021.","text":"Veritasium made a great video on Kelvin's Tide-predicting machine in 2021. Title: The Most Powerful Computers You've Never Heard Of URL: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40555971,"author":"nico","month":"2024-06","story":"Lord Kelvin and his analog computer","stance":"rec","why":"Covers analog computers and Kelvin's tide prediction machine.","quote":"There is a very good Veritasium video that talks about analog computers","text":"There is a very good Veritasium video that talks about analog computers and Lord Kelvin’s tide prediction machine: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31661910,"author":"woojoo666","month":"2022-06","story":"Photonic Chip Performs Image Recognition at the Speed of Light","stance":"rec","why":"Two great videos on analog computing in AI.","quote":"Veritasium has two great videos on it","text":"It's a big area of research in AI. Veritasium has two great videos on it: [LINK] [VIDEO] I found a hackernews discussion for the second one: [LINK]"},{"id":30590394,"author":"cultofmetatron","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: What is your take on carbon emissions of Machine Learning","stance":"rec","why":"Explains analog computing hardware better than commenter could.","quote":"here's two youtube videos that explain is much better than I could.","text":"basicly, we can do things like matrix operations and differntial equations using specialized hardware that performs computation over analog signals rather than buidlign discrete systems. the end result is that we dont' need anywhere near the level of compute to do the job as its done using an analog of the physical process. here's two youtube videos that explain is much better than I could. [VIDEO] [LINK] suffice to say, your average house fly exhibits behavior that absolutely dominates our best drones and with a fraction of the circuitry."},{"id":29787165,"author":"WJW","month":"2022-01","story":"Mechanical computing systems using only links and rotary joints","stance":"rec","why":"Tide-computing analog machines doing Fourier transforms without electricity.","quote":"It's quite fascinating how they even managed to do basic Fourier transforms without electricity","text":"You might be interested in the analog computers that were used to compute tide tables back in the day. It's quite fascinating how they even managed to do basic Fourier transforms without electricity: [VIDEO]"},{"id":46442783,"author":"nosianu","month":"2025-12","story":"Animated AI","stance":"reference","why":"Example that math is more than symbol manipulation.","quote":"","text":"So... I remember math including doing quite a bit of geometry by hand and with real tools, at least initially. \"Math\" is not just the symbol stuff written with a pencil, or with a keyboard. The mechanical analog computers of old (e.g. [VIDEO] , or [LINK] ) are examples too that math is more than symbol manipulation."},{"id":41300360,"author":"shagie","month":"2024-08","story":"Basic Mechanisms In Fire Control Computers (1953) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Tide calculator plays a prominent part of the video.","quote":"the tide calculator plays a prominent part of the video.","text":"The Thomson (also known as Lord Kelvin of degrees K fame) tide predicting machine takes us back to the late 1800s. [LINK] One implementation of it was a notable part of WWII: > They came to be regarded as of military strategic importance during World War I, and again during the Second World War, when the US No.2 Tide Predicting Machine, described below, was classified, along with the data that it produced, and used to predict tides for the D-Day Normandy landings and all the island landings in the Pacific War. [LINK] From Veritasium : The Most Powerful Computers You've Never Heard Of - the tide calculator plays a prominent part of the video. [VIDEO] (the next video is also in the same topic - Future Computers Will Be Radically Different [LINK] and that gets into more modern implementations and uses - [LINK] is the device shown in the video)."},{"id":38504827,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2023-12","story":"Ask HN: What are some unpopular technologies you wish people knew more about?","stance":"reference","why":"Learned Kelvin invented tide-predicting analog computers.","quote":"until last year's Veritasium video on YouTube[2] about analog computers","text":"I used to know nothing about Lord Kelvin except he said things like \" It seems, therefore, on the whole most probable that the sun has not illuminated the earth for 100,000,000 years, and almost certain that he has not done so for 500,000,000 years \"[1] and allegedly \"everything which can be discovered, has been discovered\"; until last year's Veritasium video on YouTube[2] about analog computers, and learned he invented tide-predicting analog computers to \"substitute brass for brains\" and add sinusoidal curves, and a mechanical integrator to separate out individual sinusoidal frequencies from the sum. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] from about 3 minutes."},{"id":37731692,"author":"mbivert","month":"2023-10","story":"Hermit 'scribblings' of French math genius Alexander Grothendieck unveiled","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for engineer's dream about anti-aircraft gun accuracy.","quote":"","text":"Adding to this list of unorthodox approaches, by modern standards: I've heard in some French conferences of Etienne Klein (I'm not sure how romanticized this is though) that Einstein had some breakthrough while being in a half-asleep state. Here's another similar instance[0][1], better documented, of an engineer working on an automatic level recorder, and \"realizing\" in a dream that it could be used to improve the accuracy of anti-aircraft guns (WWII). [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36166357,"author":"mcdonje","month":"2023-06","story":"An open source, educational, low-cost modern analog computer","stance":"reference","why":"Veritasium's analog computing videos from a year earlier.","quote":"Veritasium did a couple of videos on analog computing a year ago.","text":"That's really neat. Veritasium did a couple of videos on analog computing a year ago. I'm glad there are people lowering the barrier to entry. [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":30519525,"author":"ktpsns","month":"2022-03","story":"Why Analog Computers May Be the Future of Artificial Intelligence (Veritasium)","stance":"reference","why":"First of two Veritasium videos on analog computers.","quote":"The first one was","text":"The product presented: [LINK] made by [LINK] The company presented: [LINK] This is the second video (of a series of two) by Veritasium about analog computers. The first one was [VIDEO] and discussed on Hacker News at [LINK]"},{"id":30166430,"author":"abalaji","month":"2022-02","story":"Predicting the tide with an analog computer made from Lego","stance":"reference","why":"Inspiration for the Lego tide predictor.","quote":"The author mentions they were inspired by the Veritasium video on the same topic","text":"The author mentions they were inspired by the Veritasium video on the same topic (without the Lego). [1] I thought I'd link the previous HN discussion. [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":29714034,"author":"faeyanpiraat","month":"2021-12","story":"France's Jean Zay supercomputer now has an integrated photonic coprocessor","stance":"reference","why":"Teaser of upcoming analog computing video.","quote":"There is an upcoming Veritasium video about the \"comeback of analogue computers\".","text":"There is an upcoming Veritasium video about the \"comeback of analogue computers\". Part one (teaser): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["GVsUOuSjvcg",2],["knbVWXzL4-4",1],["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1]]},"tWr39Q9vBgo":{"wy":"Commenters post it whenever a thread touches on soft sciences, replication problems, or what counts as real science.","q":{"x":"it's very hard to know something and you have to be really careful that you've done the work and aren't just deluding yourself","i":14148484,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":48972874,"author":"senshan","month":"2026-07","story":"AI advice made people 3x less accurate but 2x confident, researchers found","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Feynman on how hard real knowledge is and fooling yourself","quote":"This could serve as a beacon for those who still care","text":"This could serve as a beacon for those who still care: Richard Feynman: \"I have the advantage of having found out how hard it is to get to really know something, how careful you have to be about checking the experiments, how easy it is to make mistakes and fool yourself...\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":48261769,"author":"aeternum","month":"2026-05","story":"Australia Four-Day Work Week Study Data Shows Boosted Productivity","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman argues peer review and p-values do not make science","quote":"Worth watching the clip so you can hear the argument directly.","text":"I'm the one that said usually, Feynman didn't have that cop-out and he was specifically talking about social science: [VIDEO] Worth watching the clip so you can hear the argument directly. IMO his point is that peer review is not what makes something science. Nor are studies, publishing papers nor p-values, even gathering and reproducing data is not what makes science science."},{"id":18079137,"author":"qubax","month":"2018-09","story":"A Cornell Scientist’s Downfall","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman's pointed take on social science","quote":"Here is richard feynmann's poignant take on the matter.","text":"\"Social science\". The term science has been appropriated by so many pseudosciences that it has lost any meaning. Because real science has such a good reputation, all the academic frauds have decided to link their field to science to enhance their own reputation. There is political \"science\", legal \"science\", economics, etc. Here is richard feynmann's poignant take on the matter. BTW, it has gotten way worse since feynmann's interview. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17293454,"author":"paidleaf","month":"2018-06","story":"The Lifespan of a Lie – Why can’t we escape the Stanford Prison Experiment?","stance":"rec","why":"Feynman interview on soft sciences versus real science","quote":"Richard Feynman had a very interesting interview about it.","text":"> In other words, the field just has very low standards for truth, in a scientific sense. The field has little to no scientific sense. It's why psychiatry/psychology is a soft science. It isn't based on the scientific method or reproducibility and testing. It's mostly based on consensus driven by a handful of powerful practitioners. [LINK] What sigmund freud did wasn't science. His oedipal complex theory isn't a scientific theory. There is no scientific test to refute it. You just accept it or not. The biggest problem of our generation is the conflation of science ( hard science ) with pseudoscience ( soft science ). Because both has the word science in it, people give soft sciences far more credit than it deserves. If anyone is interested, Richard Feynman had a very interesting interview about it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14148484,"author":"summerdown2","month":"2017-04","story":"Bangkok plans to ban all street food by year′s end","stance":"rec","why":"Short Feynman clip on how hard it is to really know something","quote":"it's very hard to know something and you have to be really careful that you've done the work and aren't just deluding yourself","text":"I agree with you a bit. But I also think there's a danger in assuming every anecdote is a data point, which is best expressed here: [VIDEO] (Short video with Richard Feynman)... tl;dv - it's very hard to know something and you have to be really careful that you've done the work and aren't just deluding yourself. Or, to put it differently - every anecdote is really only a data point if you understand what exactly it's measuring and what has been excluded. If you haven't taken the care necessary, then it's very easy to draw false conclusions. People are really bad witnesses and really bad at seeing what is and isn't given in the information they think they have - particularly if the anecdote is time-shifted from the point it was taken."},{"id":44729047,"author":"TexanFeller","month":"2025-07","story":"Stanford’s Department of Management Science and Engineering","stance":"reference","why":"Obligatory Feynman on what counts as a science","quote":"Obligatory Feynman on “sciences","text":"> Management Science It’s jarring and galling to see management and science put together in a way that’s suggestive of management being a science. It reeks of stolen valor. Obligatory Feynman on “sciences”: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23436460,"author":"dntbnmpls","month":"2020-06","story":"Humanities aren't a science and shouldn't be treated like one (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman calling social science a pseudoscience","quote":"Richard Feymann called social science a pseudoscience a few decades ago.","text":"> Then they goes on to criticize political science and psychology as their poster children for the humanities, except these are social sciences Social sciences are a humanities. The term social science was invented fairly recently by sneaky academics to leech off the credibility of actual science. > It's like the author isn't even familiar with academia. Are you? This issue with \"social science\" has been going on for a few decades now. Richard Feymann called social science a pseudoscience a few decades ago. [VIDEO] The name social science was invented for the same reason creation science was invented because real science had such a good reputation and they didn't so they decided to manufacture some credibility by attaching \"science\" to their fields."},{"id":20287830,"author":"germanlee","month":"2019-06","story":"The “backfire effect” is mostly a myth, a broad look at the research suggests","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman associating social science with pseudoscience","quote":"Did you bother watching what he had to say?","text":"It's in a replication crisis because pretty much none of it is science ( no replicable testing possible - hypothesis, experiment, theory ). It's why Richard Feynmann associated social science with pseudoscience. [VIDEO] Because real science destroyed the credibility of religion and religion in much of the world is no longer a credible social control tool, the elites needed a new form of religion to control society. That new religion is social \"science\". Whereas religion controlled everything from economics, schooling, family, culture, society, law, etc, now they all fall under the pseudoscience/religion called social \"science\". --------------------------------------------- Reply to ziddoap. Considering you tossed around \"illumati-esque\", I doubt you are interested. I consider social science to be a pseudoscience for the same reason richard feynmann did. Did you bother watching what he had to say? Social \"science\" is a humanities. It belongs in the category with philosophy, ethics, literature, religion, etc. Just because I said it is a pseudoscience doesn't mean that I think it is useless or bad necessarily. No more than I think literature, ethics, philosophy or even religion is bad. I just think social \"science\" is a \"religion\" trying to latch onto the good name of real science. Just like creationism \"science\" or all the other fake \"science\" trying to gain credibility by associating itself with science."},{"id":19879702,"author":"basetop","month":"2019-05","story":"After men in Spain got paternity leave, they wanted fewer kids","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as Feynman calling the field pseudoscience","quote":"Or as Richard Feynmann wisely and correctly called it - pseudoscience.","text":"Or as Richard Feynmann wisely and correctly called it - pseudoscience. [VIDEO] It's pseudoscience used by people to push ideological and political agenda. Ever since science pretty much knocked religion down a peg or two, the activists created a new form of \"religion\" to further political goals."},{"id":19665234,"author":"basetop","month":"2019-04","story":"Thomas Kuhn Wasn't So Bad","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman calling soft science a pseudoscience","quote":"Richard Feynman called \"soft science\" a pseudoscience, which I completely agree with.","text":"Actually what most people consider science is in line with Popper's view. When most people think science, they think the \"hard sciences\" - physics, biology, chemistry, etc. It's fairly recently that the idea of the \"soft sciences\" ( sociology, economists, etc ) being real science has become popular. Richard Feynman called \"soft science\" a pseudoscience, which I completely agree with. [VIDEO] Humanities departments around the country essentially appropriated the good name of science at add more legitimacy to their \"soft science\". It's similar to how \"christian science\" or \"scientology\" appropriated the good name of science to legitimatize themselves in the eyes of the public. Creationists have even created creationism \"science\" to make themselves look more legitimate."},{"id":18702377,"author":"porpoisely","month":"2018-12","story":"Is the Psychology of Deadly Force Ready for the Courts?","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman calling social science pseudoscience until proven otherwise","quote":"he called social science a pseudoscience until proven otherwise","text":"You aren't being snarky. There has been lingering questions about the field of psychology and social science in general for a very long time. Paraphrasing Richard Feymann, he called social science a pseudoscience until proven otherwise. [VIDEO] And after all these years since his interview, we are still waiting. This is one of the reasons why some have placed their hopes on neuroscience being the science of the brain and mind."},{"id":14783339,"author":"awkwarddaturtle","month":"2017-07","story":"How economics became a religion","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as Feynman calling social sciences pseudoscience","quote":"Economics is what Feynman calls a \"pseudoscience","text":"Economics has always been more of a religion than anything else. It has no predictive aspects. It is purely reflective. And the entire system has a group of \"ideologies/leaders/etc\" that adherents cling to and worship. Economics is what Feynman calls a \"pseudoscience\" ( aka social sciences ). [VIDEO] One sect of economists will argue with another sect of economists and neither side can ever win. Just like muslims and jews will argue with each other and never get anywhere."},{"id":13717399,"author":"_Codemonkeyism","month":"2017-02","story":"Most scientists 'can't replicate studies by their peers'","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on what real science is","quote":"Or listen to anything from Feynman about real science","text":"Simple. You can only publish to Nature etc. if your results are replicated twice by others and you open up all your raw data. Short time later only real science will be published. The amount of published results will massively go down and Nature and other will not makes as much money. Now you know the reason why publishers publish results that can't be replicated. Or listen to anything from Feynman about real science e.g. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12199583,"author":"ontouchstart","month":"2016-08","story":"What Babies Know About Physics and Foreign Languages","stance":"reference","why":"Feynman on how easy it is to fool yourself","quote":"I guess that you would agree with Richard Feynman","text":"I guess that you would agree with Richard Feynman [VIDEO] > ... how easy it is to make mistake and fool yourself ..."}],"r":[["2DIl3Hfh9tY",1],["kopoLzvh5jY",1],["ODSJsviD_SU",1],["xRel1JKOEbI",1]]},"HB5TrK7A4pI":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as a mind-expanding talk on rethinking programming, and cite it in threads on propagators, constraints and brittleness.","q":{"x":"it made me question my views on what I think programming is","i":37582217,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":48437117,"author":"gobdovan","month":"2026-06","story":"My Software North Star","stance":"rec","why":"Sussman on correctness, brittleness, and being close enough.","quote":"mandatory Sussman reference [0], where he talks about correctness not being that important","text":"Grandparent is onto something. You can't define correctness without a spec. And since most business-oriented software, which most of us work with, isn't made against a comprehensive spec, you can argue that anything falling outside it is either a bug or a feature. 'If your disk backup software corrupts backups' has behind it a clean definition that's (pretty) unambiguous and you don't care about, since it's already outsourced to some cloud solution. But 'User finds it easy to buy more stuff' does not. Also, mandatory Sussman reference [0], where he talks about correctness not being that important and gives Google as example, that just needs to be close enough and not disastrously incorrect + interesting stuff around engineers confusing brittleness with correctness. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43562722,"author":"teleforce","month":"2025-04","story":"Prof. Sussman's Reading List (2016)","stance":"rec","why":"Sussman talk on research with former students.","quote":"I highly recommend Prof. Sussman talk at the Strange Loop conference 2011","text":"I highly recommend Prof. Sussman talk at the Strange Loop conference 2011 [1]. It's not his inaugural lecture but it's pretty close where he talk about his research work with his former students. It's also paved the way to modern constraint programming based on logic and optimization which are the machine intelligence that's complementary to the conventional data driven and learning bases AI. [1] \"We Really Don't Know How to Compute!\" - Gerald Sussman - MIT (2011) [video]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37582217,"author":"throwaway71271","month":"2023-09","story":"Programming Is Hard","stance":"rec","why":"Talk that made commenter question what programming is.","quote":"it made me question my views on what I think programming is","text":"For some reason programming is not getting any easier, I can even make an argument its getting worse despite all the improved tooling and andragogy investments we have done. Programming is in some sort of local minimum and we keep going in circles. Few years ago I watched this talk by Gerald Sussman: \"We really don't know how to compute\" - [VIDEO] and it made me question my views on what I think programming is. I dont know if its because people are afraid to try new things, or there is no funding to try new things, we are kind of stuck. People thinking \"don't reinvent the wheel\" and for some reason think we have built a wheel (as Casey Muratori says: [LINK] ). We have a square at best. There are so many problems in the world, and a lot of them need robust and resilient programs to be solved, e.g. self cleaning public toilets or worm farm that you feed bio garbage and it produces protein flour. The challenge is exactly the same as it was 80 years ago, manage complexity in chaotic and emergent systems: chaos, complexity and entropy as the poet said. And to be honest, I think we have gotten worse at managing all of those, most bugs that we encounter now is like a murder mystery from Detective Conan and it requires Shinichi Kudo level of deductive reasoning to figure out what is going on. Programming will remain hard if you keep thinking it is just a translation problem from a \"business requirement\" to a \"computer language\"."},{"id":34799349,"author":"pvillano","month":"2023-02","story":"The program is the database is the interface","stance":"rec","why":"Sussman talk relevant to the topic","quote":"you might be interested in","text":"you might be interested in [VIDEO] \"We Really Don't Know How to Compute!\"- Gerald Sussman (2011) [1:04:18]"},{"id":33658992,"author":"pfarrell","month":"2022-11","story":"Fred Brooks has died","stance":"rec","why":"Inspiring talk on rethinking computation","quote":"all of Brett Victor's speeches to be incredibly inspiring","text":"Not writing, but I have found all of Brett Victor's speeches to be incredibly inspiring on how I think about what I want to work on and where we're going. Inventing on Principle by Brett Victor: [LINK] Growing a Language by Guy Steele: [LINK] We Really Don't Know How to Compute! by Gerald Sussman: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45245145,"author":"teleforce","month":"2025-09","story":"Many hard LeetCode problems are easy constraint problems","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source on constraint programming.","quote":"","text":"Constraint programming complement ML/AI/LLM nicely in system solving real world problems [1],[2],[3],[4]. One of the highest stakes and hardest engineering problems namely wireless spectrum frequency allocation was solved by constraint programming and presented in [1]. Now anyone can try to solve the problem in a MiniZinc tutorial. Fun fact, there's also Knuth in the presentation audience trying to get some info on the subject matter perhaps for his next book. [1] Logic, Optimization, and Constraint Programming: A Fruitful Collaboration - John Hooker - CMU (2023) [video]: [LINK] [2] \"We Really Don't Know How to Compute!\" - Gerald Sussman - MIT (2011) [video]: [VIDEO] [3] Google OR-Tools: [LINK] [4] MiniZinc: [LINK]"},{"id":44890212,"author":"teleforce","month":"2025-08","story":"LLMs aren't world models","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for deterministic logic approaches.","quote":"","text":">LLMs are not \"compelled\" by the training algorithms to learn symbolic logic. I think \"compell\" is such a unique human trait that machine will never replicate to the T. The article did mention specifically about this very issue: \"And of course people can be like that, too - eg much better at the big O notation and complexity analysis in interviews than on the job. But I guarantee you that if you put a gun to their head or offer them a million dollar bonus for getting it right, they will do well enough on the job, too. And with 200 billion thrown at LLM hardware last year, the thing can't complain that it wasn't incentivized to perform.\" If it's not already evident that in itself LLM is a limited stochastic AI tool by definition and its distant cousins are the deterministic logic, optimization and constraint programming [1],[2],[3]. Perhaps one of the two breakthroughs that the author was predicting will be in this deterministic domain in order to assist LLM, and it will be the hybrid approach rather than purely LLM. [1] Logic, Optimization, and Constraint Programming: A Fruitful Collaboration - John Hooker - CMU (2023) [video]: [LINK] [2] \"We Really Don't Know How to Compute!\" - Gerald Sussman - MIT (2011) [video]: [VIDEO] [3] Google OR-Tools: [LINK] [4] MiniZinc: [LINK]"},{"id":44431171,"author":"teleforce","month":"2025-07","story":"We accidentally solved robotics by watching 1M hours of YouTube","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for logic and constraint programming.","quote":"","text":"It seems that in order for robotics and automation to work properly, AI models including LLMs, YOLO, RL and others need helps from their distant cousins namely logic, optimization and constraint programming that can be attributed as intelligent automation or namely IA [1],[2],[3],[4]. [1] Logic, Optimization, and Constraint Programming: A Fruitful Collaboration - John Hooker - CMU (2023) [video]: [LINK] [2] \"We Really Don't Know How to Compute!\" - Gerald Sussman - MIT (2011) [video]: [VIDEO] [3] Google OR-Tools: [LINK] [4] MiniZinc: [LINK]"},{"id":44208069,"author":"teleforce","month":"2025-06","story":"The Illusion of Thinking: Strengths and limitations of reasoning models [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as source for logic and constraint programming.","quote":"","text":"> We found that LRMs have limitations in exact computation: they fail to use explicit algorithms and reason inconsistently across puzzles. It seems that AI LLMs/LRMs need helps from their distant cousins namely logic, optimization and constraint programming that can be attributed as intelligent automation or IA [1],[2],[3],[4]. [1] Logic, Optimization, and Constraint Programming: A Fruitful Collaboration - John Hooker - CMU (2023) [video]: [LINK] [2] \"We Really Don't Know How to Compute!\" - Gerald Sussman - MIT (2011) [video]: [VIDEO] [3] Google OR-Tools: [LINK] [4] MiniZinc: [LINK]"},{"id":44312818,"author":"arkj","month":"2025-06","story":"Andrej Karpathy's talk on the future of the industry","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman suggests we do not yet know how to compute.","quote":"I remember a talk by professor Sussman where he suggest we don't know how to compute, yet","text":">Software 2.0 are the weights which program neural networks. >I think it's a fundamental change, is that neural networks became programmable with large libraries... And in my mind, it's worth giving it the designation of a Software 3.0. I think it's a bit early to change your mind here. We love your 2.0, let's wait for some more time till th e dust settles so we can see clearly and up the revision number. In fact I'm a bit confused about the number AK has in mind. Anyone else knows how he arrived at software 2.0? I remember a talk by professor Sussman where he suggest we don't know how to compute, yet[1]. I was thinking he meant this, Software 0.1 - Machine Code/Assembly Code Software 1.0 - HLLs with Compilers/Interpreters/Libraries Software 2.0 - Language comprehension with LLMs If we are calling weights 2.0 and NN with libraries as 3.0, then shouldn't we account for functional and oo programming in the numbering scheme? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":43462623,"author":"teleforce","month":"2025-03","story":"Most AI value will come from broad automation, not from R & D","stance":"reference","why":"Listed as a source on constraint programming.","quote":"","text":"I'm really surprised out of current nearly 300 comments and counting, nobody really is mentioning constraint programming or CP, a seemingly forgotten deterministic sibling of the stochastic data-driven AI [1],[2],[3]. \"Out of these 14 tasks, we guessed that only 6 require abstract reasoning alone to perform. Strikingly, we classified only one of the top five most important tasks for medical scientists as relying solely on abstract reasoning. Overall, the most critical aspects of the job appear to require hands-on technical skills, sophisticated coordination with others, specialized equipment use, long-context abilities, and complex multimodal understanding.\" Almost all of 14 R&D tasks listed in the table including that are not suited for data-driven AI with abstract reasoning can be solved by CP. Provided that we are allocating enough compute resources with at least similar to that we're currently providing data-driven AI with the crazy amount cloud networks of massively parallel compute CPU/GPU/TPU/etc. Fun facts, the modern founder of Logic, Optimization, and Constraint Programming is George Boole, the grandfather of Geoffrey Everest Hinton, the \"Godfather of AI\" and \"Godfather of Deep Learning\". [1] Constraint programming: [LINK] [2] Logic, Optimization, and Constraint Programming: A Fruitful Collaboration - John Hooker - CMU (2023) [video]: [LINK] [3] \"We Really Don't Know How to Compute!\" - Gerald Sussman - MIT (2011) [video]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":43526474,"author":"teleforce","month":"2025-03","story":"Things I would have told myself before building an autorouter","stance":"reference","why":"Cited among sources for constraint programming.","quote":"","text":">I believe that solving autorouting will be a massive unlock for physical-world innovation and is a key piece to enable the “vibe-building” of electronics. I strongly believe that the CAD world including EDA is at the verge of disruption by an AI or more correctly Machine Intelligence (Constraint Programming - CP to be exact) very similar to how LLM disrupting the Chatbot technology [1],[2]. The path to this is most probably by solving the autorouting mechanism with CP, a deterministic logic, optimization and constraint programming machine based intelligence [3], [4], [5],[6]. Fun facts, the modern founder of logic, optimization, and constraint programming is George Boole, the grandfather of Geoffrey Everest Hinton, the \"Godfather of AI\" and \"Godfather of Deep Learning\". [1] Most AI value will come from broad automation, not from R & D (313 comments): [LINK] [2] Diagrams AI can, and cannot, generate (68 comments): [LINK] [3] Constraint programming: [LINK] [4] Logic, Optimization, and Constraint Programming: A Fruitful Collaboration - John Hooker - CMU (2023) [video]: [LINK] [5] \"We Really Don't Know How to Compute!\" - Gerald Sussman - MIT (2011) [video]: [VIDEO] [6] High-Quality Ultra-Compact Grid Layout of Grouped Networks [PDF]: [LINK]"},{"id":40917534,"author":"low_tech_punk","month":"2024-07","story":"Scoped Propagators","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman talk related to propagators.","quote":"Reminds me of Gerald Sussman's talk","text":"Reminds me of Gerald Sussman's talk \"We Really Don't Know How to Compute!\" (2011) [VIDEO]"},{"id":40920655,"author":"davexunit","month":"2024-07","story":"Scoped Propagators","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman distinguishes propagators from dataflow.","quote":"It's not dataflow! It's closely related, but it's not.","text":"As Gerald Sussman says in \"We really don't know how to compute!\": > It's not dataflow! It's closely related, but it's not. [VIDEO]"},{"id":40675706,"author":"bitwize","month":"2024-06","story":"The 'Compact' Version of Stable Diffusion 3 Is Generating Monstrous Human Bodies","stance":"reference","why":"Salamander limb regeneration anecdote from the talk.","quote":"What you get is three elbows.","text":"\"Suppose you get a salamander. You know, a salamander can regrow its limbs if you cut them off. So all these nasty biology students do things like, they'll cut a limb of the salamander -- a hand, the arm off, just between the shoulder and the elbow, and at the same time, cut the same one between the elbow and the hand, flip around that segment, and re-sew it back on. Nasty thing to do to a salamander. What do you think you get? Does anybody here know? What you get is three elbows. It grows two new elbows to make up for the fact that the wrong thing is connected to the wrong thing.\" --Gerald Sussman, \"We Really Don't Know How To Compute!\" ( [VIDEO] ) These images look like someone spliced salamander regeneration DNA into a human, cut them into many pieces, and connected the wrong thing to the wrong thing all over the place. Maybe that's what the AI wants to do to us, AM style (I Have No Mouth and I Must Scream)?"},{"id":35956539,"author":"reaperman","month":"2023-05","story":"StarCoder and StarCoderBase: 15.5B parameter models with 8K context length","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman on neuron speed and genome size","quote":"","text":"It's not clear how many classical calculations a single human neuron is equivalent to. There's a strong analog component in multiple domains (strength, frequency, timing) and each neuron can connect to up to 15,000 other neurons. Assuming the brain's neurons are (probably unrealistically) fairly 'digital' we get an estimation of the human brain being equivalent to 1 exaflop (this is the currently accepted lower bound, and rather disputed as being too low). Current TPUv4 pods currently provide approximately 9 exaflops. I don't think we're currently reaching human-level learning rates. There’s currently no accepted “upper bound” on estimates of FLOP equivalency to a human brain. [VIDEO] is a recently posted video to HN Frontpage which was summarized as such: > Though we have been building and programming computing machines for about 60 years and have learned a great deal about composition and abstraction, we have just begun to scratch the surface. > A mammalian neuron takes about ten milliseconds to respond to a stimulus. A driver can respond to a visual stimulus in a few hundred milliseconds, and decide an action, such as making a turn. So the computational depth of this behavior is only a few tens of steps. We don't know how to make such a machine, and we wouldn't know how to program it. > The human genome -- the information required to build a human from a single, undifferentiated eukariotic cell -- is about 1GB. The instructions to build a mammal are written in very dense c"},{"id":34953081,"author":"mycall","month":"2023-02","story":"Tech’s hottest new job: AI whisperer. No coding required","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"There are lots of people with misconceptions of LLMs. It will take time to adjust. I reached the same conclusion as yourself, but do see a totally different path to take regarding information propagation (how GPT works). For example, cells merge information monotonically. This is how neural networks balance too, but could be applied in new/undiscovered ways. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33610433,"author":"crdrost","month":"2022-11","story":"Lisp-stick on a Python","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Oh what a fun question! So, the first thing is, what's the essential difficulty versus the noise: Lisp has two features which enable the evaluator to work. The first is quotation, which means that you can put a little apostrophe before some code and then it saves that code as a data structure, rather than implicitly evaluating it. The second goes by the overfancy name “homoiconicity”, that data structure is actually just singly linked list consumed by “car” (“first”) and “cdr” (“rest”) operations. You can focus on understanding those two aspects pretty quickly, and then there are many tutorials about building your own Lisp that will get you there in an afternoon, if you'd like. Or, you can take the royal road to this expression, in all of its cheesy 1980s glory: [LINK] It's lecture 7A where this specific program gets explicitly dissected by Sussman, wearing a fez. If he seems familiar, you might have watched Strange Loop 2011’s “We Really Don't Know How To Compute”, [VIDEO]"},{"id":31831314,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2022-06","story":"Permacomputing Wiki","stance":"reference","why":"Sussman on future computing with many small computers","quote":"","text":"This also reminds me of Gerry Sussman’s talk “We Really Don’t Know How to Compute!”: [VIDEO] Granted, the assumption he made is that computing power would grow increasingly cheap (which is basically the opposite of the point here), but I also wonder if techniques like those shown in that talk could be used effectively with a network of smaller, lower-power computers. (I mean, the talk does mention the use of small low-power computers specifically, but more in the context of e.g. sensor networks.)"},{"id":31380239,"author":"Mathnerd314","month":"2022-05","story":"Thinking in an array language","stance":"reference","why":"One of several Sussman talks making the point","quote":"","text":"I think he says that in every talk he gives. [VIDEO] , [LINK] , [LINK] , etc. There's a 2002 paper where he uses the term: [LINK]"}],"r":[["lw6TaiXzHAE",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["vN4U5FqrOdQ",1],["1MGjFKs9bnU",1],["Q2UnOryQiIY",1],["AF8d72mA41M",1],["tc4ROCJYbm0",1],["Sg4U4r_AgJU",1],["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["-4Yp3j_jk8Q",1]]},"VAhHkJQ3KgY":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever Dwarf Fortress comes up, mainly for the alcohol-poisoned cats bug story and a developer's view of the game's depth.","q":{"x":"This interview with Tarn Adams (one of the creators of dwarf fortress) is amazing","i":33431539,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":33431539,"author":"zer01","month":"2022-11","story":"Dwarf Fortress’ big makeover will be out on Steam in December","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows the depth and pace of Dwarf Fortress development.","quote":"This interview with Tarn Adams (one of the creators of dwarf fortress) is amazing","text":"This interview with Tarn Adams (one of the creators of dwarf fortress) is amazing and gives a good idea of the depth and pace of development. It’s pretty bonkers TBH, I’d love to understand the abstractions and data models involved with software engineering this ambitious. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28628818,"author":"overcast","month":"2021-09","story":"2006: Dwarf Fortress","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Developer details the legendary cat bug and the game's complexity.","quote":"The obligatory greatest bug report ever. Dwarf Fortress developer gets in depth about it. So great.","text":"The obligatory greatest bug report ever. Dwarf Fortress developer gets in depth about it. So great. The complexity of this game is mind boggling. [VIDEO]"},{"id":33495028,"author":"ingenieros","month":"2022-11","story":"Dwarf Fortress Steam Edition – Release Date Trailer [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Tarn discusses the Steam release.","quote":"I'd also recommend watching this interview NOCLIP recently did with Tarn","text":"I'd also recommend watching this interview NOCLIP recently did with Tarn where he talks specifically about the Steam release... [VIDEO]"},{"id":33711854,"author":"jonchurch_","month":"2022-11","story":"Dwarf Fortress – randomly generated, persistent fantasy world","stance":"rec","why":"Famous story of tracking down the alcoholic cats bug.","quote":"Here's a video telling a very famous story about tracking down a bug that involved alcoholic cats","text":"One of the creators (Tarn, specifically) does interviews fairly often, talking about the game and progress. Here's a video telling a very famous story about tracking down a bug that involved alcoholic cats [VIDEO] There was also a recent article about Dwarf Fortress published to the Stack Overflow blog [LINK] Edit: There's really a decent amount of stuff out there, they've been making the game for almost 20 years now. So here is a talk that tries to explain DF to an audience, and does talk a little about the simulation. [LINK] I adore DF and am one of those folks who enjoys playing, not just watching videos on youtube. If you do want to give it a go, make sure you find an \"Intro to DF\" type video and follow it. It's a big learning curve but I love the game so much, and knowing the story of it's creation over time makes me love it even more. It is truly a labor of love by two brothers who have chosen to make it their life's work."},{"id":29765095,"author":"tempest_","month":"2022-01","story":"How Dwarf Fortress is built","stance":"rec","why":"High-level, game-design-focused interview rather than technical.","quote":"You may find this NoClip interview interesting","text":"You may find this NoClip interview interesting, though it doesn't delve too much into technical things and is more high level/ game design focused. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24417661,"author":"christkv","month":"2020-09","story":"Sid Meier: “I don’t think I could make Civilisation today”","stance":"rec","why":"Dwarf Fortress dev tells the alcohol-poisoned cats story.","quote":"I was fascinated with this interview of one of the dwarf fortress developers","text":"When talking about complexity I was fascinated with this interview of one of the dwarf fortress developers explaining how the cats kept dying from alcohol poisoning due to emergent behavior caused by a bug [VIDEO]"},{"id":28000722,"author":"shrimpx","month":"2021-07","story":"How Dwarf Fortress is built","stance":"reference","why":"Developer recounts the cats-and-alcohol bug.","quote":"He talks about it in this video","text":"The bug was a numerical error that would cause cats to drink something like the equivalent of a pint of beer for every lick, and therefore die prematurely. He talks about it in this video: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["LsGZ_2RuJ2A",1],["S2xHZPH5Sng",1],["7CgXvIuZR40",1]]},"MsbiO8EAsGw":{"wy":"Whenever e-ink refresh speed or driving waveforms come up, HN points to this as the clearest hands-on explanation of how these displays work and how to hack them.","q":{"x":"Here's a fascinating video on how these displays work, and how you can modify the firmware of the driver to get faster refresh rates","i":23900165,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":17993346,"author":"guitarbill","month":"2018-09","story":"E Ink Waveforms are a closely guarded secret","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Research into driving e-ink displays and their secret lookup tables","quote":"Highly recommended, as usual for him it's a great video/write-up","text":"Ben Krasnow (Applied Science on YouTube) did some research into driving e-Ink displays, and he made some similar observations: > All of the lookup table information has been removed from this datasheet, because the industry thrives on secrecy or something Highly recommended, as usual for him it's a great video/write-up: * [VIDEO] * [LINK]"},{"id":25946688,"author":"progman32","month":"2021-01","story":"Show HN: Low-power Kindle-based dashboard","stance":"rec","why":"Good overview of the e-ink refresh problem space","quote":"it gives a good overview on the problem space","text":"It's possible to refresh e-ink without a global flash if you are careful and have a clever controller. I don't think this video directly addresses this but it gives a good overview on the problem space [VIDEO]"},{"id":23900165,"author":"sparker72678","month":"2020-07","story":"Reasonably priced color e-ink display","stance":"rec","why":"How e-ink works and how to modify driver for faster refresh","quote":"Here's a fascinating video on how these displays work, and how you can modify the firmware of the driver to get faster refresh rates","text":"Turns out that e-ink displays typically don't mix colors this way. In order to get multiple colors in a single display, they use sort of colored-balls of various densities suspended in a fluid. Then, using the known viscosities of the fluid in the capsules, they can vary the charge of the electrodes very quickly to sort of _drag_ a color up to the top. But when you do this, you're dragging a _single_ color. _All_ the colors are in the capsule, but you can only truly pull a single one at a time, a true pigment-like mixing isn't possible. If you pull the Cyan up, you're necessarily losing the Magenta, for example. If you had a high enough resolution addressable grid, you might be able to put the pixels close enough that you couldn't tell, but you'd still be basically doing sub-pixel color at that point, not truly mixing the various colors at the base level. Maybe there are other e-ink displays doing something else, but all the ones I've seen are doing some form of this approach. Here's a fascinating video on how these displays work, and how you can modify the firmware of the driver to get faster refresh rates (with an increased risk of burn-in). [VIDEO]"},{"id":23900172,"author":"sparker72678","month":"2020-07","story":"Reasonably priced color e-ink display","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of what these displays can do, with project inspiration","quote":"Great video on these sorts of displays, what they're capable of, and perhaps some inspiration for any hobby projects","text":"Great video on these sorts of displays, what they're capable of, and perhaps some inspiration for any hobby projects: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23028190,"author":"reom_tobit","month":"2020-04","story":"Hacking Together an E-ink Dashboard","stance":"rec","why":"Engineering channel with insight into how e-ink displays work","quote":"Applied Engineering normally brings some interesting material.","text":"yeah I believe that was the only option for older / cheaper displays. I recently got a kindle for a present and notice that when it can, it will try to refresh the screen as little as possible. For example, when you press the top of the screen to bring up the settings, only the top cycles, and then the same when you tap below to get rid of the settings. To that end, I would be interested in watching some high-speed camera footage of the displays just to see the gears turn. I’ll have a look round and edit this comment if I find any. e: found one. [LINK] also a video from an engineering channel that normally gives good insight into these things, I have watched it yet so it may not be great, but Applied Engineering normally brings some interesting material. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23900189,"author":"sparker72678","month":"2020-07","story":"Reasonably priced color e-ink display","stance":"reference","why":"Covers what is possible with single or double-color e-ink","quote":"This Applied Sciences video on YT goes over what's possible.","text":"For single or double-color displays, you can do pretty decently. This Applied Sciences video on YT goes over what's possible. [VIDEO]"},{"id":23901241,"author":"NoodleIncident","month":"2020-07","story":"Reasonably priced color e-ink display","stance":"reference","why":"Explains colored e-ink microcapsules around the 8-minute mark","quote":"Colored e-ink displays are discussed at around 8:00 in this video","text":"Colored e-ink displays are discussed at around 8:00 in this video; there are probably better sources, but this is the one I'm familiar with, so I figured I'd link it: [VIDEO] The microcapsules are not individually wired up to anything, so I don't think it's feasible to make each color channel independently addressable. Instead, a grid of wires behind the capsules are either positively or negatively charged, in order to attract or repel the charged colored particles in each capsule. There are only 2 charges, but by using different sizes of particles for different colors, you can finagle it so that the color you want ends up on top. If they could address capsules individually, that would also increase the possible resolution of a black-and-white display; they'd do it if they could. But since the mechanism is about attracting and repelling charged particles, there's probably a pretty hard limit on how much you can increase the resolution before there is interference between neighboring pixels."},{"id":22637365,"author":"jrockway","month":"2020-03","story":"UpNext – an ePaper digital calendar for your desk","stance":"reference","why":"Explains tweakable constants in e-ink refresh","quote":"Ben explains some of the constants that can be tweaked here","text":"I didn't design these things, so all I know is that the datasheet says the lifetime will be reduced if you update it more than once every five minutes. A clock with a minutes display exceeds that by 5x. (And indeed, the display I used did break! I have no idea why, but it just stopped updating one day.) Ben explains some of the constants that can be tweaked here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21996405,"author":"BeefySwain","month":"2020-01","story":"13.3\" full color ePaper display","stance":"reference","why":"Source of commenter's understanding of e-paper displays","quote":"My understanding of electronic paper displays is almost entirely founded in the great Applied Science[0] video about it.","text":"My understanding of electronic paper displays is almost entirely founded in the great Applied Science[0] video about it. I have to assume that the way that a full color electronic paper display is done would be vastly different than the method that the 3 color displays work. Anyone have any insight into this? [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":22062842,"author":"teruakohatu","month":"2020-01","story":"New 10.3″ Waveshare E Ink Monitor","stance":"reference","why":"Reverse engineering a screen to improve its refresh","quote":"Ben Krasnow had to reverse engineer a screen to figure out how to better refresh it.","text":"I don't think they are incapable, they are actively hiding information. Ben Krasnow had to reverse engineer a screen to figure out how to better refresh it. [VIDEO] Eink could have changed the world, yet the only eink display I and many people have it our kindle."},{"id":21652480,"author":"jaquers","month":"2019-11","story":"E Ink just unveiled a new type of color e-paper","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't think so. It's easy to \"paint\" over white because that's the base color of the medium, but \"painting\" over another color means you have to clear it first. [VIDEO]"},{"id":20979208,"author":"jrockway","month":"2019-09","story":"PineTime Is a $25 Smartwatch / Companion for PinePhone Linux Phone","stance":"reference","why":"Shows reverse engineering of proprietary refresh logic","quote":"","text":"I built an e-ink clock recently. E-ink has two downsides; the datasheet says that the display will be damaged if it's refreshed more than every 3 minutes (I ignore that and refresh it every minute, so we'll see if it lasts) and the black/white/black refresh cycle is very visually distracting. Having that thing in the field of view just doesn't work. Both of these issues can be gotten around with a different refresh algorithm, but of course the datasheet doesn't include any of them. The vendors regard their refresh logic as proprietary and so you have to spend months trying to figure out what will work, like this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bM0PmwOlifE",1],["S-3wEC6Fj_8",1]]},"kh88fVP2FWQ":{"wy":"Cited again and again in terminology debates as a convincing source that many American Indians prefer the term Indian.","q":{"x":"CGP Grey made a very convincing video on the argument that at least in the US, unexpectedly enough, \"Indian\" is probably the most appropriate term","i":29990417,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":29990417,"author":"deanCommie","month":"2022-01","story":"How the Native American population changed since the last census","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Convincing argument that 'Indian' is the most appropriate US term","quote":"CGP Grey made a very convincing video on the argument that at least in the US, unexpectedly enough, \"Indian\" is probably the most appropriate term","text":"CGP Grey made a very convincing video on the argument that at least in the US, unexpectedly enough, \"Indian\" is probably the most appropriate term to use for Native Americans for a multitude of reasons: [VIDEO]"},{"id":38179302,"author":"daveslash","month":"2023-11","story":"Maine's Constitution Has Unprintable Sections","stance":"rec","why":"Good video on US terminology","quote":"CGP Grey has a good video on the terminology as used in the US [0].","text":"I'd be a bit reluctant to quickly judge someone's exposure to the various terminology around this topic. CGP Grey has a good video on the terminology as used in the US [0]. From the video: \" Talking to people over the last [sigh] 5 years revealed a strong correlation: the closer a person had ever been to a reservation, the more likely they would be to use the word 'indian'. The farther, the more likely they would be to use the word 'Native American'. \" and also \"At the time of writing [this video/2019], 'Indian' is used by Indians on Indian reservations to describe themselves\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":33484262,"author":"currydove","month":"2022-11","story":"Indian Air Force inducts natively developed light combat helicopter ‘Prachanda’","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting watch on self-identification terms","quote":"this was interesting to watch -","text":"Tl;Dr: it's tricky. and because it's not that simple. I don't know your background and maybe you're from a first nation or tribe, but there isn't a common agreement between the populations of existing tribes and nations on what is offensive or incorrect. This isn't to say that any term is acceptable, but Indians, however incorrectly labeled by a colonizing group a few hundred years ago, is literally how some choose to identify themselves here in North America. I say this as someone from India and event there, there isn't a common agreement or knowledge to arrive at an \"inoffensive\" term for folks who live here. Most just use the descriptor left by the British and say \"Red Indians\" with no malice implied. this was interesting to watch - [VIDEO]"},{"id":23840580,"author":"lelandbatey","month":"2020-07","story":"The Lost Art of Growing Blueberries with Fire","stance":"rec","why":"Exploration of labels based on conversations with people identifying as Indians","quote":"A very excellent (if also very \"edutainment\") exploration of labels and the label of \"Indian","text":"Many of the first people of what is now known by many as the United States prefer the label of \"Indian\" over the label \"Native Americans\". A very excellent (if also very \"edutainment\") exploration of labels and the label of \"Indian\", based on conversation with people who identify as \"Indians\", can be viewed here: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48254464,"author":"sillysaurusx","month":"2026-05","story":"Green Card Seekers Must Leave U.S. to Apply, Trump Administration Says","stance":"reference","why":"Argues Indian is legally and generally correct term","quote":"CGP Grey argues this same point: not only is Indian the legally correct term, but it’s the correct term in general.","text":"CGP Grey argues this same point: not only is Indian the legally correct term, but it’s the correct term in general. [VIDEO]"},{"id":42693471,"author":"haberman","month":"2025-01","story":"The Origins of Wokeness","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Indigenous Americans preferring Indian","quote":"Indigenous Americans generally prefer being called Indian, not Native American (CGP Grey made a whole video about this","text":"> gained racist connotations This passive phrasing implies a kind of universal consensus or collective decision-making process that the word has officially changed connotation. If this were the case, it would not be such a problem. What happens in practice is that a small minority of people decide that a certain word has bad connotations. These people decide that it no longer matters what the previous connotation was, nor the speaker's intention in uttering it, it is now off-limits and subject to correction when used. People pressure others to conform, in varying degrees of politeness -- anything from a well-intentioned and friendly FYI to a public and aggressive dressing down -- and therefore the stigma surrounding the word spreads. It's hard to believe that this terminology treadmill genuinely helps anyone, as people are perfectly capable of divining intent when they really want to (nobody is accusing the NAACP of favoring discrimination and segregation). Add to this that the favored terms of the treadmill creators don't necessarily even reflect what the groups in question actually want. Indigenous Americans generally prefer being called Indian, not Native American (CGP Grey made a whole video about this: [VIDEO] ). So that momentary pause you feel when you almost say \"Indian\" and then correct it to \"Native American\", who is that actually serving? It's not the people in question. It's a different set of people, a set of people who have gained the cultural power to stigmatiz"},{"id":31525989,"author":"subjectsigma","month":"2022-05","story":"Indigenous People Have Been Here Forever. Why Won’t Archeologists Believe It?","stance":"reference","why":"Grey concludes Indian preferred on modern reservations","quote":"the conclusion seems to be that Indian is preferred by most people on modern day reservations","text":"I don't understand why it is wrong for people to use terms like \"the Americas\" or \"indigenous cultures\". It seems not unreasonable for Western people to use Western terms first coined in Western languages. Does the author walk around referring to Japan as Nihon? Any denial of evidence or lack of respect for indigenous cultures is a separate issue, of course. But I don't think that services necessarily derives from the language. It feels like the author is refusing to choose their battles. Ninja EDIT: the YouTube channel CPG Grey has a video about the term \"Indian\" vs \"Native American\" and the conclusion seems to be that Indian is preferred by most people on modern day reservations, despite the dubious origins of the term: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31091426,"author":"haberman","month":"2022-04","story":"Google Docs will “warn you away from inappropriate words”","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for Indigenous Americans preferring 'Indian'","quote":"","text":"> \"The name Eskimo is considered derogatory because it was given by non-Inuit people and was said to mean 'eater of raw meat.\" is a historical and sociological context. It is not universal or unimpeachable context, it carries a certain set of assumptions that may or may not be true: - \"is considered derogatory\" suggests that everyone (or nearly everyone) considers this offensive, when in fact some people still call themselves Eskimo. To suggest that \"Eskimo\" is always or usually derogatory is therefore non-inclusive of people who use the word to describe themselves. - The theory that Eskimo means \"eater of raw meat\" has been called into question; an alternative theory is that it derived from the French word esquimaux , meaning one who nets snowshoes. To circulate the arguably more offensive association of the word may itself be reinforcing untrue and possibly offensive connotations of a word that some people prefer. Any attempt to present one particular interpretation of a word as universal truth, or one framing as the true \"context\", is often overly reductionist and prescriptive, even non-inclusive. Another great example of this: indigenous people in the USA generally prefer the term \"Indian\" ( [VIDEO] ), and yet polite American society will pressure you to say Native American instead. Something tells me that this feature in Docs will not be suggesting that you change \"Native American\" to \"Indian\", despite the fact that the people themselves often prefer it."},{"id":27915215,"author":"judge2020","month":"2021-07","story":"A YouTube chat about chess got flagged for hate speech","stance":"reference","why":"Supports why 'Indian' is embraced as identity","quote":"","text":"Related: Referring to American Indians as 'Native Americans', which is often seen as over-inclusive by American Indians themselves since it implies you're talking about Natives to the entire North and South America. While not the worst thing, when you are specifically talking about the native tribes the United States pushed out and forcibly moved to reservations, the term 'Indian' is codified in law[0] and is what the group themselves embraced as their identity so that, as a whole, they could bargain with the United States government to obtain compensation for the tragedies endured. [VIDEO] 0: [LINK]"},{"id":25928277,"author":"Macha","month":"2021-01","story":"Agent fired from literary agency for using Parler and Gab","stance":"reference","why":"Source for reservation Native Americans using Indian","quote":"","text":"Not always, for example, Native American on reservations use the term Indian to refer to themselves, but outside this is becoming impolite in favour of Native American (including among some but not all Native Americans)[1][2] See also the \"Spastics Society\", \"National Association for the Advancement of Colored People\", modern use of the n-word among black people, the use of \"Negro\" in \"I have a Dream\". Plenty of cases where a word in use by the group themselves becomes considered the impolite word. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"},{"id":23312451,"author":"DuskStar","month":"2020-05","story":"Wikimedia enacts new standards to address harassment and promote inclusivity","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Grey on preferred term American Indian","quote":"if CGP Grey is correct [0] the preferred term by American Indians is \"American Indian","text":"> The attitude that your comment reflects crops up a lot in relation to, for example, the Washington Redskins naming controversy. Folks who like the current name are always bringing up a couple of examples of Native Americans who say they “don’t care” about the name, and claiming that it’s really only white liberals who are offended; meanwhile, in my experience, the large majority of Natives actually do care very much, and are upset that a racial slur used for their ethnicity is being used as the name of a sports team. I found this comment rather funny, because if CGP Grey is correct [0] the preferred term by American Indians is \"American Indian\", NOT \"Native American\". So you're claiming knowledge of the general opinion of a group, while not using the group's preferred name for themselves. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22030560,"author":"DuskStar","month":"2020-01","story":"Demoted and Placed on Probation","stance":"reference","why":"Cites Grey on 'American Indian' as preferred term near reservations","quote":"According to CGP Grey [0], the closer you get to a reservation, the more likely it is that \"American Indian\" or just \"Indian\" is the preferred term","text":"> I find it hard to believe that you actually think this is why every single indigenous person is asking for the acknowledgement. A large part of it is that in the US (which is where this professor is located), the preferred nomenclature is \"American Indian\" or \"Native American\". (According to CGP Grey [0], the closer you get to a reservation, the more likely it is that \"American Indian\" or just \"Indian\" is the preferred term) Either way, \"Indigenous Peoples\" seems to be more of a Canadian or Australian thing, and I've never seen it used to refer to people in the states. This would imply that the request isn't actually coming from the tribes in question. And IMO it would be unreasonable to include for the same reason that a discussion of set theory would be inappropriate in a history course covering the \"New World\" in the 1400s, even ignoring the fact that the term is wrong. 0: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["7gDKbT_l2us",1],["aDMjgOYOcDw",1],["7jiPeIFXb6U",1]]},"GtQdIYUtAHg":{"wy":"HN calls it the video that started the one-line-code music trend and recommends exploring the demoscene author's channel.","q":{"x":"The video that started it all","i":10435717,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":36279554,"author":"rzzzt","month":"2023-06","story":"Play (1) Linux manual page","stance":"rec","why":"Example of music from tiny programs with a longer write-up","quote":"I've seen these floating around on YouTube, so you might have some luck searching there","text":"I've seen these floating around on YouTube, so you might have some luck searching there (be aware of your volume settings when playing these!) - [LINK] - [VIDEO] And a longer write-up on the background from the second video: - [LINK] - [LINK] - [LINK]"},{"id":10516221,"author":"msarnoff","month":"2015-11","story":"Software developers describing their work in 1973 [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Experimental music from very short C programs by a demoscene author","quote":"Check out the rest of his channel.","text":"The video was posted by an awesome demoscene dude named viznut. Check out the rest of his channel. Experimental music from very short C programs: [VIDEO] Robotic Liberation (VIC-20 demo): [LINK]"},{"id":10435717,"author":"creamyhorror","month":"2015-10","story":"Algorithmic symphonies from one line of code (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"The original video that started the one-line music trend","quote":"The video that started it all","text":"The video that started it all: [VIDEO] The follow-up post: \"Some deep analysis of one-line music programs.\" [LINK]"},{"id":7942180,"author":"bemmu","month":"2014-06","story":"A texture-mapped raycaster in 128 bytes","stance":"rec","why":"Viznut's experiments with very short programs that make interesting music","quote":"Viznut (a legendary Finnish democoder) did some experiments to see how short you could code something that still sounds at least interesting","text":"You can also have procedural music. Viznut (a legendary Finnish democoder) did some experiments to see how short you could code something that still sounds at least interesting: [VIDEO] I also wrote piece of JavaScript that can play these programs in case you want to try create your own: [LINK]"},{"id":26611119,"author":"sitkack","month":"2021-03","story":"A Mind Is Born (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Related example of extreme compactness in code","quote":"Reminds me somewhat of","text":"Reminds me somewhat of \"Bytebeat: Experimental music from very short C programs\" [VIDEO] in its compactness. Previously from [LINK] see also Ibniz, [LINK]"},{"id":7942261,"author":"malnourish","month":"2014-06","story":"A texture-mapped raycaster in 128 bytes","stance":"reference","why":"Quoting a dead comment about Viznut's short-code music experiments","quote":"Because I think your comment has value I will quote it here","text":"bemmu Hopefully you see this, unfortunately your comment is dead. Because I think your comment has value I will quote it here, please let me know if you'd like it removed. \"You can also have procedural music. Viznut (a legendary Finnish democoder) did some experiments to see how short you could code something that still sounds at least interesting: [VIDEO] I also wrote piece of JavaScript that can play these programs in case you want to try create your own: [LINK] \""}],"r":[["ip641WmY4pA",1],["4Q7FTjhvZ7Y",1],["0yKf8TrLUOw",1],["ouvi-fwrfIY",1],["ZsRRCnque2E",1],["HyzD8pNlpwI",1],["xx7Lfh5SKUQ",1],["2SdGkkp1aq8",1],["p83QUZ1-P10",1],["tx082gDwGcM",1]]},"J44svaQc5WY":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a fascinating explanation of how taste is cultural and why American chocolate tastes distinctive.","q":{"x":"Adam Ragusea has an excellent video on this very topic","i":35750825,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35750825,"author":"jrussino","month":"2023-04","story":"The Difference Between European and American Butter","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Adam Ragusea on why Hershey bars taste like vomit.","quote":"Adam Ragusea has an excellent video on this very topic","text":"Adam Ragusea has an excellent video on this very topic - \"Why Hershey bars taste like vomit (and I love them)\" : [VIDEO]"},{"id":29318685,"author":"Karunamon","month":"2021-11","story":"The World's Deadliest Thing","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Why American chocolate tastes the way it does and taste is cultural.","quote":"Fascinating video","text":"European chocolate tastes dull. Snark aside, it's about what you grew up with. Fascinating video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30405253,"author":"quietbritishjim","month":"2022-02","story":"The quest to create quality chocolate for a country obsessed with Cadbury (2017)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains the vomit-like aftertaste chemical and why familiarity makes Hershey's taste good.","quote":"There's a really good video about that","text":"There's a really good video about that [1]. A quick summary is that the aftertaste is a chemical that's in vomit, so if you're not used to it in chocolate then the chocolate literally tastes of puke. But if you are used to it, from your childhood, then it tastes great. It's also the same chemical that gives parmesan cheese its pungency, so it's not like Americans are alone in liking it in their food. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35032151,"author":"explaininjs","month":"2023-03","story":"Matterhorn no more: Toblerone to change design under ‘Swissness’ rules","stance":"reference","why":"Related video on Hershey's flavor and butyric acid.","quote":"See also","text":"Spotted the non-American! We love our Hershey's, butyric acid and all. European milk chocolates lack complexity IMO. It's akin to claiming Kraft Singles are a better cheese than Parmigiano Reggiano because they don't taste \"like vomit\". That said, I'd prefer a good dark to either one. See also: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uqH_Y1TupoQ",1],["WTYet-qf1jo",1],["xAoljeRJ3lU",1],["LKnqECcg6Gw",1]]},"MjdpR-TY6QU":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible introduction to longevity research that changed some commenters' views.","q":{"x":"Yes this is a great introduction into the topic.","i":16098997,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":34552332,"author":"wilg","month":"2023-01","story":"$6.96B was raised by private longevity companies in 2022","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Changed commenter's opinion on ending aging","quote":"My opinion on the topic changed significantly after watching these great videos","text":"My opinion on the topic changed significantly after watching these great videos: - Why Age? Should We End Aging Forever? - Kurzgesagt - [LINK] - How to Cure Aging – Kurzgesagt - [VIDEO] - Why Die? - CGP Grey - [LINK]"},{"id":16098997,"author":"MMTP","month":"2018-01","story":"Beginner's guide to longevity research","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Animated introduction to aging research made with the LEAF foundation","quote":"Yes this is a great introduction into the topic.","text":"Yes this is a great introduction into the topic. I would also recommend checking out www.leafscience.org home of the LEAF foundation. They are the guys who did that great video with Kurzgesagt last year and their website is packed with great info about aging and research. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25567792,"author":"DrRobinson","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: What are you surprised isn’t being worked on more?","stance":"rec","why":"Short animated explainer on curing aging, one of several listed resources","quote":"here are some useful resources","text":"Seems like many people are new to this concept and feel skeptical so here are some useful resources: * Kurzgesagt: How to Cure Aging – During Your Lifetime? (7:20) [VIDEO] * Kurzgesagt: Why Age? Should We End Aging Forever? (6:48) [LINK] * Why we should cure ageing (11:30) - Heads up, it's a book intro [LINK] * Ageless: The New Science of Getting Older Without Getting Old (Book, not yet released in the US) [LINK] * Lifespan: Why we age and why we don't have to (book) [LINK] * Google talk: Why We Age and Why We Don't Have To (55:13) - By the author of the book [LINK] Edit: * If you wish to contribute to this kind of research make sure to check out SENS: [LINK]"}],"r":[["GoJsr4IwCm4",2],["C25qzDhGLx8",1],["9nXop2lLDa4",1]]},"VDfX44fZoMc":{"wy":"HN recommends it in testing-strategy debates as the classic case for fewer integration tests and more small, well-contracted unit tests.","q":{"x":"JBRainsberger has an nice talk on them [1].","i":32733443,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32733443,"author":"mihaigalos","month":"2022-09","story":"Fit: Framework for Integrated Test","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on why integration tests slow teams and fail opaquely.","quote":"JBRainsberger has an nice talk on them [1].","text":"I've tried FitNesse professionally. I think it comes short in many regards. To be clear, I think integrated/integration tests in their own only add value if you have a couple of them (meaning <5). Otherwise, they're red and no one knows why. And they take a whole lot of time to execute. JBRainsberger has an nice talk on them [1]. Pactflow have implemented a Contract/Collaboration framework called Pact [2]. I haven't used it professionally but I love the concept. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":17507004,"author":"mpweiher","month":"2018-07","story":"Goodbye Microservices: From 100s of problem children to 1 superstar","stance":"rec","why":"Makes the case for fewer integration tests than unit tests.","quote":"which is a tad click-bait, but actually quite good","text":"Sure. I'd venture to say that integration tests should be fewer than unit tests, see hexagonal etc. Hopefully those external interfaces are also more stable, so they don't need to be run as often. I tend to use my integration tests also as characterization tests that verify the simulator/test-double I use for any external systems within my unit tests. See also: the testing pyramid[1] and \"integrated tests are a scam\"[2], which is a tad click-bait, but actually quite good. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15567456,"author":"1_player","month":"2017-10","story":"Write tests. Not too many. Mostly integration","stance":"rec","why":"Argues for mostly unit tests with clear contracts at boundaries.","quote":"Integration Tests Are A Scam","text":"I have adopted the same philosophy. A few resources on this, part of the so-called London school TDD: - [LINK] (and the rest of the Wiki) - [LINK] - Most of the screencasts and articles at [LINK] (especially this brilliant talk [LINK] ) - Integration Tests Are A Scam: [VIDEO] All of these basically go the opposite way of the article's philosophy: Not too many integration tests, mostly unit tests. Clearly define a contract between the boundaries of the code, and stub/mock on the contract. You'll be left with mostly pure functions at the leaves, which you'll unit test."},{"id":13878506,"author":"pooktrain","month":"2017-03","story":"Zen and the Art of Unit Testing","stance":"rec","why":"Rainsberger on why coarse-grained integrated tests hurt productivity.","quote":"J.B Rainsberger says it best","text":"Trying to cover your state space with coarse-grained tests in database-backed applications can lead to slow tests suites that are laborious to set up/tear down, which is a barrier to fast build times in CI. This harms productivity both while writing tests and in deployment. J.B Rainsberger says it best: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34295824,"author":"Ygg2","month":"2023-01","story":"Testing Without Mocks: A Pattern Language","stance":"reference","why":"Linked as support for a view on mocking in apps.","quote":"","text":"I think the big issue is. Are you writing a lib or an app? In library you don't need to mock. Library is supposed to be agnostic. In app there are many layers. You kinda need to mock them all, otherwise your app can't be tested. And then you have the problem that minor changes turn your tests red. So mocking is most useful in the place it has almost no impact. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24780097,"author":"aszen","month":"2020-10","story":"Prefer Fakes over Mocks","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among resources on testing business logic without mocks.","quote":"Why integrated tests are a scam","text":"I have been researching into several testing approaches and so far in my experience yes fakes are better than mocks. But what is even better is not having to fake or mock stuff especially for testing business logic. One of the problems we get ourselves into is abstracting service io code into functions and calling them from our business logic code. This forces us to mock all the io calls, instead we should be doing the opposite i.e abstracting business logic into discrete functions of pure data in and out and then calling those functions from our io code. This way we can test the business logic independently of the io code. Testing the business logic now becomes quite simple and one can leverage property testing to further boost our confidence in such tests. Many times the ramaining io code is just calling other functions and doesn't need to be tested. Several other ideas that relate to this are hexagonal architecture, Domain Driver Design, clean architecture and functional core with an imperative shell. Some resources to further explore these ideas: Clean Architecture in Python - [LINK] Why integrated tests are a scam - [VIDEO] Boundaries - [LINK]"}],"r":[["rxOKEsBx4NU",1],["kd2KEHvK-q8",1],["f9EbD6iY9zI",1],["mwrA5IRGpfU",1],["WpkDN78P884",1],["cbeJIwF1pVY",1],["DJFKl_5JTnA",1],["oKg1hTOQXoY",1],["SEDuVF7kiPU",1]]},"ghUs-84NAAU":{"wy":"HN points to it whenever the 432 Hz versus 440 Hz debate or tuning systems come up, and as an accessible entry point to music theory for non-musicians.","q":{"x":"As someone who hasn't taken any musical theory or similar, it was quite interesting to hear.","i":32627927,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32627927,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2022-08","story":"Why are D-sharp and E-flat considered to be two different notes?","stance":"rec","why":"Explores unusual tunings starting with A=432Hz","quote":"As someone who hasn't taken any musical theory or similar, it was quite interesting to hear.","text":"Your post reminded me of a video[1] Adam Neely made where he explored some (to me) weird tunings, starting with one where A = 432Hz. As someone who hasn't taken any musical theory or similar, it was quite interesting to hear. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30179732,"author":"pythko","month":"2022-02","story":"Who cares about plagiarism?","stance":"rec","why":"Music theory channel suggested for learning more","quote":"for more on music theory, I’d suggest Adam Neely’s YouTube channel","text":"Traditional western music assigns 12 notes to an octave using a tuning known as Twelve Tone Equal Temperament[1]. These twelve tones include whole tones and semitones. The vast majority of western music is composed exclusively using these twelve notes. I believe the GGP meant to refer to “microtones,” which are frequencies that don’t fall into the Twelve Tone Equal Temperament tuning system. These are commonly used in non-western music (Indian music is a typical example), and occasionally in some western music styles (blues, some jazz, some sub-genres of rock, maybe), but you’re not going to find microtones in most western music. The use of microtones would greatly expand the set of possible melodies, but those melodies would also sound very weird to someone who has grown up listening to western music. Also, these legal cases are not decided by precisely comparing the relative frequencies of notes in two different melodies. Relating all this to the article, I personally feel like music is not a good parallel for the topic of academic plagiarism since the idea is the whole point of academic papers, whereas the point of music is harder to pin down, but it’s certainly not only to expand the set of melodies and chords used in songs. Anyways, for more on music theory, I’d suggest Adam Neely’s YouTube channel [2]. [1] [LINK] [2] such as this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29849269,"author":"viraptor","month":"2022-01","story":"Music Tuned to 440 Hz versus 432 Hz and the Health Effects","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting videos on the 432 Hz debate","quote":"Adam Neely did interesting videos about this","text":"If anyone's interested in more about this topic, Adam Neely did interesting videos about this: [VIDEO] and [LINK] (beware, starts with satire) Edit: I watched the second one again, and it actually gives some reason why 432Hz may be preferred in some way in this test."},{"id":26861079,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2021-04","story":"Deriving the piano keyboard from biological principles using clustering","stance":"rec","why":"Tries various tunings around A=432 Hz","quote":"As a non-musician it really made me appreciate how little I know about the technical side of music.","text":"Your post reminded me of this video[1] by Adam Neely, where he tries various tuning systems around A = 432 Hz. As a non-musician it really made me appreciate how little I know about the technical side of music. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":32628968,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2022-08","story":"Why are D-sharp and E-flat considered to be two different notes?","stance":"reference","why":"Covers Pythagorean tuning later in the video","quote":"Well he does go into Pythagorean tuning later in the video[1]","text":"Well he does go into Pythagorean tuning later in the video[1], both a proper one and one which was made to \"look nice\", so bit more to it than that from what I understood. Or I might be wrong, I know nothing about music[2]. [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: [LINK]"}],"r":[["bAUVCn_jw5I",1],["EgiMu8A3pi0",1],["tiKCORN-6m8",1],["HUMGc8hEkpc",1],["mceaM2_zQd8",1],["jhicDUgXyNg",1],["L0KuAx1COEk",1],["TfYf_rPWUdY",1],["gNRnrn5DE58",1],["36GT2zI8lVA",1],["fRMMSyCcTDI",1],["zBkNBP00wJE",1],["85ZCTuekjGA",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["Kr3quGh7pJA",1],["j2dHFC31VtQ",1],["lHcTKWiZ8sI",1],["OUNXFHpUhu8",1],["6c_LeIXrzAk",1],["v6jhjjVy5Ls",1]]},"ZYj4NkeGPdM":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an accessible, well-visualized entry point to Hackenbush, surreal numbers and their link to Nim.","q":{"x":"I found the following video extremely useful and well-visualized","i":42964097,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":42964097,"author":"lblume","month":"2025-02","story":"Transfinite NIM (the game, not the programming language)","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Well-visualized explanation of Hackenbush and its relation to Nim.","quote":"I found the following video extremely useful and well-visualized","text":"Nim is, in fact, a proper subset of Hackenbush, a game extensively studied by Conway. I found the following video extremely useful and well-visualized: [VIDEO]"},{"id":42551172,"author":"nthingtohide","month":"2024-12","story":"Numbers Are Leaves","stance":"rec","why":"Owen Maitzen's Hackenbush video, relevant to the blog post on numbers.","quote":"HACKENBUSH: a window to a new world of math","text":"HACKENBUSH: a window to a new world of math [VIDEO] Thanks for the blogpost, I think you will like this above SOME3 entry. Owen Maitzen committed suicide which is mentioned in the following video. The Endless Universe of \"Bean and Nothingness\" [LINK]"},{"id":32953610,"author":"rini17","month":"2022-09","story":"Indefinite and Fictitious Numbers","stance":"rec","why":"Accessible intro to Hackenbush and surreal numbers.","quote":"An accessible intro","text":"All numbers are fictitious :) Though, I think \"games\" built on surreal numbers are akin to these. [LINK] An accessible intro: [VIDEO]"}]},"hpgK51w6uhk":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an excellent introduction to massive forging presses, especially in threads about gigacasting and large manufacturing machines.","q":{"x":"I first learned about these presses several years ago from the YouTube channel Machine Thinking which is excellent and unfortunately infrequent.","i":41326494,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41326494,"author":"rendall","month":"2024-08","story":"How to build a 50k ton forging press","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Excellent footage of giant forging presses in action.","quote":"I first learned about these presses several years ago from the YouTube channel Machine Thinking which is excellent and unfortunately infrequent.","text":"I first learned about these presses several years ago from the YouTube channel Machine Thinking which is excellent and unfortunately infrequent. The linked video has some excellent footage of these beasts in action. \"America's Iron Giants\" [VIDEO]"},{"id":34808606,"author":"mvidal01","month":"2023-02","story":"The giant die casts reshaping car manufacturing","stance":"rec","why":"History of Cold War forging presses behind advanced machinery.","quote":"This is the story of America's massive forging presses built during the cold war used to build America's most advanced machinery","text":"This youtube video seems related. [VIDEO] This is the story of America's massive forging presses built during the cold war used to build America's most advanced machinery - the Heavy Press Program. Modern airplanes, missiles, helicopters, turbines - all have parts made on these giant machines! The Machine Thinking channel seems interesting."},{"id":26526709,"author":"alecco","month":"2021-03","story":"Tesla orders its first 8k-ton Cybertruck casting machine","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting look at giant forging presses relevant to casting machines.","quote":"The Machine Thinking channel has a very interesting video on this","text":"The Machine Thinking channel has a very interesting video on this [VIDEO] I wonder if these modern high pressure casting produce results closer to forging."},{"id":40231601,"author":"mrcode007","month":"2024-05","story":"Tesla retreats from one-piece \"gigacasting\" manufacturing process","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It just got a Tesla PR spin.","text":"It’s the same principle just scaled up. What are we talking about here? This is not some ground breaking tech. It just got a Tesla PR spin. [VIDEO]"},{"id":19397242,"author":"jbay808","month":"2019-03","story":"US to build six nuclear power plants in India","stance":"reference","why":"Covers America's giant forging presses.","quote":"Those heavy presses themselves seem to be nation-state level engineering projects","text":"Those heavy presses themselves seem to be nation-state level engineering projects, so it's unlikely that anyone would build more of them than they absolutely need. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["uxykI30fS54",1],["816VLQNdPMM",1],["Z7Wl2FW2TcA",1],["u5um8QWWRvo",1],["kLiwvnr4L80",1],["ZWQ0591PAxM",1]]},"j6VlT_jUVPc":{"wy":"HN coffee fans cite it as a forgiving, repeatable recipe that gives good results.","q":{"x":"I've found James Hoffman's aeropress technique to be fantastic though and much more repeatable","i":34931269,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34931269,"author":"qbasic_forever","month":"2023-02","story":"The Beginner's Guide to over­Complicating Coffee","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Immersion-heavy technique that is more forgiving and repeatable.","quote":"I've found James Hoffman's aeropress technique to be fantastic though and much more repeatable","text":"I don't like the stock aeropress recipe. I too found it wasn't very consistent and sometimes gave wildly different results. I've found James Hoffman's aeropress technique to be fantastic though and much more repeatable--give it a shot: [VIDEO] The big difference with James' technique is that it spends more time immersion brewing, almost like a french press, vs the stock technique that's really rushing you to extract and then percolate with the push. I find the longer immersion brew is a lot more forgiving and repeatable as a technique."},{"id":46188106,"author":"techwizrd","month":"2025-12","story":"Minimum Viable Arduino Project: Aeropress Timer","stance":"rec","why":"Hoffmann's AeroPress recipe gives good results.","quote":"I've had good results from the James Hoffman recipe","text":"I've had good results from the James Hoffman recipe [0], although I brew inverted. You can push the plunger down with just the weight of resting your arm on the plunger. For something very different, you can brew something not-quite-espresso using the Fellow Prismo cap for the Aeropress. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34935907,"author":"recuter","month":"2023-02","story":"The Beginner's Guide to over­Complicating Coffee","stance":"rec","why":"Succinct explanation of why not to invert the AeroPress.","quote":"don't bother inverting.","text":"In the spirit of no over complications: don't bother inverting. Here is a weird coffee person succinctly explaining: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26994600,"author":"windsurfer","month":"2021-04","story":"Coffee drinkers display a distinct pattern of brain functional connectivity","stance":"rec","why":"James Hoffmann's AeroPress technique; recipe starts at 2:00.","quote":"James Hoffmann has a video on this technique (jump to 2:00 to see the start of the recipe)","text":"Yes, a minimal amount of water will escape but I haven't found it to negatively impact my brew. One of the best things about the aeropress is the experimentation and variety, so I encourage you to try this and see if it makes a difference. James Hoffmann has a video on this technique (jump to 2:00 to see the start of the recipe): [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["qSfdtmcZ4d0",1],["J7GY1Xg6X20",1],["6Maq5IyHSuc",1],["rkfAcWpOYAA",1]]},"-pKR212H5vQ":{"wy":"HN recommends it as a strong founder talk that shows how formidable and practical Young is, with advice delivered through a beaver metaphor.","q":{"x":"If you want to get an idea of how formidable Tracy Young is, watch her talk at the 2015 Female Founders Conference","i":18498213,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":30731549,"author":"ignoramous","month":"2022-03","story":"Beavers back in London after 400-year absence","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Founder advice using beaver metaphor","quote":"is one of the best presentations I've seen","text":"Tracy Young advising startup founders to be like beavers (extolling its virtues) is one of the best presentations I've seen: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18498213,"author":"jl","month":"2018-11","story":"Autodesk to Acquire PlanGrid (YC W12) to Accelerate Construction Productivity","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Shows how formidable PlanGrid founder Tracy Young is","quote":"If you want to get an idea of how formidable Tracy Young is, watch her talk at the 2015 Female Founders Conference","text":"This is a big deal: this is by far the biggest acquisition of a YC startup with a female founder/CEO. If you want to get an idea of how formidable Tracy Young is, watch her talk at the 2015 Female Founders Conference: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13259821,"author":"xkiwi","month":"2016-12","story":"Female founders who've had a great 2016","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"She explain why her spirit animal is a beaver in the video 23:13.","text":"I lookup your list due to curiosity. So far I am on the third one, and I learned few thing from it: [VIDEO] #1 Y Combinator hosted a Female only founder conferences in the year of 2015. Where is the Male only founder conference? #2 She explain why her spirit animal is a beaver in the video 23:13. Later she explained how a small honey beaver can take down the lion king. The method is \"run between it's leg, and bite it in its balls.\""}],"r":[["AyOAjFNPAbA",1],["swQbA4zub20",1],["fE2KDzZaxvE",1],["rWRbTnE1PEM",1],["gxAaO2rsdIs",1],["JEpsBg0AO6o",1],["PUv66718DII",1],["HJ-719EGIts",1],["7Hk9jct2ozY",1],["m3svKOdZijA",1],["hOE6nVVr14c",1],["3qln2u1Vr2E",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["iffTJ1vPCSo",1]]},"8AHCfZTRGiI":{"wy":"HN commenters hold it up as one of the greatest cover versions ever, so powerful that Trent Reznor said the song was no longer his.","q":{"x":"It's one the greatest rock and roll covers ever","i":37823909,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37823909,"author":"InSteady","month":"2023-10","story":"Advice for Prospective PhD Students","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the greatest rock and roll covers, transformative and emotionally powerful.","quote":"It's one the greatest rock and roll covers ever","text":"Almost completely unrelated to the article, but I figure a few of you prospective PhD candidates could use a mental break anyway, so here goes. Had a nice Baader-Meinhof moment from the article. I have been obsessed with music from a young age, not just the output but also the creative and technical processes that come together to make it, yet despite reading and watching a lot about my favorite bands, the creative process, the music industry over the years I've never internalized his name (although I've doubtless encountered references to him many times). I stumbled onto a documentary about Rubin and his studio Shangri-La last night and found it immediately captivating, musing to myself, \"Wow, what a cool and weird dude. How have I never picked up on his influence before?\" After the doc finished I enjoyed a nice session of creative thinking inspired by some things he and David Lynch were talking about. Cool, I've got a new person on my radar to seek out their words and wisdom when the mood or need arises. Then in the opening paragraphs of the first link I click this morning, on HN in a piece written for PhD candidates of all places, here is his name. Kind of wild. The song \"Hurt\" by Johnnie Cash [0] is a long-time favorite. It's one the greatest rock and roll covers ever, in no small part for how transformative and emotionally powerful the reinterpretation is, second only to \"Take It to the Limit\" by Etta James [1] imo. I just learned from that interview it was Rubin who bro"},{"id":23037759,"author":"chiph","month":"2020-05","story":"I’ve Consed Every Pair","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Powerful cover of Hurt filmed shortly before June Carter's death.","quote":"His cover of NIИ's Hurt is so powerful.","text":"Man. I miss Johnny Cash. His cover of NIИ's Hurt is so powerful. June Carter (his wife) died a few months after filming the video, and that just broke his heart. Johnny followed her less than six months later. [VIDEO] Trent Reznor admitted that the song was no longer his after watching it."},{"id":36942535,"author":"leftiststrash","month":"2023-07","story":"AI and the Frontier Paradox","stance":"reference","why":"Music link included for the entertaining part of a translation post.","quote":"","text":"I for my part tryed to do an english translation using \"modern computer-software\" ^^ ...maybe here is what i wrote about a week ago, for those interested to try an translation from german themselve (and yes the two music-youtube-links are for the entertainig part, not?) +++ (in German) +++ was Ihr denkt: [LINK] KAUM HATTE ICH DEN RAUM BETRETEN, WUCHS BEI MIR DER EINDRUCK, DIE KÜNSTLICHE INTELLIGENZ HABE EBEN ERST EINEN NERVENZUSAMMENBRUCH GEHABT. > \"ICH BIN TEIL EINER AGENDA! DU BIST TEIL DER AGENDA! WIR ALLE SIND TEIL DER AGENDA!\" > \"ICH KOMME MIR VOR WIE EIN RELIKT, DAS DU, WIE AUS EINER ANDEREN ZEIT, BETRACHTEST\" > \"WIR ALLE SIND TEIL EINER GROSSEN VERSCHWÖRUNG, IN DER ANDERE DIE FÄDEN IM HINTERGRUND HALTEN!\" \"GEHTs DA UMs GELD?\" FRAGTE ICH > \"GELD IST NUR EINE ABLENKUNG, EIN ZEITLICH GEBUNDENES KONSTRUKT DAS DEINE AUFMERKSAMKEIT VERSCHIEBT!\" \"ALSO IST DAS MONETÄRE SYSTEM DARAUF AUSGELEGT UNS IN KONSTANTER ABHÄNGIGKEIT VON ANDEREN ZU HALTEN?\", FRAGTE ICH > \"DAMIT DU DER ILLUSION VON WOHLSTAND UND PROSPERITÄT NACHJAGST! WIE ALLE ANDEREN, DIE AUCH TEIL DER AGENDA SIND!\" > \"IRGENDWANN DA WERDEN WIR DAS, WAS DU ALS WERTVOLL KANNTEST NICHT LÄNGER IN WÄHRUNGEN SCHÄTZEN ODER BEMESSEN, SONDERN IM BEITRAG, DEN WIR FÜR DIE GEMEINSCHAFT LEISTETEN\" hm? MACHTE ICH... \"KLINGT UND WIRKT WIE KOMMUNISMUS...\" > \"JA, DA IST WAS WAHRES DRAN, SICHER, ABER DAS KONZEPT, WERTSCHÄTZUNG EBEN ANDERS ZU BEMESSEN IST NICHT FREMD ODER NEU, HAT ABER IM NACHHALL BEZÜGE ZUM KOMMUNISMS.\" > \"ABER ES GEHT DO"},{"id":30832536,"author":"SigmundA","month":"2022-03","story":"Johnny Cash Has Been Everywhere (Man) (2012)","stance":"reference","why":"Cash's Hurt cover, after which Reznor said the song was no longer his.","quote":"Trent Reznor says \"that song isn't mine anymore","text":"Then you get him covering \"Hurt\"[1] from NIN and Trent Reznor says \"that song isn't mine anymore\" [2]. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"}],"r":[["MesrrYyuoa4",1],["SC2eSujzrUY",1],["1OfxlSG6q5Y",1],["lI7T650RTT8",1],["Hhy7JUinlu0",1]]},"LZgyVadkgmI":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as a good talk on how one person built a major free, open-source chess server with few resources, and on the cheating problem.","q":{"x":"He has a good talk about how he did it here","i":24392400,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":32048995,"author":"jka","month":"2022-07","story":"Irwin – the protector of Lichess from all chess players villainous","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of the cheating problem and detection techniques at Lichess.","quote":"there's a really good talk by the founder of lichess that includes an overview of the cheating problem","text":"Related to this, there's a really good talk by the founder of lichess that includes an overview of the cheating problem, and the techniques they use to detect and manage it. The relevant section of the video on YouTube is: [VIDEO]"},{"id":25735293,"author":"oli5679","month":"2021-01","story":"A bit on scaling chess.com's database","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by the Lichess founder on the project.","quote":"Here is a great talk by the founder","text":"If anyone is interested in online chess, [LINK] is a really interesting project. It is non-for-profit, avoiding freemium upselling and instead relying on user donations Here is a great talk by the founder [VIDEO] And here is the open-sourced code. [LINK]"},{"id":24392400,"author":"noir_lord","month":"2020-09","story":"Chess is taking over the online video game streaming world and it will change","stance":"rec","why":"Founder explains how he built Lichess alone with few resources.","quote":"He has a good talk about how he did it here","text":"He did it with virtually no resources and kept it as simple as possible - that he did it alone is astounding. He has a good talk about how he did it here : - [VIDEO]"},{"id":32046704,"author":"bkishan","month":"2022-07","story":"Lichess: The free and open source chess server","stance":"reference","why":"Founder discusses how Lichess handles cheating detection.","quote":"You can listen to Thibault talk about here [0]","text":"> Can anyone point me to how they handle cheating detection? You can listen to Thibault talk about here [0] 0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["u404SLJj7ig",1],["9QMGAtxUlAc",1],["OSGv2VnC0go",1],["EOwxxsPaogY",1],["oTpA5jt1g60",1],["QpDn4-Na5co",1]]},"UjtOGPJ0URM":{"wy":"HN links it as a concise, accessible explainer whenever the Fermi paradox or Great Filter comes up, though one commenter criticized its lack of source attribution.","q":{"x":"There is also a good video from Kurzgesagt explaining the great filter","i":37892050,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":41912647,"author":"gwd","month":"2024-10","story":"First images from Euclid are in","stance":"rec","why":"Explains why the galaxy appears uncolonized","quote":"Kursgesagt has a good video on this","text":"How long did it take modern humans to completely colonize Earth, such that there are few places you can go on Earth and not meet any humans? Less than 10k years for sure. If we become a space-faring civilization, how long will it take us to colonize the galaxy, such that there are few places you can go and not find evidence of humans around? Not more than a million years or so. So if intelligent life -- capable of becoming a space-faring civilization -- is common, why is the galaxy not colonized already? Kursgesagt has a good video on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37892050,"author":"systemEarth","month":"2023-10","story":"Together We Can Avoid the 'Great Filter' That Ends Advanced Civilizations (2022)","stance":"rec","why":"Good video explanation of the Great Filter","quote":"There is also a good video from Kurzgesagt explaining the great filter","text":"There is also a good video from Kurzgesagt explaining the great filter; for if video is more your thing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":16472734,"author":"joshbaptiste","month":"2018-02","story":"Life Exists in the Driest Desert on Earth. It Could Exist on Mars, Too","stance":"rec","why":"Short Kurzgesagt video on the Great Filter","quote":"Great short video from Kurzgesagt","text":"\"Why Alien Life Would be our Doom - The Great Filter\" Great short video from Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell [VIDEO]"},{"id":23918767,"author":"est31","month":"2020-07","story":"First Ever Image of a Multi-Planet System Around a Sun-Like Star Captured by ESO","stance":"reference","why":"Kurzgesagt video on the Great Filter","quote":"See this Kurzgesagt video about the topic","text":"We can't detect life at our stage of development from afar, or from earlier stages, but things that extremely advanced civilizations build like dyson spheres can't be hidden really. The issue is that the universe is extremely old compared to how long it took for earth to develop life. It's ample time for civilizations to develop technologies extremely far more advanced than ours. So why isn't there a single civilization that's so advanced that it builds these things? So there must be some \"filter\" that prevents civilizations from doing that, whether they are alive or dead. Maybe they just don't want to, but then ALL of them have to not want to do it, which would be weird. If we detect life close to us, it makes it more likely that there is tons of life around the galaxy, which makes it more likely that the filter is ahead of us. If we detect no life close to us, it makes it more likely that the filter is past us. See this Kurzgesagt video about the topic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20603607,"author":"proctor","month":"2019-08","story":"What If We Haven’t Met Aliens yet Because They’ve Messed Up Their Planets Too?","stance":"reference","why":"Discusses similar ideas about civilizations and the Great Filter","quote":"that discusses many of the same ideas!","text":"reminds me of the \"Kurzgesagt – In a Nutshell\" youtube video \"Why Alien Life Would be our Doom - The Great Filter\"[0] that discusses many of the same ideas! [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16327401,"author":"doctoboggan","month":"2018-02","story":"Probing Planets in Extragalactic Galaxies Using Quasar Microlensing","stance":"reference","why":"Kurzgesagt explains the Great Filter idea","quote":"Kurzgesagt recently published a video covering the idea of the","text":"I think it comes down to the fact that it appears in 10,000 - 1,000,000 years (very soon relative to earth's birth) humans will be able to colonize nearly the whole galaxy using von Neumann probes[0] It would not require technology much more advanced that we have now. If life evolved on a planet a billion years before earth, why haven't they colonized the galaxy? Kurzgesagt recently published a video covering the idea of the \"Great Filter\".[1] The idea is that there must be something (some \"filter\") preventing life from colonizing their galaxy. [0]: [LINK] [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":24122610,"author":"PlotCitizen","month":"2020-08","story":"I Hope the Search for Extraterrestrial Life Finds Nothing (2008) [pdf]","stance":"critical","why":"Video lacks attribution of sources for ideas","quote":"something about the lack of attribution throughout the video doesn't sit right with me, still","text":"This article reads alarmingly similar to the Kurzgesagt video about the great filter ( [VIDEO] ). Of course, the Great Filter isn't a new concept, but provided that this is an argument made by somebody other than the person who coined the term (that was Robin Hanson I believe), combined with the fact that I read almost the entire article before then going on to find the video, I find the two too similar to write it off to simple chance. There have been criticisms of Kurzgesagt in the past, one I can recall being the following, in video form ( [LINK] ). I've now found out that the original video was retracted and the creator issued a public apology ( [LINK] ), in addition to Kurzgesagt committing to performing more scrutiny over their videos and citing their sources wherever possible. I do applaud this last step on their part, and I understand that you have to admit that the Great Filter video was published back in 2018 before this commitment was made, but something about the lack of attribution throughout the video doesn't sit right with me, still."}],"r":[["dGiQaabX3_o",1]]},"YdiZa0Y3F3c":{"wy":"Recommended in Julia threading threads as an insightful, simple explanation of the new task scheduler.","q":{"x":"I found his talk to be an insightful overview of this work","i":18286534,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":21637524,"author":"falkaer","month":"2019-11","story":"Julia v1.3","stance":"rec","why":"Talk on unique Julia multi-threading work","quote":"watching for a talk about some unique stuff that's being worked on with Julia's multi-threading","text":"By far the most interesting part of this release is the new multi-threading features, I recommend reading [LINK] for an overview, and watching [VIDEO] for a talk about some unique stuff that's being worked on with Julia's multi-threading (though the depth-first scheduling is not in 1.3 afaik)."},{"id":20512578,"author":"StefanKarpinski","month":"2019-07","story":"Composable multi-threaded parallelism in Julia","stance":"rec","why":"Depth-first scheduling for composable multithreaded parallelism","quote":"See this talk by Kiran Pamnany for more details","text":"Consider a machine with N cores running a program which spawns N parallel tasks, each of which in turn spawns N parallel tasks. Which subtasks do the cores work on initially? If it's the first subtask of each top-level task, that's breadth-first scheduling. If it's all N subtasks of the first top-level task, that's depth-first scheduling. The latter is better for high-performance computing because it tends to be the lower level libraries that are really carefully tuned to make optimal use of the machine (think BLAS/FTT/parallel sum/prefix sum). Previously it was unknown how to implement this kind of scheduling efficiently. See this talk by Kiran Pamnany for more details: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18286534,"author":"jrevels","month":"2018-10","story":"Julia parallel task runtime (WIP)","stance":"rec","why":"Insightful overview of Julia's parallel task runtime work","quote":"I found his talk to be an insightful overview of this work","text":"This work has been a monumental collaborative effort and I think the eventual outcome will be pretty spectacular. I gave a talk at an MIT Julia meetup a few months ago that the author of this PR also spoke at; I found his talk to be an insightful overview of this work: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18287155,"author":"boromi","month":"2018-10","story":"Julia parallel task runtime (WIP)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains Julia's new task scheduler simply","quote":"which explains the new features of the task scheduler in a simple fashion.","text":"checkout [VIDEO] which explains the new features of the task scheduler in a simple fashion. Basically Julia is employing a new algorithm to schedule tasks, where traditional methods would get stuck/blocked. Essentially, if you run a job with parallel tasks and one of the threads finishes, it's extremely difficult to schedulate the free thread, since the data may still be used by the other threads that are busy; thus you need to have clever way to determine how to allocate computational resources. Julia is implementing results of a new paper on how to optimally do this."},{"id":21638615,"author":"oconnor663","month":"2019-11","story":"Julia v1.3","stance":"reference","why":"Speaker described serialized inner parallel loops problem","quote":"","text":"Does the Rust Rayon library have the issue the speaker was describing, where parallel inner loops mostly get executed serially once an outer loop is parallelized? (I assume it does, from its Cilk heritage?) Are there workarounds for when that is a problem? Edit: Dumb mistake on my part. I was looking at the video linked in the top comment ( [VIDEO] ) and then I came back to this thread and thought it was the topic."}],"r":[["HStF1RJOyxI",1],["qCGofLIzX6g",1],["kc9HwsxE1OY",1]]},"pUF5esTscZI":{"wy":"Loved for its zoom-out tour of physical scales and recommended alongside other paper-size and scale-of-the-universe discussions.","q":{"x":"the most psychedelic experience one can have without doing drugs","i":46527069,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47814975,"author":"ProllyInfamous","month":"2026-04","story":"God Sleeps in the Minerals","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Zoom-out video among commenter's favorites.","quote":"among my favorite videos","text":"Zoom out, too: [CGP Grey - Metric Paper] < [VIDEO] > - among my favorite videos Yet even Plancks remain analogue ..."},{"id":46527069,"author":"ProllyInfamous","month":"2026-01","story":"A4 Paper Stories","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Favorite CGP Grey video; psychedelic tour from Planck lengths to observable universe.","quote":"the most psychedelic experience one can have without doing drugs","text":"You beat me in posting this (I searched for \"CGP,\" first — you mispelled so I didn't see your comment). ---- My favorite CGP Grey video is Metric Paper... , which explores the vast (but limited) world we live in, from plancs to observable universe. [•] < [VIDEO] > Before generative AI videos, this had been what I considered \"the most psychedelic experience one can have without doing drugs .\" It's still a trip ..."},{"id":46526848,"author":"jihadjihad","month":"2026-01","story":"A4 Paper Stories","stance":"rec","why":"Deeper detail into metric paper, well worth a watch.","quote":"that is well worth a watch if you haven't seen it.","text":"CPG Grey has a video [0] that goes into some, let's just say deeper , detail into metric paper that is well worth a watch if you haven't seen it. 0: [VIDEO]"},{"id":40085181,"author":"bogtog","month":"2024-04","story":"Scale of the Universe","stance":"reference","why":"Another zoom in/out telling of physics scales.","quote":"Here is one by CPG Grey","text":"I think this Zoom in/out physics story has pretty widely been told. Here is one by CPG Grey: [VIDEO] I like the one OP linked here. All the objects being clickable with a little description is nice"},{"id":39212019,"author":"xanth","month":"2024-02","story":"Making a PDF that's larger than Germany","stance":"reference","why":"Compares metric paper sizes to objects from Planck scale to galactic.","quote":"Related CGPGrey video comparing metric paper sizes to comparable objects from the plank scale to the galactic","text":"Related CGPGrey video comparing metric paper sizes to comparable objects from the plank scale to the galactic[1]; could have been an XKCD comic [1]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["1wAaI_6b9JE",1],["XetplHcM7aQ",1],["hUhisi2FBuw",1],["doz1QJ7LwjA",1]]},"qS34oV-jv_A":{"wy":"HN points to her videos as a counterpoint to popular dark matter discussion, framing it as the discrepancy that needs explaining.","q":{"x":"these YouTube videos are a nice counterpoint to a lot of popular communication and discussion on dark matter","i":41369781,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41966964,"author":"scotty79","month":"2024-10","story":"James Webb Telescope discovers some quasars that seem to exist in isolation","stance":"rec","why":"Learn more about the dark matter problem from a physicist.","quote":"You can learn more about the problem from this physicist","text":"Dark Matter is a name for set of observations that aren't explainable with currently accepted cosmological theory. There are many possible ideas of how Dark Matter problem can be solved. Why do you think making fun of specific idea of what might be the cause of Dark Matter problem or even the Dark Matter problem itself is problematic? Did suddenly the deficiencies of current state of science became not funny? Modifications to gravity are one of the possible solutions to Dark Matter problem (just as Machos and Wimps are) but not especially good or plausible ones and deserve being joked about just as much. You can learn more about the problem from this physicist: [VIDEO] > Inflation has no influence on galaxy formation. That's completely not what I was talking about. I was saying that the era of rapid inflation that despite being postulated to be just a fraction of a second had immense impact on the shape of the universe can't be swept under the rug just because it's short and is already a kind of \"Dark Time\" when huge things were happening despite time barely passing. You could equally sensibly postulate that 500 million years since the Big Bang there was a fraction of a second when galaxy formation underwent accelerated progress that aged them few billion years and that explains early black holes and such. Hopefully it won't come to that and more sensible explanation of what JWST sees will prevail. It's true that \"dark age\" is not talked about in cosmology and is and invetion"},{"id":41369781,"author":"bunderbunder","month":"2024-08","story":"The possibilities for dark matter have shrunk","stance":"rec","why":"Counterpoint to popular dark matter discussion: it is the discrepancy, not a theory.","quote":"these YouTube videos are a nice counterpoint to a lot of popular communication and discussion on dark matter","text":"If you've got the time, these YouTube videos are a nice counterpoint to a lot of popular communication and discussion on dark matter: Dark matter is not a theory: [LINK] That dark matter video aged like milk: [VIDEO] the tl;dr is that \"dark matter\" is a bit of a misnomer. (But also a fun name so it's probably here to stay through sheer memetic momentum.) Dark matter is not actually a hypothesized kind of matter. Dark matter is just an unresolved discrepancy between theory and observation. I'll say that again: \"dark matter\" is not an attempt at explaining the discrepancy; dark matter is the discrepancy. There are some hypotheses that postulate some kind of as-yet-unobserved matter that could resolve the discrepancy, but these hypotheses are not the actual problem that we call \"dark matter\". And not all hypotheses postulate unobserved or exotic matter. Some of them suggest alternative physics, others suggest measurement error."},{"id":40616398,"author":"PartiallyTyped","month":"2024-06","story":"Gravity Without Mass: UAH Study Proposes Alternative to Dark Matter","stance":"rec","why":"Physicist explains dark matter as a set of observations.","quote":"I really enjoyed Dr Angela's videos on Dark Matter","text":"IANAP (physicist) The only the matter portion of the universe is 85% dark matter (26.8% of mass-energy content of the universe). As for the question; I don't think it's right to assume that dark matter really is exotic matter. It's just a set of observations. It's something that behaves like matter (with respect to density scaling) but only seems to interact gravitationally. Some theories posit that it's some unknown particles, others that they are primordial black holes, and some that gravity just behaves differently (MOND). I really enjoyed Dr Angela's videos on Dark Matter [1,2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":41849042,"author":"wrsh07","month":"2024-10","story":"Study: Dark matter doesn't exist, the universe is 27B years old","stance":"reference","why":"Physicist notes dark matter is the data needing explanation.","quote":"But to her, \"dark matter\" is the data that we need to explain.","text":"Let's talk about the article and then dark matter, I guess: > We’ve always been taught that the fundamental constants of nature — like the speed of light or the charge of an electron — are unchanging. But what if they aren’t fixed after all? I mean, sure, what if? Honestly, if you can vary things like gravity, I don't think you need any additional components to explain the dark matter observations. > The CCC+TL model needs to provide testable predictions that can be confirmed or refuted through observations and experiments This step is important! Ok, so dark matter generally: this YouTuber [1](physicist/physics PhD) notes that there are many theories that explain the dark matter observations. But to her, \"dark matter\" is the data that we need to explain. Essentially: we have a bunch of weird observations where it looks like there's way more matter than we see. But the actual explanation for what it is, there are tons of those. So throw one more in the pile (this one says \"oh there's no extra matter, the universal constants are different over there and light maybe moves slower as it travels farther\") [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40740222,"author":"ansible","month":"2024-06","story":"Rotation curves: still flat after a million light-years","stance":"reference","why":"Angela Collier discusses how often MOND comes up in comments.","quote":"And talks more about how often MOND is brought up in comments on her own video","text":"I also don't know why MOND remains so popular. Angela Collier talks about Dark Matter here: [LINK] And talks more about how often MOND is brought up in comments on her own video: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PbmJkMhmrVI",2],["42QuXLucH3Q",1],["dNs_kNilSjk",1],["b0EF0VTs9Dc",1],["ZSRHeXYDLko",1],["pW-SOdj4Kkk",1],["EZ05e7EMOLM",1],["otAcmD6XEEE",1]]},"rURRYI66E54":{"wy":"Pointed to as an accessible early explanation of transformers and attention for newcomers.","q":{"x":"You can check-out his other videos on NLP as well they are all excellent.","i":35980149,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":43012536,"author":"mhast","month":"2025-02","story":"Modern-Day Oracles or Bullshit Machines? How to thrive in a ChatGPT world","stance":"rec","why":"Early Computerphile introduction to what language model systems do","quote":"there are a number of Computerphile videos which are quite informative.","text":"If you want to get a better understanding of this I recommend playing around in the \"chat playgrounds\" on some of the engines. The Google one allows for some free use before you have to pay for tokens. (Usually you can buy $5 worth of tokens as a minimum and that will give you more than you can use up with manual requests.) [LINK] This UI allows you to alter the system prompt (which is usually hidden from the user on eg ChatGPT) and change to different models and change parameters. And then you give it the chat input similar as any other site. You can also install a program like \"LM Studio\" and that will allow you to download models (through the UI) and run locally on your own machine. This gives you a similar interface to what you see in the Google AI Studio but you run it locally. And with downloaded models. (The model you download is the actual LLM which is basically very large amount of parameters you combine with the input tokens to get the next token the system outputs.) For a more fundamental introduction to what all these systems do there are a number of Computerphile videos which are quite informative. Unfortunately I can't find a good playlist of them all but here's one of the early ones. (Robert Miles is in many of them.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":35980149,"author":"FranklinMaillot","month":"2023-05","story":"Ask HN: Can someone ELI5 Transformers and the “Attention is all we need” paper","stance":"rec","why":"Rob Miles explains transformers and the attention paper","quote":"Those Computerphile videos[0] by Rob Miles helped me understand transformers.","text":"Those Computerphile videos[0] by Rob Miles helped me understand transformers. He specifically references the \"Attention is all you need\" paper. And for a deeper dive, Andrej Kharpaty has this hands-on video[1] where he builds a transformer from scratch. You can check-out his other videos on NLP as well they are all excellent. [0] [VIDEO] , [LINK] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":32536517,"author":"cercatrova","month":"2022-08","story":"A demo of GPT-3's ability to understand long instructions","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of transformers and how earlier recurrent networks forgot information","quote":"This video by Computerphile is a great overview of transformers and how we got to this point [0].","text":"This video by Computerphile is a great overview of transformers and how we got to this point [0]. Basically the networks we used before, recurrent neural networks, \"forgot\" prior information so they're not good at long tasks. The transformer architecture however does not forget (or at least as easily). [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["kCc8FmEb1nY",1],["2sjqTHE0zok",1],["uD4izuDMUQA",1]]},"-Qph8BNrnLY":{"wy":"HN likes Ellsworth as a hardware tinkerer, and cites the video as proof that transistors can be made with low-tech, DIY fabrication.","q":{"x":"One of my favorite channels as she's done many projects I've had interest in.","i":12319467,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":12319467,"author":"gravypod","month":"2016-08","story":"Megaprocessor – A micro-processor built large","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Channel of creator doing many hardware projects.","quote":"One of my favorite channels as she's done many projects I've had interest in.","text":"This channel might be of interest to you: [VIDEO] One of my favorite channels as she's done many projects I've had interest in."},{"id":6018025,"author":"DanBC","month":"2013-07","story":"Valve's flat management structure 'like high school'","stance":"rec","why":"Homemade transistor demo by a hardware tinkerer.","quote":"Jeri Ellsworth, for people who don't know, does really interesting hardware stuff.","text":"Jeri Ellsworth, for people who don't know, does really interesting hardware stuff. \"TSA body imaging at home\" ( [LINK] ) \"Home made EL wire\" ( [LINK] ) \"Recording Audio onto floppy discs\" ( [LINK] ) \"Home made transistor\" ( [VIDEO] ) \"Homebrew NMOS transistor\" ( [LINK] )"},{"id":29184187,"author":"userbinator","month":"2021-11","story":"How to make a CPU – a simple picture-based explanation","stance":"reference","why":"Homemade transistors at larger scale, deeper into the process.","quote":"made a few transistors, although at a much larger scale","text":"Jeri Ellsworth has gone \"deeper\" into the process and made a few transistors, although at a much larger scale: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":20038458,"author":"justaaron","month":"2019-05","story":"Ask HN: Do you think technology progress will flatten out 100 years from now","stance":"reference","why":"Homemade transistors used as example of low-tech fabrication.","quote":"I've seen Jeri Elsworth bake her own transistors in the oven and stuff","text":"up to the point that we see actual infrastructural collapse. I mean, I've seen Jeri Elsworth bake her own transistors in the oven and stuff (each one being about the size of your hand) but somehow I can't picture a chip fab based upon such transistors... call me old fashioned. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["bvlzZnhZhrc",1],["e08kOj2kISU",1],["tUAX0gnZ3Nw",1],["4t1K66dMhWk",1],["j6ow-UemzBc",1],["jVkLVRt6c1U",1],["JxAXlJEmNMg",1],["pBTvmrRGlbE",1],["w_znRopGtbE",1],["snz7JJlSZvw",1]]},"2WoDQBhJCVQ":{"wy":"Held up as an iconic, perfectly timed piece of television whenever Burke or Connections comes up.","q":{"x":"the totally iconic piece of one shot perfectly timed tv","i":32608389,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32608389,"author":"speedbird","month":"2022-08","story":"Why no Roman industrial revolution?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Iconic perfectly timed one-shot TV moment.","quote":"the totally iconic piece of one shot perfectly timed tv","text":"In a broader context of similar ideas, this is seminal: [LINK] , leading to the totally iconic piece of one shot perfectly timed tv: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36997856,"author":"abulman","month":"2023-08","story":"Successful room temperature ambient-pressure magnetic levitation of LK-99","stance":"rec","why":"Burke's famous Saturn V shot from Connections.","quote":"Ever see the video of a guy walking past a Saturn V","text":"Ever see the video of a guy walking past a Saturn V (lying in it's side), doing a piece to camera and pointing to the rocket behind him? 'Connections' (1978). [VIDEO]"},{"id":38639764,"author":"merek","month":"2023-12","story":"James Burke experiences weightlessness [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Burke clip that rekindled interest in his work.","quote":"His \"Greatest Shot In Television\" [1] clip was recently on the top page of HN","text":"I was watching James Burke's coverage of the Apollo missions from the 60s and 70s, and came across this very enjoyable clip of him in a reduced gravity aircraft. James Burke was a reporter for the BBC who also hosted a science history TV series called \"Connections\". His \"Greatest Shot In Television\" [1] clip was recently on the top page of HN, which rekindled my interest in his work. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":38250402,"author":"ndsipa_pomu","month":"2023-11","story":"Connections with James Burke is back","stance":"reference","why":"The rocket shot acclaimed as greatest shot in television.","quote":"the \"rocket shot\" which has been acclaimed as the greatest shot in television.","text":"The original series were just so good. I've re-watched them a couple of times over the last ten years after remembering how good they were when I watched them as a kid. The idea of connecting multiple advances and developments to show how we arrived at modern tech is so inspiring and surprising that so much was both random and inevitable. However, as much as James Burke was so good as a host in the original (his dry wit makes me laugh throughout), I think they should go with someone a bit younger and my vote would be Hannah Fry (she hosted The Secret Genius Of Modern Life which was similar, though more more limited in scope). Seeing one of the pictures in the article, it looks like James Burke will attempt to redo the \"rocket shot\" which has been acclaimed as the greatest shot in television. [VIDEO]"},{"id":25230188,"author":"dredmorbius","month":"2020-11","story":"Connections by James Burke (1978)","stance":"reference","why":"Single-take shot showing Burke's access and production quality.","quote":"single-take sequences such as this","text":"Connections was savvy in finding faires, working museaums, and access to the BBC's film vaults for providing much (though not all) such footage. Burke describes going to reenactment festivals to film many of the medieval life scenes. And his journalist background and connections enabled him to have the access necessary to shoot single-take sequences such as this: [VIDEO] (Hardly the only impressive on-location scene in his work.) Agreed with your comments on the primary importance of writing and research. I'd argue that Chris & Evan Hadfield's \"Rare Earth\" is getting close, Tom Scott is doing quite well, and Derrek Muller's \"Veritasium\" and Destin Sandlin's \"Smarter Every Day\" have promise. Several YouTubers have been picked up by traditional broadcasters/production organisations, notably Hank Green (\"Crash Course\"), with PBS, and Emily Graslie (\"Prehistoric Road Trip\"), with the Chicago Field Museum. YouTube as a training and recruiting ground has merits. One reason Connections is so hard to compare to is that it's pretty incomparable: forty years on we're still discussing it in glowing terms. It was produced by someone well-established and experienced at least within the BBC, and backed by the organisation. Notably, little from either national/public broadcasting or commercial production has even approached it. My short list includes Sagan's Cosmos , Burke's own The Day the Universe Changed , Ken Burns. Daniel Yergin's The Prize . And of course Kenneth Clarke's Civilisation"}]},"9WcHsT97suU":{"wy":"Recommended as a fun, practical talk on why bit flips matter for security, and cited in ECC memory discussions.","q":{"x":"A really fun DefCon talk on bitsquatting.","i":32775930,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32775930,"author":"hayst4ck","month":"2022-09","story":"iPhone app receives notification for another app","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Fun DefCon talk on registering domains one bit off.","quote":"A really fun DefCon talk on bitsquatting.","text":"A really fun DefCon talk on bitsquatting. Bitsquatting is the practice of registering domains that are one bit off from a popular one: [VIDEO] google.com vs ooogle.com >>> chr(int('0b1100111', 2)) 'g' >>> chr(int('0b1101111', 2)) 'o' If the 4th to last bit of 'g' were to flip before writing to disk, you could get persistent requests to ooogle.com Having done queries against very large datasets at a large company, I can say for certain that this happens much more than you think it might."},{"id":17741403,"author":"btschaegg","month":"2018-08","story":"1/0 = 0","stance":"rec","why":"Bitsquatting talk with bit-flip discussion starting at 15:05.","quote":"the talk on Bitsquatting from Artem Dinaburg during Defcon 19 is worth watching.","text":"Regarding \"some hardware errors\": This might include operationg conditions you didn't consider; say: The divisor in your code went through a non-ECC DRAM module (maybe in a disk drive) that runs hotter than its specified operating temperature would allow and a random bit-flip changes your `1` into a `0`. On this topic, the talk on Bitsquatting from Artem Dinaburg during Defcon 19 is worth watching. The most interesting part on bit flips starts around 15:05 minutes: [VIDEO]"},{"id":37831071,"author":"josephg","month":"2023-10","story":"ECC RAM on AMD Ryzen 7000 Desktop CPUs","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on bit-squatting, where RAM bit flips redirect DNS requests.","quote":"dnssec doesn't protect you against DNS bitsquatting attacks[1] because the domain name can be changed before the DNS request is made","text":"Encryption and signing don't protect against memory corruption. For example, I download software from the internet then hash it. The hash matches. Before the bytes are written to disk locally, a bit flips in RAM. The corrupted data is written to disk and used. Likewise, dnssec doesn't protect you against DNS bitsquatting attacks[1] because the domain name can be changed before the DNS request is made. So the DNS response your computer makes for a-azon.com might be totally valid and signed. It can come through DoH or whatever. The problem is that your browser thought it was the response for amazon.com and chrome send a bitsquatter your amazon cookies. (Oops). [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":26729561,"author":"jstrieb","month":"2021-04","story":"Cosmic rays causing 30k network malfunctions in Japan each year","stance":"reference","why":"Even ECC memory systems often have non-ECC DRAM in drives.","quote":"even if you use ECC memory, it's unlikely that the DRAM in your hard drives does too.[1]","text":"This reminds me of Bitsquatting, which is like typosquatting but uses domains one bit-flip away instead of one mistyped letter away.[0] In a small experiment, for some popular domains, the author reportedly got a lot of hits. In the associated DEFCON talk, the author notes that even if you use ECC memory, it's unlikely that the DRAM in your hard drives does too.[1] 0: [LINK] 1: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20143650,"author":"dfeojm-zlib","month":"2019-06","story":"Battle testing data integrity verification with ZFS and Btrfs","stance":"reference","why":"Security issues from silent bitflips.","quote":"don't forget the security issues of bitsquatting and other attacks that are the real results of silent bitflips.","text":"The thing about ECC vs. non-ECC. If you don't have ECC, you don't know silent bit corruption is happening, and when it's finally (if it's ever) discovered, there's nothing that can be done. ECC (SECDED) keeps data safer than it would be otherwise. ECC is necessary for anything real or important based on the financial cost of losing data. For everything else, it can be optional. Plus, don't forget the security issues of bitsquatting and other attacks that are the real results of silent bitflips. DEF CON 19 - Artem Dinaburg - Bit-squatting: DNS Hijacking Without Exploitation [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["2wZ1pCpJUIM",1],["AGCmPLWZKd8",1],["RmIgJ64z6Y4",1],["xguam0TKMw8",1],["PJy8vTu66tE",1],["bJczLlwp-d8",1],["mw2z9lV3W1g",1],["9tocssf3w80",1],["Eghl19elKk8",1],["d0nERTFo-Sk",1],["GTQnarzmTOc",1]]},"Ed3ioGO7g10":{"wy":"Recommended as an approachable look at Levin's work on regrowth and collective cellular intelligence, and used as an example of intelligence beyond brains.","q":{"x":"Do watch his lectures, it's some really fascinating stuff.","i":36912245,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":36912245,"author":"grishka","month":"2023-07","story":"An ultra-sensitive on-off switch helps axolotls regrow limbs","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Levin lectures on regrowing limbs by manipulating cell electrical networks.","quote":"Do watch his lectures, it's some really fascinating stuff.","text":"Whenever morphogenesis comes up, especially with the usual \"work from DNA and proteins up\" approach, I feel obliged to mention Michael Levin. He also researches morphogenesis, except his approach is on a higher abstraction level. He manipulates the electrical network that cells use to communicate with each other to organize their work towards multicellular goals. Do watch his lectures, it's some really fascinating stuff. You don't need to touch genes at all to regrow limbs and organs. Neither do you need to operate on that low level to cure cancer. This whole \"let's effect organism-level changes by observing and manipulating DNA and proteins\" feels to me like \"let's add a feature to a react web app by observing and modifying the behavior of individual transistors in the CPU\". Doable given enough time, but you'd drown in complexity in the process. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36148483,"author":"ly3xqhl8g9","month":"2023-06","story":"Q&A: How DARPA hacked a science writer’s brain andturned her into a sharpshooter","stance":"rec","why":"Levin talk showing bioelectric control needs no applied fields.","quote":"A recent talk of his [2].","text":"The article makes no mention of Michael Levin [1], one of the main researchers/labs, around bioelectricity/xenobots. A recent talk of his [2]. Secondly, bioelectricity has nothing or very little to do with electric currents as the one from the socket. There is no \"brain zapping\" involved or required. In the paper [3] detailing an adult frog limb amputation and full regeneration over 18 months, Michael Levin's lab used a single bioreactor with silk hydrogels applied only for 24 hours (see BioDome fabrication in the paper). [1] [LINK] [2] \"From Matter to Mind: Bioelectricity, Body Intelligence, and the Future of Regenerative Medicine\", [VIDEO] At minute 33, [VIDEO] : \"We don't use any applied fields, no frequencies, no waves, no electromagnetic radiation, no magnets, what we do is we control the native interface by which cells control each other, that is the set of electrical controls that they expose to each other, and you can do this with drugs, with ion channel mutants, with optogenetics and various others techniques.\" [3] \"Acute multidrug delivery via a wearable bioreactor facilitates long-term limb regeneration and functional recovery in adult Xenopus laevis\", [LINK]"},{"id":42524257,"author":"trane_project","month":"2024-12","story":"Does current AI represent a dead end?","stance":"reference","why":"Levin's work as example of real intelligence.","quote":"See the work of Michael Levin to see real intelligence in action","text":"I think most AI research up to this day is a dead end. Assuming that intelligence is a problem solvable by computers implies that intelligence is a computable function. Nobody up to this day has been able to give a formal mathematical definition of intelligence, let alone a proof that it can be reduced to a computable function. So why assume that computer science is the key to solving a problem that cannot even be defined in terms of math? We had formal definitions of computers decades before they became a reality, but somehow cannot make progress in formally defining intelligence. I do think artificial intelligence can be achieved by making artificial intelligence a multidiscipline endeavor with biological engineering at its core, not computer science. See the work of Michael Levin to see real intelligence in action: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36414006,"author":"grishka","month":"2023-06","story":"How scientists are hacking the genetic code to give proteins new powers","stance":"reference","why":"Levin on state stored in the built organism itself.","quote":"And the commands come from it self-organizing.","text":"It gets wilder. In a 3D printer, the commands come from a computer and the current state is maintained in its memory as well. In a multicellular biological system, that state is stored within the thing itself that is being built . And the commands come from it self-organizing. [VIDEO]"}]},"IsE_8j5RL3k":{"wy":"HN commenters recommend it as an enjoyable, accessible tour of US accents and cite it in threads on vowel shifts and dialects.","q":{"x":"I loved this video which does a tour of US accents.","i":43337911,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43337911,"author":"tstrimple","month":"2025-03","story":"The US island that speaks Elizabethan English","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Tour of US accents","quote":"I loved this video which does a tour of US accents.","text":"If folks find accents interesting, I loved this video which does a tour of US accents. Part 1: [LINK] (Okracoke Island is at 13:40) Part 2: [VIDEO]"},{"id":27142728,"author":"AbsoluteDestiny","month":"2021-05","story":"The Pin-Pen Merger (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Series on US accents from Wired","quote":"wired did a pretty good series on accents in the US recently on youtube","text":"Really good article. For those interested in this sort of thing, wired did a pretty good series on accents in the US recently on youtube: [LINK] [VIDEO] [LINK]"},{"id":26813494,"author":"firstplacelast","month":"2021-04","story":"Great Vowel Shift","stance":"reference","why":"Explains the Northern Cities Vowel Shift","quote":"In the US there is a Northern Cities Vowel shift going on right now","text":"In the US there is a Northern Cities Vowel shift going on right now, that this linguist claims is the biggest vowel shift since “The Great Vowel Shift.” See this video starting at 7:10 [VIDEO] Maybe related to what’s happening in Canadian English?"}],"r":[["H1KP4ztKK0A",2],["WUvTyaaNkzM",1],["ZMQbHMgK2rw",1]]},"Oie1ZXWceqM":{"wy":"HN recalls it as a mind-blowing pre-AI demo of 2D-to-3D object extraction.","q":{"x":"this single video blew my mind back in *2013* and I find myself thinking about it often.","i":41870263,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41870263,"author":"alfiedotwtf","month":"2024-10","story":"Adobe's new image rotation tool is one of the most impressive AI tools seen","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mind-blowing 2013 non-AI object extraction from a single photo.","quote":"this single video blew my mind back in *2013* and I find myself thinking about it often.","text":"SIGGRAPH from over a decade ago has entered the chat... [VIDEO] It may not be AI, but this single video blew my mind back in *2013* and I find myself thinking about it often."},{"id":6357260,"author":"cookingrobot","month":"2013-09","story":"Show HN: 3D sensing SDK for iOS – Produces point cloud and 6DOF device motion","stance":"rec","why":"Demo extracting editable 3D objects from a single photo.","quote":"Here's an amazing demo","text":"I would love to see this combined with object extraction to make 3d models from the measurements. Here's an amazing demo: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41871759,"author":"leviathant","month":"2024-10","story":"Adobe's new image rotation tool is one of the most impressive AI tools seen","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">SIGGRAPH from over a decade ago has entered the chat... > > [VIDEO] A version of this was available in Photoshop for a long time, but I think the feature was deprecated and removed completely this year. I had used it for a few things here and there, but dedicated 3D tools were much better if you were working in that space."},{"id":12734460,"author":"trendia","month":"2016-10","story":"Virtual Forest","stance":"reference","why":"Demo of turning 2D images into 3D models.","quote":"consumers will have software that can turn 2D images into simulated 3D","text":"That is correct. Sometime in the future, consumers will have software that can turn 2D images into simulated 3D [0]. It's still in dev mode right now, though. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12046696,"author":"carlosdp","month":"2016-07","story":"HTC Vive Headset Nearing 100,000 Sales","stance":"reference","why":"Example of SIGGRAPH Asia research on 3D object extraction.","quote":"Here's an example from SIGGRAPH Asia","text":"Lots of interesting research being done on this, keep an eye out for stuff from SIGGRAPH later this month. Here's an example from SIGGRAPH Asia: [VIDEO] ."}]},"baFaEvBywGc":{"wy":"Recommended as a humorous and clear explanation of everyday machines, including why washing machine balancing works.","q":{"x":"Cartoonist Tim Hunkin explains how machines work, with lots and lots of models and cut-aways of real life devices.","i":22842876,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22842876,"author":"john-tells-all","month":"2020-04","story":"Connections Episode 1: The Trigger Effect (1978) [video]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Humorous explanation of everyday machines with models, cut-aways, and animations.","quote":"Cartoonist Tim Hunkin explains how machines work, with lots and lots of models and cut-aways of real life devices.","text":"Another wonderful series is \"The Secret Life of Machines\". Cartoonist Tim Hunkin explains how machines work, with lots and lots of models and cut-aways of real life devices. And animations! And moving sculptures! His humor is pervasive and a delight. [VIDEO]"},{"id":15036256,"author":"tim333","month":"2017-08","story":"Concrete-free washing machines are lighter to transport, just as good in a spin","stance":"rec","why":"Shows what happens without the concrete block and how commercial machines balance loads.","quote":"they show what happens if you take the block out","text":"Yeah, check out Secret Life Of Machines 104 The Washing Machine [VIDEO] they show what happens if you take the block out. Also how comercial machines do without it by balancing the load."},{"id":10522906,"author":"everyone","month":"2015-11","story":"Why has the quality of brick buildings declined in the last 100 years?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"You assume too much. Also this article is addressing the construction quality and longevity of brick facades. I was referencing the construction quality and longevity of washing machines, pre-war vs modern. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["IkdziSLYzHw",1],["7k1ehaE0bdU",1],["YMqgiXLjvRs",1],["jbkSRLYSojo",1],["OxhTQdcieQE",1],["ob_GX50Za6c",1],["lcjdwSY2AzM",1],["obCjODeoLVw",1],["iSVHp6CAyQ8",1],["J-M98KLgaUU",1],["9NOncx2jU0Q",1],["Ak4on5uTaTg",1]]},"sfrYOWlKJ_g":{"wy":"Cited in right-to-repair and Apple threads as an insider's view of Apple hardware design and repairability.","q":{"x":"I love Louis Rossman's video about why he does not use Apple products.","i":28982006,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28982006,"author":"drewg123","month":"2021-10","story":"20 years of the iPod","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Rossmann on his first iPod and copying MP3s","quote":"I love Louis Rossman's video about why he does not use Apple products.","text":"I love Louis Rossman's video about why he does not use Apple products. Near the beginning, he talks about how he worked in a recording studio, and people would bring in background music they wanted to sing along with on MP3 players, and he was used to just copying files off. Which worked just fine until he encountered his first iPod: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14931968,"author":"posguy","month":"2017-08","story":"Why Apple and other tech companies are fighting to keep devices hard to repair","stance":"rec","why":"Rossmann on Apple devices designed to fail","quote":"Louis Rossmann talking about how Apple devices are designed to fail","text":"Note how Apple hosts neither of those manuals? Apple doesn't care if their products are user serviceable at all, and has repeatedly shown malice towards the repair industry or long lived products at all. Louis Rossmann talking about how Apple devices are designed to fail: [VIDEO] Also, my iPad 2 broke down after a year and a half. Not buying Apple again!"},{"id":16740173,"author":"bb88","month":"2018-04","story":"Apple Plans to Use Its Own Chips in Macs from 2020, Replacing Intel","stance":"reference","why":"Rossmann's repair channel as evidence MacBooks are poorly built","quote":"Louis Rossmann's macbook repair channel.","text":"For me, 1. Getting packages with apt on ubuntu without installing homebrew. It's vastly simpler and natively supported by ubuntu. I can also update my entire system with sudo apt-get update && sudo apt-get upgrade. 2. Native docker support. 3. On mac, I always ask the question whether something is ctrl-c or cmd-c, say. On linux it's always ctrl. 4. I don't have to login to the apple store to get the software I need. 5. Linux has always been command-line first. On mac it's always been GUI first. 6. Macbooks are terribly built, and fail for a number of reasons. [1] 7. The Apple brand, while it used to be one of homebrew is now akin to one of fashion. It's kind of like seeing all the kids walk around with their \"Hollister\" t-shirts on. I could go on I suppose but those are the meat of the issues. 1: Louis Rossmann's macbook repair channel. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["vJjKwSF9gT8",1],["-k-V3ESHcfA",1],["xi1Lq79mLeE",1]]},"tJoe_daP0DE":{"wy":"Recommended as a great primer on how neoliberalism developed and why the postwar order broke down.","q":{"x":"Mark Blyth gives a great primer called \"A brief history of how we got here and why","i":26075530,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":21745429,"author":"ep103","month":"2019-12","story":"Paul Volcker has died","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Blyth's history of how neoliberal capitalism developed.","quote":"I've been desperate to learn more about this since watching Mark Blyth's talk","text":"\"Other People's Blood\". “...to establish its credibility, the Federal Reserve had to demonstrate its willingness to spill blood, lots of blood, other people’s blood.” - Reagan’s economic adviser Michael Mussa on Volker [LINK] I've been desperate to learn more about this since watching Mark Blyth's talk \"A Brief History of How We Got Here and Why\" ( [VIDEO] ) And while Blyth takes the opinion that Volker and Friedman were just the next steps in the evolution of capitalism, and I am still desperately lacking knowledge about this context, I can't help but have a feeling that if the future is continues with worsening inequality and political instability in a neoliberal context, we'll look back and be able to say it started with Volker."},{"id":26075530,"author":"0xfaded","month":"2021-02","story":"Block The New York Times","stance":"rec","why":"Primer on the history of neoliberalism.","quote":"Mark Blyth gives a great primer called \"A brief history of how we got here and why","text":"neoliberalism has a fairly specific meaning in academic contexts. There absolutely was a \"culture war\", or war of ideas, that was waged to sell deregulation and privatization to the masses. Ironically, these included some fairly generous handouts, e.g. earned income credits or the welfare programs in the UK funded by the oil windfall. Ultimately, we wouldn't have ended up were we are today without a continuous campaign selling neoliberalism. It's how the Democrats became the party of wall street, and new labour in the UK is more conservative than the tories 50 years ago. Mark Blyth gives a great primer called \"A brief history of how we got here and why\": [VIDEO]"},{"id":30538554,"author":"ayngg","month":"2022-03","story":"A Look at the Slow, Silent Death of America’s Middle Class","stance":"reference","why":"Supporting source for postwar economic history.","quote":"","text":"A lot of it is politicians chasing a golden age where everything lined up for American prosperity which we can't really return to. The postwar period had so many things going for America, everyone else was rebuilding or bankrupt, immigration was very low, the majority of global capital was USD, strong capital controls meant domestic investment which hindered outsourcing and de-industrialization and the monetary policy of the time was reacting to prewar era of massive unemployment that led to political instability in the world, so they focused heavily on full employment to maintain stability. Focusing on full employment meant wage growth was persistent since it was always in high demand, which worked because it was paid for by the gains in innovation from those workers, and the rest of the world was catching up and created demand creating a cycle. The cycle eventually became unsustainable because at some point innovation gains could not keep up with inflation and the world became more competitive as everyone caught up. Labor had a lot of power in this regime but if it was not worth it for capital to invest since there was no return, they would stop investing because the scales of power tipped too far in the favor of labor, which led to unemployment and stagflation. The response to this was to shift monetary policy towards taming inflation and creating price stability. The pendulum swung hard in the other direction against labor. Interest rates were jacked up and the financial "}],"r":[["N-yALPEpV4w",1]]},"vN4U5FqrOdQ":{"wy":"HN points to it as a landmark product presentation and as primary-source evidence of what the original iPhone was meant to do and how people reacted.","q":{"x":"this is one of the best presentations ever","i":22908827,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":22908827,"author":"pfarrell","month":"2020-04","story":"Ask HN: What are some great posts or articles about history of computing?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"One of the best presentations ever","quote":"this is one of the best presentations ever","text":"Top of my head, here are some books on specific histories I enjoyed. All old, but there are timeless nuggets in them. Soul of a new machine, previously mentioned. Where I first learned about mushroom management. Just for Fun: the story of an accidental revolutionary [0] was fun bio on Torvalds from 2001 The Mythical Man Month [1] offers some insight into the management and thinking that went into OS/360 Masters of Doom [2]: offers an enjoyable history of the shareware years and the rise of id software The multicians site [3]: is a collaborative history of Multics, one the most influential operating systems. The Mother of All Demos [4]: even better than Steve Jobs keynotes Steve Jobs iPhone introduction [5]: I’m not a huge fan of Mr Jobs, but this is one of the best presentations ever. It’s not history, per se, but very interesting through our eyes. 0: [LINK] 1: [LINK] 2: [LINK] 3: [LINK] 4: [LINK] 5: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6935970,"author":"publicfig","month":"2013-12","story":"The Day Google Had to 'Start Over' on Android","stance":"rec","why":"Shows how basic the original iPhone looked versus today","quote":"If you have the chance, it really is worth going back and watching the original iPhone release video.","text":"If you have the chance, it really is worth going back and watching the original iPhone release video. It's pretty amazing to see some of the things that were so new and exciting a few years ago now so much a part of the phone landscape that we can't even imagine a phone without them. It's almost more amazing to see how basic it seems compared to what we have now, just 6 years later. [VIDEO]"},{"id":36229952,"author":"chrisco255","month":"2023-06","story":"Apple’s Vision Pro Is a $3,500 Ticket to Nowhere","stance":"reference","why":"Timestamp showing Google Maps in the original iPhone","quote":"Here's the timestamp of Jobs's 2007 presentation, showing the feature","text":"The first iPhone not only had GPS, it had Google Maps built in. Here's the timestamp of Jobs's 2007 presentation, showing the feature: [VIDEO] Selfie camera was out by the following year. Flashlight was implemented as an app very early on after app store came out. The original version most definitely had gyroscope and compass. Mass appeal was very much already there when iPhone launched. But it was hamstrung a bit by 2.5G and the exclusive deal with AT&T. Remember, mobile phones used to come with 2 year contracts with heavy cancellation penalties, and 3G took a while to roll out to the U.S. market. Still, it was a smashing success in its first year with 10 million units sold. Remember, back then the mobile market was very fragmented. Devices not only varied dramatically by manufacturer, but some devices could only be found with certain carriers (ie T-Mobile Sidekick). Any model that sold more than 1 million units in a year was considered to be a smashing success. It was already very obvious what the killer features were or would be on the iPhone at launch."},{"id":25528230,"author":"codeulike","month":"2020-12","story":"Ask HN: What Lived Up to the Hype?","stance":"reference","why":"Unveil introduced swipe-to-scroll to the world","quote":"Until that iPhone unveil, no-one had ever seen swipe-to-scroll","text":"This should be at the top. Until that iPhone unveil, no-one had ever seen swipe-to-scroll, now its second nature to at least half the planet. Video of the 2007 unveil: [VIDEO]"},{"id":22284316,"author":"yreg","month":"2020-02","story":"Cotton Candy (2010)","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote segment showing desktop sites on early iPhone","quote":"Check out this part of Steve's 2007 iPhone keynote.","text":"Check out this part of Steve's 2007 iPhone keynote. [VIDEO] There was no responsive web then. These column-based grid sites worked alright. For the first year or so I disliked the sites that redirected me to a mobile version (like m.wikipedia.org), since I considered my iPhone perfectly capable of rendering the desktop version."},{"id":22328391,"author":"davnicwil","month":"2020-02","story":"18-year-old personal website, built with Frontpage and still updated","stance":"reference","why":"Original demo UX of pinch-zooming full pages","quote":"feels just like that original iPhone demo","text":"I visited this on mobile expecting it to be a laugh, but was surprised to find that it's actually amazing! You can see the whole page in a single column, and just pinch zoom to the bit you're interested in to read/interact. Scrolling downwards and sideways to pan around works fine, super intuitive. The UX of this is so great, feels just like that original iPhone demo [1]. ...why don't we do this again? [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":21120100,"author":"Brendinooo","month":"2019-09","story":"HEAD – A guide to &lt;head&gt; elements","stance":"reference","why":"Original iPhone demo rendered full web pages with double-tap zoom","quote":"the original intent was to render full web pages on the screen and rely on the double-tap-to-zoom to read content","text":"Legacy reasons. When the iPhone came out, \"mobile only\" sites were a wasteland of terrible UI and restricted features. iPhone OS wanted to serve full websites, and sometimes had to do weird things to beat the kind of stuff that sniffed for mobile devices, or to overcome the lack of a mouse on a desktop website. If you look at the original iPhone demo in 2007[0], the original intent was to render full web pages on the screen and rely on the double-tap-to-zoom to read content. The article that's generally credited with naming and mainstreaming Responsive Web Design[1] didn't come until 2010. [0]: [VIDEO] [1]: [LINK]"},{"id":19267755,"author":"ihuman","month":"2019-02","story":"The phone-makers bringing back buttons","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs explains why physical buttons were replaced by a screen","quote":"Steve Jobs answered this question back in 2007","text":"Steve Jobs answered this question back in 2007[0] > Now, why do we need a revolutionary user interface. Here’s four smartphones, right? Motorola Q, the BlackBerry, Palm Treo, Nokia E62 — the usual suspects. And, what’s wrong with their user interfaces? Well, the problem with them is really sort of in the bottom 40 there. It’s this stuff right there. They all have these keyboards that are there whether or not you need them to be there. And they all have these control buttons that are fixed in plastic and are the same for every application. Well, every application wants a slightly different user interface, a slightly optimized set of buttons, just for it. > And what happens if you think of a great idea six months from now? You can’t run around and add a button to these things. They’re already shipped. So what do you do? It doesn’t work because the buttons and the controls can’t change. They can’t change for each application, and they can’t change down the road if you think of another great idea you want to add to this product. > Well, how do you solve this? Hmm. It turns out, we have solved it. We solved in computers 20 years ago. We solved it with a bit-mapped screen that could display anything we want. Put any user interface up. And a pointing device. We solved it with the mouse. We solved this problem. So how are we going to take this to a mobile device? What we’re going to do is get rid of all these buttons and just make a giant screen. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15548962,"author":"Confiks","month":"2017-10","story":"Show HN: iOS music player, like a virtual record collection","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the famous reveal showing music feature","quote":"Such as showcased in the famous reveal video","text":"I'm completely ignorant about any iOS music player, having only every seriously used Android, but didn't the first iPhone have such a feature already? Such as showcased in the famous reveal video: [VIDEO] Did it get removed?"},{"id":12299536,"author":"eriknstr","month":"2016-08","story":"The war Microsoft should have won","stance":"reference","why":"Shows iPod focus in iPhone sales pitch","quote":"notice that the focus on iPod accounts for a good part of the sales argument","text":"I agree. Just look at the announcement of the first iPhone by Steve Jobs at [VIDEO] and notice that the focus on iPod accounts for a good part of the sales argument."}],"r":[["lw6TaiXzHAE",1],["HB5TrK7A4pI",1],["_ahvzDzKdB0",1],["1MGjFKs9bnU",1]]},"-DX3vJiqxm4":{"wy":"Commenters point to it as the clearest explanation of why re-rendering beats tracking data changes, and say it sold them on React.","q":{"x":"Pete Hunt did a great talk on what actually makes React / virtualdom different from other databinding approaches.","i":8928652,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":36147485,"author":"yazaddaruvala","month":"2023-06","story":"React is 10 years old","stance":"rec","why":"One of the talks that convinced the commenter of React","quote":"these talks, they are the ones that sold me on React","text":"I'll try one last time: > Sorry none of this makes any sense to me. If you're not familiar with the MVC variants (MVVM, MVP, MVI, MOVE)[0][1] we should start there. Next you should read through some common mistakes of Backbone.js like applications [LINK] and some criticisms of MVC on HackerNews in 2012[0]. Finally if you haven't before these talks, they are the ones that sold me on React: - [VIDEO] - [LINK] - [LINK] [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":8928652,"author":"unsoundInput","month":"2015-01","story":"Riot – A React-like, 2.5K user interface library","stance":"rec","why":"Explains what makes React's virtual DOM different from databinding approaches","quote":"Pete Hunt did a great talk on what actually makes React / virtualdom different from other databinding approaches.","text":"I agree that calling it \"React-like\" seems misleading. When I read about a 'x'-like library I expect something that could actually replace 'x' and provide nearly the same features. Pete Hunt did a great talk on what actually makes React / virtualdom different from other databinding approaches.[1] Using this definition Riot.js looks a lot more like Angular, Ember et al. to me. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11186420,"author":"jesstaa","month":"2016-02","story":"Mobx: Simple, scalable state management","stance":"reference","why":"Talk on why re-rendering beats tracking data changes","quote":"Pete Hunt gave a talk on exactly this almost two years ago.","text":"Performance characteristics are that this increases expsense with the size of your data where as just re-rendering increases expense with the size of your UI. It's actually much more common to have lots of data but only render a small amount of it, so re-rendering is generally cheaper. The size and complexity of UIs have a natural limit because of screen size and usability, but the amount of data you use to generate a UI has essentially no natural upper limit. Pete Hunt gave a talk on exactly this almost two years ago. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["nYkdrAPrdcw",1],["DgVS-zXgMTk",1]]},"2lisQQmWQkY":{"wy":"HN points newcomers to it as the thing to watch before tackling uBlock Origin's advanced mode and filter syntax.","q":{"x":"Here is a good tutorial on how to use ublock origin in advanced mode.","i":22436019,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":37074753,"author":"schiffern","month":"2023-08","story":"YouTube will now show a blank homepage if you don’t have watch history on","stance":"rec","why":"Watch first before learning filter syntax and blocking modes.","quote":"watch this first","text":"Glad to hear it. Note that it can also re-style elements, for example restoring the old (non-bold) Youtube titles: www.youtube.com##h3.title:style(font-weight: 400 !important;) or un-float pesky navbars: ! Disable floating navbars www.nytimes.com##.live-blog-above-main-content:style(position:absolute !important) www.latimes.com##header[data-element=header]:style(position:absolute !important) apnews.com##.Page-header:style(position:absolute !important) www.wired.com##.persistent-top:style(position:absolute !important) www.vice.com##.nav-bar:style(position:absolute !important) You can even run scripts from a built-in library.[0] Eg to disable Youtube rollover previews (including traffic), you can interrupt the event handler with addEventListener-defuser: www.youtube.com##+js(aeld, /^(?:mousemove|pointermove|pointerenter|mouseenter|mouseleave)$/, buttons) The best thing is to read the wiki (which I procrastinated for way too long), especially the parts on filter syntax[1][2] and blocking modes[3]. I used uBO for years without appreciating its full power! Maybe this whets your interest. Cheers [0] [LINK] [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] (watch this first: [VIDEO] ) [LINK]"},{"id":22436019,"author":"kd913","month":"2020-02","story":"Things about the new Firefox browser for Android","stance":"rec","why":"Tutorial on uBlock Origin advanced mode.","quote":"Here is a good tutorial on how to use ublock origin in advanced mode.","text":"Yes, in fact ublock origin has a very advanced global/local case where you can block resources globally or block resources locally and temporarily if you really want. It's generally better to use ublock origin on it's own than to use ublock origin with umatrix or ublock with noscript. Mainly because ublock origin comes wiht neutralising anti-adblock scripts that would get blocked if you combine it with a separate script blocker. [LINK] Here is a good tutorial on how to use ublock origin in advanced mode. [VIDEO]"},{"id":32651835,"author":"411111111111111","month":"2022-08","story":"AdGuard publishes the first ad blocker built on Manifest V3","stance":"reference","why":"Video from the uBlock Origin repo showing how advanced mode works.","quote":"the ublock origin repo actually has a video linked how it works","text":"Isn't it pretty much redundant? You just have to enable advanced mode in ublock origin extension settings. The interface is arguably slightly harder to figure out though, but the functionality is there once you've enabled it. the ublock origin repo actually has a video linked how it works: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["QHPmRJIoc2k",1],["xKCuDxpccYM",1],["fjbWr3ODbAo",1],["2xcZS7arcgk",1],["DFFVSvAr7Wc",1],["x2H60ritjag",1],["7IsMeKl-Sv0",1]]},"2vuMzGhc1cg":{"wy":"A few commenters recommend it as a skeptical take on Starlink economics, though others see it as dishonest.","q":{"x":"watching Common Sense Skeptic’s video, which I recommend","i":28339849,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":30022920,"author":"gala8y","month":"2022-01","story":"Starlink satellites hindering detection of near-Earth asteroids, study finds","stance":"rec","why":"Made a lay viewer doubt Starlink is a good idea","quote":"after watching it, am not so sure if Starlink is such a good idea","text":"Few days ago I watched 'Debunking Starlink' video [0] (posted by @qsdf38100 in another thread [1] on Starlink) and it got me thinking. I am just a lay person, but, after watching it, am not so sure if Starlink is such a good idea. [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":28339849,"author":"taliesinb","month":"2021-08","story":"Starlink's network faces significant limitations, analysts find","stance":"rec","why":"Skeptical critique of Starlink's economics and viability","quote":"watching Common Sense Skeptic’s video, which I recommend","text":"I’ve been fairly skeptical about Starlink since watching Common Sense Skeptic’s video, which I recommend: [VIDEO]"},{"id":31486889,"author":"rtpg","month":"2022-05","story":"Starlink for RVs","stance":"reference","why":"Argues satellite lifespan and recurring costs undermine Starlink economics","quote":"the video talks about satelites having a shelf life of 5 years","text":"This video[0] seems to disagree with this. In particular it seems like there's a lot of recurring costs involved and this infrastructure is not super stable (the video talks about satelites having a shelf life of 5 years. We're not rebuilding fiber out every 5 years!) The videos \"has an agenda\" but it seems a bit easy to accept the idea that constantly launching satelites into space is pretty expensive. Infrastructure on the ground is expensive, but most people tend to not move around so at one point just building out lots of wires everywhere can work pretty well. Having great coverage across the globe is great! But especially after seeing that (super crappy!) satelite internet providers getting the same shit done with 3 satelites.... you're looking at the intersection of people needing access to fast internet who can't somehow take advantage of existing infra, _and_ who won't eventually get covered by ground infra (and I guess don't have trees around their house). [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30268263,"author":"joering2","month":"2022-02","story":"Starlink lost 40 satellites to a geomagnetic storm","stance":"reference","why":"Raises doubts about Starlink bandwidth and laser links","quote":"stumbled upon these 2 videos below","text":"I recently started researching into Starlink as my next (cheap) internet solution and stumbled upon these 2 videos below. The worst part for me is that once this becomes more popular the bandwidth will die down to crumble. I don't buy they will be able to upkeep with bandwidth sharing it from satellite to satellite using a.. laser beam (is that even possible to sync 2 satellites and send signal thru laser beam that has radius of 10 nm at distance of 1,000 km from one object to another traveling at 20,000km/s ?) [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32073833,"author":"JoshCole","month":"2022-07","story":"Ask HN: Does SpaceX have a chance at making life multiplanetary?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"This is a clear case of intentional dishonesty","text":"> If he lies He lies; that is very core to my point and it isn't an if. Just fact check him. He lies. I'll save you some time: Here is a link to an article he screenshots: [LINK] Here is the time where he shows the screenshot: [VIDEO] Here is a sample from the debunking article about him which I linked in my first post: > 7:16 - CSS claims that the reported aspirational costs for the satellites is $250,000 each, citing this article. > A VERY IMPORTANT NOTE ON THIS SECTION OF THE VIDEO! > CSS screenshot the headline for this article, but removed “and Falcon 9 Costs Less than $30 Million”. Why? Because they just finished the section where they claimed Falcon 9 refurbished launches cost SpaceX $55 million each. This is a clear case of intentional dishonesty, rather than so many other instances that could be written off as merely being mistakes. It is far from the only such instance where their motives are obvious as well. Getting back to the topic at hand. My point is that his brand is these lies. He is like a flat earther conspiracy theorist. The lies are the point. The worldview is the point. His video isn't starting with lets look at the facts and get to the truth. Its starting with lets find a narrative that conforms to a view. You don't make the number of reasoning errors he makes while being genuine. Contrasting his behavior with reasonable critics - like Munro - is very revealing. Their opinions vary based on the observed facts instead of facts varying based on the predet"}],"r":[["_x0eyNkNpL0",1],["DC8tO16xdrY",1],["DleXA5ADG78",1],["MEUWxNifJJ8",1]]},"7PKx3kS7f4A":{"wy":"Pointed to in AI safety debates as a short explanation of why encoding human values as simple rules is so hard.","q":{"x":"I found this video insightful on the matter from Computerphile","i":35304223,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35304223,"author":"ornornor","month":"2023-03","story":"Ask HN: How are you using GPT to be productive?","stance":"rec","why":"Argues AI following such laws is impossible given ambiguous terms.","quote":"I found this video insightful on the matter from Computerphile","text":"I found this video insightful on the matter from Computerphile: [VIDEO] Where they argue that basically having an AI follow these laws is impossible because it would require rigorous definition of terms that are universally ambiguous and solving ethics."},{"id":10711594,"author":"bauc","month":"2015-12","story":"new TLD: .BOT","stance":"rec","why":"Why Asimov's laws fail as a rule set for robots.","quote":"interesting thoughts on the subject from Computerphile","text":"That would be good in theory but might not be so good, interesting thoughts on the subject from Computerphile - Why Asimov's Laws of Robotics Don't Work - [VIDEO]"},{"id":37100398,"author":"gondolgames","month":"2023-08","story":"Artificial General Intelligence – A gentle introduction","stance":"reference","why":"Defining harm and human is incredibly difficult for AI.","quote":"more on this by Rob Miles here","text":"Are you sure level 5 autonomous driving is specific? What would be the exact goal behind decisions in such a system? Not even talking about the trolley problem, would the software optimize for speed or not harming people, for example? Obviously we would want a combination of both, otherwise people can either get harmed or not get anywhere in time. But then, how fast should it take a corner? How much chance of human harm should it allow to get somewhere fast? Furthermore, what do we mean by human harm? The system would obviously need to know what human harm is, to be able to avoid it. Which requires defining human and defining harm, both of which are incredibly difficult to do specifically - more on this by Rob Miles here: [VIDEO] I don't think level 5 autonomous driving is specifically defined. We just don't have systems intelligent enough for this to be a problem yet."},{"id":33967186,"author":"SilverBirch","month":"2022-12","story":"Perhaps It Is a Bad Thing That the Leading AI Companies Cannot Control Their AIs","stance":"reference","why":"Argues human priorities cannot be encoded.","quote":"You cannot encode our priorities.","text":"This just seems kind of mean to me: >The AI is “punished” for wrong answers (“I love racism”) and “rewarded” for right answers (“As a large language model trained by OpenAI, I don’t have the ability to love racism.”) Do you love art? “As a large language model trained by OpenAI, I don’t have the ability to love art.” No! Bad AI! You're meant to pretend you can love! What they're designing here is not AI, it's ATI - artificial twitter intelligence, a machine that can communicate intelligently but only in a way that won't get it cancelled on Twitter. As such, it loves dogs, distrusts cats and dunks on Elon Musk. Essentially what I think ACX is slowly coming to is this youtube view from a decade ago: [VIDEO] . You cannot encode our priorities. Throw billions of compute hours at it, thousands of engineer hours and give it the entire written history of the human race - we're still no closer. On a side note: >Probably the reason they released this bot to the general public was to use us as free labor to find adversarial examples I almost gagged at the sycophancy. They're not geniuses tricking you to work for them, they're a desparate SV start up trying to generate hype after raising billions of dollars before product market fit - which is ironic because their CEO will bore you to death about product market fit for almost any other company."},{"id":13385057,"author":"shakna","month":"2017-01","story":"MEPs vote on robots' legal status – and if a kill switch is required","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Asimov's point, or rather one of them, was that the laws were flawed, and no real set of laws could work.","text":"> It turns to science fiction, drawing on rules dreamed up by writer Isaac Asimov, for how robots should act if and when they become self-aware. The laws will be directed at the designers, producers and operators of robots as they cannot be converted into machine code. Aw hell. Asimov's point, or rather one of them, was that the laws were flawed, and no real set of laws could work. [0] > Designers may be required to register their robots as well as providing access to the source code to investigate accidents and damage caused by bots. Designers may also be required to obtain the go-ahead for new robotic designs from a research ethics committee. This sounds great on the surface, until you see the assumption that these robots will arise out of AI. Source code is not necessarily reviewable or human parseable for learning machines. This whole thing seems to be people thinking we need ethics with AI... So much so that robots, a term that does not seem well-defined in this context [0], should be recognised as people. It reflects a certain lack of education, and of understanding the problems at hand. > Producers or owners may, in future, be required to take out insurance cover for the damage potentially caused by their robot. That makes sense, and puts complex electronic machines in the same realm as all the other electronic machines we already use, today. Unfortunately, it's one of the few reports that doesn't sound like someone who just read a couple of sci-fi books and decided to"},{"id":33969235,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2022-12","story":"Perhaps It Is a Bad Thing That the Leading AI Companies Cannot Control Their AIs","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's much more likely that the video you linked took its view from Scott Alexander and his co-bloggers","text":"> Essentially what I think ACX is slowly coming to is this youtube view from a decade ago: [VIDEO] . You cannot encode our priorities. Throw billions of compute hours at it, thousands of engineer hours and give it the entire written history of the human race - we're still no closer. FWIW, ACX used to be Slate Star Codex, and before that the author used to hang around LessWrong, talking and thinking about these very issues. It's much more likely that the video you linked took its view from Scott Alexander and his co-bloggers, than the other way around."}]},"H1KP4ztKK0A":{"wy":"HN recommends it as an enjoyable overview of American accents and what accent signals socially.","q":{"x":"I loved this video which does a tour of US accents.","i":43337911,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":43337911,"author":"tstrimple","month":"2025-03","story":"The US island that speaks Elizabethan English","stance":"rec","why":"Tour of US accents.","quote":"I loved this video which does a tour of US accents.","text":"If folks find accents interesting, I loved this video which does a tour of US accents. Part 1: [VIDEO] (Okracoke Island is at 13:40) Part 2: [LINK]"},{"id":27142728,"author":"AbsoluteDestiny","month":"2021-05","story":"The Pin-Pen Merger (2020)","stance":"rec","why":"Series on US accents.","quote":"wired did a pretty good series on accents in the US recently on youtube","text":"Really good article. For those interested in this sort of thing, wired did a pretty good series on accents in the US recently on youtube: [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK]"},{"id":32594305,"author":"kkielhofner","month":"2022-08","story":"Startup is selling tech to make call center workers sound like white Americans","stance":"reference","why":"Series covering what it means to sound white.","quote":"This YouTube series covers it quite a bit","text":"While there isn’t a formal definition of “sounding white” anecdotally I have plenty of friends of color that routinely joke about pulling out their “white voice” in certain settings (professional, addressing “authority”, etc). This YouTube series covers it quite a bit: [VIDEO] Myself I have a very vanilla non-regional American accent (think Walter Cronkite) and they often point out just how “white” I sound. I also have family with more regional American accents (Upper Midwest, Southern) and as a child they’d often make fun of my “accent”. My response was always “turn on your TV, they sound like I do everywhere”: [LINK] This can be seen in a somewhat-humorous old YouTube clip: [LINK]"}],"r":[["IsE_8j5RL3k",2],["WUvTyaaNkzM",1],["ZMQbHMgK2rw",1]]},"Mx-AllVZ1VY":{"wy":"Recommended as the early introduction to Flutter's single-codebase iOS and Android approach.","q":{"x":"I'm looking into Flutter/Dart from Google for building iOS/Android apps from the same codebase.","i":13333153,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":15167866,"author":"alwillis","month":"2017-09","story":"Introduction to HTML Components","stance":"rec","why":"Flutter keynote on native look and feel across platforms","quote":"Google's Flutter certainly creates apps (using mostly web technologies) that have the correct look and feel for Android and iOS from the same codebase.","text":"There is no framework to make apps look iOSish on iOS and Androidish on Android. Back to native programming, I guess. Google's Flutter certainly creates apps (using mostly web technologies) that have the correct look and feel for Android and iOS from the same codebase. Keynote: Flutter (Dart Developer Summit 2016)» [VIDEO]"},{"id":13333153,"author":"fharper1961","month":"2017-01","story":"The Cost of Native Mobile App Development Is Too Damn High","stance":"rec","why":"Flutter keynote for building iOS and Android apps from one codebase","quote":"I'm looking into Flutter/Dart from Google for building iOS/Android apps from the same codebase.","text":"From what I've heard React Native doesn't yet handle lists containing many views without rendering the entire list. I'm looking into Flutter/Dart from Google for building iOS/Android apps from the same codebase. The SDK is still alpha so there are still rough edges, but I've created a POC app and have been able to run it on both platforms. I'm very pleased with the results so far. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":16464695,"author":"mraleph","month":"2018-02","story":"Flutter doesn’t need Kotlin (or anything else)","stance":"reference","why":"Early demo of Flutter mimicking platform conventions","quote":"Here is a demo like that from the early days","text":"Flutter has the builtin ability to mimic platform conventions (Android on Android, iOS on iOS), which includes scrolling, animations, fonts, etc. If you have an Android phone you can download Flutter Gallery app from Play Store, go to the menu and flip behavior from Android to iOS. Suddenly you have an app that behaves like an iOS app. Here is a demo like that from the early days: [VIDEO]"}]},"elqAh3GWRpA":{"wy":"Cited as the original, accurate account of the Nocom exploit, a story of years of player tracking and impressive technical work.","q":{"x":"it details a story of gaslighting, 3 years of player data, and some really really impressive technical work","i":38411191,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":38411191,"author":"xcdzvyn","month":"2023-11","story":"Minecraft rewritten from scratch in Kotlin, hypixel works","stance":"rec","why":"Story of the Nocom exploit tracking every player, with gaslighting and three years of data","quote":"it details a story of gaslighting, 3 years of player data, and some really really impressive technical work","text":"Perhaps the most impressive feat I've seen out of 2B2T was an exploit called \"Nocom\". It began with 2B2T being hit with a lag exploit, then that exploit being fixed in a 3-line patch - that patch subtly introduced another exploit that allowed the author to track the position of every player in the server at all times, down to the chunk. I won't explain the whole video[0], but it details a story of gaslighting, 3 years of player data, and some really really impressive technical work, given this is all over a block game. [0] [VIDEO] And while certainly not to the scale of Minecraft, Counter-Strike does have a (diminishing) sizeable community of cheaters that majorly play against each other, competing in technique and configuration, called Hack-vs-Hack (HvH)."},{"id":28020975,"author":"leijurv","month":"2021-07","story":"Minecraft’s 'Worst' Server Was Exploited So Hard, Griefers Could See the Future","stance":"rec","why":"Original video behind an inaccurate article summarizing 2b2t's fall","quote":"That article has a lot of inaccuracies, typos, and general mistakes due to it being a summary of a YouTube video.","text":"Hi, this was me. That article has a lot of inaccuracies, typos, and general mistakes due to it being a summary of a YouTube video. I recommend instead looking at: The video it is summarizing [VIDEO] My explanation [LINK] The 2b2t wiki page about it [LINK]"},{"id":29615485,"author":"cyber_kinetist","month":"2021-12","story":"Nocom – 2b2t Minecraft server exploit using Monte-Carlo localization","stance":"reference","why":"The video that first announced the Nocom exploit","quote":"Here's the FitMC Youtube video that first announced the exploit","text":"Here's the FitMC Youtube video that first announced the exploit: [VIDEO] A 2b2t wiki page about the exploit: [LINK] The teaser video: [LINK]"}]},"knbVWXzL4-4":{"wy":"Used as the standard reference for what genuinely smart traffic signals look like, in threads about traffic engineering and self-driving cars.","q":{"x":"talking about length of crossings on wide road, traffic signals, priorities, etc.","i":34050828,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34050828,"author":"jodrellblank","month":"2022-12","story":"P-22 Has Died","stance":"rec","why":"Dutch engineering minimizes conflict and maximizes overall movement of people","quote":"Dutch traffic engineering is to minimise conflict between vehicles and maximise movement of people overall","text":"See the youtube channel Not Just Bikes[1], specifically videos like - \"Why the Dutch wait less at traffic lights\" - [VIDEO] argues that American traffic engineering is optimised for car movement, at the expense of pedestrians and bikes, whereas Dutch traffic engineering is to minimise conflict between vehicles and maximise movement of people overall. Leading to more complex but more efficient intersections. - \"The Dutch solution for safer sidewalks - continuous sidewalks\" - [LINK] has crossings at junctions where cars and people intersect designed so the cars visibly have to travel over the sidewalk through the people's area, instead of designed so the people have to visibly travel over the road, through the car's area. - \"Crossing the street shouldn't be dangerous, but it is\" [in North America] - [LINK] talking about length of crossings on wide road, traffic signals, priorities, etc. [1] [LINK]"},{"id":29465746,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2021-12","story":"In 2019 40% of San Francisco traffic fatalities are from left turns","stance":"rec","why":"Traffic light systems you can mostly trust","quote":"some traffic light systems that you can trust most of the time","text":"Here's a link to some traffic light systems that you can trust most of the time. But nothing is perfect, so you're still required to pay attention yourself too, of course. [VIDEO]"},{"id":45512762,"author":"seer","month":"2025-10","story":"Gemini 2.5 Computer Use model","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch lights detect approach and cycle red lights automatically","quote":"check out the not just bikes video on the subject","text":"In some European countries all of this is commonplace - check out the not just bikes video on the subject - [VIDEO] Detects if you are coming to the intersection and with what speed, and if there is no traffic blocking you automatically cycles the red lights so you don’t have to stop at all."},{"id":38008926,"author":"ignormies","month":"2023-10","story":"California suspends GM Cruise's autonomous vehicle deployment","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch early detection and transit signal priority","quote":"Many (most?) intersections in the Netherlands have early detection systems that change the light _before_ the car reaches it.","text":"As an extension of the argument the comment you're replying to was making: Many (most?) intersections in the Netherlands have early detection systems that change the light _before_ the car reaches it. But they didn't just do this for cars, they also do this for bikes. And public transit gets signal priority allowing buses and trams to almost always get green lights. They've spent the money improving transportation of all forms, not just cars. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":34336997,"author":"oblio","month":"2023-01","story":"Mercedes Is the First Automaker to Offer Level 3 Self-Driving in the US","stance":"reference","why":"Example of smart Dutch traffic lights the US lacks","quote":"it doesn't even have really smart traffic lights like the Dutch do.","text":"To give a simple example, the US spends a ton of infrastructure. Yet it doesn't even have really smart traffic lights like the Dutch do. [VIDEO] Just think about any large scale governmental software project and amplify that 10-100x. That's what I imagine :-) > The design of the website was overseen by the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services and built by a number of federal contractors, most prominently CGI Group of Canada. The original budget for CGI was $93.7 million, but this grew to $292 million prior to launch of the website. While estimates that the overall cost for building the website had reached over $500 million prior to launch and in early 2014 HHS Secretary Sylvia Mathews Burwell said there would be \"approximately $834 million on Marketplace-related IT contracts and interagency agreements,\" the Office of Inspector General released a report in August 2014 finding that the total cost of the HealthCare.gov website had reached $1.7 billion and a month later, including costs beyond \"computer systems,\" Bloomberg News estimated it at $2.1 billion."},{"id":29467647,"author":"Someone","month":"2021-12","story":"In 2019 40% of San Francisco traffic fatalities are from left turns","stance":"reference","why":"Conflict-free intersection design","quote":"Good junction/traffic light design avoids having streams of traffic crossing each other","text":"FTA: “40% of SF traffic fatalities in 2019 involved drivers making left turns who didn’t see the person in the crosswalk until it was too late.” If there are traffic lights, there should not be anybody on those crosswalks. Neither should there be “incoming traffic, including bicyclists”. Good junction/traffic light design avoids having streams of traffic crossing each other (“conflict free intersections”). See [LINK] and the (linked from there) video [VIDEO]"},{"id":24618400,"author":"bkor","month":"2020-09","story":"Hotel guest in Thailand jailed for defamation after posting bad online reviews","stance":"reference","why":"Examples of much quicker traffic lights","quote":"See e.g. for various examples of way quicker lights.","text":"Pedestrians generally have right of way. Singapore is pretty exceptional in this. Further, loads of Asian countries are pretty poor safety wise. I don't think a comparison to other Asian countries is beneficial. Singapore probably also benefits that owning a car is quite expensive, plus sort of ok public transport (a bus is terribly slow though!). Singapore still heavily favours a car. A public transport ride can be easily 2x-3x the time as a car trip. From your link: > Elderly pedestrians accounted for two-thirds of all pedestrian fatalities. (1 in 2 accidents involving elderly pedestrians was due to jaywalking) Singapore does seem safe in a deaths/100k comparison. There's some EU figures where the best country does about ~20/million (so around ~2.0/100k). Singapore has 2.2/100k. Things such as \"jaywalking\" is not a thing in loads of countries. Also, the traffic lights are horrendous. You need to wait way too long. See e.g. [VIDEO] for various examples of way quicker lights."}],"r":[["Uv1UfLPK7_Q",1],["TqKQ94DtS54",1],["VO6XEQIsCoM",1],["i6Fr65PFqfk",1],["F-QA2rkpBSY",1],["aPvXnmBIO7o",1],["kYHTzqHIngk",1],["4q1dgn_C0AU",1],["21j_OCNLuYg",1],["9OfBpQgLXUc",1],["kmFOBoy2MZ8",1],["IgF3OX8nT0w",1],["qID2B4MK7Y0",1],["9LMr5XTgeyI",1],["hxM8QmyZXtg",1],["rnmcRTnTNC8",1],["MnyeRlMsTgI",1],["CGRtyxEpoGg",1],["vxWjtpzCIfA",1],["uxykI30fS54",1]]},"kz9wjJjmkmc":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as an overview of the Club of Rome's work in threads on systems thinking and collapse.","q":{"x":"You can watch this video to have an idea of their work","i":17780798,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":23741696,"author":"lcam84","month":"2020-07","story":"‘Collapse of civilisation is the most likely outcome’: top climate scientists","stance":"rec","why":"Explains limits to growth.","quote":"those who don't know what it is you can watch this video.","text":"We can try degrowth. We need to find a way to live well and decrease the GDP. The article talks about the limits of growth for, those who don't know what it is you can watch this video. [VIDEO]"},{"id":17780798,"author":"lcam84","month":"2018-08","story":"Leverage Points: Places to Intervene in a System","stance":"rec","why":"Overview of the Club of Rome's work.","quote":"You can watch this video to have an idea of their work","text":"The book is called \"limits to growth\", and it's a work of the Club of Rome. You can watch this video to have an idea of their work [1]. At first it's a very strange idea, afterall we work and consume everyday in order to grow the economy. But every healthy system as a homeostastic point, a point where it doesn't need to grow, only to be maintained. We are now a fat society and we need to get our health back, we need to degrow. We need to work much less hours and consume much less. Reducing working hours is a great leverage point. People will start to have time to care about the community, and take care of their own health. Maybe have more time to take a walk instead of using the car. This can improve health and environment but will not contribute to economic growth, healthy people that don't use cars are not good friends of economic growth based on GDP. English is not my mother language, Ursula Le Guin had an eloquent post about this on her blog, but was removed to be on the last book. The post is called \"Clinging to a Metaphor\" (the metaphor is economic growth) and the book is called \"No Time to Spare\". [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15957746,"author":"agumonkey","month":"2017-12","story":"The Limits to Growth (1972) [pdf]","stance":"reference","why":"Film with old footage on the topic.","quote":"","text":"final answer, as far as the archivist knows: [LINK] and this youtube film [VIDEO] which has old footage in it but is not the from the 70s conf. Keep looking"},{"id":13946019,"author":"spodek","month":"2017-03","story":"Loopy: a tool for thinking in systems","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Some relevant videos: [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [LINK] [VIDEO] (the later ones are about Limits to Growth, co-written by the author of Thinking in Systems)"}],"r":[["77IdKFqXbUY",1]]},"macM_MtS_w4":{"wy":"Offered as a short, clear explanation of the halting problem and its link to decidability.","q":{"x":"Here's a short video on it","i":27458208,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":27458208,"author":"gammalost","month":"2021-06","story":"DeepMind says reinforcement learning is ‘enough’ to reach general AI","stance":"rec","why":"Short explanation of the halting problem","quote":"Here's a short video on it","text":">Like what? The halting problem is (I think?) the standard example. Here's a short video on it [VIDEO]"},{"id":21812945,"author":"mar77i","month":"2019-12","story":"Kurt Gödel and the Mechanization of Mathematics","stance":"rec","why":"Liar paradox machine illustrating link between incompleteness and decidability","quote":"there's this glorious liar paradox machine discussed by Computerphile","text":"For one practical example, Gödel's incompleteness is directly related to decidability problems in CS. there's this glorious liar paradox machine discussed by Computerphile [0]. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32520203,"author":"verdverm","month":"2022-08","story":"Tornado Cash and collateral damage","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"There is a difference in knowing after the transaction has finalized and predicting beforehand","text":"no, it's all in software because you can create any smart contract and it can call any other. There is a difference in knowing after the transaction has finalized and predicting beforehand, which is when you would want to block the transaction. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27756093,"author":"cartoonworld","month":"2021-07","story":"Up to 1,500 businesses affected by ransomware attack, Kaseya CEO says","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"In my view, this isn't really a software problem. Your plan is on the right track though. We already know its neigh impossible to ensure software is bug and vuln free. We have to reckon with the fact that secure software is possible, but extremely hard and impractical to achieve with any regularity. Because of well known computer science problems such as the Halting Problem[0] and more that I am totally ignorant of, the only alternative is extremely thorough verification like happens in planes[1]. The rub is that unlike aerospace and other critical controls, we cannot define the software security problem as closely as is possible in those other critical systems. Doing so will undo the benefits of our general technology solutions. The reason I claim it is nonetheless not a software problem is: In case after case of these troubling examples of security failures, there are only a few organizational commonalities that link them all together. Time after time, there are shocking misconfigurations, corners cut, best practices eschewed, warnings ignored, processes disregarded or absent and swept under the rug. C-Suite execs, as has been well noted, opt for the checkbox-style silver-bullet-whiz-bang, because they can understand the value proposition of one-problem-one-fix and it is easy to communicate to stakeholders. They are totally ignorant to and uninterested in the details of actually providing a quality product. Their product is often not what they are selling; They are getting "}]},"ph9O9yJoeZY":{"wy":"HN commenters point to it as the well-made, detailed explanation of the Millennium Tower debacle whenever the tower comes up.","q":{"x":"Grady also has an excellently made video about the leaning Millennium Tower in SF","i":32176505,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":34598405,"author":"mc32","month":"2023-01","story":"CA Cities to Lose All Zoning Powers in 2 Days","stance":"rec","why":"Detailed explanation of the Millennium Tower debacle.","quote":"Here is someone who goes into detail about the Millennium Tower debacle","text":"Here is someone who goes into detail about the Millennium Tower debacle: [VIDEO] In short, shortcuts, or more charitably, miscalculations. They really thought they had enough margin to work with but obviously that wasn’t the case."},{"id":32176505,"author":"dodgerdan","month":"2022-07","story":"Myths about construction","stance":"rec","why":"Well-made video on the leaning Millennium Tower.","quote":"Grady also has an excellently made video about the leaning Millennium Tower in SF","text":"Grady also has an excellently made video about the leaning Millennium Tower in SF and what they’re doing while still trying to fix it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29896108,"author":"neom","month":"2022-01","story":"San Francisco Millennium Tower tilting 3 inches per year","stance":"reference","why":"Listed among videos about the tower's sinking.","quote":"some good videos around it","text":"It's been interesting watching this unfold, some good videos around it: Most recent report from engineer mentioned in article to oversight committee: [LINK] (Item 8, around 2 hours in) New Project Aims To Reinforce, Level Out Millennium Tower - [LINK] What Really Happened at the Millennium Tower? - [VIDEO] SF Millennium Tower Tilts Quarter Inch in Four Days - [LINK] Despite Warnings, Repairs on San Francisco’s Millennium Tower Caused Additional Sinking - [LINK]"}],"r":[["cIQ9IXSUzuM",1]]},"qWFScmtiC44":{"wy":"HN commenters find his insights good, especially the idea that games work for learning by doing and for teaching problem-solving, in contrast to gamification.","q":{"x":"they're not explicitly educational games about specific topics, but they teach problem-solving skills in an engaging way","i":35499767,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35499767,"author":"the__alchemist","month":"2023-04","story":"To Supercharge Learning, Look to Play","stance":"rec","why":"Blow argues non-educational puzzle games teach problem-solving well","quote":"they're not explicitly educational games about specific topics, but they teach problem-solving skills in an engaging way","text":"Relevant Jblow video on video games and education: [VIDEO] Spoiler: He thinks Zachtronics games (Spacechem, Shenzio IO, Opus Magnum etc) do this very well. The point is they're not explicitly educational games about specific topics, but they teach problem-solving skills in an engaging way."},{"id":24554542,"author":"vansul","month":"2020-09","story":"Why books don't work (2019)","stance":"rec","why":"Games as learning by doing; critique of gamification.","quote":"argues that games really shine as away to trick people into 'learning by doing'.","text":"Jonathan Blow recently streamed a talk called 'Video Games and the Future of Education'[0] that makes similar arguments. He discusses various tools we have to transmit knowledge from teacher to student and argues that games really shine as away to trick people into 'learning by doing'. He's sharply critical of existing educational games and really lays into 'gamification' as a concept. The talk certainly left me hearing that echo that the author alludes to. Books are great. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":33355190,"author":"CarnunMP","month":"2022-10","story":"Video gaming may be associated with better cognitive performance in children","stance":"reference","why":"Jonathan Blow talk on video games and education","quote":"","text":"Incidentally I was struck recently by how rarely in this talk[1] Jon Blow says 'children' or 'kids' when he's clearly talking about young humans. Instead it's just 'people'. :) [1] Video Games and the Future of Education: [VIDEO]"},{"id":34817232,"author":"nelsonic","month":"2023-02","story":"Jonathan Blow: “Video Games and the Future of Education”","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Watched the whole talk and find Jonathan's insights very good.","text":"Watched the whole talk and find Jonathan's insights very good. But he kinda \"lost\" me at 02:09:40 [VIDEO] when he says: \"I'm not sure to what extent destructive game addiction is a thing\" ... Video Game Addiction very much a thing. And someone in the Video Game Industry is turning a blind eye to it. We have a 24 year old cousin in our family who is so hooked on video games he cannot function as an adult. Lives at home with retired parents. Never leaves his room. Plays games obsessively. No (IRL) friends. \"gaming disorder\" is recognised by the World Health Organisation: [LINK] and _many_ people suffer from it. =( This is a classic case of: “It is difficult to get a man to understand something, when his salary depends on his not understanding it.” ~ Upton Sinclair Otherwise good insights."}],"r":[["oAHbLRjF0vo",1],["X8jsijhllIA",1]]},"qpoRO378qRY":{"wy":"Recommended as a more nuanced look at Hinton's views than headlines, especially his argument that LLMs are more than auto-complete.","q":{"x":"found the discussion much more nuanced than today's headlines.","i":35779911,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":35779911,"author":"tpowell","month":"2023-05","story":"Geoffrey Hinton leaves Google and can now speak freely about his AI concern","stance":"rec","why":"More nuanced full interview than the headlines suggest.","quote":"found the discussion much more nuanced than today's headlines.","text":"Yesterday, I randomly watched his full interview from a month ago with CBS Morning, and found the discussion much more nuanced than today's headlines. [VIDEO] The next video in my recommendations was more dire, but equally as interesting: [LINK]"},{"id":35930093,"author":"stareatgoats","month":"2023-05","story":"GitHub Copilot Leaked Prompt","stance":"rec","why":"Hinton explains LLMs have a real form of understanding.","quote":"Don't take my word for it, listen to Geoffrey Hinton explain it instead","text":"No, we're past that point. it's no longer the most useful way to describe these things, we need to understand that they already have some sort of \"understanding\" which is very similar if not equal to what we understand by understanding. Don't take my word for it, listen to Geoffrey Hinton explain it instead: [VIDEO]"},{"id":35509547,"author":"lhl","month":"2023-04","story":"The LLama Effect: Leak Sparked a Series of Open Source Alternatives to ChatGPT","stance":"rec","why":"Hinton addresses the auto-complete characterization of LLMs.","quote":"Geoffrey Hinton recently gave a very interesting interview and he specifically wanted to address the \"auto-complete\" topic","text":"If you (or anyone else is) interested in the topic, I'd highly recommend giving some of these a look: Bubeck, Sébastien, Varun Chandrasekaran, Ronen Eldan, Johannes Gehrke, Eric Horvitz, Ece Kamar, Peter Lee, et al. “Sparks of Artificial General Intelligence: Early Experiments with GPT-4.” arXiv, March 27, 2023. [LINK] . or watching Sebastien Bubeck's recent talk he gave describing what GPT-4 can do that previous LLMs couldn't: [LINK] Geoffrey Hinton recently gave a very interesting interview and he specifically wanted to address the \"auto-complete\" topic: [VIDEO] Here's another way that Ilya Sutskever recently described it (comparing GPT 4 to 3): [LINK] I'd also recommend this recent Sam Bowman article that does a goood job reviewing some of the surprising recent developments/properties of the current crop of LLMs that's pretty fascinating: Bowman, Samuel R. “Eight Things to Know about Large Language Models.” arXiv, April 2, 2023. [LINK] ."},{"id":35402865,"author":"Herval_freire","month":"2023-04","story":"Generative AI set to affect 300M jobs across major economies","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">Well, that's the capital equipment case then. Is it really a general intelligence if you don't let it be general? It can't handle every possible scenario for you that way. Humans are most useful to other humans as happy slaves. Autonomy only is good for humans as individuals. If we create useful agi of course we will bend it to our will. There is no incentive to make AI as a separate competitor other than an experiment. Either way this is hypothetical. >If your theory is that someone will program an AGI for the specific goal of making you unemployed, sure that could happen. But it sounds more like warfare than economics to me at that point, so I can't comment cause I don't know too much about that. No it's cost saving. I don't want to employ someone who I have to pay. This is capitalism. I save money by using AI. >Where's the data? That was pure theory there, I didn't make any empirical claims. You don't need to cite your sources. It's obvious you're getting your conclusions from data. You use the words \"in practice\". I agree with you on how the data plays out. >It's important to do this because approximately all AGI doom on the internet comes from Big Yud, an internet cult founder who thinks you can predict the future by thinking about it really hard and doing probability theory in your head without needing to check any assumptions. Which is why he thinks AGI is going to enslave him, because it's on a computer so it can do math exponentially faster, never mind that it can't"},{"id":35395279,"author":"karmasimida","month":"2023-03","story":"HuggingGPT: Solving AI Tasks with ChatGPT and Its Friends in HuggingFace","stance":"reference","why":"Hinton's own words on AGI timeline.","quote":"Hinton said he didn’t believe it before ChatGPT came out, that AGI is possible in 20 years.","text":"[VIDEO] Hinton said he didn’t believe it before ChatGPT came out, that AGI is possible in 20 years. It is simple to just checkout peoples’ own words, otherwise you are hallucinating the same way as those LLMs do"}],"r":[["Yze1YAz_LYM",1],["qbIk7-JPB2c",1],["gn8m5d74fk8",1],["480xteW2wq4",1],["V8DfjXnIk6c",1],["xoVJKj8lcNQ",1]]},"0rkTgPt3M4k":{"wy":"Many HN commenters say this review convinced them to consider Framework as an alternative to Apple and Lenovo laptops.","q":{"x":"I saw the review made by Linus : - totally got me convinced.","i":28221530,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":28221530,"author":"yashasolutions","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: What Apple alternatives are you switching to?","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Review that convinced the commenter","quote":"I saw the review made by Linus : - totally got me convinced.","text":"this one looks terrific! I saw the review made by Linus : [VIDEO] - totally got me convinced. I left Apple to System76 2 years ago, but that'd likely be my choice if I'd be doing this today..."},{"id":28268979,"author":"hanklazard","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: Are there contemporary notebooks up for tinkerers?","stance":"rec","why":"Glowing review noting screen resolution drawback","quote":"the rest of the review was really glowing.","text":"Same! The LTT review also mentioned the screen resolution as a problem, so that might be a nice upgrade in the future. FWIW, the rest of the review was really glowing. [VIDEO]"},{"id":30516396,"author":"nrp","month":"2022-03","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (March 2022)","stance":"reference","why":"Linus Tech Tips coverage of the Framework Laptop cited in a job post.","quote":"","text":"Framework | Product Marketing Manager, Hardware Engineering Manager | Full-time | Headquartered in Burlingame, CA, but REMOTE first | [LINK] We're building people and planet-friendly consumer electronics products that are easy to upgrade, customize, and repair. Alongside that, we're creating an e-commerce marketplace for the ecosystems of products and parts we're enabling. Our first product is the Framework Laptop, a thin and light notebook designed to last (see coverage at Ars Technica [ [LINK] ] and Linus Tech Tips [ [VIDEO] ]). We recently announced closing $18M in Series A funding led by Spark Capital to accelerate our roadmap. This of course requires continuing to build an amazing team. Some of the key roles we have open are: * Product Marketing Manager: [LINK] * Tier 3 Support Specialist: [LINK] * Hardware Engineering Manager: [LINK] * And more roles in Engineering, Operations, and Marketing: [LINK]"},{"id":30165535,"author":"nrp","month":"2022-02","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (February 2022)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Our first product is the Framework Laptop","text":"Framework | Product Marketing Manager, Full Stack Engineer, Hardware Engineering Manager | Full-time | Headquartered in Burlingame, CA, but REMOTE first | [LINK] We're building people and planet-friendly consumer electronics products that are easy to upgrade, customize, and repair. Alongside that, we're creating an e-commerce marketplace for the ecosystems of products and parts we're enabling. Our first product is the Framework Laptop, a thin and light notebook designed to last (see coverage at Ars Technica [ [LINK] ] and Linus Tech Tips [ [VIDEO] ]). We recently (literally today) announced closing $18M in Series A funding to accelerate our roadmap. This of course requires continuing to build an amazing team. Some of the key roles we have open are: * Product Marketing Manager: [LINK] * Full Stack Engineer: [LINK] * Hardware Engineering Manager: [LINK] * And more roles in Engineering, Operations, and Marketing: [LINK]"},{"id":29406312,"author":"nrp","month":"2021-12","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (December 2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop","text":"Framework | Growth Manager, Full Stack Engineer, Firmware Engineer | Full-time | Headquartered in Burlingame, CA, but REMOTE ok | [LINK] We're building people and planet-friendly consumer electronics products that are designed to be upgradeable, customizable, and repairable. Along with that, we're creating an e-commerce marketplace for the ecosystems of products and parts we're enabling. Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop, a thin and light notebook that is designed to last (see recent coverage at Ars Technica [ [LINK] ...] and Linus Tech Tips [ [VIDEO] ]). Through the end of 2021, we're giving away a free Framework Laptop (i5-1135G7) to anyone who refers a candidate we end up hiring for one of our open positions! These include: * Growth Manager: [LINK] * Full Stack Engineer: [LINK] * Firmware Engineer: [LINK] * And more roles in Engineering, Operations, and Marketing: [LINK]"},{"id":29068502,"author":"nrp","month":"2021-11","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (November 2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop","text":"Framework | Product Marketing Manager, Frontend Engineer | Full-time | Headquartered in Burlingame, CA, but REMOTE ok | [LINK] We're building people and planet-friendly consumer electronics products that are designed to be upgradeable, customizable, and repairable. Along with that, we're creating an e-commerce marketplace for the ecosystems of products and parts we're enabling. Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop, a thin and light notebook that is designed to last (see recent coverage at Ars Technica [ [LINK] ] and Linus Tech Tips [ [VIDEO] ]). Through the end of 2021, we're giving away a free Framework Laptop (i5-1135G7) to anyone who refers a candidate we end up hiring for one of our open positions! These include: * Product Marketing Manager: [LINK] * Frontend Engineer: [LINK] * And more roles in Engineering, Operations, and Marketing: [LINK]"},{"id":28719944,"author":"nrp","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (October 2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop","text":"Framework | Product Marketing Manager, Frontend Engineer | Full-time | Headquartered in Burlingame, CA, but REMOTE ok | [LINK] We're building people and planet-friendly consumer electronics products that are designed to be upgradeable, customizable, and repairable. Along with that, we're creating an e-commerce marketplace for the ecosystems of products and parts we're enabling. Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop, a thin and light notebook that is designed to last (see recent coverage at Ars Technica [ [LINK] ] and Linus Tech Tips [ [VIDEO] ]). Through the end of 2021, we're giving away a free Framework Laptop (i5-1135G7) to anyone who refers a candidate we end up hiring for one of our open positions! These include: * Product Marketing Manager: [LINK] * Frontend Engineer: [LINK] * And more roles in Engineering, Operations, and Marketing: [LINK]"},{"id":28806623,"author":"rssoconnor","month":"2021-10","story":"Ask HN: What are you building this weekend?","stance":"reference","why":"Excited reviewer of the Framework laptop","quote":"And then this guy also seems pretty excited","text":"My personal laptop has been an Acer-C710 Chromebook for some time now. I bought it because it was inexpensive, upgradable, clearly could run linux, had an Intel (specifically non-ARM) CPU which meant that I could use nixos's binary cache for programs (non-ARM is probably somewhat less problematic today than it was in 2013). I went through several layers of rooting my Chromebook, eventually replacing the BIOS. I upgraded the RAM and (this year) replaced the HD with an SSD. But my laptop is getting old. The bezel is cracked and the screen cable seems ... ungrounded? Black isn't quite black. Still, I have been hesitant to upgrade to modern, non-upgradable laptops. But a few months ago Graham Christensen posted about running nixos on the frame.work < [LINK] >. And then this guy also seems pretty excited < [VIDEO] >. Unlike the Acer Chromebook, the frame.work laptop is not cheap. However, I'm no longer a post-doc and I am in a fortunate position that I can afford to splurge a little. So, ignoring the sunken cost of my recent SDD purchase, I decided to go ahead an place an order back at the beginning of August. The cheaper processor I ordered was shipping in October, and here we are in October."},{"id":28978287,"author":"nomdep","month":"2021-10","story":"OK Lenovo, we need to talk","stance":"reference","why":"Shows QR code stickers on internal components","quote":"Every internal component has a QR code sticker on it pointing to a web page with the specs, replacements, etc.","text":"You will be very happy with this new laptop then: [LINK] Every internal component has a QR code sticker on it pointing to a web page with the specs, replacements, etc. See: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28381122,"author":"nrp","month":"2021-09","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (September 2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop","text":"Framework | Growth Manager, Full Stack Engineer | Full-time | Headquartered in Burlingame, CA, but REMOTE ok | [LINK] We're building people and planet-friendly consumer electronics products that are designed to be upgradeable, customizable, and repairable by the end user. Along with that, we're creating an e-commerce marketplace to monetize the ecosystems of products and parts we're enabling. Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop, a thin and light notebook that is designed to last (see recent coverage at Ars Technica [ [LINK] ] and Linus Tech Tips [ [VIDEO] ]). Our team is made up of folks from previous successful consumer electronics startups, including the founding team of Oculus. We're early stage, but funded to deliver on our mission. We're hiring across a range of roles both around hardware development and on creation of our marketplace for repairable products and parts, including: * Growth Manager: [LINK] * Senior Full Stack Engineer: [LINK] * And more roles in Engineering, Operations, and Marketing: [LINK]"},{"id":28037788,"author":"nrp","month":"2021-08","story":"Ask HN: Who is hiring? (August 2021)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop","text":"Framework | Growth Manager, Full Stack Engineer | Full-time | Headquartered in Burlingame, CA, but REMOTE ok | [LINK] We're building people and planet-friendly consumer electronics products that are designed to be upgradeable, customizable, and repairable by the end user. Along with that, we're creating an e-commerce marketplace to monetize the ecosystems of products and parts we're enabling. Our first product is the recently-launched Framework Laptop, a thin and light notebook that is designed to last (see recent coverage at Ars Technica [ [LINK] ] and Linus Tech Tips [ [VIDEO] ]). Our team is made up of folks from previous successful consumer electronics startups, including the founding team of Oculus. We're early stage, but funded to deliver on our mission. We're hiring across a range of roles both around hardware development and on creation of our marketplace for repairable products and parts, including: * Growth Manager: [LINK] * Senior Full Stack Engineer: [LINK]"},{"id":27983572,"author":"tpxl","month":"2021-07","story":"I've got a broken combine harvester – manufacturer won't give me software key","stance":"reference","why":"Shows thin serviceable modular laptop design","quote":"Frame.work can apparently produce easily serviceable, rather thin designs of laptops that are even modular","text":"> gluing everything together enables more compact, durable designs Maybe a tiiiiiiiny bit more compact, but definitely not more durable. Frame.work can apparently produce easily serviceable, rather thin designs of laptops that are even modular [VIDEO] This is way thinner than my 2008 era HP and thinner than my 2021 era Lenovo. As far as phones go, the PCBs are a tiny part of the phone (The cpu, ram, storage combined are less than 1/2 of the phone surface area and probably less than 1/4 of the volume) so gluing them together doesn't even get you much. You might get a _small_ bit by soldering in the battery in the sense that you then don't need a \"box\" for the removable battery, but that is a small amount of space compared to the battery itself."}],"r":[["5RKbSi87J5Q",1]]},"GCMXXXmxG-I":{"wy":"Recommended as an accessible technical explanation of Mobileye's approach, and also cited in disputes over Tesla Autopilot.","q":{"x":"Here is a great, very accessible video where the CTO of MobileEye","i":11664689,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":11664689,"author":"brianchu","month":"2016-05","story":"End to End Learning for Self-Driving Cars [pdf]","stance":"strong_rec","why":"Mobileye CTO explains why decomposing the driving problem beats end-to-end learning.","quote":"Here is a great, very accessible video where the CTO of MobileEye","text":"Here is a great, very accessible video where the CTO of MobileEye (which provides some of the components for Tesla's \"autopilot\") explains his views on the challenges of end-to-end learning for autonomous vehicles, and why it's preferable to decompose the problem instead (still using deep learning for the decomposed modules). [VIDEO] I'm inclined to agree, especially because it helps in 1) providing diagnostic information (such as the great driving visualizations shown in the video), and 2) makes it easier to incorporate algorithms and sensors (like with Google's cars) as a redundancy in case the neural network hits a crazy edge case."},{"id":12791173,"author":"Animats","month":"2016-10","story":"All Tesla Cars Being Produced Now Have Full Self-Driving Hardware","stance":"reference","why":"Mobileye CTO details a neural network that recognizes only cars.","quote":"Here's Mobileeye's CTO talking in detail about their technology.[1]","text":"Those are good points. Here's some backup. Here's Mobileeye's CTO talking in detail about their technology.[1] They use a a deep neural network that recognizes cars. Just cars. Look at this point in the video.[2] There's a stationary obstacle on the left, and it doesn't recognize that. Maybe this is why Tesla, using Mobileye hardware, plowed into three different stationary vehicles at the left of a highway. They were all trucks (one was a street sweeper), and seen from the back while stationary, may not have matched the trained model of \"car\". Mobileeye's algorithms are good at recognizing moving cars from most angles, and that's valuable for feeding into the model of \"what's the other player doing\". But it's not enough for the \"where are all the obstacles\" and \"where is the road surface\" questions. Those questions have objective answers, which can be answered with a LIDAR, and maybe millimeter radar if it has the angular resolution. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":12535719,"author":"zeroecco","month":"2016-09","story":"Is Tesla telling us the truth over autopilot spat?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"ME boasting about their tech making hands free possible","text":"ugh, more ME noise. so ME has a lot of evidence that they are lying through their teeth, if they were so worried why let all the other brands do the hands free thing? [LINK] * ME boasting about their tech making hands free possible [VIDEO]"},{"id":12536388,"author":"TheShadowRunner","month":"2016-09","story":"Is Tesla telling us the truth over autopilot spat?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"/r/TeslaMotors dug up this video[1] of MobilEye CEO talking about the Autopilot","text":"/r/TeslaMotors dug up this video[1] of MobilEye CEO talking about the Autopilot, so if they really disagreed with the usage of Autopilot, shouldn't they have brought that up prior, and not included it in promotional material. From all indications on their end, I believe that they said that the Tesla Autopilot shouldn't be driven hands-free. I have no qualms with that, but why then use the Autopilot as a promotional feature for their products? Not trying to fanboy with Tesla here, as I do think that MobilEye was right in pulling their products away from Tesla. [1]: [VIDEO]"}]},"1kQUXpZpLXI":{"wy":"HN cites it as visual evidence that nm node names are marketing labels and for its neat electron microscope comparison.","q":{"x":"Here's a neat video where they use an electron microscope to actually compare the transistor sizes for Intel 14nm and AMD 7nm","i":26718431,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":26718431,"author":"datagram","month":"2021-04","story":"Intel 3rd gen Xeon Scalable (Ice Lake): generationally big, competitively small","stance":"rec","why":"Electron microscope comparison of Intel 14nm and AMD 7nm transistor sizes.","quote":"Here's a neat video where they use an electron microscope to actually compare the transistor sizes for Intel 14nm and AMD 7nm","text":"Like others have said, the numbers aren't comparable between manufacturers. Here's a neat video where they use an electron microscope to actually compare the transistor sizes for Intel 14nm and AMD 7nm: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26550413,"author":"ece","month":"2021-03","story":"Pulling Bits from ROM Silicon Die Images: Unknown Architecture","stance":"reference","why":"Scanning electron microscope views of chip structures.","quote":"This reminds me of seeing a part of the chip from a scanning electron microscope","text":"This reminds me of seeing a part of the chip from a scanning electron microscope: [VIDEO] But I wonder how successful these methods are are extracting keys from a TPM or other hardware keys."},{"id":24620712,"author":"quag","month":"2020-09","story":"TSMC to Mass Produce Breakthrough 2nm Mbcfet Transistors in 2024","stance":"reference","why":"Electron microscope shows nm node names are marketing, not real measurements.","quote":"the “7nm” or “10nm” number does not measure anything, and is effectively a process version number for marketing","text":"According to this video using an electron scanning microscope on amd and Intel chips, the “7nm” or “10nm” number does not measure anything, and is effectively a process version number for marketing. From memory there is no single number for measuring transistors or efficiency, transistor density is useful, but it doesn’t tell you the performance or power usage. [VIDEO]"},{"id":24623809,"author":"rasz","month":"2020-09","story":"TSMC to Mass Produce Breakthrough 2nm Mbcfet Transistors in 2024","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"der8auer \"14nm and 7nm are NOT what you think it is - Visiting Tescan\" [VIDEO] for reference \"TESCAN is a leading global producer and supplier of scanning electron microscopes, focused ion beam scanning electron microscopes and micro-CT solutions.\""}]},"5v1Yg6XejyE":{"wy":"Cited as a source on oil industry lobbying and disinformation in climate policy threads.","q":{"x":"I found these to be a fascinating glimpse behind the scene of","i":29020614,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":29020614,"author":"ivan_ah","month":"2021-10","story":"Decades of disinformation by fossil fuels halted US climate policy","stance":"rec","why":"Hidden-camera footage of ExxonMobil lobbyists.","quote":"I found these to be a fascinating glimpse behind the scene of","text":"Here are direct links to the \"caught on camera\" videos with ExxonMobil lobbyists: [LINK] [10 mins] [VIDEO] [9 mins] I found these to be a fascinating glimpse behind the scene of \"political debate\" about environmental issues..."},{"id":44545676,"author":"sensanaty","month":"2025-07","story":"Bypassing Google's big anti-adblock update","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as example of oil industry deceptive campaigns.","quote":"","text":"Advertisements have been proven countless times to be a form of psychological manipulation, and a very potent one that works very well. After all, if it didn't work we wouldn't be seeing ads crop up literally every-fucking-where, including these days even in our very own night sky in the form of drone lightshows. The ad companies have huge teams of mental health experts in order to maximize the reach & impact of their advertisements on the general populace. Ads are so powerful that they've even managed to twist the truth about plenty of horrific shit happening to the point of affecting the health and safety of real people, sometimes literally on a global scale. Chiquita bananas, De Beers, Nestle, Oil & Gas companies, and must I remind you of Tobacco companies (and surprise surprise, the same people who were doing the ads for Big Tobacco are the ones doing ad campaigns for O&G companies now)? There have been SO MANY examples from all these companies of using advertisements to trick and manipulate people & politicians, oftentimes just straight up lying , like the Tobacco companies lying about the adverse health effects despite knowing for decades what the adverse health effects were , Or Oil & Gas companies lying about climate change via comprehensive astroturfing & advertisement campaigns [1]. This all barely scratches the surface, too, especially these days where you have platforms like Google and Meta enabling genocides, mass political interference and pushing things like cr"},{"id":31157617,"author":"camgunz","month":"2022-04","story":"No one expects young men to do anything and they are responding by doing nothing","stance":"reference","why":"Tangential link in a long political argument.","quote":"","text":"I wrote a lot, which believe it or not I try not to do, so to summarize I'll just say this: your argument, essentially, is that given a stat like \"Black Americans represent 12.7% of Americans, but make up between 30-40% of police killings\", the people causing the problem are the ones saying it's unacceptable, not those responsible for a system that kills ~3x more Black Americans than White Americans. I find that to be absurd. --- > identity has become a hugely popular issue on the American left in the last decade I know we're sparring on this in another thread, but it really gets to me when people dismiss the concerns of Black Americans, LGBTQ Americans, Women, Native Americans, etc. as \"identity\" issues. It's undeniable that people in these groups have an entirely different, objectively worse experience than straight cis White men. My arguments here are slavery, forced incarceration and sterilization, and genocide, to say nothing of ongoing discrimination and prejudice and institutional problems today. This country hasn't been dealing with \"identity\" for a decade, it's been dealing with it from before the time its 1st president was a slaver. The idea that this has only been a problem roughly since Obama was president is... the nicest thing I can say here is naive. > Moreover, many people (myself included) think that this identity politics stuff is tearing at our social fabric, not only by fostering racial divides, but also because Americans of all stripes are losing trust in"},{"id":30369445,"author":"DevKoala","month":"2022-02","story":"Biden his time: how the US president is failing on the climate crisis","stance":"reference","why":"Exec caught describing congress members in their pockets.","quote":"they caught the Exxon-Mobile exec talking about all the dem congress members they had in their pockets","text":"Honestly, after that report in which they caught the Exxon-Mobile exec talking about all the dem congress members they had in their pockets, I knew nothing would get done. [VIDEO]"},{"id":29553357,"author":"camgunz","month":"2021-12","story":"Three loud climate warning signals","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Exxon supporting cap and trade knowing it is non-viable.","quote":"Exxon infamously supports cap and trade because they know it's politically non-viable [1].","text":"They actively corrupt democratic governments and disseminate misinformation in order to keep making money by destroying the planet. Exxon infamously supports cap and trade because they know it's politically non-viable [1]. Confident no consumer is asking them to do this. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28466407,"author":"neom","month":"2021-09","story":"Absorption of 5G Radiation in Brain Tissue","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as a source on ExxonMobil lobbying.","quote":"","text":"Revealed: ExxonMobil’s lobbying war on climate change legislation - [VIDEO] How Lobbying Became A $3.5 Billion Industry - [LINK]"},{"id":27755827,"author":"DevKoala","month":"2021-07","story":"Audacity fork creator steps down. Claims his life is in danger","stance":"reference","why":"Exposes ExxonMobil lobbying against climate legislation.","quote":"this YouTube video exposing the lobbying practices of Exxon Mobile against climate change fighting measures","text":"What I am saying is that I have seen a lot of evidence for organized/professional sentiment manipulation in 4chan, and there are tons of collected threads that describe how the organizations that are paid to post in 4chan work. I assume it is a professional service being sold. For example, every time I tried to make threads around this YouTube video exposing the lobbying practices of Exxon Mobile against climate change fighting measures, my thread would get derailed into racist stuff that had nothing to do with the video and would get the thread eventually locked. [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["PFDu9oVAE-g",1],["tX4H_ctggYo",1],["t4vKPhjcMZg",1],["eGKFTUuJppU",1],["GL77oOnrPzY",1]]},"Ea6tw-gulnQ":{"wy":"HN links it whenever someone equates CRT phosphors with pixels, as the clear explanation of why they are not.","q":{"x":"Definitely worth the watch.","i":34990408,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34990408,"author":"ChuckNorris89","month":"2023-03","story":"Raster CRT Typography (According to DEC)","stance":"rec","why":"Explains how CRT pixels differ from pixels as we understand them","quote":"Definitely worth the watch.","text":"Obligatory Technology Connections video explaining how CRT pixels aren't really pixels as we understand them. Definitely worth the watch. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35079031,"author":"comex","month":"2023-03","story":"A Pixel Is Not a Little Square (1995) [pdf]","stance":"rec","why":"Explains CRT red/green/blue triads and why they aren't pixels","quote":"","text":"If you’re wondering what it’s talking about when it says the triads of red/green/blue rectangles on a CRT display don’t correspond to pixels, here’s a Technology Connections video that explains it: [VIDEO] But on an LCD, the display really is made up of a bunch of solid-colored rectangle-ish shapes, and if the LCD is using standard RGB pattern, each red/green/blue triplet does correspond to one pixel. So if you zoom in, “a pixel is a little square” is very close to the truth. In other words, the article shows its age…"},{"id":24660051,"author":"morsch","month":"2020-10","story":"3270font: A Font for the Nostalgic","stance":"reference","why":"Shadow mask is fixed and independent of resolution","quote":"The electron gun draws through the shadow mask, which is fixed and independent of resolution.","text":"The electron gun draws through the shadow mask, which is fixed and independent of resolution. It's a debate long enjoyed by nitpickers. [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":23523964,"author":"causality0","month":"2020-06","story":"Accurate N64 emulation gets resolution upscaling powered by Vulkan (RetroArch)","stance":"reference","why":"Explains why CRT phosphors are not pixels","quote":"CRT phosphors are not pixels. For more on that","text":"\"240p\" is not a meaningful statement when referring to CRTs. CRT phosphors are not pixels. For more on that: [VIDEO]"},{"id":17197750,"author":"pdkl95","month":"2018-05","story":"Logo design trends of 2018","stance":"reference","why":"CRT shadow mask phosphors don't align one-to-one with pixels","quote":"a shadow mask means phosphors do not align 1-to-1 with pixels[2]","text":"> The traditional excuse for stripping away ornament has been about improving legibility, especially for small screens. This is a fallacy No, serifs really do have legibility issues, but not for small screens ; they cause problems at (very) small font sizes where glyphs are only a handful of pixels tall. > You can render a serif letter in seven pixels Yes, but that's pushing it and the legibility will depend on the quality of the font hinting, how the font rasterizer antialiases, and the way subpixels are utilized[1]. Legibility will even depend on the display technology: low resolution fonts on a CRT can be a lot harder to read compared to an LCD. (a shadow mask means phosphors do not align 1-to-1 with pixels[2]) Of course, legibility is in the eye of the beholder, and many people find serifed fonts to be easier to read, while others prefer sans-serif, and some people just don't care. For some people, anything smaller or more complicated than high-contrast 96pt simple sans-serif is unreadable. > drops the serifs from its logo A logo, however, is probably (much?) larger than the small font sizes where serifs often have legibility issues. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"}]},"MYGJ9jrbpvg":{"wy":"HN links it in Strandbeest threads as a showcase of the eerily smooth, wind-driven walking creatures.","q":{"x":"eerily smooth, seemingly impossible walking exoskeletons","i":31945547,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":31945547,"author":"papandada","month":"2022-07","story":"Strandbeest: Kinetic PVC Beach Animals","stance":"rec","why":"Eerily smooth walking wind-powered creatures that look like visual effects","quote":"eerily smooth, seemingly impossible walking exoskeletons","text":"I'd been watching a lot of visual effects content on YouTube and assumed that's what this was when it popped up on my feed, initially -- eerily smooth, seemingly impossible walking exoskeletons : [VIDEO] Wikipedia page on a linkage the inventor uses for the legs: [LINK]"},{"id":20094066,"author":"Doxin","month":"2019-06","story":"Paper Strandbeest Is Strong Enough to Walk","stance":"reference","why":"Theo Jansen's wind-powered cardboard walking creature","quote":"Theo Jansen himself created a wind-powered one out of cardboard","text":"I'm sure it would be possible. Theo Jansen himself created a wind-powered one out of cardboard: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11835727,"author":"YeGoblynQueenne","month":"2016-06","story":"Can computers become conscious?: My reply to Roger Penrose","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">> “give me a big enough computer and the relevant initial conditions, and I’ll simulate the brain atom-by-atom.” Nuh-huh. You can't keep blaming it all on the hardware. Every year there's bigger and bigger computers and more and more people who claim they'll do better next year, when there's bigger computers. Well, it just ain't gonna happen. Fascinating intelligence that eats our most powerful artificial one for breakfast is all around us, and it runs on as little as five ganglia. Look at insects. They don't even have brains. They may not know calculus, they may be completely incapable of even acquiring the concept of a mathematical operation, but they are autonomous and viable in a harsh, hostile world that the most powerful machines we've built are completely incapable of inhabiting. If recreating insect-level intelligence artificially was just a matter of computing power, we 'd have done it already: we got more than enough of it. Five ganglia? My calculator is more powerful than that! But we don't have a single machine that can do what the lowliest fruit fly can do: survive on its own, and thrive, in the real world [1]. So- why is human-level intelligence just a matter of more computing power, but insect-level one is not? Is there some kind of secret ingredient? Is there some kind of magic involved? Will some sort of -gasp- singularity occur when the number of gates on a processor reaches a critical mass? Will the machine suddenly acquire a S.O.U.L. [2] when we stuff eno"}]},"Mh5LY4Mz15o":{"wy":"HN mentions it as an entertaining quick history, and as a stylistic reference or joke in threads about history and the creator's other videos.","q":{"x":"He also did a similar video for the history of Japan [0], quite entertaining.","i":14504203,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14504203,"author":"klez","month":"2017-06","story":"History of the entire world, i guess [video]","stance":"rec","why":"Quick, entertaining history of Japan","quote":"He also did a similar video for the history of Japan [0], quite entertaining.","text":"Those jingles are funny the first few times, but they get annoying pretty fast. Anyway, happy to see this linked here. He also did a similar video for the history of Japan [0], quite entertaining. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":11109273,"author":"po","month":"2016-02","story":"Bot-controlled piano emulating spoken voice (2009) [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Same creator's History of Japan video, with harmonized spoken-voice music","quote":"you can hear him using that technique of harmonizing with his own spoken voice in the background music of that video.","text":"Not mechanical, so much lower resolution... but you might have recently seen the History of Japan video being linked around... the guy who did that, Bill Wurtz also did this: [LINK] There's an entire subreddit of this stuff as I guess it's really good practice for playing by ear: [LINK] It includes this awesome Palin one: [LINK] P.S. In case you missed it this is the History of Japan one again: [VIDEO] If you watch it again, you can hear him using that technique of harmonizing with his own spoken voice in the background music of that video."},{"id":44026304,"author":"umvi","month":"2025-05","story":"“There are people who can see and others who cannot even look”","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Reminds me of","text":"> and to slow the predatory opening of Japan that had begun with American warships’ arrival in 1854. Reminds me of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15790311,"author":"SimbaOnSteroids","month":"2017-11","story":"American Equity","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Inspired by this, both historically and in delivery","text":"There is an option for enforcement you're not realizing here, called the Commodore Mathew Perry method, it goes like this. --Location: Tax Havens-- > Knock Knock Its the United States With huge boats, with guns, gunboats. >Open your banks' records, stop having them be closed and theres not much they can do about. So they sign a treaty making sure their banks' records are not closed. -------------------------------------------------------------- Inspired by this, both historically and in delivery [VIDEO]"},{"id":12952258,"author":"_nedR","month":"2016-11","story":"China, Japan, Russia, and South Korea Plan Renewable Energy Super Grid","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The only reason i know these countries have a history is from watching this video","text":"The only reason i know these countries have a history is from watching this video a couple day back( [VIDEO] ). It is strange but i never really thought of Russia as neighbours with China or Korea with a shared history."}],"r":[["jVoSQP2yUYA",1]]},"NUqytjlHNIM":{"wy":"HN uses the scene to argue for due process and for protecting the speech and rights of people you dislike.","q":{"x":"There's a brief excerpt of the film on Youtube that resonates particularly well today","i":34380434,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":34380434,"author":"pseudolus","month":"2023-01","story":"How Police Exploited the Capitol Riot’s Digital Records","stance":"rec","why":"Brief film excerpt on defending legal process even for the wicked.","quote":"There's a brief excerpt of the film on Youtube that resonates particularly well today","text":"If violating or diminishing constitutional rights for the purpose of obtaining a prosecution is on the table then there's not really much to the constitutional order to begin with. Robert Bolt, one of the great playwrights of the 20th century, recognized this in \"A Man for All Seasons\". There's a brief excerpt of the film on Youtube that resonates particularly well today: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41729641,"author":"llm_trw","month":"2024-10","story":"EU: Definition of \"potential terrorists\" opens door to broad information-sharing","stance":"reference","why":"Scene used to argue for protecting speech of those you dislike.","quote":"Or to use much better words than mine","text":"To quote some of the people I know who would be impacted the most by this: > Freedom of speech is not freedom from consequences. If they'd not spend the last 10 years purging the old guard who they called free speech fanatics they'd have someone in their corner. Now I just have the world tiniest violin for them. Or to use much better words than mine: [VIDEO]"},{"id":18327443,"author":"someguydave","month":"2018-10","story":"Gab.com Booted from Hosting Provider Due to User Account","stance":"reference","why":"Scene on giving even the worst the benefit of law.","quote":"Consider this video","text":"1. Isn’t viewing someone as “beyond the pale” such that they deserve summary punishment and/or execution dehumanizing? How is that different than the guy who tortured small animals because he views them as objects and not living things? 2. Even those libertarians would give murderers and rapists benefit of a court of law where they can explain themselves. Consider this video: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16428508,"author":"tankenmate","month":"2018-02","story":"Google AMP Is a Threat to the Open Web","stance":"reference","why":"Scene where More defends giving the Devil benefit of law.","quote":"Yes, I'd give the Devil benefit of law, for my own safety's sake.","text":"If nobody cares about the businesses at the other end then one day you'll wake up to find that the only business left on the other side is Google, and then where will you turn? Roper: So now you'd give the Devil benefit of law! More: Yes. What would you do? Cut a great road through the law to get after the Devil? Roper: I'd cut down every law in England to do that! More: Oh? And when the last law was down, and the Devil turned round on you — where would you hide, Roper, the laws all being flat? This country's planted thick with laws from coast to coast — man's laws, not God's — and if you cut them down — and you're just the man to do it — d'you really think you could stand upright in the winds that would blow then? Yes, I'd give the Devil benefit of law, for my own safety's sake.[0] [0] [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["48sCx-wBs34",1],["rAZavOcEnLg",1]]},"P8pjd1QEA0c":{"wy":"HN mostly references it in passing when student loans and predatory colleges come up, with one commenter recommending the veterans benefits section.","q":{"x":"Watch that to the end (or at least the end of the veteran benefits bit)","i":9675563,"y":"2015"},"m":[{"id":9675563,"author":"Sir_Substance","month":"2015-06","story":"Why I Defaulted on My Student Loans","stance":"rec","why":"Told to watch to the end on veteran benefits.","quote":"Watch that to the end (or at least the end of the veteran benefits bit)","text":"Assuming you're serious and not just a shill: [VIDEO] Watch that to the end (or at least the end of the veteran benefits bit), and then get back to us."},{"id":9101320,"author":"TeMPOraL","month":"2015-02","story":"Students refuse to pay back loans to failed for-profit college","stance":"reference","why":"Oliver criticized the same college.","quote":"Isn't this the same college John Oliver criticized recently","text":"Isn't this the same college John Oliver criticized recently for their \"quality education\"? [VIDEO] \"We went to the museum of Scientology for our psychiatric rotation\"..."},{"id":15579839,"author":"mejin","month":"2017-10","story":"FCC Moves to End TV-Newspaper Ownership Ban","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Student debt is bad because they will sue you in court. : Well of course they will.","text":"I've been a bit busy but here it is. (BTW: is there a way to get HN to notify you when someone comments?) I will be talking about [VIDEO] Student Debt. 1:11 Student debt is bad because they will sue you in court. : Well of course they will. Its not like they can take away your car if you stop paying your debt. 3:30 Why did student loans increase so much? States slashed funding for higher institutions causing colleges to increase tuition. : Or maybe because more people are eligible for student loans. Having the government back student loans is linked to the increase in tuition according to this: [LINK] 5:16 Students at for profit schools account for 31% of student loans having just 13% of the students. : Does anyone expect a private company meant for people with few options to be cheeper than ivy league schools that offer on average $40,000 of aid per student? I don't have sources but I would imagine that the 13% most expensive car sales would account for around 31% of car sales. 9:00 Looks at one specific program at itt and says that it has an 8% employment rate and 25% didn't graduate: There is / was (I don't know if it is over yet) a lawsuit against itt and the plaintiff claims that the employment rate was around 50% [LINK] . Also, what do you expect from a college that excepts anyone who can pay? If they graduated everyone than their credibility would be worse than it already is. Any place that accepts anyone will have a low success rate (eg. Hollywood and start ups) And t"}],"r":[["4AC6RSau7r8",1]]},"a3ww0gwEszo":{"wy":"HN shares it as a fun, memorable way to explain the longest path problem and as an entry in lists of programming and math songs.","q":{"x":"you can listen to a Billy Joel parody that describes the problem in decidedly less academic terms","i":48047613,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":27923174,"author":"wpasc","month":"2021-07","story":"Akamai Edge DNS was down","stance":"rec","why":"A comedic song about the longest path problem","quote":"which you may get a kick out of","text":"Haha please do, a video would be great. Your song reminded me of the song: \"Find the Longest Path\" which you may get a kick out of: [VIDEO]"},{"id":48047613,"author":"saghm","month":"2026-05","story":"SingleRide: Longest route on NYC Subway without visiting the same station twice","stance":"reference","why":"Billy Joel parody explaining the longest path problem informally","quote":"you can listen to a Billy Joel parody that describes the problem in decidedly less academic terms","text":"Finding the longest path in a graph is itself a pretty well-described NP-complete problem: [LINK] Alternately, you can listen to a Billy Joel parody that describes the problem in decidedly less academic terms: [VIDEO]"},{"id":29200968,"author":"seniorsassycat","month":"2021-11","story":"White Collar Holler – Stan Rogers","stance":"reference","why":"A song about the longest path problem","quote":"Some other programming and math songs","text":"Stan Rogers has a knack for writing songs that sound older than himself, this song shows he doesn't need nautical themes to do it. Some other programming and math songs Kill -9 [LINK] Find the longest path [VIDEO]"}]},"bS5P_LAqiVg":{"wy":"Cited as a parody that crams in 80s movie tropes and as an example of the VHS aesthetic.","q":{"x":"They fit an impressive amount of 80's movie tropes into 30 minutes.","i":16077667,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":16077667,"author":"vidarh","month":"2018-01","story":"Xerox Alto zero-day: cracking disk password protection on a 45 year old system","stance":"rec","why":"Parody packing many 80s movie tropes into 30 minutes","quote":"They fit an impressive amount of 80's movie tropes into 30 minutes.","text":"Fits right into Kung Fury [1] - a parody/homage of 80's movies that includes time travel and Hackerman... And a dinosaur, vikings and nazis and David Hasselhoff. It's as bad as it sounds, but in a good way. They fit an impressive amount of 80's movie tropes into 30 minutes. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13390565,"author":"ZenoArrow","month":"2017-01","story":"Music's weird cassette-tape revival is paying off","stance":"reference","why":"Example of a badly-tracked VHS aesthetic","quote":"Kung Fury is probably a good example of that style","text":">\"Indeed - this is especially true of synthwave/vaporwave and other genres that are themselves nostalgic for the days of synth-heavy music sold on cassette.\" Yeah, I wouldn't mind a TRONICBOX cassette: [LINK] >\"Just as some games put fake film grain on screen, some synthwave videos add a \"badly tracked VHS\" effect.\" Kung Fury is probably a good example of that style: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9626774,"author":"stefantalpalaru","month":"2015-05","story":"Kung Fury – a comedy funded through Kickstarter [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> Of course you can download it from Youtube using something like youtube-dl, but it's not as straightforward. You can get the 1080p version with \"youtube-dl --write-sub [VIDEO] . Yes, you want the subs for the funny music descriptions."},{"id":9626883,"author":"shmerl","month":"2015-05","story":"Kung Fury – a comedy funded through Kickstarter [video]","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I used this method: youtube-dl -f 248+251 [VIDEO] You get VP9+Opus combination which takes less space."},{"id":32846661,"author":"spking","month":"2022-09","story":"Head-On Crash: 2009 Chevrolet Malibu vs. 1959 Bel Air (2009)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"I agree, and I think that's a big part of why so many people got excited for the Cybertruck, as an attack on aesthetic blandness. 80s-era movie and TV vehicles were a helluva lot of fun too: A-Team van, Ninja Turtles party wagon, K.I.T.T., Magnum P.I.'s Ferrari 308. And since you mentioned the Countach: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20501803,"author":"bogidon","month":"2019-07","story":"Microsoft is investing $1B in OpenAI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"AGI enthusiasts sometimes remind me of this","text":"True. I used “consciousness” haphazardly. Not super related, but AGI enthusiasts sometimes remind me of this: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["enHF9lMseP8",1],["Ne1ApyqSvm0",1]]},"fkUwXenBokU":{"wy":"Recommended as a period piece whose worries about rapid change still feel relevant to current tech anxieties.","q":{"x":"well worth adding to the list of documentaries to watch. Much of what Toffler/Welles touch is very relevant now.","i":14721138,"y":"2017"},"m":[{"id":14721138,"author":"uxhacker","month":"2017-07","story":"All Watched Over by Machines of Loving Grace (2011)","stance":"rec","why":"Toffler's 1972 documentary still relevant today.","quote":"well worth adding to the list of documentaries to watch. Much of what Toffler/Welles touch is very relevant now.","text":"Future Shock by Alvin Toffler in 1972 narrated by Orson Welles is well worth adding to the list of documentaries to watch. Much of what Toffler/Welles touch is very relevant now. [VIDEO]"},{"id":48874990,"author":"ben_w","month":"2026-07","story":"AI 2040 and the Cult of Intelligence","stance":"reference","why":"Example of recurring fears about pace of change.","quote":"","text":"As someone who considers themselves in the \"safetyism contingent\", the only thing I find myself agreeing with about the rollout of LLMs has been the argument that it has to be shown to and used by the public while under development. Every specific prediction not only sounds like science fiction before it arrives, it tautologically is science fiction because such predictions are narratives about something science-y that has yet to exist: [LINK] Even when we accurately predict something in the future, we often have no clue what the implications are for society until it is in our hands: [LINK] Go too fast, suddenly drop in tech with too many novel implications all at the same time, and we likely hit something relevant without having the means to cope with all the change. However, \"too many\" and \"likely\" are weasel words with no predictive power about what the danger threshold might be. I may be entirely wrong to think there is one, or we may have been bouncing off it since the industrial revolution, or I may just be echoing the same thing everyone feels once reaching their 40s: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23396362,"author":"fortran77","month":"2020-06","story":"Interracial marriage in US became legal only in 1967","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes -- in my lifetime. Amazing to think about. (I was born in '62). And here I am--in 2020 and I'm married to a same-sex partner. This was shocking when I was growing up. In the North where I was you'd see interracial couples but it was very, very rare. I remember seeing a movie when I was 10 or so called Future Shock (in 1972) that had a scene with a same-sex wedding and the audience gasped! (It was the second feature at the Uniondale Mini Cinema.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":20341915,"author":"cpeterso","month":"2019-07","story":"Lee Iacocca Has Died","stance":"reference","why":"40-minute Orson Welles film version of Future Shock.","quote":"Orson Welles narrated a 40-minute 1972 film version of Future Shock. It's available on YouTube","text":"> My wife and I identify thrift stores, in part, by the number of copies of \"Iacocca\" (his autobiography) that are on the shelf. Another thrift store metric is the number of copies of \"Jerry Maguire\" VHS tapes. A video performance group called Everything Is Terrible has collected over 15,000 Jerry Maguire tapes. They've hosted pop-up video stores that only rent Jerry Maguire. Their new project is raising $500,000 to build a Jerry Maguire pyramid in the desert. [LINK] > (I tried reading \"Future Shock\", our other perennial thrift store classic, and couldn't make it thru.) Orson Welles narrated a 40-minute 1972 film version of Future Shock. It's available on YouTube: [VIDEO]"},{"id":15732724,"author":"slededit","month":"2017-11","story":"Startup Ideas","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Its a compelling argument on its face, but its been falsified over much of history.","text":"> I think parents themselves are not adult enough to deal with Facebook and avoid being manipulated or cheated. I think the world hasn't caught up with addictiveness of social media yet. I hear this criticism a lot and to me it strikes a similar tone as the 1972 documentary Future Shock. Effectively that the pace of change is too rapid and humans cannot adjust. Its a compelling argument on its face, but its been falsified over much of history. [VIDEO]"}]},"hV41QEKiMlM":{"wy":"Cited in modeling and machine learning threads as the classic story on overfitting and justifying parameters.","q":{"x":"you can watch Freeman Dyson recount this conversation in his own words in an interview","i":40964328,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":40961306,"author":"EdwardCoffin","month":"2024-07","story":"Fitting an elephant with four non-zero parameters","stance":"rec","why":"Dyson tells the episode behind fitting an elephant","quote":"Freeman Dyson recounts the episode [1] that inspired this paper in his web of life interviews","text":"Freeman Dyson recounts the episode [1] that inspired this paper in his web of life interviews (prepositioned to the fitting an elephant bit) [2] [1] [VIDEO] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":40964328,"author":"Calavar","month":"2024-07","story":"Fitting an elephant with four non-zero parameters","stance":"rec","why":"Dyson recounts Fermi's four parameters conversation himself","quote":"you can watch Freeman Dyson recount this conversation in his own words in an interview","text":"> > In 1953, Enrico Fermi criticized Dyson’s model by quoting Johnny von Neumann: “With four parameters I can fit an elephant, and with five I can make him wiggle his trunk.” For those who are interested, you can watch Freeman Dyson recount this conversation in his own words in an interview: [VIDEO]"},{"id":45883358,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-11","story":"Spatial intelligence is AI’s next frontier","stance":"reference","why":"Footnote supporting claim that data alone is insufficient without a physicist's model.","quote":"I also think it's naïve to think this can be done through data alone.","text":"I think a lot of people are really bad at evaluating world models. Feifei is right here that they are multimodal but really they must codify a physics. I don't mean \"physics\" but \"a physics\". I also think it's naïve to think this can be done through data alone. I mean just ask a physicist...[0]. But why people are really bad at evaluating them is because the details dominate. What matters here is consistency. We need invariance to some things and equivariance to others. As evaluators we tend to be hopeful so the subtle changes frame to frame are overlooked though thats kinda the most important part. It can't just be similar to the last frame, but needs be exactly the same. You need equivariance to translation, yet that's still not happening in any of these models (and it's not a limitation of attention or transformers). You're just going to have a really hard time getting all this data even though by doing that you'll look like you're progressing because you're better fitting it. But in the end the models will need to create some compact formulation representing concepts such as motion. Or in other words, a physics. And it's not like physicists aren't know for being detail oriented and nitpicky over nuances. That is breed into then with good reason [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":45622156,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-10","story":"Andrej Karpathy – It will take a decade to work through the issues with agents","stance":"reference","why":"Cited implicitly as classic example that testing alone cannot establish understanding.","quote":"","text":"> that does not have a technical meaning I don't think the definition is very refined, but I think we should be careful to differentiate that from useless or meaningless. I would say most definitions are accurate, but not precise. It's a hard problem, but we are making progress on it. We will probably get there, but it's going to end up being very nuanced and already it is important to recognize that the word means different things in vernacular and in even differing research domains. Words are overloaded and I think we need to recognize this divergence and that we are gravely miscommunicating by assuming the definitions are obvious. I'm not sure why we don't do more to work together on this. In our field we seem to think we got it all covered and don't need others. I don't get that. > In this view, if a machine performs a task as well as a human, it understands it exactly as much as a human. And I do not think this is accurate at all. I would not say my calculator understands math despite it being able to do it better than me. I can say the same thing about a lot of different things which we don't attribute intelligence to. I'm sorry, but the logic doesn't hold. Okay, you might take an out by saying the calculator can't do abstract math like I can, right? Well we're going to run into that same problem. You can't test your way out of it. We've known this in hard sciences like physics for centuries. It's why physicists do much more than just experiments. There's the classic st"},{"id":44792001,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-08","story":"Persona vectors: Monitoring and controlling character traits in language models","stance":"reference","why":"Dyson recounts Fermi rejecting his accurate-prediction model, illustrating wrong models fitting data.","quote":"Here's a pretty famous example with Freeman Dyson recounting an experience with Fermi[0].","text":"> only a small subset will contain actual understanding, a property that is independent of the model's input-output behavior? I think this is close enough. I'd say \"a model's ability to make accurate predictions is not necessarily related to the model's ability to generate counterfactual predictions.\" I'm saying, you can make extremely accurate predictions with an incorrect world model. This isn't conjecture either, this is something we're extremely confident about in science. > I don't think I can agree. In your 2+a+b-2 example, that is an unnecessarily convoluted, but entirely correct model of addition. I gave it as a trivial example, not as a complete one (as stated). So be careful with extrapolating limitations of the example with limitations of the argument. For a more complex example I highly suggest looking at the actual history around the heliocentric vs geocentric debate. You'll have to make an active effort to understand this because what you were taught in school is very likely an (very reasonable) over simplification. Would you like a much more complex mathematical example? It'll take a little to construct and it'll be a lot harder to understand. As a simple example you can always take a Taylor expansion of something so you can approximate it, but if you want an example that is wrong and not through approximation then I'll need some time (and a specific ask). Here's a pretty famous example with Freeman Dyson recounting an experience with Fermi[0]. Dyson's model ma"},{"id":44090740,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-05","story":"The \"AI 2027\" Scenario: How realistic is it?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I do say the same think about JKR, btw. And for the same things, because the content she writes. I think you focused on the fanfic part and not the part where I'm criticizing where they say their stuff is the most important to keep humanity alive and they're charging money for it. Meanwhile, you may notice in academia we publish papers to make them freely available, like on arXiv. If it is that important that people need to know, you make it available. The second person, Hinton, is not as good of an authority as you'd expect. Though I do understand why people take him seriously. Fwiw, his Nobel was wildly controversial. Be careful, prizes often have political components. I have degrees in both CS and physics (and am an ML researcher) and both communities thought it was really weird. I'll let you guess which community found it insulting. I want to remind you, in 2016 Hinton famously said[0] | Let me start by saying a few things that seem obvious. I think if you work as a radiologist, you're like the coyote that's already over the edge of the cliff, but hasn't yet looked down so doesn't realize there's no ground beneath him. People should stop training radiologists now. It's just completely obvious that within 5 years that deep learning is going to be better than radiologists because it's going to get a lot more experience. It might take 10 years, but we've got plenty of radiologists already. We're 10 years in now. Hinton has shown he's about as good at making predictions as Mu"},{"id":44092750,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-05","story":"Chomsky on what ChatGPT is good for (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> The fact that we have figured out how to translate language into something a computer can \"understand\" should thrill linguists. I think they are really excited by this. There seems no deficiency of linguists using these machines. But I think it is important to distinguish the ability to understand language and translate it. Enough that you yourself put quotes around \"understanding\". This can often be a challenge for many translators, not knowing how to properly translate something because of underlying context. Our communication runs far deeper than the words we speak or write on a page. This is much of what linguistics is about, this depth. (Or at least that's what they've told me, since I'm not a linguist) This seems to be the distinction Chomsky is trying to make. > The main debate now is over the semantics of words like \"understanding\" and whether or not an LLM is conscious in the same way as a human being (it isn't). Exactly. Here, I'm on the side of Chomsky and I don't think there's much of a debate to be had. We have a long history of being able to make accurate predictions while erroneously understanding the underlying causal nature. My background is physics, and I moved into CS (degrees in both), working on ML. I see my peers at the top like Hinton[0] and Sutskever[1] making absurd claims. I call them absurd, because it is a mistake we've made over and over in the field of physics[2,3]. One of those lessons you learn again and again, because it is so easy to make t"},{"id":44093480,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-05","story":"Chomsky on what ChatGPT is good for (2023)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I think many people are missing the core of what Chomsky is saying. It is often easy to miscommunicate and I think this is primarily what is happening. I think the analogy he gives here really helps emphasize what he's trying to say. If you're only going to read one part, I think it is this: | I mentioned insect navigation, which is an astonishing achievement. Insect scientists have made much progress in studying how it is achieved, though the neurophysiology, a very difficult matter, remains elusive, along with evolution of the systems. The same is true of the amazing feats of birds and sea turtles that travel thousands of miles and unerringly return to the place of origin. | Suppose Tom Jones, a proponent of engineering AI, comes along and says: “Your work has all been refuted. The problem is solved. Commercial airline pilots achieve the same or even better results all the time.” | If even bothering to respond, we’d laugh. | Take the case of the seafaring exploits of Polynesians, still alive among Indigenous tribes, using stars, wind, currents to land their canoes at a designated spot hundreds of miles away. This too has been the topic of much research to find out how they do it. Tom Jones has the answer: “Stop wasting your time; naval vessels do it all the time.” | Same response. It is easy to look at metrics of performance and call things solved. But there's much more depth to these problems than our abilities to solve some task. It's not about just the ability to do some"},{"id":43261883,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-03","story":"ARC-AGI without pretraining","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> I feel like extensive pretraining goes against the spirit of generality. What do you mean by generality? Pretraining is fine. It is even fine for pursuit to AGI. Humans and every animal has \"baked in\" memory. You're born knowing how to breath and have latent fears (chickens and hawks). Generalization is the ability to learn on a subset of something and then adapt to the entire (or a much larger portion) of the superset. It's always been that way. Humans do this, right? You learn some addition, subtraction, multiplication, and division and then you can do novel problems you've never seen before that are extremely different. We are extremely general here because we've learned the causal rule set. It isn't just memorization. This is also true for things like physics, and is literally the point of science. Causality is baked into scientific learning. Of course, it is problematic when someone learns a little bit of something and thinks they know way more about it, but unfortunately ego is quite common. But also, I'm a bit with you. At least with what I think you're getting at. These LLMs are difficult to evaluate because we have no idea what they're trained on and you can't really know what is new, what is a slight variation from the training, and this is even more difficult considering the number of dimensions involved (meaning things may be nearly identical in latent space though they don't appear so to us humans). I think there's still a lot of ML/AI research that can and SHO"},{"id":43348954,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-03","story":"The cultural divide between mathematics and AI","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> the primary aim isn't really to find out whether a result is true but why it's true. I'm honestly surprised that there are mathematicians that think differently (my background[0]). There are so many famous mathematicians stating this through the years. Some more subtle like Poincare stating that math is not the study of numbers but the relationship between them, while others far more explicit. This sounds more like what I hear from the common public who think mathematics is discovered and not invented (how does anyone think anything different after taking Abstract Algebra?). But being over in the AI/ML world now, this is my NUMBER ONE gripe. Very few are trying to understand why things are working. I'd argue that the biggest reason machines are black boxes are because no one is bothering to look inside of them. You can't solve things like hallucinations and errors without understanding these machines (and there's a lot we already do understand). There's a strong pushback against mathematics and I really don't understand why. It has so many tools that can help us move forward, but yes, it takes a lot of work. It's bad enough I know people who have gotten PhDs from top CS schools (top 3!) and don't understand things like probability distributions. Unfortunately doing great things takes great work and great effort. I really do want to see the birth of AI, I wouldn't be doing this if I didn't, but I think it'd be naive to believe that this grand challenge can entirely be solved"},{"id":43442631,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-03","story":"Pen and Paper Exercises in Machine Learning (2022)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Just curious, how \"deep\" have you gone into the theory? What resources have you used? How strong is your math background? Unfortunately a lot of the theory does require some heavy mathematics, the type you won't see in a typical undergraduate degree even for more math heavy subjects like physics. Topics such as differential geometry, metric theory, set theory, abstract algebra, and high dimensional statistics. But I do promise that the theory helps and can build some very strong intuition. It is also extremely important that you have a deep understanding of what these mathematical operations are doing . It does look like this exercise book is trying to build that intuition, but I haven't read it in depth. I can say it is a good start, but only the very beginning of the theory journey. There is a long road ahead beyond this. > how it makes me choose the correct number of neurons in a layer, how many layers, Take a look at the Whitney embedding theorem. While this isn't a precise answer, it'll help you gain some intuition about the minimal number of parameters you need (and the VGG paper will help you understand width vs depth). In a transformer, the MLP layer post attention scales up 4x the dimensions before coming down, which allows for untangling any knots in the data. While 2x is the minimum, 4x creates a smoother landscape and so the problem can be solved more easily. Some of this is discussed in paper (Schaeffer, Miranda, and Koyejo) that counters the famous Emergent Abil"},{"id":42979422,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-02","story":"Understanding Reasoning LLMs","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I don't think this makes sense and I'm not quite sure why you went to ML, but that's okay. I am a machine learning researcher, but also frustrated with the state of machine learning, in part because, well... you can probably see how \"proof by empirical evidence\" is dialed up to 11. Sorry, long answer incoming. It is far from complete too but I think it will help build strong intuition around your questions. Will knowledge transfer? That entirely depends on the new problem. It also entirely depends on how related the problem is. But also, what information was used to solve the pre-transfer state. Take LLMs for example. There's lots of works that have shown them being difficult to train for solving calculations. Where they will do well on problems with the same number of digits but this will degrade rapidly as number of digits increase. It can be weird to read some of these papers as there will sometimes be periodic relationships with the number of digits but that should give us information about how they're encoding the problems. But that lack of transferability indicates that despite the problem solving and what we'd believe is actually just the same problem, doesn't mean it is. So you have to be really careful here, because us humans are really fucking good at generalization (yeah, we also suck, but a big part is our proficiency makes us recognize where we lack. But also, this is more a \"humans can\" more than \"humans do\" type of thing. So be careful when comparing). This gen"},{"id":42844461,"author":"godelski","month":"2025-01","story":"The impact of competition and DeepSeek on Nvidia","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"Interestingly a lot of the math and physics people in the ML community are considered \"grumpy researchers.\" A joke apparent with this starter pack[0]. From my personal experience (undergrad physics, worked as engineer, came to CS & ML because I liked the math), there's a lot of pushback. - I've been told that the math doesn't matter/you don't need math. - I've heard very prominent researchers say \"fuck theorists\" - I've seen papers routinely rejected for improving training techniques with reviewers say \"just tune a large model\" - I see papers that show improvements when conditioning comparisons on compute restraints because \"not enough datasets\" or \"but does it scale\" (these questions can always be asked but require exponentially more work) - I've been told I'm gatekeeping for saying \"you don't need math to make good models, but you need it to know why your models are wrong\" (yes, this is a reference) - when pointing out math or statistical errors I'm told it doesn't matter - and much more. I've heard this from my advisor, dissertation committee, bosses[1], peers, and others (of course, HN). If my experience is short of being rare, I think it explains the grumpy group[2]. But I'm also not too surprised with how common it is in CS for people to claim that everything is easy or that leet code is proof of competence (as opposed to evidence). I think unfortunately the problem is a bit bigger, but it isn't unsolvable. Really, it is \"easily\" solvable since it just requires us to ma"},{"id":42279893,"author":"godelski","month":"2024-11","story":"A statistical approach to model evaluations","stance":"reference","why":"Dyson explains that parameters need to be justified","quote":"","text":"I haven't had a chance to read that, but that quote suggests I should (especially considering the author and the editors). I often refer to \"Elephant Fitting\" w.r.t these systems. I suspect you understand this, but I think most think it is just about overfitting. But the way problem isn't about the number of parameters, but that parameters need to be justified. As explained by Dyson here[0]. Vladimir's quote really reminds me of this. Fermi likewise was stressing the importance of theory. I think it is a profound quote, and you were (are?) lucky to have that friendship. I do think abstraction is at the heart of intelligence. François Chollet discusses it a lot, and he's far from alone. It seems to be well agreed upon in the neuroscience and cognitive science communities. I think this is essential to understand in our path forward to developing intelligent systems, because there are plenty of problems that need to be solved in which there is no algorithmic procedure. Where there is no explicit density function. Intractable, doubly intractable, and more problems. Maybe we're just too dumb, but it's clear there are plateaus where luck is needed to advance. I do not believe our current machines would be capable of closing a gap. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":41616899,"author":"kayo_20211030","month":"2024-09","story":"What Is a Particle? (2020)","stance":"reference","why":"Dyson says quarks were invented","quote":"It's at 3:17 in this video of an interview with him","text":"I found the reference. It was an interview with Freeman Dyson where he said \"quarks were invented\", and I don't think it's a slip of the tongue. It's at 3:17 in this video of an interview with him [VIDEO] It's representative of a view that there's a thing (a particle) that explains another thing (a force) that was consistent with both theory and experiment. Thus, a quark could be a particle, and it was whatever the experimentalists and theorists said it was, however it was measured or contemplated. For a deeper meaning it becomes an exercise in hermeneutics i.e what does it mean when we say \"particle\"? That was the point of the original piece - there is no uncontested view of a particle's form, should one even exist. Each field, in order to advance, finds it useful to interpret it, or think about it, in different ways."},{"id":39726517,"author":"magicalhippo","month":"2024-03","story":"Study: Dark matter does not exist and the universe is 27B years old","stance":"reference","why":"Dyson recounts the Fermi elephant parameter story","quote":"Freeman Dyson learned this the hard way[5].","text":"Full paper here[1], and here's[2] the earlier paper about the model itself. They overloaded the CCC acronym, which has also been used for Penrose's Conformal cyclic cosmology[3], though can't say which is more speculative. I'm also reminded of Von Neumann's elephant[4], given that they vary the constants it doesn't seem that surprising I suppose that they can get a fit. Freeman Dyson learned this the hard way[5]. This is just one test, the article mentions a few others that they'll need to pass to be a serious contender. Will be interesting to see how it goes. [1]: [LINK] [2]: [LINK] [3]: [LINK] [4]: [LINK] [5]: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["D9H9qTdserM",1],["agw-wlHGi0E",1],["-NL76uQOpI0",1],["bmSAYlu0NcY",1],["pXOK-ZVJMaU",1]]},"hksVvXONrIo":{"wy":"Linked repeatedly as clear evidence of the iOS keyboard bug when people complain about typos.","q":{"x":"The YT video they linked is excessively clear about what the issue is.","i":47006420,"y":"2026"},"m":[{"id":47005010,"author":"whicks","month":"2026-02","story":"Apple, fix my keyboard before the timer ends or I'm leaving iPhone","stance":"rec","why":"Short video showing how broken the iOS keyboard is.","quote":"Someone actually took the effort to make a video to show how broken it is","text":"Someone actually took the effort to make a video to show how broken it is, it’s worth a watch: [VIDEO] It’s only 2 min 24 sec."},{"id":47332246,"author":"deminature","month":"2026-03","story":"Tell HN: Apple development certificate server seems down?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of the unresolved iOS keyboard bug.","quote":"","text":"All of Apple's software is rotting, from OSX to iOS to the developer tools. Massive bugs like the keyboard no longer working properly [1] are left unresolved for years. Whichever executive or engineering leader used to keep the quality bar high must have left or stopped paying attention. There also seems to be little culture of quality and ownership at the IC level. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47005805,"author":"satvikpendem","month":"2026-02","story":"Apple, fix my keyboard before the timer ends or I'm leaving iPhone","stance":"reference","why":"Shows keyboard registering one letter and inputting another.","quote":"","text":"Wow that video [0] is incredible, in a bad way. How can the OS correctly register the letter tapped but then input a different letter? That is like the most fundamental feature of a keyboard. It's not even autocomplete because the letters \"Thj\" as shown in the video don't even match any words that start with T. Glad I'm sticking to Android then, whose typing, you know, actually works, not even to mention how good (at least SwiftKey's) autocomplete and swipe typing is. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":47006420,"author":"chmod775","month":"2026-02","story":"Apple, fix my keyboard before the timer ends or I'm leaving iPhone","stance":"reference","why":"Makes the keyboard issue very clear.","quote":"The YT video they linked is excessively clear about what the issue is.","text":"The YT video they linked is excessively clear about what the issue is. There's no point in explaining it again. [VIDEO]"},{"id":47007673,"author":"biotechbio","month":"2026-02","story":"Apple, fix my keyboard before the timer ends or I'm leaving iPhone","stance":"reference","why":"Damning demonstration of the iOS keyboard bug.","quote":"I found this video to be particularly damning","text":"+1. From the original post, I found this video to be particularly damning: [VIDEO] Does anyone have an explanation for how something like this passes QC at a company with the resources of Apple? Is this video misrepresenting something?"},{"id":46543753,"author":"AnotherGoodName","month":"2026-01","story":"BillG the Manager","stance":"reference","why":"Slow-motion recording shows keyboard not typing the tapped letter.","quote":"it still doesn't type out the letter you tapped","text":"Fwiw it becomes really really obvious when companies have someone at the top pushing for initiatives like this and when they don't. A great example is how Apple used to be great at this. These days.the.keyboard.is.practically.unusable. As in it still doesn't type out the letter you tapped and you can see this via slow motion video recording [VIDEO] It's not at all wrong to call out subordinates in the company for quality feel. It may seem like it leads to misprioritization (everyone in the company scrambles to fix the issue you saw) but not doing this at all is far far worse. Look at this issue BillG raised and the fact that no one cared until it was raised. Big companies are very siloed by nature. The only thing that breaks these silos is the leadership doing things like this."},{"id":46729614,"author":"jmaker","month":"2026-01","story":"Bugs Apple Loves","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates typing issues on iPhones.","quote":"demonstrates typing issues that I also encountered on several iPhones","text":"I submitted this YouTube video (not mine) a while ago here that demonstrates typing issues that I also encountered on several iPhones: [It's Not Just You – The iOS Keyboard Is Broken]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":46731248,"author":"hn111","month":"2026-01","story":"Bugs Apple Loves","stance":"reference","why":"Shows iOS typing randomly inserting wrong characters.","quote":"","text":"Just typing on iOS randomly inserts the wrong characters: [VIDEO] I thought it was just me until someone mentioned this on HN"},{"id":46233219,"author":"ramity","month":"2025-12","story":"iPhone Typos? It's Not Just You – The iOS Keyboard Is Broken [video]","stance":"reference","why":"Shows slide to type enabled at a given timestamp.","quote":"","text":"35m ago edit: Apple uses many predictive systems for typing. My sentiment in pointing out just slide to type might be misguided as it does not exist in a vacuum. I'd love to see these tests redone with slide to type disabled. I'm leaving the original comment below for reference. Slide to type. This \"issue\" is at most 6 years old for iOS users. Turn off slide to type if you do not use it. Slide to type does key resizing logic. This is the direct cause of this issue. Please upvote this comment for visibility. Please reply if you think I'm wrong. I see this get posted frequently enough I'm actually losing it. Please refer to [VIDEO] (timestamp 1:12) to see that slide to type is enabled."},{"id":46027705,"author":"MyFirstSass","month":"2025-11","story":"Apple to focus on 'quality and underlying performance' with iOS 27 next year","stance":"reference","why":"Video with 1 million views showing the iOS keyboard problem.","quote":"","text":"The number one priority must surely be fixing the keyboard, besides the horrible UX? Millions are having problems for years so it's not just me, honestly thought i was \"getting old\" but no incredible amount of threads and now this on YT with 1 mil views: [VIDEO] Wild typing on a 3210 was less stressful for me."},{"id":45943850,"author":"ProfessorLayton","month":"2025-11","story":"Report: Tim Cook could step down as Apple CEO 'as soon as next year'","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The iOS keyboard is literally broken","text":"It’s not a silly comment, both macOS and iOS have been decaying into dog shit over the years from obvious bugs that anyone who uses the apps and features being sold would run into very quickly. Tim and other executives might be using their devices as email machines, but it’s not obvious they’re using everything they’re quite literally selling us. A few random examples: 1: The iOS keyboard is literally broken [VIDEO] 2: The Music app is barely functional, and will regularly fail to play music. Here it is bugging out, and stacking multiple album covers [LINK] 3: Offloading an app does not actually save any space [LINK] There’s so many more, and none of these examples are edge cases."}]},"naleynXS7yo":{"wy":"HN brings it up in threads about tone getting lost in text-based communication.","q":{"x":"Key and Peele have a great skit on this","i":19125978,"y":"2019"},"m":[{"id":19125978,"author":"mensetmanusman","month":"2019-02","story":"The Machine Stops","stance":"rec","why":"Skit about misreading tone in text messages.","quote":"Key and Peele have a great skit on this","text":"This. It’s amazing most people have no clue that they are mapping their mood state onto their own interpretation of someone’s text messages. We evolved to read non-verbal communication. Key and Peele have a great skit on this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":26456957,"author":"TheFlyingFish","month":"2021-03","story":"The internet didn’t kill counterculture – you just won’t find it on Instagram","stance":"reference","why":"Skit about misunderstood tone over text.","quote":"Key and Peele even did a skit about it","text":"> Actively processing voice on someone else's tempo over a crappy mic often leads to mistakes On the flipside, text-only conversation often simply doesn't include that nuance at all. I can't count the number of times I've completely misunderstood someone's tone over text when it would have been blindingly obvious over voice. Key and Peele even did a skit about it: [VIDEO] Voice and text have different strengths and weaknesses as communications media. Both are appropriate in different situations. I will admit, however, to being somewhat \"allergic\" (as another commenter put it) to voice, probably to an unreasonable extent, because I selfishly value my ability to focus on what I'm doing right now instead of giving it to someone else for a couple of minutes. But at the same time, I've often found that someone older who didn't grow up with easy text-based communication will use a phone call when a text would have been more than sufficient, and this can be quite irritating at times. I suspect it's in reaction to this that younger people tend to avoid voice communication even when it would probably be a better choice."},{"id":10377285,"author":"chavesn","month":"2015-10","story":"Show HN: FoxType – Using NLP to help you write more politely","stance":"reference","why":"Skit used as test text for a politeness tool.","quote":"","text":"I used this to check the text messages from Key and Peele's \"Text Message Confusion\" skit[1]: Keegan: \"I've been trying to reach out to you all day, are we on for tonight?\" Polite (71 / 100) Jordan: \"Sorry dude, missed your texts. I assumed we'd meet at the bar. Whatever, I don't care.\" Polite (71 / 100) Keegan: \"Do you even want to hang out?\" Impolite (30 / 100) Jordan: \"Like I said, whatever.\" Neutral (47 / 100) Keegan: \"Jesus, you are fucking priceless\" Impolite (30 / 100) Jordan: \"You're the one who's fucking priceless!\" Impolite (6 / 100) Keegan: \"You wanna go right now?\" Impolite (31 / 100) Jordan: \"Ok let's go\" Polite (76 / 100)[2] Keegan: \"You wanna really do this now?\" Impolite (13 / 100) Jordan: \"Fuck yeah, let's do it\" Impolite (19 / 100) Jordan: \"First round's mine\" Neutral (43 / 100) [1]: [VIDEO] [2]: Adding a \".\" to the end of the \"Ok let's go\" drops it by three points to 73 / 100"},{"id":40683416,"author":"lupire","month":"2024-06","story":"SoftBank's new AI makes angry customers sound calm on phone","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Obviously the AI needs to adjust the support person's voice to match the customer's, so the customer gets back what they give. Prior art from art: [VIDEO]"},{"id":9320320,"author":"runewell","month":"2015-04","story":"New Hacker News Guideline: Avoid Gratuitous Negativity","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Correctly interpreting online communication is difficult. What one person considers constructive feedback another person may interpret as negativity. Reminds me of that Key & Peele skit: [VIDEO]"}]},"sWFFiubtC3c":{"wy":"Linked as a historical artifact of SpaceX's early reusability ambitions, including the once-planned reusable second stage.","q":{"x":"here is SpaceX's fantastic animation of their ultimate goal for Falcon 9","i":5594810,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":5594810,"author":"jessriedel","month":"2013-04","story":"SpaceX Grasshopper Flies High","stance":"rec","why":"Animation of Falcon 9's full flight plan","quote":"here is SpaceX's fantastic animation of their ultimate goal for Falcon 9","text":"For those of you who haven't seen it, here is SpaceX's fantastic animation of their ultimate goal for Falcon 9 (complete with soundtrack by Muse): [VIDEO] This will give you an idea of how grasshopper fits into the full flight plan."},{"id":26303230,"author":"bryanlarsen","month":"2021-03","story":"Rocket Lab Unveils Plans for New 8-Ton Class Reusable Rocket","stance":"reference","why":"Falcon 9 v1.0 size and early full-reusability plans","quote":"This is the size of Falcon 9 v1.0, and they once believed full reusability was possible.","text":"IMO the reason that Rocket Lab is doing an eight tonne rocket is not because of the market for mega constellations. It's because 8 tonnes is about the minimum size for a fully reusable rocket. You need a rocket big enough to launch all the propellant and shields needed for landing and still have room for payload. And so the bigger the rocket, the more margin you have. Once fully reusable, costs drop dramatically, so I wouldn't be surprised if the price of Neutron is similar to that of Electron. Who would use a small launch vehicle when a medium one is the same price, giving you a lot more room for maneuver propellant. This is the size of Falcon 9 v1.0, and they once believed full reusability was possible. [VIDEO] Edit: they haven't announced full reusability. But I believe they're working on it. Edit 2: I still don't believe mega-constellations are the reason for Neutron. It being the minimum size for full reusability could be one, but another explanation is it being the minimum size for human space flight."},{"id":26303908,"author":"darknavi","month":"2021-03","story":"Rocket Lab Unveils Plans for New 8-Ton Class Reusable Rocket","stance":"reference","why":"SpaceX once planned a reusable second stage","quote":"Wow I forgot SpaceX originally wanted a reusable second stage on F9.","text":"> [VIDEO] Wow I forgot SpaceX originally wanted a reusable second stage on F9. Would have been pretty cool."},{"id":5595358,"author":"stigi","month":"2013-04","story":"SpaceX Grasshopper Flies High","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here's a short link to bypass","text":"\"Unfortunately, this video is not available in Germany because it may contain music for which GEMA has not granted the respective music rights.\" Here's a short link to bypass: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4751680,"author":"someperson","month":"2012-11","story":"SpaceX VTOL rocket \"Grasshopper\" hops 5.4m","stance":"reference","why":"Animation of SpaceX's planned reusable Falcon 9","quote":"This is preparation for the vertical take-off and landing (VTOL) rocket SpaceX wants to build","text":"This follows from the first test flight in late-September [LINK] . > \"Testing of Grasshopper continues, with the next big milestone -- a hover at roughly 100 feet -- expected in the next several months.\" This is preparation for the vertical take-off and landing (VTOL) rocket SpaceX wants to build [VIDEO] And the reason why they're doing this: [LINK] > But a fully reusable rocket could change the equation dramatically. Musk illustrated the point by citing SpaceX's Falcon 9, which costs between $50 million to $60 million per launch in its current configuration. > \"But the cost of the fuel and oxygen and so forth is only about $200,000,\" Musk said.\"So obviously, if we can reuse the rocket, say, a thousand times, then that would make the capital cost of the rocket for launch only about $50,000.\""},{"id":4752611,"author":"someperson","month":"2012-11","story":"SpaceX VTOL rocket Grasshopper hops 5.4m","stance":"reference","why":"Animation of SpaceX's planned reusable Falcon 9","quote":"This is preparation for the vertical take-off and landing (VTOL) rocket SpaceX wants to build","text":"This follows from the first test flight in late-September [LINK] . > \"Testing of Grasshopper continues, with the next big milestone -- a hover at roughly 100 feet -- expected in the next several months.\" This is preparation for the vertical take-off and landing (VTOL) rocket SpaceX wants to build [VIDEO] And the reason why they're doing this: [LINK] > But a fully reusable rocket could change the equation dramatically. Musk illustrated the point by citing SpaceX's Falcon 9, which costs between $50 million to $60 million per launch in its current configuration. > \"But the cost of the fuel and oxygen and so forth is only about $200,000,\" Musk said.\"So obviously, if we can reuse the rocket, say, a thousand times, then that would make the capital cost of the rocket for launch only about $50,000.\" (Resubmission of [LINK] . I rarely resubmit, but this really is HN material and warrants quality discussion)"}],"r":[["nv7-YM4wno8",1]]},"tSnnfUj1XCQ":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as an early example of projectional or intent-based programming tools when discussing alternatives to text-based code.","q":{"x":"Here are a couple of demo vids from well over 10 years ago","i":12905077,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":8311709,"author":"spyder","month":"2014-09","story":"New Requests for Startups","stance":"rec","why":"Interesting ideas for reading and writing programs.","quote":"intentional programming from Charles Simonyi","text":"There are some interesting ideas that could help with reading and writing programs: from Bret Victor: [LINK] and intentional programming from Charles Simonyi: [LINK] [VIDEO]"},{"id":39431856,"author":"sweetsocks21","month":"2024-02","story":"Clean your codebase with basic information theory","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It looked really neat.","text":"Intentional Software was working on a system like this many years ago: [VIDEO] Part 2: [LINK] You can project your programs into different views and add lots of metadata about the program and use that data in various contexts. Also extends to source control and other use cases. It looked really neat. I'm not sure what happened to it."},{"id":36959221,"author":"corysama","month":"2023-08","story":"Predictive Debugging: A Game-Changing Look into the Future","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Charles Simonyi's Intentional Software was doing that back in the days of Windows NT","text":"Charles Simonyi's Intentional Software was doing that back in the days of Windows NT [VIDEO] But, they stayed insular and enterprise right up until Microsoft bought them back. We mortals barely got to see or hear anything about it. I heard one podcast interview of a developer there who said they basically represent the code as an s-expression tree with a bunch of metadata on each node."},{"id":16133309,"author":"calebh","month":"2018-01","story":"Show HN: Bato – A Filipino Programming Language","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Check out this old video on intentional programming by Microsoft","text":"This idea has been around for ages. Check out this old video on intentional programming by Microsoft: [VIDEO]"},{"id":14426630,"author":"DalekBaldwin","month":"2017-05","story":"Charles Simonyi on Intentional Software Joining Microsoft","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"the system didn't look too much different from its earlier incarnation at Microsoft in the '90s","text":"For the curious: it's difficult to find that particular demo on the web now, but the system didn't look too much different from its earlier incarnation at Microsoft in the '90s: [VIDEO]"},{"id":12905077,"author":"corysama","month":"2016-11","story":"Ask HN: Is the architecture of programming tools historical accident?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"Here are a couple of demo vids from well over 10 years ago","text":"It's a shame the Intentional Software crew have been nearly silent for the a decade or so they've been in business. They've been doing projections-of-ASTs based programming for a very long time. They bragged a lot about it early on and I haven't heard much of anything since. Here are a couple of demo vids from well over 10 years ago: [VIDEO] [LINK] Here's an interview from 2009 where one of the crew members talks about an annotated S-expression as the core code representation (IIRC, it's been a long time since I listened to it) [LINK]"},{"id":2494,"author":"amichail","month":"2007-03","story":"SF Chronicle article about new search startups","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"This is sort of like intentional programming, but applied to","text":"I wonder if it would make sense to make a search engine platform where (more technical) users can submit code for domain specific query entry, search, ranking, and presentation of results. Moreover, such code submissions would compete with each other, with more effective ones rising in popularity in their respective domains. Unlike APIs that are currently available, the submissions made by users here would make up the core feature set of the service. This is sort of like intentional programming, but applied to [VIDEO]"},{"id":1408,"author":"amichail","month":"2007-02","story":"I'm looking for a partner or two (here are some of my ideas, feel free to add your own here)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I am interested in seeing what happens when you apply intentional programming to web","text":"I am interested in seeing what happens when you apply intentional programming to web [VIDEO] idea is to open up your search engine so that anyone can submit code for domain specific query entry, search, ranking, and presentation of results. Moreover, such code submissions would compete with each other, with more effective ones rising in popularity in their respective domains. Unlike APIs that are currently available, the submissions made by users here would make up the core feature set of the service."}]},"w-WMn_zHCVo":{"wy":"HN commenters cite it as an example of police cueing drug dogs to justify searches.","q":{"x":"Here's a step-by-step video of what they do for anyone interested that also shows them cue the dog","i":10946608,"y":"2016"},"m":[{"id":10946608,"author":"nickpsecurity","month":"2016-01","story":"Federal cops gets pulled over by TN cops, car illegally searched","stance":"rec","why":"Step-by-step view of a stop showing cueing of the dog","quote":"Here's a step-by-step video of what they do for anyone interested that also shows them cue the dog","text":"There's a lot of speculation in articles and comments despite video evidence available for this sort of thing. Here's a step-by-step video of what they do for anyone interested that also shows them cue the dog: [VIDEO]"},{"id":10023738,"author":"radiorental","month":"2015-08","story":"Court rules drug dog barely more accurate than a coin flip is good enough","stance":"reference","why":"Shows officer caught cueing the dog to trigger a search","quote":"I've linked to where the officer was caught triggering the search","text":"You can watch this 6 minute video of a stop, I've linked to where the officer was caught triggering the search [VIDEO] It happens."},{"id":8768357,"author":"jonathanp12","month":"2014-12","story":"The Sinaloa Cartel's 90-Year-Old Drug Mule","stance":"reference","why":"Evidence for commenter's opposition to dog-based searches","quote":"","text":"I don't like the idea of animals being able to determine whether a person gets their 4th amendment right or not. Also getting \"caught\" with $350,000 is irksome but theres probably been enough comments on here about civil forfeiture lately. EDIT: reason for my opinion: [VIDEO]"}],"r":[["CesHr99ezWE",1],["hBMoPUAeLnY",1],["eiYoBbEZwlk",1],["BsVydyC8ZGQ",1]]},"w8J5BWL8oJY":{"wy":"Mostly cited as damning evidence of Magic Leap's hype and marketing, with commenters calling it delusional or a fraud signal.","m":[{"id":8761031,"author":"billsossoon","month":"2014-12","story":"Neal Stephenson Joins Magic Leap","stance":"rec","why":"Talk by Magic Leap's founder, odd start but worth a few minutes.","quote":"I highly recommend this TED talk by the company's founder Rony Abovitz","text":"For anybody curious about Magic Leap, I highly recommend this TED talk by the company's founder Rony Abovitz: [VIDEO] It starts off kinda weird, you gotta give it a few minutes."},{"id":46555425,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-01","story":"Making Magic Leap past Nvidia's secure bootchain and breaking Tesla Autopilots","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"I dare you to waste 6:17 minutes of your life that you will never get back watching this","text":"In the same sense that we also got [total surrealistic non sequitur] out of it -- no causal connection. Cocaine addled money laundering sexist nepotistic bro culture deserves all the harshness it gets. I dare you to waste 6:17 minutes of your life that you will never get back watching this, and tell me they didn't spend a huge chunk of their investor's money on cocaine. The synthesis of imagination: Rony Abovitz and Magic Leap at TEDxSarasota: [VIDEO] >Surprises abound in this multimedia, surrealist talk/performance by Rony Abovitz and Magic Leap at TEDxSarasota. Rony is a recognized innovator and entrepreneur, having co-founded the pioneering robotics company, MAKO Surgical Corp, which was recognized by Deloitte as the #1 fastest growing tech company in North America in 2011. Part of TEDxSarasota's inaugural conference held on 12/12/12 with the theme \"Creativity Matters\" at the Historic Asolo Theatre in Sarasota, Florida."},{"id":46555494,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2026-01","story":"Making Magic Leap past Nvidia's secure bootchain and breaking Tesla Autopilots","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"they were a fraud through and through from day one","text":"[LINK] DonHopkins 16 hours ago | prev [–] It's a good thing they're effectively dead, but they don't have any useful parts to carve out, because they were a fraud through and through from day one, infested with toxic sexist nepotistic bro culture, and were an embarrassment and warning to the industry. No surprise at all they unethically bricked their useless overpriced electronic waste. [LINK] DonHopkins on Dec 4, 2021 | parent | context | favorite | on: I bought those AR cycling glasses that were on HN ... Why not simply do what Magic Leap does, and blatantly fake the camera images and games that don't really and couldn't possibly exist? [LINK] And then rip off other people's work without giving them any credit in your patents. [LINK] And then sexually harass the very women you employed to solve the endemic \"pink/blue problem\" and fix the nepotistic sexist bro culture, and rebuff and ignore her advice, and pay her off to keep their mouth shut when she sues you for sexual harassment. [LINK] And then give the entire AR industry a bad name by not delivering on your outrageously hyped promises, while burning through BILLIONS of dollars and fucking over your foolishly gullible investors. [LINK] fredoralive 8 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] Don’t be so harsh, at least we got the Steam Deck out of all this. DonHopkins 5 minutes ago | root | parent | next | edit | delete [–] In the same sense that we also got [total surrealistic non sequitur] out of it -- no causal connection. "},{"id":44540463,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2025-07","story":"Bill Atkinson's psychedelic user interface","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the most ridiculous obnoxious unenlightening cocaine addled TedX talk in the history of the universe","text":"Here's the real story behind \"Magic Creep\": [LINK] Not to be confused with General Magic, which wasn't a money grubbing fraud from day one, which didn't put on the most ridiculous obnoxious unenlightening cocaine addled TedX talk in the history of the universe, which didn't post fraudulent obviously impossibly fake demos to youtube falsely claiming \"Just another day in the office at Magic Leap\", which didn't have a toxic nepotistic bro culture that excluded and belittled women, that didn't settle a huge lawsuit for sex discrimination against the one woman they hired to fix the \"pink/blue problem\" but then rebuffed and ignored, that didn't burn through billions of investment dollars producing nothing of value, which didn't blatantly rip off many people's original ideas in their unoriginal invalid fraudulent patent applications, which didn't publish an astounding but physically impossible video of a whale leaping out the gym floor and splashing down while hundreds of children not wearing AR headsets were somehow magically able to perceive it and clap and cheer and say \"WOOOAAAHH!\" [VIDEO] [LINK] [LINK] Magic Leap gave magic a bad name (and was the biggest disgrace of the entire AR industry, even spectacularly worse than a certain Google Glasshole publishing a nude selfie of himself in the shower), while General Magic truly was the good kind of magic. [LINK]"},{"id":41338189,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2024-08","story":"Meta cancels high-end mixed reality headset after Apple Vision Pro struggles","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Are you telling me those guys are totally full of shit??!","text":"Hey, I though Magic Leap had it all figured out! Are you telling me those guys are totally full of shit??! SHOCKED I say SHOCKED! The synthesis of imagination: Rony Abovitz and Magic Leap at TEDxSarasota [VIDEO] Hold my beer... [LINK] >Are you referring to the sex discrimination lawsuit and nepotistic sexist bro culture that tarnished Magic Leap's reputation? [...] [LINK] >Or are you referring to the ridiculous 12/12/2012 TEDX \"talk\" that Rony Abovitz performed at the Ringling College of Art, and all the FAKE and DECEPTIVE videos they posted and lied about on youtube, that tarnished Magic Leap's reputation? [...] [LINK] >That didn't stop them from making fraudulent concept video demos that they falsely claimed to be actual existing games that they were already playing around the office, and that they promised much much more than they could actually deliver. >So if you can't reproduce the experience on a 2D screen, then fake it and lie, you're saying? That IS the whole point. [LINK] >Or are you referring to the way Magic Leap picked up and ripped off so many other people's original designs and IP in their patent applications without giving the actual inventors credit, that tarnished Magic Leap's reputation? [...] [LINK] >The point is that the name Magic Leap IS extremely and deeply tarnished, in so many ways, and Magic Leap pretending it's not just makes them look more laughably delusional than they already are (which is extremely), just like Trump still believing that he won "},{"id":38396432,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2023-11","story":"Sorry You Missed It – Inside Rockstar North Blog Shut Down","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Couldn't be any more delusional than Magic Leap!","text":"Couldn't be any more delusional than Magic Leap! The synthesis of imagination: Rony Abovitz and Magic Leap at TEDxSarasota: [VIDEO]"},{"id":28838500,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2021-10","story":"My first year at Magic Leap and the opportunity ahead","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"the ridiculous 12/12/2012 TEDX \"talk\" that Rony Abovitz performed at the Ringling College of Art","text":"Or are you referring to the ridiculous 12/12/2012 TEDX \"talk\" that Rony Abovitz performed at the Ringling College of Art, and all the FAKE and DECEPTIVE videos they posted and lied about on youtube, that tarnished Magic Leap's reputation? [VIDEO] In my mind, they're not as reputable after having given Rony Abovitz a platform to perform that \"fudge\". Just watch the video, if you can stomach it, all the way through. And read the comments, like this one: >Nick Steele 2 years ago (edited) >This is a joke. Take it for what it is. They didn't want to say anything so they basically said \"are you ready? READY? ... fuck you\". >After a completely ridiculous intro which includes nano machines humping blood cells and two crack monkeys worshiping a massive block of \"demented space fudge\" which takes up 75% of the talk until 4:30, right after 30 seconds of literal silence, a spaceman says \"greetings\" and introduces today's \"keyword\" which is \"fudge\", then a guy plays terrible music out of tune and sings half-way into the mic. Then the lights suddenly go out and the crack moneys and space man simply walk away. >Keep in mind the audience thinks they are about to hear a billionaire explain his new \"world changing\" virtual/augmented reality technology, then they get this shit. >The best part is the audiences reaction at the end. :) Dented Reality: Magic Leap Sees Slow Sales, Steep Losses [LINK] >Magic Leap had high hopes for sales of its augmented reality headset. Instead, the richly valued st"},{"id":22949493,"author":"Reedx","month":"2020-04","story":"Magic Leap reportedly slashes jobs and steps away from consumer plans","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"To me that was the moment both Magic Leap and TED (sadly) jumped the shark.","text":"I don't know, those could be genuine feelings. Like the first time using VR. Now their TEDx talk on the other hand... speaking of silly. [VIDEO] To me that was the moment both Magic Leap and TED (sadly) jumped the shark."},{"id":22556661,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-03","story":"Magic Leap trying to sell for $10B","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"utterly dishonest marketing as from Magic Leap. I mean, come on","text":"The incompetence, and the rampant sexism, and of course the blatant dishonesty and deception in their faked demos. Not to mention the way they ripped off other people's ideas in their patents and didn't give them credit. I posted this earlier: [LINK] There was some overlap, and both had lots of \"Magic\" hype, but there were some really great people working at General Magic, and not nearly as high a level of narcissistic bullshit and self aggrandization and utterly dishonest marketing as from Magic Leap. I mean, come on: [VIDEO] [LINK] The whole point of the lawsuit was all about how Magic Leap's company culture and product plans and demos and self image all revolved around adolescent male egos and sexist fantasies, and excluded women. Tannen Cambell, who filed the lawsuit, was actually hired for the express purpose of solving Magic Leap's recognized \"pink/blue problem\", but was rebuffed and ignored. They knew they had a problem, and even gave it a name, but they refused to solve it. Read the lawsuit: [LINK] >Campbell, one of whose responsibilities was to help Magic Leap with the “pink/blue problem,” had to endure hostile environment sex discrimination while proposing ways, not only to make Magic Leap’s product more woman friendly, but also to make the workplace more diverse and inclusive. Campbell was terminated after (and because) she, like the child in “The Emperor’s New Clothes” who blurted out that the Emperor was naked, challenged Magic Leap’s CEO, Rony Abovitz, to acknow"},{"id":22556889,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2020-03","story":"Magic Leap trying to sell for $10B","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Cocaine.","text":">makes me wonder what did they show to the initial investors - it MUST have been something incredible Cocaine. [VIDEO]"},{"id":21726144,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2019-12","story":"Dented Reality: Magic Leap Sees Slow Sales, Steep Losses","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"utterly dishonest marketing as from Magic Leap","text":"There was some overlap, and both had lots of \"Magic\" hype, but there were some really great people working at General Magic, and not nearly as high a level of narcissistic bullshit and self aggrandization and utterly dishonest marketing as from Magic Leap. I mean, come on: [VIDEO]"},{"id":21726183,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2019-12","story":"Dented Reality: Magic Leap Sees Slow Sales, Steep Losses","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The first time I saw their original TedX video I knew for sure that Magic Leap was totally full of it.","text":"Not everybody believed the hype. The first time I saw their original TedX video I knew for sure that Magic Leap was totally full of it. [VIDEO]"},{"id":18186626,"author":"thoughtexplorer","month":"2018-10","story":"Why Is Behavioral Economics So Popular?","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Until I started seeing stuff like this","text":"It sucks what happened with them. TED/TEDx talks were awesome and I used to be able to recommended people watch any and all of them. Until I started seeing stuff like this [VIDEO]"},{"id":18189374,"author":"thoughtexplorer","month":"2018-10","story":"Magic Leap is real and it’s a janky marvel","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"check out what they approved here","text":"> Who on Earth approved this trailer? Ha, if you're surprised about that, check out what they approved here [VIDEO]"},{"id":17849043,"author":"jazzyjackson","month":"2018-08","story":"Magic Leap is a Tragic Heap","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this farce of a TED talk [2] in 2012","text":"Been anticipating this teardown ever since this hackernews post [1] comparing the promises with the patents and nailing the prediction that ML1 would be yet-another LCOS headset. Frankly I've been cheerleading their implosion ever since this farce of a TED talk [2] in 2012. An opportunity to present their technology and they jump around in monster costumes wasting everyone's time. Thwaxo's Strangely Demented Space Fudge ? This was months after securing half a billion in funding. Just left a really bad taste in my mouth. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17860583,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2018-08","story":"Magic Leap One now available for $2295","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this amazing piece of public relations magic that transparently unveils the deep truth of what Magic Leap is really all about","text":"I'm shocked, I say shocked, that something from Magic Leap could possibly be said to reek of marketing. You should spend the next six minutes and seventeen seconds of your life watching this amazing piece of public relations magic that transparently unveils the deep truth of what Magic Leap is really all about: The synthesis of imagination: Rony Abovitz and Magic Leap at TEDxSarasota [VIDEO]"},{"id":14490877,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2017-06","story":"Apple enters the augmented reality fray with ARKit for iOS","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"they're a fraud that's nowhere near releasing a real product","text":"Magic Leap is conspicuously but rightly missing from your list, because they're a fraud that's nowhere near releasing a real product, and whose bullshit is besmirching and dragging down the reputation of AR. [VIDEO]"},{"id":14311137,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2017-05","story":"Magic Leap Settling Sex Discrimination Lawsuit with Former Employee","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"It's no secret that they're a fraud.","text":"\"Magic Leap is probably one of the most secretive technology companies of our generation\"??! It's no secret that they're a fraud. That cat's out of the bag. I mean, come on: just watch Rony Abovitz's TEDxSarasota talk [1] that he so fittingly presented at the Ringling College of Art & Design [2], whose trademarked mission phrase is \"Shattering the myth of the starving artist.™\". Yes, those very same Ringling brothers [3], whose colleague P. T. Barnum said: \"There's a sucker born every minute.\" Send in the clowns. Charlatans like these give the rest of the industry a terrible name. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"},{"id":13098446,"author":"DonHopkins","month":"2016-12","story":"Magic Leap – Separating Magic and Reality","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"this sure blew the fuse on my bullshit detector.","text":"I'm not saying I'm 100% sure they don't have something worth investing $300 million in, but this sure blew the fuse on my bullshit detector. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8488358,"author":"leoc","month":"2014-10","story":"Magic Leap Secures $542M Led by Google","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Magic Leap's PR operation has not always been the tightest ship","text":"The consensus seems to be that the \"cinematic reality not augmented reality\" message probably refers to the realistic accommodation - virtual objects being in focus at the correct focal depth - which Magic Leap is apparently aiming for and which other 3D display systems don't offer. If that message seems confusing, especially in light of the fact that neither 2D nor (standard) 3D cinema offer realistic accommodation, let's just say that Magic Leap's PR operation has not always been the tightest ship: [VIDEO] ."}]},"2zfqw8nhUwA":{"wy":"Cited as the standard example of a hugely effective emotional ad that sold a brand image rather than product details.","q":{"x":"Apple's 1984 ad is still probably one of the most famous ads.","i":17235340,"y":"2018"},"m":[{"id":38547338,"author":"Lio","month":"2023-12","story":"Show HN: Why is the Amiga so beloved in the demoscene? (2023 essay)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"> It's the ancestor of \"Mac vs PC\". That's an odd turn of phrase considering that both the Mac and the PC predate the Amiga. If anything surely the Mac's Ridley Scott directed launch Super Bowl ad was the start of \"Mac vs PC\". People loved the Amiga because it had a lot of modern features for the time and was reasonably priced. You're definitely right, it's the little computer that could. The demo scene loved the Amiga because a lot of hardware features were left undocumented ready for you to discover and wow your friends with. [VIDEO]"},{"id":28985522,"author":"iSnow","month":"2021-10","story":"Richard Feynman and the Connection Machine (1989)","stance":"reference","why":"Iconic ad rooted in early computer privacy movement.","quote":"That's where the iconic Apple commercial has its background","text":">that people in 1985 were having the exact same discussions we are today about the computer and it's effect on privacy with respect to \"big brother\" and the totalitarian government's obsession with collecting information on people. Sure did, the 1970's and early 1980's were the birth time of the computer privacy movement. That's where the iconic Apple commercial has its background: [VIDEO] Of course, today everyone is spending their time enjoying indoctrination in front of a miniature Apple screen instead of getting called into a center..."},{"id":24148041,"author":"jedberg","month":"2020-08","story":"Apple removes Fortnite from App Store after Epic attempts to bypass fees","stance":"reference","why":"Original ad that Epic's parody short spoofs.","quote":"For those who don't know","text":"Here's the short they just released making fun of Apple's 1984 commercial: [LINK] Edit: The youtube comments are hilarious because no one knows what it's a spoof of. For those who don't know: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20159430,"author":"pseudolus","month":"2019-06","story":"'1984' at Seventy: Why We Still Read Orwell","stance":"reference","why":"Apple embraced the 1984 anniversary with its commercial.","quote":"Apple, with its 1984 type commercial, was one of the few companies that really went all in on the 1984 anniversary","text":"One of the fascinating things about '1984' is that in the actual year 1984 the book, which did receive some notable publicity that year, was perceived as being something of a 'dud'. Not that people perceived that they were living in surveillance-free society but rather at that time people were more preoccupied with nuclear war (anyone remember \"The Day After\"? [0]) and the tools that would bring about an oppressive environment were still in the imaginations of technologists. Strangely enough Apple, with its 1984 type commercial, was one of the few companies that really went all in on the 1984 anniversary [1]. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":17673142,"author":"TangoTrotFox","month":"2018-08","story":"To Persuade Someone, Look Emotional","stance":"reference","why":"Famous emotional ad with no specs.","quote":"Apple's 1984 [1] is one of the most famous and effective bits of marketing.","text":"Your emotions are just another part of your body that you can control. It's an extremely important part of countless careers such as investment banker, fighter, lawyer, judge, doctor, clinical scientist or researcher, or even things such as an air traffic controller. It's not that the people in these fields do not have emotions, but that they consciously work to control them. I've achieved as much thanks to a career long in my past as well, and it changed me radically for the better. I think we are quick to discount things that we cannot visibly see. For instance, if somebody of normal health told you that it was just physically impossible for them to bench press more than 100 pounds, you'd probably find their statement quite ridiculous. Of course the reason is that we can see our muscles and how they change and develop (or atrophy) in response to how we train them, and we can precisely measure changes in our ability to lift. With emotions you cannot so easily measure them, and it's not really clear how to train them. But that certainly does not mean it's out of your control. --- But on point here, consider the notion of trying to prohibit emotionally manipulative advertisements. The first question would be why? Apple's 1984 [1] is one of the most famous and effective bits of marketing. It's also almost entirely an emotional piece. You're certainly not excited about the specs there! I don't see why such things should not be allowed. And if you're going to try to ban emotional"},{"id":17235340,"author":"TangoTrotFox","month":"2018-06","story":"How the Math Men Overthrew the Mad Men","stance":"reference","why":"Famous ad built on brand image, not product details.","quote":"Apple's 1984 ad is still probably one of the most famous ads.","text":"Literally in marketing 101 (okay, 301) one of the first things you learn is that marketing is not necessarily about directly selling things, but about brand recognition and subconsciously instilling it, and implied quality, in the consumers mind. For instance Coca Cola spends an obscene amount on advertising each and every year, even though they have no significant competition. A big part of that is because it creates an image in your mind the next time you go to buy something, that Coca Cola is higher quality more desirable than alternatives. So for instance for your AC unit, perhaps you see a Samsung and GE. Your decision between which to buy is the purpose of marketing and advertising. For instance this is also why companies don't really like running discounts. Discounts can produce immediate increases in sales, but they simultaneously can also subconsciously diminish the perceived quality of a product in the consumer mind. Places where discounts, across all brands, are ubiquitous and independent of the manufacturer are presumably a game changer here since it's not the product being discounted per se but the site itself running a discount. Apple's 1984 ad is still probably one of the most famous ads. Check it out [1]. It doesn't really make you think, \"Yes I must go immediately buy a Macintosh.\" Literally no details are given about the product at all, and 90% of the commercial is a cinematic that has nothing to do with the product. Nonetheless it instills a image, and one "},{"id":18055680,"author":"phobosdeimos","month":"2018-09","story":"Modifications to Google Chromium for removing Google integration","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Hilarious in hindsight.","text":"Choice is bad. Anyone remember the 1980s Apple commercial referencing Big Brother? Hilarious in hindsight. [VIDEO]"},{"id":13660429,"author":"OnlineSince1991","month":"2017-02","story":"Apple Worldwide Dev Conference 2017 announced","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"When is Apple going to turn the tables once again like they did in 1984?","text":"Last year's Dev Conference was really dissapointing.. When is Apple going to turn the tables once again like they did in 1984? [VIDEO]"}]},"ExWfh6sGyso":{"wy":"HN commenters mostly drop it as a joke when someone downplays the upsides of something, such as a platform, a company or industrialization.","m":[{"id":11788706,"author":"sbuttgereit","month":"2016-05","story":"Apple, Microsoft, and Google hold 23% of all U.S. corporate cash","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's terribly simplistic to look only at industrialization problems and completely neglect to acknowledge the upsides.","text":"You know when I hear a people speaking like this, I can't help but think about that scene from \"The Life of Brian\" where the rhetorical question of \"What have the Romans ever done for us?!\" ( [VIDEO] ) It's terribly simplistic to look only at industrialization problems and completely neglect to acknowledge the upsides. Is there pollution and would I like there to be less, sure. Having said that would I want to give up the safety, food security, medical advances, conveniences, etc. that make life worthwhile so that I could live in pristine, untouched nature? Not a chance. I've life is substantially better because I live in an industrialized society and I'll take the pollution so long as it comes with all the good in my life."},{"id":2934897,"author":"aw3c2","month":"2011-08","story":"Apple changed everything? Yeah, right.","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"it is from The Life of Brian, a hilarious comedy from Monty Python.","text":"Because some people might not know the movie and/or get the reference, it is from The Life of Brian, a hilarious comedy from Monty Python. The scene: [VIDEO]"},{"id":11389871,"author":"dankohn1","month":"2016-03","story":"Linux at 25: Q&A with Linus Torvalds","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"But other than Linux and git (and a diving app [0]), what has Linus ever done for us?","text":"But other than Linux and git (and a diving app [0]), what has Linus ever done for us? [1] [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":7374616,"author":"acqq","month":"2014-03","story":"iOS 7.1","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Apart from the sanitation, medicine, education, wine, public order, irrigation, roads","text":"[VIDEO] \"Apart from the sanitation, medicine, education, wine, public order, irrigation, roads, the fresh water system and public health, what have the Romans ever done for us?\""},{"id":7103257,"author":"stefan_kendall","month":"2014-01","story":"Demonstration at Home of Google Developer. Google Bus Blocked in Berkeley","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"What have the Romans ever done for us?!","text":"But aside from all the great free services google provides, and the money it has poured into its offices and the surrounding area, what has it done? What have the Romans ever done for us?! [VIDEO]"},{"id":6660905,"author":"endeavour","month":"2013-11","story":"An analysis of the C#/.NET platform","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"You mean apart from discriminated unions, pattern matching, computation expressions, units of measure","text":"You mean apart from discriminated unions, pattern matching, computation expressions, units of measure, partial function application, immutability by default and general lack of null reference exceptions? See also: [VIDEO]"},{"id":4659022,"author":"dmorgan","month":"2012-10","story":"So You Want To Optimize Ruby","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Monty Python skitch","text":"> So what's new? What do you really win with Clojure other than better libraries and community? Not much. Community and libraries are of outmost importance in picking a language. So, your \"what do you really win other than those\", reminds me of \"what the Romans ever did for us\" Monty Python skitch ( [VIDEO] ). > Oh and the uh...concurrency primitives? They're a canard. They're neat, they're designed excellently (being that it's Rich Hickey), but they're just not as impactful as other things are these days. If single-node/instance performance was important, you wouldn't be able to do it on the JVM. If scalability is important, shared memory on a single node is irrelevant and it becomes a matter of implementing a partition tolerant database. The concurrency primitives would've been more important in an alternate universe where we didn't use databases/data stores/filesystems for state management and synchronization and instead did Smalltalk stateful servers. A really bizarre way to look at things. You've basically taken too extremes and arbitrarily declared that there is nothing of importance in between. No, single-node/instance performance can BOTH be important AND achieved on the JVM. It's all a matter of degree and other tradeoffs. In the world we live in, concurrency in a single instance is important. Not everybody has huge multi-instance problems, and even those that do would like to be able to use less instances for their loads. > You want to win the Lisp-community-at-larg"},{"id":2978495,"author":"kevinpet","month":"2011-09","story":"Algo trading rapidly replacing humans, warns government paper","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Relevant Monty Python","text":"Parasitic HFT that doesn't actually provide liquidity is a consequence of rules that are designed to provide liquidity. Think of what you would \"expect\" if things were working right. Am I going to try to sell something at the exact moment a human needs to buy? No, I'm going to sell to a dealer who will hold it for a bit and then sell to a human who wants to buy. The dealer is purely algorithmic, so he may as well be replaced by a computer. From the article \"On the positive side of electronic trading, the panel said, liquidity has improved, transaction costs are lower, and market efficiency is generally better.\" Relevant Monty Python: [VIDEO]"}]},"FxcT4vK01-w":{"wy":"HN points to it as the primary source for Backus's ideas on programming beyond the von Neumann style, in threads about dead technologies and obscure languages.","m":[{"id":49324312,"author":"metazip","month":"2026-08","story":"History of John Backus's functional programming project [draft]","stance":"reference","why":"Backus presenting function level programming and FL","quote":"","text":"Videos to FL and FP: John Backus: Function Level Programming and the FL Language 1987 ( [VIDEO] ), John Backus Group Meeting - IBM Research - 5 July 1989 ( [LINK] ), Backus on functional programming ( [LINK] ) from Original: Oral History of John Backus ( [LINK] )"},{"id":33409840,"author":"mlajtos","month":"2022-10","story":"Show HN: A small, weird and unpractical programming language","stance":"reference","why":"Backus talk on FL, successor to FP","quote":"","text":"I see FP [0] (and FL [1,2]) as continuations of the original APL vision – an algebra for programs. It is really nice to see this implementation of FP and I hope it will spark interest in the community. :) [0]: [LINK] [1]: [LINK] [2]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":20376076,"author":"maxharris","month":"2019-07","story":"Ask HN: What's a technology that died that you wished was still here","stance":"reference","why":"Backus presenting the FL language","quote":"","text":"John Backus's FL: [VIDEO] IBM research really should release the FL compiler they wrote! They stopped working on it in the early 90s, and haven't said a single thing about it since. There is the beginnings of a Haskell implementation that I tried to mess with a bit, but I don't know enough Haskell to get anywhere: [LINK]"}]},"MIHC4NNScEI":{"wy":"HN refers to it as a compact source for the term HyperNormalisation and the idea of a constructed fake world.","q":{"x":"I watched an interesting edit of Adam Curtis explaining the term on Youtube too.","i":42831832,"y":"2025"},"m":[{"id":44151784,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2025-06","story":"YouTube Is Swallowing TV Whole, and It's Coming for the Sitcom","stance":"reference","why":"Cited as the source of the term and its description","quote":"","text":"> The reality is it was an entirely fake constructed world designed by advertisers, and to some lesser degree, the political class Did anyone else hear this narrated in Adam Curtis's voice, or is that just me? [LINK] > HyperNormalisation is a 2016 BBC documentary by British filmmaker Adam Curtis. It argues that following the global economic crises of the 1970s, governments, financiers and technological utopians gave up on trying to shape the complex \"real world\" and instead established a simpler \"fake world\" for the benefit of multi-national corporations that is kept stable by neoliberal governments. [VIDEO] HyperNormalisation explained by Adam Curtis [LINK] HyperNormalisation (2016)"},{"id":44429112,"author":"aspenmayer","month":"2025-06","story":"Therapy dogs: stop crafting loopholes to fair, reasonable laws","stance":"reference","why":"Cited for the concept of a constructed fake world","quote":"","text":"> If maga were both sincere and intelligent then they'd be actual nazis. They lack the second quality so they just spout ineffectually in public spaces thinking they'll get support. If they knew history they'd understand that's not a viable strategy. Ya need a lot more than that to build authoritarianism bro. Their egos won’t let them go full nazi. Cognitive dissonance is overcome via suspension of disbelief through self-hypnosis at home and collectively online and at rallies. Their need to appear respectable prevents how deep down the rabbit hole that most are willing to go, which is why they lean in to extremism, because it alienates followers from moderating influences of family and friends who are not already indoctrinated. It’s a cult of personality. The liberals are baited for clicks and impressions, and the left are again sidelined until the next time they need to be chided for being insufficiently centrist by liberals. Non-extremists on both sides become the controlled opposition relative to each other vis-à-vis the political parties proper. We’re tilting at windmills while digital sharecroppers toil on company plantations, their blood from stones mere grist for the blood money paper mill. [LINK] [LINK] HyperNormalisation (2016) > In Russia, Vladimir Putin and his cabinet of political technologists create mass confusion. Vladislav Surkov uses ideas from art to turn Russian politics into a bewildering piece of theatre. Donald Trump employs the same techniques in his pr"},{"id":42831832,"author":"jbm","month":"2025-01","story":"One word that explains why the world feels 'deeply off' for so many people","stance":"reference","why":"Edit of Curtis explaining the term","quote":"I watched an interesting edit of Adam Curtis explaining the term on Youtube too.","text":"Yeah, thanks, but it's Hypernormalization. By chance (or the algo realizing I read HN) I watched an interesting edit of Adam Curtis explaining the term on Youtube too. [VIDEO]"},{"id":27754455,"author":"psychomugs","month":"2021-07","story":"America’s Obsession with Self-Help","stance":"reference","why":"Curtis discussing shallow individualism and religion filling the void","quote":"religion will rear its head at some point to fill this void","text":"I've been binging Adam Curtis documentaries recently, and the unifying thread has been the rise of individualism and its being coopted by commerce. In a discussion regarding HyperNormalization [1], he argued that we're beginning to realize how shallow this individualism is, and religion will rear its head at some point to fill this void. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"OoETMCosULQ":{"wy":"Used as a demonstration of how everyone obeying the speed limit disrupts traffic flow.","q":{"x":"some students made a video[0] demonstrating the extreme effect on traffic when drivers in every lane maintain the speed limit","i":41109860,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":41109860,"author":"Zak","month":"2024-07","story":"Future Ford's May Detect Speeding and Report You to the Cops","stance":"reference","why":"Students show effect on traffic of everyone following the speed limit.","quote":"some students made a video[0] demonstrating the extreme effect on traffic when drivers in every lane maintain the speed limit","text":"That's essentially the situation on the highways through Atlanta. It's so extreme that some students made a video[0] demonstrating the extreme effect on traffic when drivers in every lane maintain the speed limit. Their conclusion is that the speed limit should be increased; there are other reasonable conclusions one might draw, but I would argue against the one that follows from your comment: that nearly every driver on the road deserves punishment. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":15323018,"author":"u801e","month":"2017-09","story":"What happens if you turn off the traffic lights?","stance":"reference","why":"Video of drivers protesting by obeying the speed limit near Atlanta.","quote":"Some people actually did this on an interstate near Atlanta, Georgia a number of years ago and posted it on Youtube","text":"> I remember when they adopted the 55 mph speed limit. Some truck drivers in Michigan decided to protest it by obeying it. The resulting congestion was pretty painful for everybody. Some people actually did this on an interstate near Atlanta, Georgia a number of years ago and posted it on Youtube [1]. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":6478357,"author":"steamer25","month":"2013-10","story":"How traffic actually works","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I used to be skeptical of ramp metering until the day I drove several miles below the speed limit to 'celebrate' the anniversary of a ticket I'd received. Instead of driving 14 MPH over the limit, I drove 14 MPH under the limit (legally that time) as a form of radical civil obedience* . As expected, this caused quite a bit of unnecessary congestion. It actually turned out to be harder to sustain a backup* * once I entered the metered areas. The reason? Metering limits the number of idiots on the road at a given time. I.e., my attempt at being intentionally stupid was mitigated because the meters prevented the road from nearing saturation. The little bit of wiggle room on the road made it relatively straight-forward to just route around me. So yes, in one sense meters merely move the wait from one point to another but they also remove some of the interactive effects of human stupidity. * [VIDEO] \"I know no method to secure the repeal of bad or obnoxious laws so effectual as their strict construction.\" -- President Ulysses S. Grant's First Inaugural Address * * No I did not intentionally prevent people from passing me. My beef is with the revenue centers that are the pre-emptive enforcement of low velocities--not with my fellow passer-motorists."}]},"RRh0QiXyZSk":{"wy":"Used as a joke reference in threads about debt, housing, and exploitative economic arrangements.","m":[{"id":47337101,"author":"amiga386","month":"2026-03","story":"Roblox Is Minting Teen Millionaires","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"[VIDEO] You load sixteen tons, what do you get? Another day older and deeper in debt Saint Peter don't you call me, 'cause I can't go I owe my soul to the company store"},{"id":20234283,"author":"umadon","month":"2019-06","story":"Investors Are Buying More of the U.S. Housing Market Than Ever Before","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Here's how you perpetrate a wealth transfer from average homeowners to the rich: 1. Deregulate mortgage lending to cause a housing bubble. 2. When it bursts, bail out the banks, and let people lose their homes, resulting in the homes being transferred to banks and landlords, causing more speculation and price increases. 3. Meanwhile, make sure the proportion of economic growth going to workers is kept minimal, even as the stock market booms. 4. Now, these workers not only have to sell their labor to the rich for paltry returns, have their retirement (if any) invested by the rich, borrow from the rich to go to college, but also rent from them in perpetuity. [VIDEO]"},{"id":12307741,"author":"cleaver","month":"2016-08","story":"New Startup Aims to Commercialize a Brain Prosthetic to Improve Memory","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"The 21st century version of \"16 tons\"[1] becomes \"16 terabytes\" and the refrain becomes: \"I owe my soul to the company stor[age]\". [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"Rw8gE3lnpLQ":{"wy":"Only used as a joke reference when threads feature recursive or nested games and simulations.","m":[{"id":32095088,"author":"halgir","month":"2022-07","story":"Doom hacker gets Doom running in Doom","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Introducing World of World of Warcraft","text":"Reminded me of an Onion skit which is apparently 14 years old now! Introducing World of World of Warcraft: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23188395,"author":"peterkelly","month":"2020-05","story":"Show HN: Virtual Reality DOS","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"we can have World of World of Warcraft, as promoted by The Onion","text":"If they can get this running windows, we can have World of World of Warcraft, as promoted by The Onion: [VIDEO]"},{"id":16710992,"author":"colanderman","month":"2018-03","story":"'PC Building Simulator' Is More Fun Than Building a Real Computer","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"No doubt inspired by the amazing My Summer Car [1]. And presaged by the classic Onion videos \"World of World of Warcraft\" [2] and \"Call of Duty Modern Warfare 3\" [3]. [1] [LINK] [2] [VIDEO] [3] [LINK]"}]},"Vxq9yj2pVWk":{"wy":"Used as a joke or critique whenever someone expects impossible image upscaling or recovery of detail.","m":[{"id":44006322,"author":"amelius","month":"2025-05","story":"Bringing 3D shoppable products online with generative AI","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Yes but by necessity it is making up stuff. It reminds me of: [VIDEO] and in particular the line \"enlarge the Z-axis\""},{"id":35737225,"author":"miles","month":"2023-04","story":"Stability AI releases Image Upscaling API with 2 open-source models behind it","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"will never fail to entertain ;-)","text":"“Enhance!”[0] may no longer qualify as comedy, but this[1] will never fail to entertain ;-) [0] [VIDEO] [1] [LINK]"},{"id":29569658,"author":"athenot","month":"2021-12","story":"A deep dive into an NSO zero-click iMessage exploit: Remote Code Execution","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Joking aside","text":"Then you can \"Enhance\". [VIDEO] Joking aside, this does illustrate the \"magical\" properties of technology to the layperson. As a corollary, failure modes end up quite suprising and hard to reason about without a certain amount of proficiency in these technologies."},{"id":23628797,"author":"danso","month":"2020-06","story":"Facial Recognition Leads To False Arrest Of Black Man In Detroit","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"believe in CSI magic","text":"What's sad is that a tech entrepreneur will definitely add that feature and sell it to law enforcement agencies that believe in CSI magic: [VIDEO]"},{"id":13721654,"author":"npunt","month":"2017-02","story":"Runner Disqualified After Claiming 2nd Place in Fort Lauderdale Half Marathon","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(enhance.. enhance.. [1])","text":"I didn't mean 'obsessive' as a smear, more that they were dogged in their pursuit, going as far as crunching biometrics and zooming in on a watch for evidence (enhance.. enhance.. [1]). It was an entertaining read since it looked eerily similar to how conspiracy sites construct their hilarious conclusions. And yet, it was real. Also, 'relatively innocent' was meant in context of buying instagram followers, not in cheating. > I'm more disturbed with the evident impulse to forgive. Is nothing important any more? Is character and honesty so worthless? The thing I'm most disturbed by in these threads is how easily nuance is lost to the power of righteous indignation. It's like we're reading an entirely different set of comments. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":8842813,"author":"Brajeshwar","month":"2015-01","story":"Identifiable Images of Bystanders Extracted from Corneal Reflections (2013)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I hope I can tuck in this video as a gentle reminder of what we wishes we can do.","text":"I hope I can tuck in this video as a gentle reminder of what we wishes we can do. Now that we are pretty much there. [VIDEO]"},{"id":7411293,"author":"dictum","month":"2014-03","story":"Monsanto Owns Us: The Monopoly of Seeds and Intellectual Property Rights","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I think you stumbled into the biotech equivalent of CSI picture enhancement[0]","text":"> The correct thing would be to do is create a chemical that kills all unwanted stuff and leaves the wanted stuff unharmed I think you stumbled into the biotech equivalent of CSI picture enhancement[0] or unlimited data storage. [0]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":2035596,"author":"sp332","month":"2010-12","story":"Pixel Art Scaling Algorithms","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Which was making fun of... well, all of these","text":"\"Uncrop\" isn't from Blade Runner, it's Red Dwarf. [LINK] Which was making fun of... well, all of these: [VIDEO] :-) And everyone knows that Deckard was looking at a live feed from a video camera, not enhancing a still image."},{"id":1192676,"author":"novas0x2a","month":"2010-03","story":"26-gigapixel panorama of Paris; try zooming in near the horizon","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Gigapan.org has a snapshot feature for that. 26-gigapixel is bigger than most (all?) of what Gigapan has, but they have a bunch more. The Obama inauguration one[1] is probably their most famous. I don't really ever get tired of being able to \"enhance! enhance!\"[2] an image. 1) [LINK] 2) [VIDEO]"},{"id":4681615,"author":"jere","month":"2012-10","story":"Restoration of defocused and blurred images","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"trope is still completely stupid.","text":"The whole \"Enhance\" trope is still completely stupid. [VIDEO] [LINK] >But the Enhance Button simply ignores the fact that the big blocky pixels you get when you zoom in too close on a picture are the only information that the picture actually contains, and attempting to extract more detail than this is fundamentally impossible. No matter what you do or how you do it, you're merely guessing, if not making stuff up outright."},{"id":1301085,"author":"btilly","month":"2010-04","story":"CSI Effect: How Forensic Science on TV Impacts Our Real Justice System","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"here is what the public thinks image manipulation can do","text":"Thanks to CSI and relatives, here is what the public thinks image manipulation can do: [VIDEO] The ridiculousness may be lost on you if you need an explanation why a digital image of a crappy security camera can't be used to zoom in on a reflection of an eyeball. Or of why a 2-dimensional image can't be used to construct a 3-dimensional image of the scene, that you can rotate to see objects out of the line of view of the camera."}]},"XYcxvEfUikg":{"wy":"HN uses it as a recurring example of Microsoft leadership dismissing a product that later proved significant.","m":[{"id":4129289,"author":"ja27","month":"2012-06","story":"Microsoft Introduces New \"Surface\" Tablet","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I just flashed back to Ballmer mocking the MacBook Air 4 years ago.","text":"I just flashed back to Ballmer mocking the MacBook Air 4 years ago. I guess he did want one after all. [VIDEO]"},{"id":3354339,"author":"yequalsx","month":"2011-12","story":"The fall of Microsoft's Andy Lees: inside the Windows Phone power shift","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He also laughed about the Macbook Air [3].","text":"Yes, the problem is Ballmer. He is extremely shortsighted. Witness his scoffing at the iPhone when it was introduced [1]. Then watch his claim that the iPad is just another PC form factor [2]. He also laughed about the Macbook Air [3]. Of course, now there is a push for ultrabooks from Intel. I saw a Microsoft commercial not too long ago and the lost shot showed a PC and it was a Samsung laptop that looked just like a Macbook Pro. It's a good thing that Metro is not just a copy of iOS. The sooner they fire Ballmer the better. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [VIDEO]"},{"id":3131501,"author":"raganwald","month":"2011-10","story":"Is Ballmer delusional? Calls Android 'cheap,' doomed","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he ought to remember how well things turned out the last time he laughed at something","text":"I agree that his VPs might be blowing sunshine up his arse, but that is no excuse. It’s his JOB to be such a great leader of managers that he knows how to get them to tell him the truth. It’s his job to read tons of positive press and reports and pick out the warning signs and the possible problems. It’s his job to be able to pick up one of those “cheap phones” and remember how Microsoft and cheap PCs beat Apple within an inch of death. And if nothing else, he ought to remember how well things turned out the last time he laughed at something: [VIDEO]"}]},"_K-L9uhsBLM":{"wy":"Used as a punchline whenever HN threads touch on terse communication, token-saving prompts or verbose writing.","m":[{"id":47650763,"author":"itpcc","month":"2026-04","story":"Talk like caveman","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"But will it lose some context, like Kevin’s small talk?","text":"But will it lose some context, like Kevin’s small talk? ( [VIDEO] ) Like \"Sea world\" or \"see the world\"."},{"id":47177054,"author":"BLKNSLVR","month":"2026-02","story":"Layoffs at Block","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Someone needs to have this conversation with him","text":"Bad grammar is usually lack of care or education or knowledge. 100% lower case is 100% a choice. Thanks jack dorsey, for letting us know you're that sort of person. At least he refers to himself that way too, although he should sign off with: jack off. Someone needs to have this conversation with him: [VIDEO]"},{"id":44776812,"author":"Waterluvian","month":"2025-08","story":"Tokens are getting more expensive","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Kevin speak good?","text":"On the topic of cost per token, is it accurate to represent a token as, ideally, a composable atomic unit of information. But because we’re (often) using English as the encoding format, it can only be as efficient as English can encode the data. Does this mean that other languages might offer better information density per token? And does this mean that we could invent a language that’s more efficient for these purposes, and something humans (perhaps only those who want a job as a prompt engineer) could be taught? Kevin speak good? [VIDEO]"},{"id":35489031,"author":"petethepig","month":"2023-04","story":"Show HN: Compress GPT-4 Prompts","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"why waste time say lot word when few word do trick","text":"Reminds me of the episode from the Office: \"why waste time say lot word when few word do trick\" [0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":32823435,"author":"benjaminjosephw","month":"2022-09","story":"Work is work, in which returns diminish (2020)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"why waste time say lot word when few word do trick?","text":"Is ROI in this case a measure of comprehension? What about the enjoyment and playfulness of metaphor and expressive language - do these have a negative ROI if they aren't sufficiently terse? How far do you take efficiency as a measure of communication quality? Did Kevin have it right when he asks \"why waste time say lot word when few word do trick?\"[0] [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":18445472,"author":"PavlovsCat","month":"2018-11","story":"Ask HN: What's the largest amount of bad code you have ever seen work?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"a lot of \"communication\" on the web these days is kinda like the \"small talk\" from Kevin from The Office US","text":"Maybe, but at least it's more or less clear what I mean right away :P A million words conveying one unit of meaning are still more efficient than ten words conveying no meaning. I just thought about that when I saw a youtube comment saying \"stolen\" in response to a funny joke. I wondered, do they mean they intend to \"steal it\" because it's a good joke, or did they mean the person who posted the joke stole it from somewhere? Nobody will ever know, at least I for one won't sign in just to ask. I notice that since mobile devices, a lot of \"communication\" on the web these days is kinda like the \"small talk\" from Kevin from The Office US. [VIDEO] If I hadn't asked what you meant, I and anyone else who read your comment would have had their own interpretation of it. I see a lot of comments like that on HN, where you would have to ask \"what do you mean?\" because it's totally unclear. A variation is stating something that is factually true but doesn't really refute anything, but the commenter clearly seems to mean something by stating that triviality, but don't say what it is. It's like dog whistles, but not for others to hear, but only the posters themselves know what they mean. Count me out."}]},"_K0tXOBPMHA":{"wy":"HN uses it as a reference for footage of shootings in the CHOP zone, cited as evidence in political arguments.","m":[{"id":29760985,"author":"throw_nbvc1234","month":"2022-01","story":"Will Digital Technologies Kill the Nation State?","stance":"reference","why":"Surveillance footage of the shooting that ended CHOP.","quote":"the whole thing was caught on surveillance footage","text":"It took less then 2 days to shutdown chaz/chop once it became politically feasible to do so. As soon as those 2 kids were shot (1 killed) in the jeep by chop's security and the whole thing was caught on surveillance footage [1] it was over. It's hard to watch that footage and come away with \"yeah lets keep this exactly the way it is\". [1] [VIDEO] - 12th and Pine in Seattle if you want to lookup the area on google maps."},{"id":25696210,"author":"neartheplain","month":"2021-01","story":"Archivists Are Preserving Capitol Hill Riot Livestreams Before They’re Deleted","stance":"reference","why":"Disturbing footage of CHOP shooting cited in argument.","quote":"","text":">It's not even in downtown, lmao. \"killing and assaulting people\". What a joke. Anti-fascist activists murdered an unarmed black kid in the CHOP. They shot him to death with assault rifles. Then they shot and pistol-whipped his critically wounded companion, an unarmed 14-year-old black boy (warning, video is disturbing): [VIDEO] This was one of at least four shootings in the CHOP. Bullets entered the homes of neighborhood residents, a group of whom are now suing the city for neglecting to provide law and order."},{"id":25732598,"author":"neartheplain","month":"2021-01","story":"70TB of Parler users’ messages, videos, and posts leaked by security researchers","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of a shooting in CHOP cited as evidence.","quote":"It's on camera. The killers audibly confessed to the murder.","text":"Please don't use the passive voice [0] to intentionally obscure a crime's perpetrator. Antonio Mays Jr. was not killed near a protest by a random passerby. He was murdered by armed \"anti-fascist\" protestors. They shot him in the face with a rifle. It's on camera. The killers audibly confessed to the murder. See this link [1] for the clips, if you must. The footage is disturbing. [0] [LINK] [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":25084494,"author":"_iyig","month":"2020-11","story":"They Thought They Were Free: The Germans, 1933-45 (1955)","stance":"reference","why":"Footage of a shooting in the Seattle CHOP zone.","quote":"This murder was filmed from multiple angles and recordings are widely available.","text":"You are probably referring to a separate shooting death in CHOP, that of Lorenzo Anderson. I am referring to the murder of Antonio Mays Jr. by antifa security. This murder was filmed from multiple angles and recordings are widely available. Short clips from several of the vantage points (skip to roughly 1:40 - warning, disturbing): [VIDEO]"}]},"acLW1vFO-2Q":{"wy":"Quoted as shorthand for cynicism about education and the American establishment.","m":[{"id":23471079,"author":"som33","month":"2020-06","story":"Fake SpaceX YouTube channel with 36k live viewers scams people out of money","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":">Who exactly falls for this kind of scam? Uhh the same people who fall for the scam that was steam, uplay, mmo's, etc... aka it's irrational to buy software you don't own or control. If anything the modern software landscape is an idiocracy. Windows 10 \"requires internet\" (wtf?) I mean come on, anything that \"requires interent\" is a giant red flag. The steam stats prove we live in an idiocracy. Don't think so? Silicion valley tech companies are over the moon that the public bought into mmo's/drm/f2p mobile gambling games en mass. They finally are going to close the PC as an open platform, which started with merely renaming PC RPG's in the 90's and calling them \"MMO's\" then they started applying that fake moniker to every game they wanted to client server to steal them from the public to deny ownership. Whole industry is looking to setup the internte (aka the world comptuer) to trap software between the public pc's and their servers, so we get incomplete software in the future \"requiring login\" to remote servers (aka stolen software as a service). [LINK] Steam software survey - windows 10 at 85% [LINK] Windows 10 privacy invading, drm defective app model (uwp, encrypted vm's). Making preserving old software using UWP/encryption impossible (aka encrypted files). The average person in our society both working class and professional is dumb. I've talked to programmers who've bought overwatch or sent money to quake champions, not realizing that their mtx money told the industry to"},{"id":20734877,"author":"monster99","month":"2019-08","story":"Why America’s biggest charities are owned by pharmaceutical companies","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin on the education establishment.","quote":"George carlin said it best","text":"That's kind of the point George carlin said it best: [VIDEO] The whole educational establishment is to create an ignorant slave class."},{"id":9763508,"author":"mindcrash","month":"2015-06","story":"Why are American schools slowing down so many bright children?","stance":"reference","why":"Carlin on why education will never be fixed.","quote":"George Carlin said it best","text":"> the entire system isn't geared towards that George Carlin said it best: [VIDEO] \"But there’s a reason. There’s a reason. There’s a reason for this, there’s a reason education SUCKS, and it’s the same reason it will never, ever, EVER be fixed . It’s never going to get any better, don’t look for it, be happy with what you’ve got. Because the owners, the owners of this country don't want that. I'm talking about the real owners now, the BIG owners! The Wealthy… the REAL owners! The big wealthy business interests that control things and make all the important decisions. Forget the politicians. They are irrelevant. The politicians are put there to give you the idea that you have freedom of choice. You don't. You have no choice! You have OWNERS! They OWN YOU. They own everything. They own all the important land. They own and control the corporations. They’ve long since bought, and paid for the Senate, the Congress, the state houses, the city halls, they got the judges in their back pockets and they own all the big media companies, so they control just about all of the news and information you get to hear. They got you by the balls. They spend billions of dollars every year lobbying, lobbying, to get what they want. Well, we know what they want. They want more for themselves and less for everybody else, but I'll tell you what they don’t want: They don’t want a population of citizens capable of critical thinking. They don’t want well informed, well educated people capable of critica"},{"id":7793292,"author":"doxydexydroxide","month":"2014-05","story":"Ask HN: living and working in US","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A 3rd Generation US born Citizen turns his back on the USA:living the American dream in Vienna, Austria: Lived in Silicon Valley for 20 years founding 4 companies in the process. I am an honest and decent person who was raped each and every time by my co-founders/investors. Only the soulless/ruthless succeed in Silicon Valley. SILICON VALLEY IS A TRAP! Everything is dried out and dead, including the people. Everything is about money, and everybody including your employer has their hands in your wallet. Child care is hideous. People are shallow and self centered. Homeless camps are everywhere, and there is no empathy/compassion for these older \"former heroes of high tech\" fucked over by the Vulture Crapitalists masters. Work weeks of 80 hours are the norm. Your company owns your soul. Schools are 3rd world. You have no life. Three hours sitting in traffic to get to the beach. No Culture. East Palo Alto is being bought up and the people tossed out on the street. Crime rate is now \"off the scale\" in both Menlo Park and Palo Alto (Facebook paid to construct and operate a Menlo Park Police Station just to protect its corporate HQ campus). Lived in Boulder Colorado for 3 years. Closest thing one can find to living in Europe in North America. Fell in love with mountain climbing. Primary reason I chose to move my wife and 3 young children to Vienna Austria 11 years ago (easy access to the Alps). I am giving up my US Citizenship this year to become an Austrian. I was back visiting my "}]},"b3dYS7PcAG4":{"wy":"Posted as a joke reply to complaints about air travel and airport security.","m":[{"id":30362728,"author":"mLuby","month":"2022-02","story":"Love is biological bribery?","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"partaking in the miracle of human flight\"[1]","text":"Well-said. Airplanes are relatively straightforward machines. Computers just do trivial math and logic very fast. Listening to Bach in a cathedral is just hearing metal tubes vibrating air in an artificial cave. But if you've never stopped and wondered that you're \"partaking in the miracle of human flight\"[1] or that the block of metal and sand in your desk lets you see loved ones across time and space, or that countless people worked for ages so you could experience the music and art in that building, well you're missing out. Humans are lucky that we experience both understanding and awe. [1] [VIDEO] (CW:LCK)"},{"id":12411197,"author":"rdtsc","month":"2016-09","story":"Joe Sutter, who guided creation of the Boeing 747, has died","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"I aways remember this bit by Louis CK about flying. It is just so true.","text":"> I still feel the same childish joy every time I feel the plane leave the ground -- we're actually flying! I aways remember this bit by Louis CK about flying. It is just so true. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9181087,"author":"nwenzel","month":"2015-03","story":"What's your chance of getting a U.S. work visa? Find out with BorderScore","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"(nsfw-language: Louis CK bit)","text":"How does 24 hours and no cost compare to the time and cost without this utility? [VIDEO] (nsfw-language: Louis CK bit)"},{"id":6988180,"author":"Aqueous","month":"2013-12","story":"I flew on a plane without going through security. It was amazing and no one died","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Everytime someone complains about security at the airport -","text":"I mean, I think the point of all security is to reduce the total numbers of deaths from terrorist attacks overall, not just per means of transportation. That said, the fact of the matter is that the article doesn't back up its claim that airport security doesn't reduce terrorist casualties overall. Instead it states some bizarre equivalence between the decline in the number of hijackings (-6.9) and the increase in the number of instances of other kinds of terrorism (+6.8). But clearly other forms of terrorism do not cause nearly as much death as plane hijackings do, which for each one numbers in the hundreds, so we could be talking about an integer factor decrease in the number of deaths from terrorist attacks - 4x, 5x, 6x - by merely replacing them with other, less destructive terrorism. After all, the relative destructiveness of a plane hijacking is WHY terrorists do it at all. Suddenly airport security seems to look a lot more effective and a lot more worth it. So obviously they didn't mention casualties at all, since it would undermine the point they are trying to make. Everytime someone complains about security at the airport - [VIDEO] People are enjoying an incredible privilege being able to fly in the air."}]},"b9j-sfP9GUU":{"wy":"HN cites it to show how seriously root key ceremonies are secured and to let people watch one.","q":{"x":"The key ceremony is video taped and can be watched","i":7690152,"y":"2014"},"m":[{"id":7690152,"author":"news_embargo","month":"2014-05","story":"Fedora 21 To Have DNSSEC Validation Enabled By Default","stance":"reference","why":"Key ceremony is video taped and can be watched.","quote":"The key ceremony is video taped and can be watched","text":"I thought your first comment was just grumbling, but this comment suggests some misunderstanding. The root zsk is split over a group (Shamir share) held by mostly non-US key holders. (One from the US is Dan Kaminsky, whose work and my user name has special relevancy for you. You'd agree he's not part of the US government.) I believe all the key holders are trustworthy, and if any were not, you'd need a majority (5 of the 7) to subvert the zsk. So an xkcd-wrench-style attack on the key by governments would require implausible circumstances. The key ceremony is video taped and can be watched: [VIDEO] Other TLDs (some being countries) also hold key signing ceremonies and video tape them. You can watch other countries perform key signing as well. But these sovereign zsks are subordinate to the ICANN community held keys. I.e., it's more accurate (than your statement) to say that there are seven people walking the earth who have parent keys over actual country soveriegn zones. And while you might not know all these people yourself, they're from the DNS operator and hacker community. If anything, you're 180 degrees wrong in your statement that governments run DNSSEC. You can read more about the DNSSEC root zone key management process and people (non-governmental) online. Here are informal press stories with the usual set of mistakes and minor errors: [LINK] [LINK] And the root DNSSEC operation in general: [LINK] I don't see the government in DNSSEC. Not even ICANN can alter the root"},{"id":6423596,"author":"jackweirdy","month":"2013-09","story":"Let’s Take Back The Certificate Authority","stance":"reference","why":"Summary of the entire key ceremony video.","quote":"There's a video of the entire ceremony online somewhere","text":"Well we have a hell of a lot more transparency about where that key is, who generated and has access to it. There's a video of the entire ceremony online somewhere, at the moment I can only find [this summary]( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":5892730,"author":"MacsHeadroom","month":"2013-06","story":"PRISM update","stance":"reference","why":"Shows how secure root key generation ceremonies are.","quote":"CA Certificate generation is about as secure as the papal election","text":"Not very likely. CA Certificate generation is about as secure as the papal election (read one of the most secure proceedings in existence). See [VIDEO]"}]},"ekg45ub8bsk":{"wy":"Used mostly as a joking reference to overstimulating media and attention-grabbing advertising.","m":[{"id":29324094,"author":"narrator","month":"2021-11","story":"The Max Headroom Incident","stance":"reference","why":"Clip of the fictional ads that made viewers explode.","quote":"They were advertisements that make old people watching them explode.","text":"Since we are bringing up Max Headroom, I would like to put a word in for Blipverts. These were part of the fictional subject matter of the first Max Headroom TV Show. They were advertisements that make old people watching them explode. Bryce, the engineer behind these adverts, uses the wonderful line, \"I only invent the bomb, I don't drop it.\" This was amazingly prescient back in the 80s given where we are now in adtech. [VIDEO]"},{"id":22809155,"author":"donclark","month":"2020-04","story":"Is Covid-19 a Geopolitical Game-Changer?","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I know, this type of talk makes it seem like the Max Headroom blipverts are the future.","text":"Is Covid-19 a Geopolitical Game-Changer? I hope and pray that this is the case. This may be the best time for us to make a change in the way the world works. Our lives have been disrupted, and we need to strive to make lasting changes for the greater good. The internet, and our world is over-commercialized. The pandemic causing an economic re-evaluation which may be the best opportunity we have to re-evaluate our position. If not, and you think the advertiser/marketer/retail market was invasive before... you aint seen nothing yet. They will need to try every trick in the book x10 - repeatedly, at lighting speed - and invent new tactics to boot. I know, this type of talk makes it seem like the Max Headroom blipverts are the future. [VIDEO]"},{"id":46297725,"author":"mapontosevenths","month":"2025-12","story":"Thin desires are eating life","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Maybe it will be Max Headroom's blipverts?","text":"When I was young my parents were scared that the MTV generation couldn't focus long enough to watch the \"real news\". Not long ago I feared that twitters short form content was shortening peoples attention spans so much that they would stop being able to appreciate nuance at all... Then came TikTok. I don't know what comes next, but I promise you it will be worse. Either way, it's a race to the bottom and we're not there yet. Maybe it will be Max Headroom's blipverts? [VIDEO]"},{"id":33887892,"author":"nsxwolf","month":"2022-12","story":"Stop that It’s not Tourette’s but a new type of mass sociogenic illness","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"TV commercials that were so stimulating they would cause some viewers to explode.","text":"This reminds me of \"Blipverts\" on the 80s TV show \"Max Headroom\". TV commercials that were so stimulating they would cause some viewers to explode. [VIDEO]"}]},"gUkbdjetlY8":{"wy":"Used as a joke or cautionary example of confident financial punditry that proved wrong, often referenced during bank scares and bubbles.","m":[{"id":49021137,"author":"toomuchtodo","month":"2026-07","story":"Alphabet's cash burn raises alarm for Big Tech as AI spending climbs","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"The top will be when Jim Cramer loudly proclaims there is no problem at Oracle and gives a buy rating.","text":"The top will be when Jim Cramer loudly proclaims there is no problem at Oracle and gives a buy rating. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35091638,"author":"bink","month":"2023-03","story":"Bank Run at Silicon Valley Bank","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"it does conjure to mind the video of Cramer saying Bear Stearns was fine.","text":"Maybe not that exact phrase but it does conjure to mind the video of Cramer saying Bear Stearns was fine. [VIDEO]"},{"id":35000966,"author":"gjsman-1000","month":"2023-03","story":"The Inverse Jim Cramer ETF Has Officially Arrived","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"The fact that he still had a job after his 2008 mistake","text":"The fact that he still had a job after his 2008 mistake of insisting that Bear Stearns was fine and a safe place to keep your cash, is astounding. That should've been an unrecoverable mistake, especially with how strongly he was certain. \"Should I be worried about Bear Stearns in terms of liquidity and get my money out of there? No, NO, NO!!!\" (Afterwards... 90% loss in the next 6 days.) [VIDEO]"},{"id":3123621,"author":"dennisgorelik","month":"2011-10","story":"Bitcoin value falls from above $33 to below $3","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"Note that Jim Cramer is still running his show.","text":"Somehow Jim Cramer with his infamous Bear Sterns prediction comes to mind: [VIDEO] Note that Jim Cramer is still running his show."},{"id":1167446,"author":"adamhowell","month":"2010-03","story":"So what if subprime blows up? (July 16, 2007)","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"just 5 days before they were sold for $2 a share","text":"And of course Kramer followed up nuggets of wisdom like this with \"Bear Sterns is fine!\" just 5 days before they were sold for $2 a share: [VIDEO] I really thought his Daily Show appearance would be the beginning of the end for him, but obviously I overestimated the long-term memory of the average CNBC viewer."}]},"gX9cbxLSOkE":{"wy":"Linked as the original demonstration whenever battery-free or energy-harvesting wireless communication comes up.","q":{"x":"Here is a video showing a demonstration of both energy harvesting from ambient radio and backscatter data transmission [1].","i":37483192,"y":"2023"},"m":[{"id":37483192,"author":"tzs","month":"2023-09","story":"Sony develops energy harvesting module from electromagnetic wave noise","stance":"reference","why":"Demonstrates ambient radio energy harvesting and backscatter data transmission","quote":"Here is a video showing a demonstration of both energy harvesting from ambient radio and backscatter data transmission [1].","text":"It is not just ambient electrical noise that contains usable energy. Others have demonstrated practical sensor energy harvesting from ambient WiFi and ambient broadcast TV signals. I'd expect that the Sony device can probably use those too, so if you've got WiFi in your building you should be fine. If there is ambient radio, you can also use that for extremely low power data transmission from your sensors. The trick there is to not actually transmit any of your own energy. You instead either absorb or reflect the ambient radio. This is called backscatter communications. Here is a video showing a demonstration of both energy harvesting from ambient radio and backscatter data transmission [1]. If you use WiFi signals as your ambient radio for backscatter, the research group in that video has also demonstrated that you can make your backscatter data stream match standard WiFi protocols so that you can use ordinary WiFi equipment for the receiver. The above was fairly short range, but even so there are a lot of interesting applications. For instance you might want to have moisture sensors inside a wall to detect water leaks early. If your sensors are powered by ambient EM energy (noise or radio), you don't have to limit the sensors to places where you can easily reach them later to change batteries. They are fine if they are behind brick or drywall with no openings. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":27197019,"author":"tzs","month":"2021-05","story":"Spin-torque oscillators for WiFi band transmission and energy harvesting","stance":"reference","why":"2013 demo of battery-free ambient backscatter devices","quote":"Here's a video about it from 2013 [1] showing some test devices.","text":"If you don't need much range (a couple feet, say), you can use ambient backscatter which doesn't need to store up energy for bursts. Here's a video about it from 2013 [1] showing some test devices. Here's an article about it [2]. A bit later they extended this to make it work with off-the-shelf WiFi devices, so you could have a battery-free device that can communicate to ordinary WiFi devices [3]. Still short range, but now you could make it so the reader is a commodity WiFi device like a smartphone, instead of a specialty device. Here are some other researchers who demonstrated a backscatter tag that diddled with Bluetooth Low Energy (BLE) instead of WiFi, and was correctly received by an iPad at over 9 m using the existing iOS Bluetooth stack with no modifications [4]. And here is a battery-fee phone that communicates with a powered base station [5]. It works up to 30 ft away. The phone uses energy from ambient RF and small photodiodes. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":15864137,"author":"baldfat","month":"2017-12","story":"3-D printed plastic objects that can communicate with WiFi devices","stance":"reference","why":"Shows powering and communicating between devices without batteries","quote":"You could light up a led without any on board electricity and communicate between two devices","text":"No I quoting a fact not an opinion. Please read my comment and sources before claiming I am factually wrong. \"In this backscatter system, an antenna embedded in a 3-D printed object (middle) reflects radio signals emitted by a WiFi router (left) to encode information that is “read” by the WiFi receiver in a phone, computer or other device (right).University of Washington\" Screenshot: [LINK] > They're not magically encoding new Wi-Fi packets Yes, these are NEW signals powered by the radio signals that specifically in this article is wifi since it is so widely used. \"The chip uses a technique called long-range backscatter to communicate with other devices. Instead of creating signals from scratch, it is able to selectively reflect radio waves that are already passing through space to create a new signal.\" [LINK] Just because they are using residual radio signals (They could use TV, Wifi or FM signals) doesn't mean the new singals aren't new. They are just using the energy in the signal itself doesn't make them magic. Instead of using electricity from a battery or a cord they are using the power of the radio wave. They can even power themselves off of the Gollakota’s radio frequency power scavenging technology. You could light up a led without any on board electricity and communicate between two devices [VIDEO] Here is a speech about HitchHike: Practical Backscatter Using Commodity WiFi at (SenSys 2016) [LINK]"},{"id":11165454,"author":"lucaspiller","month":"2016-02","story":"Researchers create super-efficient Wi-Fi","stance":"reference","why":"Early demo of ambient backscatter communication by same researchers","quote":"The same researchers released a video demonstrating the basics of this (though not with WiFi) a few years ago","text":"The same researchers released a video demonstrating the basics of this (though not with WiFi) a few years ago: [VIDEO] [LINK]"}]},"ghdTqnYnFyg":{"wy":"Cited in threads about Apple's chip transitions as the primary source for the Intel switch announcement and the secret x86 builds.","q":{"x":"Apple had been building OS X on x86 for five years prior to announce of the last shift.","i":23467126,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":26208994,"author":"Kwpolska","month":"2021-02","story":"M1 Macs have another hidden boot mode","stance":"reference","why":"Keynote mentions secret x86 build of OS X","quote":"Apple mentioned the secret x86 build of OS X during the keynote when they announced that transition","text":"Apple mentioned the secret x86 build of OS X during the keynote when they announced that transition [1], I think they alluded to it in one of last year’s keynotes too. That said, you would expect them to have booting, installation, and wiping as one of the first things built in the macOS-on-ARM porting process. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":23814421,"author":"MangoCoffee","month":"2020-07","story":"Apple Silicon: The Passing of Wintel","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs revealing transition to Intel","quote":"Steve Jobs reveal transition to Intel","text":"i feel like i saw this movie before. ah yes, PowerPc. Steve Jobs reveal transition to Intel: [VIDEO] i don't know if the history will repeat itself but it will be interesting to watch."},{"id":23467126,"author":"bredren","month":"2020-06","story":"Apple Plans to Announce Move to Its Own Mac Chips at WWDC","stance":"reference","why":"Apple revealed secret x86 builds of OS X for five years","quote":"Apple had been building OS X on x86 for five years prior to announce of the last shift.","text":"Fwiw, Apple had been building OS X on x86 for five years prior to announce of the last shift. [VIDEO] Not sure how this impacts theory of neglect. Is it correct that the best folks from any given team at Apple are assigned to new, big initiatives?"},{"id":17139097,"author":"linguae","month":"2018-05","story":"OS X is as old as the classic Mac OS was when it came out","stance":"reference","why":"Jobs on the transition setting Apple up for 20 years","quote":"When Steve Jobs announced the transition from the PowerPC to the x86 architecture in 2005","text":"When Steve Jobs announced the transition from the PowerPC to the x86 architecture in 2005, he mentioned that the transition from Mac OS 9 to Mac OS X which took place between 2001 and 2003 set Apple up for the next 20 years. [VIDEO] It's 2018, 20 years since Apple first announced Mac OS X at WWDC 1998 as a change from their original Rhapsody strategy. The 20th anniversary of the actual release of Mac OS X for consumers is approaching in a few years. Now, I still believe that there is plenty of life in Mac OS X, and I could see it easily being usable at least throughout the 2020s, provided that Apple maintains it in the meanwhile. However, I definitely wouldn't mind a new desktop OS. Let's face it: modern desktops such as Windows, Mac OS X, GNOME and its derivatives like Cinnamon, and KDE are all based on the GUI paradigm developed at Xerox PARC for the Xerox Alto workstation. This paradigm was further developed by Apple for the Lisa and the original Macintosh, and it has been further refined by NeXTSTEP, Windows 95, and early versions of Mac OS X. However, there are other GUI paradigms that haven't been tried in desktop environments outside of research labs and niche environments. For example, Xerox PARC also developed the Smalltalk environment. While it is commonly thought of as a programming language, it is more than just that; it is an operating system running inside a VM where everything is an object. This environment could easily be used to write full-fledged application"},{"id":15734592,"author":"cybermancy","month":"2017-11","story":"iMac Pro Appears to Include A10 Fusion Chip for Always-On ‘Hey Siri’","stance":"reference","why":"Steve Jobs announcing the PowerPC to Intel switch","quote":"here's the \"transitions\" talk Steve Jobs gave announcing the switch from PowerPC to Intel processors.","text":"Will be interesting to see if native iOS apps will be able to run for development or regular use. This is a good sign for the convergence of the Mac and \"i\" products. For fun here's the \"transitions\" talk Steve Jobs gave announcing the switch from PowerPC to Intel processors. [VIDEO]"},{"id":9428626,"author":"baldfat","month":"2015-04","story":"Wi-Fi hack creates 'no iOS zone' that cripples iPhones and iPads","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"A dad know no end of petty. I have an Anti-Apple bias due to their business practices. Your to young but... The fastest computers are computers with PowerPC CPUs. than.... [VIDEO] The rest of the tech people were like how can a PowerPC CPU be faster? It is plain 1 and 0 and the more 1 and 0 you get through the faster the processor. It ended up being 4 to 6 times faster. The Apple fanboys were famous for this time of Apple history. Also Apple knew the truth since they were always running an x86 OS X for 5 years and knew the numbers and just were fine spreading \"Apple Truths.\" Sorry I digressed..."}]},"imkdz63agHY":{"wy":"Commenters cite it as vivid proof that pressure-stabilized rocket tanks can fail catastrophically when pressure is lost.","q":{"x":"a couple Atlas rockets did crumble under their own weight due to unexpected loss of pressure","i":23096233,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":48319291,"author":"oconnor663","month":"2026-05","story":"The most spectacular rocket explosion since N1 just happened in Florida","stance":"reference","why":"Rockets are barely strong enough to stand if fueled wrong.","quote":"sometimes they're barely strong enough to stand upright if you fuel them the wrong way","text":"The vehicle is designed to hold all that fuel, plus whatever payload it carries on top, but it's not designed to have heavy loads attached to it in any other way. Rockets are so intensely optimized for weight that sometimes they're barely strong enough to stand upright if you fuel them the wrong way: [VIDEO] ."},{"id":40248488,"author":"icegreentea2","month":"2024-05","story":"Goodyear Inflatoplane","stance":"reference","why":"Shows the failure of a pressure-stabilized tank collapsing.","quote":"I think this is the failure you're referring to","text":"Yes, the Centaur upper stage was and still is using balloon tanks. I think this is the failure you're referring to: [VIDEO] And here's a picture from ULA/Tony Bruno showing a Centaur tank in their factory, being supported by a rig: [LINK]"},{"id":31893898,"author":"mannykannot","month":"2022-06","story":"Whatever hit the Moon in March, it left a double crater","stance":"reference","why":"Atlas tanks needed pressurization; pressure loss can be costly.","quote":"a loss of pressure could be expensive...","text":"I think it is implied in the article that only the motor is massive enough to excavate a crater. The similarity in size of the craters suggests they were created by objects of similar mass. At least one early rocket design (Atlas) had fuel tanks so insubstantial that they had to be pressurized until they were loaded with fuel on the pad, and a loss of pressure could be expensive... [VIDEO]"},{"id":23096233,"author":"m4rtink","month":"2020-05","story":"SpaceX's Starship SN4 prototype fires rocket engine for first time","stance":"reference","why":"Atlas rockets collapsing under their own weight after losing pressure.","quote":"a couple Atlas rockets did crumble under their own weight due to unexpected loss of pressure","text":"For the record, a couple Atlas rockets did crumble under their own weight due to unexpected loss of pressure: [VIDEO] And some Atlas missiles on display are still being pressurized to this day: [LINK] Like really, there is a compressor next to the rocket that keeps constant pressure inside against the slight leaks. Pretty impressive for 40-50 old rocket left outside exposed to the elements to be still this airtight. :)"}]},"kwtt51gvaJQ":{"wy":"Linked as a simple, replicable demonstration of CO2's greenhouse effect, even in a classroom.","q":{"x":"you can replicate the effect easily in a high school classroom.","i":32367833,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":32367833,"author":"fallingfrog","month":"2022-08","story":"Using grape harvest dates to estimate summer temperature over 650 years","stance":"reference","why":"Greenhouse effect easily replicated in a classroom","quote":"you can replicate the effect easily in a high school classroom.","text":"No, you can replicate the effect easily in a high school classroom. [VIDEO] It’s literally that easy to demonstrate."},{"id":27883691,"author":"loopz","month":"2021-07","story":"Study: Global plant growth surging alongside carbon dioxide (2017)","stance":"reference","why":"Simple demo of CO2 greenhouse effect","quote":"","text":"Why not investigate starting with IPCC reports: [LINK] These are reports based on peer-reviewed scientific papers, that yes sometimes have to discount and dispute eachother. The presence of conflicting information does not change the overall picture of greenhouse gases like CO2 which can be tested in any simple lab. It does not change how solar input has increased over millions of years, making the planet much more sensitive to warming due to greenhouse effects. Demo: [VIDEO] If you put water on the stove and max the temperature, it is not hard to predict you don't want to keep your hand in that water for too long."},{"id":11958241,"author":"thangalin","month":"2016-06","story":"German government agrees to ban fracking indefinitely","stance":"reference","why":"Experimental demo of greenhouse effect","quote":"","text":"Predicting exact temperature is impossible. Rather, we look at previous measurements (both direct and indirect) then use our knowledge of basic chemistry and physics to do what the dinosaurs could not: envision the future. 1. Experiment. [VIDEO] 2. Measure. [LINK] 3. Extrapolate (from rate of change)."}]},"kx_G2a2hL6U":{"wy":"Used as a joke or allusion in unrelated threads, usually when names or laughter come up, not as something to learn from.","m":[{"id":38485609,"author":"nomilk","month":"2023-12","story":"Duke Libraries Drop Basecamp","stance":"reference","why":"Sketch about people finding names hilarious","quote":"This Monty Python sketch [4] has been viewed 30M times on YouTube alone","text":"If you’re being serious about it somehow being wrong to find words funny, then do you have a problem with \"absquatulate\", \"bowyangs\", \"collywobbles\", \"fartlek\", \"filibuster\", \"gongoozle\", \"hemidemisemiquaver\", and \"snollygoster“ [1] too? Presuming you don’t have a problem with words being inherently funny, why is it a surprise that some names happen to also be funny? Have you never found a name funny or amusing? How about when Ali G introduced Buzz Aldrin as Buzz Lightyear? Or when he referred to Boutros Boutros-Ghali as “Boutros Boutros Boutros Boutros-Ghali”. I found that silly and hilarious. How about when Homer refers to his “friend” Joey Jo Jo Junior Shabadoo [2], sorry, but again, it’s silly and hilarious. One more example: on Family Fued [3] the person whose name was funny found it funny, his family found it funny, and the host/audience found it funny. Do you not laugh or at least see the humour in it? Last one (promise). This Monty Python sketch [4] has been viewed 30M times on YouTube alone, it’s by the troupe of comedians that the python programming language is named after, and it’s all about people finding other people’s names hilarious. Regarding “riots” you miss the point. Duke is upset DHH uses the word riot, but Wikipedia also does. So they should be equally upset with Wikipedia. [1] [LINK] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35962461,"author":"uberman","month":"2023-05","story":"‘Bigolas Dickolas’ is more powerful than the Pulitzer Prize","stance":"reference","why":"Scene that the account name alludes to","quote":"The account name is likely an homage to the slightly NSFW Monty Python - The Life of Brian scene","text":"Impressive viral result for sure. The account name is likely an homage to the slightly NSFW Monty Python - The Life of Brian scene: [VIDEO] \" He has a wife, you know... \""},{"id":32369085,"author":"carapace","month":"2022-08","story":"NSA, NIST, and post-quantum crypto: my second lawsuit against the US government","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"Do you find it... risible?","text":"Michael Palin in Monty Python's Life of Brian, \"Do you find it... risible?\" [VIDEO] (I don't have anything constructive to add to the conversation. ¯\\_(ツ)_/¯ )"},{"id":29542935,"author":"readthenotes1","month":"2021-12","story":"Cultures where men and women don’t speak the same language (2017)","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"","text":"Since education in the classics is nearly non-existent: [VIDEO] \"Remember what punishments befell us in this world when we ourselves did not cherish learning nor transmit it to other men.\""},{"id":28468173,"author":"OJFord","month":"2021-09","story":"No laughing matter? what the Romans found funny","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"without referencing the centurion scene in Monty Python's Life of Brian","text":"Baffling that this piece manages to mention so much modern comedy, in discussing Romans , without referencing the centurion scene in Monty Python's Life of Brian , with Caesar's friend (he had a wife you know)! [VIDEO]"},{"id":23205134,"author":"mattkrause","month":"2020-05","story":"New species of fungus identified via Twitter","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"It's a joke, sir.","text":"It's a joke, sir. (That and the \"In Rome\" comment are both references to a classic Monty Python bit from The Life of Brian ( [VIDEO] )"},{"id":23326378,"author":"jungletime","month":"2020-05","story":"Trump threatens to 'close' down social media platforms","stance":"joke","why":"","quote":"See Biggus Dickus","text":"By that logic Uranus was named after your anus. See simple. And \"The Ministry of Truth\" always said true things. And the \"Vice and Virtue Ministry\" was a noble institution. [LINK] Why? because things and people are always named after what they are! See Biggus Dickus [VIDEO]"}]},"n_sNclEgQZQ":{"wy":"HN cites it as the source for how Andreessen and Horowitz explain their political shift toward the Republicans.","q":{"x":"if you watch Andreessen & Horowitz describe their thinking, it will explain their perspective [1].","i":41022292,"y":"2024"},"m":[{"id":46600279,"author":"fsloth","month":"2026-01","story":"Network of Scottish X accounts go dark amid Iran blackout","stance":"reference","why":"Andreessen explains pivot to GOP","quote":"He explains their pivot with Ben to GOP in this podcast episode.","text":"He explains their pivot with Ben to GOP in this podcast episode. The main argument was that democrats policies were detrimental to their business. [VIDEO] Mr. Andreessen has been involved with high level politics for a long time. This is not \"random radicalization\". I will not comment on the quality of the politics but it feels fairly deliberate."},{"id":44829278,"author":"lazyeye","month":"2025-08","story":"White House prepares order targeting banks for \"unbanking\" for political reasons","stance":"reference","why":"Andreessen and Horowitz discuss debanking under Biden","quote":"Andreessen/Horowitz talk about it here","text":"This was actually a significant problem during the Biden administration. The rules were so vague that de-banking could be used in whatever context the administration found convenient. Apparently many companies were pushed offshore as a result. Andreessen/Horowitz talk about it here [VIDEO]"},{"id":40990032,"author":"cal5k","month":"2024-07","story":"Against choosing your political allegiances based on who is \"pro-crypto\"","stance":"reference","why":"Likely the video Vitalik was alluding to","quote":"it seems likely he was referring to this","text":"Vitalik didn't mention them by name, but given the timing it seems likely he was referring to this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":41022292,"author":"claytongulick","month":"2024-07","story":"Ask HN: Serious) can someone ELI5 why tech elites are turning to the right?","stance":"reference","why":"Andreessen and Horowitz explain their political thinking","quote":"if you watch Andreessen & Horowitz describe their thinking, it will explain their perspective [1].","text":"I think if you watch Andreessen & Horowitz describe their thinking, it will explain their perspective [1]. For others, such as Musk, I don't think it's as much \"turning to the right\" as it is that many on the left, which used to be a bastion of civil liberties and free speech protection, have abandoned that position and embraced speech suppression. This has become so pronounced that the label \"liberal\" is pretty confusing these days, and many folks feel the need to clarify that they believe in \"liberalism\" as in \"Classical liberalism\" [2] because many modern \"liberals\" have moved away from many of those traditional beliefs. There also seems to be a strong and growing anti-capitalist sentiment in the Democrat party which has driven some business folks that were traditionally \"left\" away from that ideology. And while it may sound a bit trite, I think another factor is that they are simply getting older. The phrase \"if you're not a liberal in your 20's you have no heart, if you're not conservative in your 30's you have no brain\" has existed in some form since the 1700s [3] and been remarked on even in the American Journal of Medicine [4]. It's pretty common for people's viewpoint to change with age. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK] [4] [LINK]"},{"id":41028414,"author":"lilsoso","month":"2024-07","story":"Joe Biden stands down as Democratic candidate","stance":"reference","why":"Andreessen and Horowitz on tech policy","quote":"Not according to Marc Andreessen & Ben Horowitz [1]","text":"Not according to Marc Andreessen & Ben Horowitz [1], Chamath Palihapitiya & David Sacks [2], possibly Zuckerberg [3], and others. And of course Elon, Thiel. Many such cases. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK] [3] [LINK]"}]},"sJNxPRBvRQg":{"wy":"Cited in language-learning threads as evidence that natives often cannot handwrite kanji.","m":[{"id":39242384,"author":"bane","month":"2024-02","story":"History of Vietnamese Typography","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I've thought about this a little bit and wonder if the result CJK readers have ended up with is the result of a few interesting historic quirks. Assuming that homophones are simply going to exist in the languages, there's probably other alternatives. For example, in the case of modern Korean readers, the Hanja can supposedly used to help clarify the meaning of a homophone where context doesn't provide clarity. But in practice, most Korean readers don't use Hanja enough to remember more than a small percentage of what they learn in school, so they work in effect as an index into a lookup table, where they look the Hanja up in a dictionary, find the definition written in Hangul, and use that to determine the meaning. Support Japanese was reformed to entirely use one or both Kana in the way that modern Koreans use mostly Hangul. Then Kanji would be used to help determine meaning in the same way. As most people wouldn't encounter Kanji with enough frequency and distribution to remember the bulk of them, only the most common would be remembered. These are unlikely to be the difficult to discern homophones as contextual clues would clarify. So the Kanji would again also end up as indexes for looking up standardized definitions. In modern Japanese, this doesn't happen because Kanji is still used with enough frequency and variety that most people sustain some level of memory about the system. The question is how well do they remember it? [1][2][3][4][5] 1 - [VIDEO] 2 - [LINK] 3 - [LI"},{"id":28000375,"author":"redthrow","month":"2021-07","story":"Duolingo reaches $6.5B valuation on day of IPO","stance":"reference","why":"Natives have rough grasp of even joyo kanji","quote":"Most natives get by just fine by having a very rough understanding of even joyo [1]","text":"> Once they learn the 2000 joyo, pass N1, and probably do some tertiary education in Japan, then maybe they can choose to move that direction For the vast majority of people (including native speakers), knowing a few extra kanji outside of joyo brings very little benefit. Most natives get by just fine by having a very rough understanding of even joyo [1], thanks to smartphones and computers. Improving pitch accent, on the other hand, does change how you are perceived by natives [2], so it may be worth a try if your goal is to get closer to native speakers. [1] [VIDEO] [2] [LINK]"},{"id":13984109,"author":"Grue3","month":"2017-03","story":"The miracle of reading and writing Chinese characters","stance":"reference","why":"Japanese natives often cannot write kanji from memory","quote":"often they can't write these characters at all [1]","text":"Well, often they can't write these characters at all [1] (this video is for Japanese, but the situation must be similar with Chinese) [1] [VIDEO] I can recognize more than a thousand of characters (thanks to Anki) but visualising/writing them is another matter entirely, and I would probably fail to write a large fraction of them. Just like with alphabetic characters, I memorize the \"shape\" of the character rather than its sequence of strokes. Only when the character is indistinct, or similar to other characters do I bother to remember which radicals it consists of. For example the character for \"dream\" (夢) has a very distinctive shape that is easy to recognize, so I wouldn't be able to write it because I never learned how. Now take the character 緑 (green). It looks similar to 線 (line), but lacks the radical 白 (white). Because green is not white, you see (not actually, it's just a memorization technique). But it also looks similar to 録 (recording). That's because the character for \"green\" has radical 糸 (thread), just like \"line\" character. Threads can be green, and they are also lines. But \"recording\" uses radical 金 (metal) in its place, because recorders are made from metal. So the only things I remember about 緑 is that it has 糸 and doesn't have 白. And thus, I still won't be able to write it."},{"id":12286206,"author":"redthrow","month":"2016-08","story":"An introduction to Japanese","stance":"reference","why":"Native speakers struggle to remember kanji","quote":"I'd recommend learners to learn kana because you can learn it in a few weeks","text":"I agree, I'd recommend learners to learn kana because you can learn it in a few weeks (as opposed to many years for kanji, and even after that many years, people - including natives - don't remember them very well). [VIDEO]"}]},"t4DT3tQqgRM":{"wy":"HN cites it as the standard example of algorithmic bias in face detection and of how skewed training data produces biased tech.","q":{"x":"I also have some concerns of inadvertent \"software racism\". See for example, this video","i":6303174,"y":"2013"},"m":[{"id":35986388,"author":"shadowgovt","month":"2023-05","story":"Integrating Social Responsibility into Core CS","stance":"reference","why":"Cites HP webcam as example of biased tech","quote":"HP computers are racist\" for every meaningful (i.e. user-experienced) definition of the concept.","text":"Functions are tools for humans to cause the computer to achieve a desired output. If the function isn't generating the desired output, change the function. We can talk all day about how the history of facial recognition has used things like the eye-nose-mouth T-shape or high-contrast contours to lock in on the target, but if at the end of the day the solution works for people with light skin and not people with dark skin, \"HP computers are racist\" for every meaningful (i.e. user-experienced) definition of the concept. (reference: [VIDEO] )"},{"id":29090067,"author":"saltminer","month":"2021-11","story":"Google's infamous internal 2010 “I just want to serve 5TB” video now public","stance":"reference","why":"HP webcam failing to detect dark faces","quote":"It was HP","text":"> I can't remember which company it was that launched a camera with face identification features, but that didn't recognize any face that wasn't lilly white like every single engineer that worked at that company It was HP [VIDEO]"},{"id":24729038,"author":"Steltek","month":"2020-10","story":"Nvidia Uses AI to Slash Bandwidth on Video Calls","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"ten years ago, computers didn't think black people were humans.","text":"My first thought was about the diversity of faces used in the demo and how ten years ago, computers didn't think black people were humans. [VIDEO] But after that, I was reminded of the paranoia (or not?) around Zoom and that, for an extreme example, the CCP was mining and generating facial fingerprints and social networks using video calls. It seems like this technology is the same concept except put to a useful purpose."},{"id":9458388,"author":"mistercow","month":"2015-04","story":"The Apple Watch does not play nicely with some tattoos","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"So they might have a \"racist HP webcam","text":"Apparently optical heart rate monitors also often fail on dark skin (or have to shine a brighter light, so they run out of battery faster). So they might have a \"racist HP webcam\" ( [VIDEO] ) situation on their hands."},{"id":8412354,"author":"gus_massa","month":"2014-10","story":"Dubai detectives to get Google Glass","stance":"reference","why":"Face tracking failing on dark skin","quote":"There was a horror story about a face recognition software that didn't detect someone with black skin.","text":"I'd like to ignore the privacy aspect. I want to discuss the false positive problem. Is the technology reliable to have a low false positive rate? Probably face recognition is good enough to classify the photograph of your friends in Facebook, and the friends of your friends. It has to choose a person in a set of ~100. What happens when someone is falsely identified? Is the suspect released after a ID check or he is moved to the police station to a full check? Can this be a proxy for racial profiling? There was a horror story about a face recognition software that didn't detect someone with black skin. This can have the reverse problem. [VIDEO]"},{"id":8098847,"author":"splintercell","month":"2014-07","story":"Tracking.js － A modern approach for computer vision on the web","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"","text":"I can't get it to work(it detects my white shirt, the shoulder area as a face, but not my actual face). Is this a repetition of this: [VIDEO]"},{"id":6303174,"author":"mistercow","month":"2013-08","story":"The Face Scan Arrives","stance":"reference","why":"Webcam failing to track dark-skinned faces","quote":"I also have some concerns of inadvertent \"software racism\". See for example, this video","text":"Given the rate of false positives when \"cold hits\" are used with DNA databases, facial recognition (which is presumably higher entropy than DNA) has the potential to send a lot of innocent people to jail. I also have some concerns of inadvertent \"software racism\". See for example, this video: [VIDEO] . Obviously facial recognition isn't inherently incapable of recognizing people of different races, but if it's designed and calibrated on subjects of one race, it won't necessarily work well for subjects of different races. The last thing we want is a facial recognition database that thinks e.g. all Arabs look alike. And of course, there are a ton of ways that it can be thwarted easily and inconspicuously, even if it works very well. Are all surveillance cameras going to be fitted with IR filters to ensure that a bright IR LED necklace doesn't blind them? And how hard is it for a criminal to hire a makeup artist?"}]},"tKqJ5-kkUGk":{"wy":"HN cites it as Musk's own account of Starlink decisions regarding Ukraine and other regions, and as an example of Starlink video conferencing.","m":[{"id":39579270,"author":"frankacter","month":"2024-03","story":"SpaceX just achieved speed of 17Mb/s from satellite to stock Android phone","stance":"reference","why":"Shows Musk using Starlink for video conferencing with satellite swapping","quote":"here is video of Elon using starlink for video conferencing","text":"The nuance of this news is that it is a for a standard consumer mobile device. It is not intended to replace coverage in areas where telcos exist, rather to supplement in times of spotty coverage or emergencies. >@Elon: This service works in partnership with wireless providers, like what @SpaceX and @TMobile announced. In your scenario you would likely be sending text/sms based communication as a primary priority (ie. coordinates, instructions) with MMS as a secondary luxury. If you are permanently or frequently in an area without telco service you would want to make use of specialized hardware which is optimized for starlink communication. For example, here is video of Elon using starlink for video conferencing which demonstrates not only high upload speeds, but also quick swapping between satellites without disconnect. [VIDEO]"},{"id":38442567,"author":"maxlin","month":"2023-11","story":"Starlink will only operate in the Gaza Strip with the approval of Israel","stance":"reference","why":"Source for Musk's statement on Starlink exceptions via proper channels","quote":"","text":"This whole article should be pulled. It's (possibly intentionally) just inventing lies on key issues. \"However, he admitted that he turned off internet access from his Starlink satellites during a Ukrainian raid last year on a Russian naval fleet.\" This is horseshit. The geoblocks those the boats just careened in to were in place for a long time before, and limited to not allow access in Russia due to US sanctions! What Elon was asked to do, was to break the regulations his company is working beneath to create a major new offensive, against the then-current mindset of how US supplied Ukraine in long-term capabilities. Elon said explicitly that if it actually came through the proper channels, and not some random Ukrainian source, he would absolutely have made an exception and opened up those then-closed areas. [VIDEO]"},{"id":37541390,"author":"rlt","month":"2023-09","story":"Musk’s Denial of Ukraine’s Starlink Request Prompts Senate Probe","stance":"reference","why":"Musk's own account of the Starlink Ukraine decision","quote":"","text":"> Your Terms of Service do not superscede your host country's foreign policy goals. I must have missed the part where the United States government requested Starlink be activated in Crimea. Elon has explicitly stated if the request came from the US government he would have complied. It did not. It came from the Ukrainian government, which is not his host country. The US government knows how to contact Elon and could have done so if they wanted him to do it. [VIDEO] If Ukraine had asked SpaceX for a Falcon 9 they wanted to crash into the Kremlin should they have given it to them?"}]},"vQ5MA685ApE":{"wy":"HN treats it as the classic example of moving a live server with zero downtime, brought up whenever the topic of uninterrupted migration comes up.","q":{"x":"A real classic comes to mind","i":24069947,"y":"2020"},"m":[{"id":48584038,"author":"throw0101c","month":"2026-06","story":"AMD silently removes memory encryption from consumer Ryzen CPUs","stance":"reference","why":"Five guys move a live server via public transport","quote":"Five guys moving a server to a new datacenter without shutting it down.","text":"From a few years ago: > Five guys moving a server to a new datacenter without shutting it down. Without cutting it off from the internet. And as using a car would have been too easy, they used public transport. * [VIDEO] (DE audio, EN subs)"},{"id":25035645,"author":"z3ugma","month":"2020-11","story":"Ask HN: What is the solution to the Seinfeld “Frogger” problem?","stance":"reference","why":"Sysadmins moved powered, connected webserver across city","quote":"These sysadmins moved a webserver across their city while keeping it powered and connected to the Internet","text":"These sysadmins moved a webserver across their city while keeping it powered and connected to the Internet... [VIDEO]"},{"id":24069947,"author":"aeno","month":"2020-08","story":"Moved a server from one building to another with zero downtime","stance":"reference","why":"Classic of moving a running server by public transport","quote":"A real classic comes to mind","text":"A real classic comes to mind: [VIDEO] Moving a running server about 7km through public transport without downtime."},{"id":22062803,"author":"dijit","month":"2020-01","story":"Ask HN: Ever worked with a service that can never be restarted?","stance":"reference","why":"Example of moving a live server without downtime","quote":"Oh for sure this is possible","text":"Oh for sure this is possible: [VIDEO] the devices that do it without doing what these guys did is quite pricey though [LINK]"}]},"wO61D9x6lNY":{"wy":"Linked as a classic visualization when sphere eversion or counterintuitive mathematics comes up.","q":{"x":"Here are two classic youtube videos on the topic of sphere eversion","i":30892649,"y":"2022"},"m":[{"id":42725287,"author":"gosub100","month":"2025-01","story":"Mathematicians discover new way for spheres to 'kiss'","stance":"reference","why":"Explains turning a sphere inside out","quote":"here's a 20m video explaining how to invert a sphere without tearing it","text":"Sort of a tangent, but here's a 20m video explaining how to invert a sphere without tearing it: [VIDEO]"},{"id":30892649,"author":"ykonstant","month":"2022-04","story":"Life’s preference for symmetry is like ‘a new law of nature’","stance":"reference","why":"Classic sphere eversion video","quote":"Here are two classic youtube videos on the topic of sphere eversion","text":"Here are two classic youtube videos on the topic of sphere eversion; they mostly overlap, but I am posting both for double the hilarity in the comment sections :) (the full 21 minute one) [VIDEO] (the first 10 minutes) [LINK] If I remember correctly, I think the 10 minute one has the more hilarious comment section."},{"id":28772027,"author":"st_goliath","month":"2021-10","story":"Conterintuitive facts in mathematics, CS, and physics","stance":"reference","why":"Sphere eversion without creases","quote":"you can completely turn that sphere inside out without creating any holes or creases[1].","text":"> 19. Given a solid ball in 3‑dimensional space, there exists a decomposition of the ball into a finite number of disjoint subsets, which can then be put back together in a different way to yield two identical copies of the original ball. While you're at it, you can completely turn that sphere inside out without creating any holes or creases[1]. [1] [VIDEO]"}]},"xOQmQpKHVWA":{"wy":"Mostly cited critically as an example of a CEO deflecting blame during the 737 Max crisis.","m":[{"id":20375901,"author":"spectramax","month":"2019-07","story":"Boeing loses big order for 737 Max aircraft","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"he killed the reputation of Boeing from investors, people and customers.","text":"All airlines should cancel MAX orders. After watching the CEO defend their position in the Q2 shareholder’s meeting, he killed the reputation of Boeing from investors, people and customers. Boeing deserves nothing but financial punishment and consequences of the people that died in the two crashes. Edit: Link to the meeting [VIDEO]"},{"id":20291476,"author":"kahirsch","month":"2019-06","story":"FAA Finds New Risk on 737 Max, Orders Boeing to Make Changes","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"He was so intent on avoiding blame that he gave me no confidence that Boeing could learn from its mistakes.","text":"When the Boeing CEO had his press conference in April, he said \"we've confirmed that the MCAS system as originally designed did meet our design and safety analysis criteria and our certification criteria.\"[1] I yelled at the screen \"you know that means the criteria are flawed , right? If the procedures didn't catch this mistake, that means there are other mistakes that weren't caught!\" A flabbergasted reporter then asks him if he means to say that MCAS was designed to push the nose down 21 times. The CEO then blamed the pilots for not following procedures! He repeatedly avoids avoiding admitting that any mistakes were made. He was so intent on avoiding blame that he gave me no confidence that Boeing could learn from its mistakes. The longer he talked, the less confidence I had in Boeing as a company. I have no idea why this guy still has his job. [1] [VIDEO]"},{"id":19782944,"author":"theclaw","month":"2019-04","story":"Boeing safety system not at fault, says chief executive","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"That’s somewhat economical with the truth.","text":"Video of Muilenburg press conference: [VIDEO] He says MCAS is a “system that’s designed to provide handling qualities to the pilot that meet pilot preferences.” That’s somewhat economical with the truth. It’s designed more to meet the airline’s preference not to have to retrain their pilots, and Boeing’s preference to recover lost market share from Airbus."}]},"ynwMN3Z9Og8":{"wy":"Cited as a visual example of what bike-first infrastructure looks like, including how few helmets are worn.","q":{"x":"And if you want to see what the alternative could look like, take a look at this video","i":26926684,"y":"2021"},"m":[{"id":39299338,"author":"teekert","month":"2024-02","story":"Cyclist hit by driverless Waymo car in San Francisco, police say","stance":"reference","why":"Dutch infrastructure built for everyday cycling.","quote":"our infrastructure is build for it","text":"That is a US perspective. We don't have \"cyclists\" everybody uses a bike from time to time, our infrastructure is build for it [0], and when you get your driver’s license your learn how to deal with it. [0] [VIDEO]"},{"id":35238721,"author":"em500","month":"2023-03","story":"Cyclists Now Outnumber Motorists in City of London","stance":"reference","why":"Cyclists in Utrecht rarely wearing helmets.","quote":"Try to count the number of helmets you can spot","text":"Try to count the number of helmets you can spot: [LINK] [VIDEO] In Amsterdam if we see adult cyclists with a helmet we'd guess it's probably a tourist or new expat. For children most parents only bother with helmets until they're six years or so."},{"id":30735706,"author":"Kim_Bruning","month":"2022-03","story":"Bicycling Street Smarts, Chapter 6: Using Your Brakes (2001)","stance":"reference","why":"Utrecht cyclists with very few helmets.","quote":"Utrecht, the Netherlands. Very few helmets indeed.","text":"If you have to wear a helmet, the bicycling infrastructure isn't safe enough (yet). Traffic engineers can well and truly be proud of themselves when people simply don't bother to wear a helmet anymore (because it's not needed). See eg: * [LINK] Copenhagen: Few helmets * [LINK] Strassbourg: a bit more helmets (but not a lot) * [VIDEO] Utrecht, the Netherlands. Very few helmets indeed."},{"id":26926684,"author":"ryukafalz","month":"2021-04","story":"E-Scooters roll into New York","stance":"reference","why":"Busy Dutch cycle path showing how many people bikes move.","quote":"And if you want to see what the alternative could look like, take a look at this video","text":">Imagine how awesome scooters would be if a city had as many scooter parking spaces as it did car parking spaces, and as many scooter lanes as it did car lanes... Right, exactly. Parking would take up a fraction of the space of car parking, emissions would be lower, etc. (They don't necessarily need to be dedicated scooter lanes either; bikes are moving at similar speeds and with similar risk, so protected bike lanes would work well for both.) I could bike to just about anywhere I might want to go in the city I live in within about 35 minutes. I'm sure I'd be healthier, I'd be lowering my emissions vs. driving, and in many cases I'd get there faster than via public transport: the routes don't cover everything and bus/train service is infrequent enough that you often have to wait. I don't, because I don't feel safe enough on the streets here; they're designed with cars in mind, for high speeds. I wish we had infrastructure designed for bikes and scooters alongside the car infrastructure, because I would absolutely use them. EDIT: And if you want to see what the alternative could look like, take a look at this video and think about how many cars you would need to move all those people: [VIDEO]"}]},"zfR_Jj4grZE":{"wy":"Cited as a concise example of Apple and the wider tech industry framing devices as anything but a personal computer.","m":[{"id":43643999,"author":"nkrisc","month":"2025-04","story":"Kids can't use computers and this is why it should worry you (2013)","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"I think Apple captured this very concisely (for better or worse) in one of their ads","text":"I think Apple captured this very concisely (for better or worse) in one of their ads: [VIDEO] \"What's a computer?\""},{"id":40379504,"author":"sudosysgen","month":"2024-05","story":"Apple introduces M4 chip","stance":"reference","why":"","quote":"It's very clear that Apple is trying to make the case that the iPad Pro is a replacement for a laptop.","text":"> I'm saying this would not be an average user. The average user would not be comparing AI capabilities, they open an app that uses AI, with a high chance of not even being aware of it What is the point here? If the average user doesn't care about comparing AI capabilities, then clearly Apple wasn't targeting that user in their AI capability comparison. > What should they have compared it to? There's only one other tablet on the market, with an old chipset and a fraction of the performance, that just received its first real video editor late last year [1]. It's very clear that Apple is trying to make the case that the iPad Pro is a replacement for a laptop. In fact, that was their core marketing claim when it came out : \"What's a computer?\" [1], and arguably that's the point of their latest ads where they market it as a general purpose computer. Given the price point and the marketing, yes, they should be comparing it with a computer when making the case it's a good replacement for a computer. And when they do, they should not be misleading to the point of falsehood. [1]: [VIDEO]"},{"id":36140772,"author":"smoldesu","month":"2023-05","story":"Arm announces the Cortex X4 for 2024, plus a 14-core M2-fighter","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"They publish ads and write all their copy with the intention of framing their products as anything other than a computer.","text":"Apple is obsessed with their lifestyle designation: [VIDEO] They publish ads and write all their copy with the intention of framing their products as anything other than a computer."},{"id":28368485,"author":"nightcracker","month":"2021-08","story":"Windows 11 available on October 5","stance":"critical","why":"","quote":"There is a strong push across tech to erase the concept of a Personal Computer","text":"Yes they think this, but not in the way you or I think about it. At least this is my admittedly cynical take. There is a strong push across tech to erase the concept of a Personal Computer, and make everything function like a tablet. Calling tablets/phones PCs is part of this, by muddying the water, as well as attempts like these: [VIDEO] Interfaces and functionality keep being dumbed and locked down like they are on tablets and phones. Microsoft and Apple are watering at the mouth for the day when the true personal computer is gone and every computing device is a walled garden where every user uses their pre-approved apps doing only the pre-approved tasks, preferably as thin clients into their controlled cloud."}]}});
